1803,psychds-bgu-classi,"# Description

<p>You are given a dataset by Kickstarter.</p>
<h2>Extract at least 20 Meaningful Features</h2>
<p>This does NOT mean that the features should be of predictive value, but that they could be, and are expected to be followed by a short explanation why they could</p>
<h2>Be Original in Your Features</h2>
<p>Nothing to say here really, just be creative</p>
<h2>Make Beautiful Figures</h2>
<p>Data Viz is the only &lt;3. Extra points for extra pretty figures</p>
<h2>Compile Everything into a Research Report</h2>
<p>Do not address this as a boring university hand-in. Make it look like a nice and crisp research report that showcase what you've learned during the semester. Let the reader (me) understand what were you requested to do and how you did it. Show your progress and way of thinking. Extra points if you do this in R Markdown</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>In this task you will be evaluated mainly on your prediction accuracy, but also on your data viz and writing.</p>
<h2>Evaluation Breakdown </h2>
<p>Prediction Accuracy - 60%</p>
<p>Data Viz - 15%</p>
<p>Research Report - 25%</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Here are the train and test files. make sure you predict the **state** of the project (i.e. successful or failed) </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_class.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_class.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given project</li>
<li><strong>name</strong> - nam of the project</li>
<li><strong>category</strong> - category of the project</li>
<li><strong>main_category</strong> - main category of the project</li>
<li><strong>deadline</strong> - deadline of the project</li>
<li><strong>goal</strong> - the amount of money the project needed</li>
<li><strong>launched</strong> - when did the project launch</li>
<li><strong>state</strong> - what is the state of the project</li>
</ul>",True,True
1804,tweetssentimentanalysis2019,"# Description

<p>
The task is to build a model that will determine the polarity (neutral, positive, negative) of the text. To do this, you will need to train the model on the provided data (train.csv). The resulting model will have to determine the class (neutral, positive, negative) of new texts (test data that were not used to build the model) with maximum accuracy.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

Mean F-score is used for the evaluation","# Dataset Description

<p>Use the train dataset to build your sentiment analysis model. The description of the dataset is provided below<p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - tweet ID</li>
<li><strong>polarity</strong> - the polarity of the tweet whether it is neutral(0), positive(1) or negative(2)</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the text of the tweet</li>
</ul>",True,True
