# Evaluation, Kernels, Code Submission

<p>
The winning submission(s)  of the Sydney Innovation Week 2019 Diabetic Retinopathy Coding Challenge will be determined by their model's accuracy score on a test set of DR experimental image data.
</p>

## Kernel/Code submission and format ##
<p>
Eligible submitted software must be designed so that it can take as an input argument the path to a folder of images. The software must then output a comma-separated variable (.csv) text file whose rows contain the image filenames and corresponding predicted DR values of 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4, representing the categories of Proliferative DR (PDR), Normal Non-proliferative DR (NPDR), mild NPDR, moderate NPDR and severe NPDR, with one row per image classification (see the SampleSubmission.csv file provided on the Data page).
</p>

To be eligible for any of the prizes offered, contestants must submit to their code / software to us for evaluation (once you have made a submission to Kaggle we will provide details to upload your code). To enable us to evaluate your models, contestants must provide software consisting of a single driver script or executable which initialises and runs their model, and which takes as input a path to a folder containing the testing set of images, and outputs a single .csv file formatted the same as the SampleSubmission.csv. For example:

*myprogam.py /path/to/test/folder output.csv*

To share your code for evaluation, please fill in this form:

[https://redcap.sydney.edu.au/surveys/?s=NYLDEW7R9F](https://redcap.sydney.edu.au/surveys/?s=NYLDEW7R9F)

## Evaluation Metric ##

<p>
The evaluation metric for accuracy in this competition is **Cohen¡¯s kappa** also known as a **Quadratic Weighted Kappa**.
</p>

<p>
Cohen¡¯s kappa [1] is a score that expresses the level of agreement between two annotators on a classification problem. It is defined as

$$ \kappa = (p_o - p_e ) / (1 - p_e) $$

where \\( p_o\\) is the empirical probability of agreement on the label assigned to any sample (the observed agreement ratio), and \\( p_e\\)  is the expected agreement when both annotators assign labels randomly. \\( p_e\\) is estimated using a per-annotator empirical prior over the class labels. Values of  \\( \kappa\\) can range from -1 to 1.
</p>


  [1]: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.cohen_kappa_score.html
