# Prizes

# Prizes and Awards
The Sydney Innovation Week 2019 Diabetic Retinopathy Coding Challenge will award three prizes for the best performing submissions. Any individual or team entrant can only win one of the three prizes. In addition, prize winners must satisfy the following conditions:

 * Satisfy the eligibility requirements listed on the Rules page.

 * Winners must be able to attend the awards ceremony on Tuesday 20th August at Sydney University.

 * The code running a winning solution must be submitted to us for evaluation. See Code submission on the Evaluation page.

 * A submission must achieve a performance accuracy of at least 0.85 on the DR test dataset (see Evaluation) in order to be eligible for any prize.

 * Winners must agree to license their submitted models under a permissive Open Source license and provide copies of their winning submission and any necessary source code to the University. See IP and Attribution below.

The three prizes are categorised as follows:

### DR Prize
The author(s), as a group, of the submitted code or algorithm ('submission') which achieves the best performance on the DR experimental test dataset, as determined by the accuracy score described on the Evaluation page, will receive:

 * $7500 prize money
 * Code review and recommendation from machine learning experts at the University, or connected with the research project
 * Introductions with industry representatives who will be guests at the Showcase and Prize Evening.
 * Invitations to collaborate with Professor Ravi Seethamraju on the DR project, to implement and extend the software or model submitted.


## Kaggle Prize
The author(s), as a group, of the submitted code or algorithm ('submission') which achieves the next best performance on the DR experimental test dataset, as determined by the accuracy score described on the Evaluation page, will receive:

* $2000 prize money
* Code review and recommendation from machine learning experts at the University, or connected with the research project
* Introductions with industry representatives who will be guests at the Showcase and Prize Evening.
* Invitations to collaborate with Professor Ravi Seethamraju on the DR project, to implement and extend the software or model submitted.


## Artemis HPC Prize
The author(s), as a group, of the submitted code or algorithm ('submission') which achieves an eligible performance on the DR experimental test dataset, as determined by the accuracy score described on the Evaluation page, AND has used novel techniques and resources (especially use of the Artemis HPC) for the development of their model, will receive:

 * $500 prize money
 * Code review and recommendation from machine learning experts at the University, or connected with the research project
 * Introductions with industry representatives who will be guests at the Showcase and Prize Evening.
 * Invitations to collaborate with Professor Ravi Seethamraju on the DR project, to implement and extend the software or model submitted.

If two or more teams or individual entrants score the same Quadratic Weighted Kappa, then the prize money will be split evenly between them. Non monetary prizes will be awarded to all such winners.

Not all entrants of the Coding Challenge will be able to access the University of Sydney's Artemis HPC, as access will depend on the University's digital security policies and applicable laws governing the use of high performance computing facilities.

## IP and Attribution

The University of Sydney may use, adapt, copy, reproduce, or publish your de-identified competition entries for analytics, research and education purposes.

You hereby grant The University of Sydney all rights to any submissions you make, without any obligation to make any royalty or other payment to you, and without any obligation of credit or notice to you, unless otherwise agreed.

Any use of contestants¡¯ submissions in publications or presentations related to the DR project will be acknowledged for the contributions made.
