  [5]: https://informatics.sydney.edu.au/images/CodingChallengeImages/logo-techlab.jpg","# Dataset Description

On this page, we describe the files we have provided and the format of each. Please read this page carefully so that you are able to answer questions like the format of the training dataset and the format in which you have to upload?? your results.

**File descriptions**

The following files are available to you:

- Train.zip - the training dataset
- training-labels.csv - the file that contains the labels for the corresponding images in the training dataset (in Train.zip)
- Test.zip  - the test dataset
- SampleSubmission.csv - a sample submission file in which the outcome of your solution has to be reported


**Training Images**

To help develop your classification model, we provide a combined dataset of 14,145 images that have the classified labeling from multiple data sources. These images are either in TIFF, JPG or JPEG image formats. Hence you must be able to rea?d images in any of these formats in your model (or convert them to a consistent data format). 

Each image in the training dataset is labelled as 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 which identifies the grade of **Diabetic Retinopathy (DR)** in the respective image. This labelling of images is provided in a separate file called ""training-labels.csv"". The content of the labels file looks like this:

Filename, DRscore

train_image2.jpeg, 0

train_image3.jpeg, 3


...





**Testing-Images**

We also provide to you a set of test images to test your algorithm against and then report the results in the format specified in the file ""SampleSubmission.csv"". There are 1,000 test images in tiff, jpg and jpeg file formats acquired from multiple sources. Your solution should be able to read images in all of these formats.

It is emphasized here that images in the testing dataset (Test.zip) do not have a corresponding label file, as in the training dataset. It is precisely the objective of your model to determine the label to be assigned to each image in the testing dataset.

The testing dataset containing 1,000 images is available as a zipped file ""Test.zip""? and is approximately 1.62GB in size.


**Sample submission**

Your task is to build a submission ??file in CSV?? format containing 2 columns (**Id, Expected**) where ""Id"" is for the file-name of an image, and ""Expected"" is the diagnosis or the **prediction value between 0 & 4 provided by your model for this image**. The format of your submission is specified in the ""SampleSubmission.csv"" file whose format is given below.

Once you have read and classified each image, please compile your results in the sample submission format. Upload the file to the competition website as the final outcome of your solution. 

The contents of your solution's submission file (in CSV format) should look something like this:

Id, Expected

image1.tif, 0

image2.tif, 3

image3.tif, 1

image4.jpeg, 2

...




where? ""Id"" is for the actual file-name of an image in the testing dataset and ""Expected"" is the predicted diagnosis from your model for each image in the testing dataset. The predicted value should be a number between 0 & 4.

Note that image1, image1000 are not the actual image file-names you will see in the test dataset but are merely provided here for the purposes of representation of the idea. 

Upload the file to the competition website as the final outcome of your solution. 

For your convenience, we have created a full SampleSubmission.csv that lists the names of all the actual file-names you will find in the test dataset, under the ""Id"" column, and the ""Expected"" column contains the value 0 in each row (the value of which your model should provide). 

#Additional Data#
These data provided here are compiled from several different datasets (as outlined on the competition overview page). To train your models you can use the raw sources of these data or any datasets available beyond what we have provided.

If you are struggling to download or unzip the data directly from Kaggle, you can use this cloudstor link (which should be very reliable for anyone at an Australian Research Institute, and available until the end of the competition):

https://cloudstor.aarnet.edu.au/sender/?s=download&token=b9832b70-6054-4f18-9ec9-7fe3baa9e909",True,True
1810,hydraulic-cross-section,"# Description

The data set contains 8000 32x32 samples, please subdivide them into a training and validation trainingset, as to counter overfitting. 

The grading metric is the RMSPE (Root Mean Square Percentual Error). note that this is actual the relative error, so 1 in Kaggle is an error of 100% (weird quirk in Kaggle). This is also included in the grading rubric correctly.","# Dataset Description

The cross sections are imported via the numpy format, the input has the following 3 dimensions:

- 0th: iterator over samples
- 1st: spatial dimension 1
- 2nd: spatial dimension 2

Please keep in mind that 0 is a closed cell (no flow), and 1 is an open cell (allows flow).
The output data is a data set with all the hydraulic throughputs, they are matched by index of the 0th dimensions. So there are the following datasets:

- pub_hydraulic_cross_section_input.npy the input that indicates whether cells are open or closed
- pub_hydraulic_cross_section_output.npy the hydraulic throughputs corresponding to the open or closed cells
- pri_hydraulic_cross_section_input.npy the input for the kaggle submssions.",True,True
1811,sweden-traffic-signs-classification-eng,"# Description

<h1>Classify road signes from Sweden!</h1>
<p>This dataset is realy tiny! You will likely need transfer learning and some other datasets to increase your score.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p>Sumbmission must consist of two columns: file_name, label </p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre>file_name,label<br>picture-00747.jpg,PASS_EITHER_SIDE<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Files description:</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - train dataset</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - test dataset</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - submission example</li>
</ul>",True,True
