§µ§é§Ö§Ò§ß§Ñ§ñ §Ù§Ñ§Õ§Ñ§é§Ñ, §à§ã§ß§à§Ó§Ñ§ß§ß§Ñ§ñ §ß§Ñ §â§Ö§Ñ§Ý§î§ß§í§ç §ã§à§Ò§í§ä§Ú§ñ§ç.  
§¥§Ñ§ß §ß§Ñ§Ò§à§â §â§Ú§Õ§à§Ó (§Ü§å§ã§à§é§Ü§à§Ó §¥§¯§¬), §á§â§à §Ü§à§ä§à§â§í§Û §Ú§Ù§Ó§Ö§ã§ä§ß§à, §é§ä§à §â§Ú§Õ§í §Ò§Ñ§Ü§ä§Ö§â§Ú§Ñ§Ý§î§ß§í§Ö §Ú, §ã§Ü§à§â§Ö§Ö §Ó§ã§Ö§Ô§à, §Ú§Ù §ß§Ö§ã§Ü§à§Ý§î§Ü§Ú§ç §â§Ñ§Ù§ß§í§ç §Ó§Ú§Õ§à§Ó. §£§Ñ§ê§Ñ §Ù§Ñ§Õ§Ñ§é§Ñ - §â§Ñ§ã§á§â§Ö§Õ§Ö§Ý§Ú§ä§î §â§Ú§Õ§í §á§à §à§â§Ô§Ñ§ß§Ú§Ù§Þ§Ñ§Þ. 
<h2>Data</h2>
<p>You are provided with a file with nucleotide reads - chunks of genomes of some organisms. Each read is a sequence of 200 letters, each one is either A, C, G or T.  We already know that there are 3 different bacterial species, and our aim is to divide all reads into 3 groups, one group for each genome.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, we know nothing about the species inhabiting our turtle. But there could be something similar between reads of one genome. What could it be? Discuss it with your colleagues and then test your hypothesis on the training dataset. Use your solution to solve the test dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is accuracy. This metric is commonly used in ML,  it is a number of correct answers divided by the overall number of answers. 
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: sequence pair Id (the same as in a test.csv file) and ""YES"" or ""NO"" depending on whether this pair of reads came from one genome. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id, Are from one genome<br />1, YES<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2, NO<br />etc.</span></p","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seq1</strong> - nucleotide sequence of the first read</li>
<li><strong>Seq2</strong> - nucltotide sequence of the second read</li>
<li><strong>Are from one genome</strong> - the answer</li>
</ul>",True,True
