1878,synthetic-image-classification,"# Description

## Intro

In this competition, you'll write an algorithm to determine which of several classes (""styles"") an image belongs to.

## Data

That data is a collection of images labelled with styles.  The styles have been named after cities.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19806%2F1bf9702fb3f8f961f749c3c57774c718%2Fstyles.png?generation=1563103892173024&alt=media)


The images are 64px x 32px.  They are several overlapping circles in different colours.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by CategorizationAccuracy (percentage of correct styles).

## Submission Format

```
id,style_name
1000000,Sydney
1000001,Brisbane
1000002,Melbourne
```","# Dataset Description

## Sample Submission

The submission file should look like:
<pre>
id,style_name
9000000,Brisbane
9000001,Sydney
9000002,Melbourne
9000003,Geneva
9000004,Geneva
9000005,Beijing
</pre>

## Overview 

The data is all in `synimg.zip`.
The files are:

- `synimg/`
  - `test/` -  the unlabelled data, 20000 images
  - `train/` - the labelled data, 100000 images
  - `readme.txt` - brief readme
  - `styles.txt` - list of categories

## train/ - directory format:
The `train/` directory contains image files divided into directories by style.

- `data.csv`
- `Beijing/`
 - `test-Beijing-10100000.jpg`
 - `test-Beijing-10100001.jpg`
 - ...
- `Brisbane/`
  - ...
- ...

## test/ - directory format
The `test/` directory contains image files divided into directories, not by style.  (`A`, `B`, etc. are just splitting up the files so there aren't too many in one directory.  They don't have any meaning).

- `data_nostyle.csv`
- `A/`
 - `test-A-9000320.jpg`
 - `test-A-9000680.jpg`
 - ...
- `B/`
  - `test-B-9000181.jpg`
  - ...
- ...

## CSV files

The CSV files (`data.csv` for train, and `data_nostyle.csv` for test) contain the ids and filenames of the images.  The training CSV also contains the styles for the images.  


### train/data.csv
<pre>
id,style_name,file,filepath
1000000,Luanda,train-Luanda-1000000.jpg,synimg/train/Luanda/train-Luanda-1000000.jpg
1000001,Luanda,train-Luanda-1000001.jpg,synimg/train/Luanda/train-Luanda-1000001.jpg
1000002,Luanda,train-Luanda-1000002.jpg,synimg/train/Luanda/train-Luanda-1000002.jpg
1000003,Luanda,train-Luanda-1000003.jpg,synimg/train/Luanda/train-Luanda-1000003.jpg
</pre>
### test/data_nostyle.csv
<pre>
id,file,filepath
9000000,test-A-9000000.jpg,synimg/test/A/test-A-9000000.jpg
9000001,test-B-9000001.jpg,synimg/test/B/test-B-9000001.jpg
9000002,test-C-9000002.jpg,synimg/test/C/test-C-9000002.jpg
9000003,test-D-9000003.jpg,synimg/test/D/test-D-9000003.jpg
</pre>",True,True
1879,dsupscale2019,"# Description

![Fake GPS Trip][1]
#Problem Definition
Among our drivers, there are drivers who use Fake GPS application to mock their location.
This FGPS usage is unfair for other GOJEK drivers who work honestly. 
Hence, we would like to apply a machine learning model to classify whether a trip is being done using fake GPS or not based on their PING behavior. 


  [1]: https://i.ibb.co/T449wDZ/Screen-Shot-2019-04-12-at-2-46-37-AM.png
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Score

The evaluation will be scored based on Accuracy.","# Dataset Description

# Description 
You are provided with an anonymized dataset of drivers PING signals containing numeric feature variables, the binary target column, and a string order_id column.

The task is to predict the value of label column in the test set.

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>order_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given order number</li>
<li><strong>service_type</strong> - service type, can be GORIDE or GOFOOD</li>
<li><strong>driver_status</strong> - status of the driver PING, can be AVAILABLE, UNAVAILABLE, OTW_PICKUP, OTW_DROPOFF</li>
<li><strong>hour</strong> - hour </li>
<li><strong>seconds</strong> - seconds in linux format</li>
<li><strong>latitude</strong> - GPS latitude </li>
<li><strong>longitude</strong> - GPS longitude </li>
<li><strong>altitude_in_meters</strong> - GPS Altitude </li>
<li><strong>accuracy_in_meters</strong> - GPS Accuracy, the smaller the more accurate </li>
</ul>",True,True
