1889,unsw-datasoc-sydney-round,"# Description

<h1> Tasks </h1>
<h3> Part A </h3>
Develop a model to predict whether an individual will have a stroke in 2018 or not. Please refer to the **Data** tab above for more information on the dataset. We recommend using multiple data points.

Hint: 
- Think about what factors may influence the possibility of a stroke (beyond the straightforward (smoker status). Things like; age, gender, marital status, residency, etc should all be considered)
- We also recommend using visualisation to gather insights that you can include in your report as graphical aids.

***The scores from Part A will determine your leaderboard ranking.***

<h3>Part B</h3>
Determine which treatment, or combination of treatments are the most effective in helping to prevent stroke for those with a heart condition. To progress to the presentation round, your code and report (part C) must address this section. 

<h3>Part C</h3> You must create a 1-2 page report summarising your methodology, findings and recommendations. This will be helpful if your team makes it to the final round of presentations (top 10) as determined by **Part A.** 
You can find more information about variables in the Data Dictionary file located in the **Data** tab above. 

<h1>Submissions</h1>
**Please note that each team is restricted to a maximum of just 20 submissions. We highly recommend you use these wisely and spend a significant amount of time on in-depth data analysis.**

**The top 10 teams will now be selected based on Kaggle Rank, Code Audit and Report.**

**All members of presenting teams must attend the presentations.**

<h1>Important Ranking Information</h1>
**Please note that the leaderboard is calculated with approximately 60% of the test data. The final results will be based on the other 40%, so the FINAL STANDINGS MAY BE DIFFERENT.**
----------------------
# Timeline

<h1>Timeline</h1>
Deadline: **10AM Sunday 21st July** 

*Please note that you are only permitted 20 submissions maximum per team*
----------------------
# Software & Technologies

<h1> Software & Technologies </h1>
You are free to use whatever tools and technologies you have at your disposal.
 
Commonly used languages for data science challenges include **Python** and **R**.

If you do not have a programming environment of your own, you may create a Kernel and work locally in Kaggle (please see the '**Kernels**' tab above).

***If you require assistance or advice with choosing a suitable working environment, please speak to a DataSoc official and they will gladly help you out.***
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h1> Evaluation Method </h1>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precision_and_recall"">precision p and recall r</a>. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every individual in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns, named as below.
<p>The file should contain a header and have the follo
wing format:</p>
<pre>id,stroke_in_2018<br />33327,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">839,0<br />11127,0 <br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">...,...<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set to build your model</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set to make your predictions/submissions on</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>data_dictionary.csv</strong> - contains information about variables</li>

</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given individual</li>
<li><strong>sex and age</strong> - sex and age of individual</li>
<li><strong>high_BP</strong> - indicates whether the individual has high blood pressure or not (hypertension)</li>
<li><strong>heart_condition_detected_2017
</strong> - indicates whether the individual had a heart condition in 2017</li>
<li><strong> married </strong> - indicates whether the individual is married or not</li>
<li><strong>job_status and living_area
</strong> - information about the individual's work life and living area</li>
<li><strong> average_blood_sugar </strong> - average blood sugar level of the individual</li>
<li><strong>BMI</strong> - Individual's body-mass index</li>
<li><strong> smoker_status </strong> - information about the individual's smoking history </li>
<li><strong>Treatment A to D</strong> - indicates whether or not the individual is currently undergoing any of 4 possible treatment programs</li>
<li><strong>stroke_in_2018</strong> - indicates whether or not the patient suffered a stroke during 2018. This is the target variable you must predict in the testing data (test.csv) </li>

</ul>",True,True
