<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - una submission de ejemplo en el formato correcto </li>
</ul>",True,True
1892,kddbr-2019,"# Description

This competition is part of the 3rd [KDD-BR](http://www.bracis2019.ufba.br/kdd.html) (Brazilian Knowledge Discovery in Databases competition) event, one of the joint activities of the 2019 editions of [BRACIS](http://www.bracis2019.ufba.br/) and [ENIAC](http://www.bracis2019.ufba.br/eniac.html). 


## Challenge objective

The objective of this competition is to predict the similarity between the partitions produced by the manual clustering of a set of molecular markers and those obtained by an auto-clustering tool.  An auto-clustered scatterplot that is identical to its manual clustered counterpart receives a score of 1, while an auto-clustered scatterplot that is completely non-identical to its manual counterpart receives a 0 score. 

## Background information

The advances in the genotyping technologies over the last decades opened the way for innumerous new applications in which the main challenge is to understand the meaning behind the DNA sequence of individuals or species. In agriculture, for example, making associations between genes and desired traits (characteristics) is key in the search for healthier and more productive crops.  

Say, for example, that a gene G is known to be associated with the resistance to a certain disease D in corn. Consider that the corn plant is disease resistant when the genotypic state of gene G is either homozygous AA or heterozygous AB, and that the plant is susceptible to that disease when genotypic state is homozygous BB. If agronomists are looking for plants resistance to disease D, they should only focus on plants that are either homozygous AA or heterozygous AB, and discard the other ones.  When there was no knowledge about the DNA information of organisms, the only way of testing a trait (e.g., disease resistance) was to put plants in fields and observe the phenotype. Now, with the DNA information, agronomists do not need to go to the field to answer that question if they know the gene of interest. They can run a genotyping test for gene G, for example, and determine which plants are disease resistant based on lab results. This does not only save time, but also natural resources, such as ground and water. 

Using this principle, Corteva Agrisciences analyzes millions of DNA samples every year. This work helps determine whether desired traits have been integrated into products under development and ensures the quality of products sold to customers.

## How does the data look like?

The genotyping output for a specific marker in a certain plant is composed of two signals whose magnitudes are displayed as X and Y coordinates. The signal magnitudes are then used to infer genotypic state of the sample (out of the three possible states - homozygous AA; homozygous BB; or heterozygous AB). The idea is that homozygous AA and homozygous BB will have very distinct signals from one another, and that heterozygous AB will be somewhere in between.

To allow high throughput, this process is not performed for every plant individually. Instead, batches of 384 plants are analyzed at a time. When the two signals produced for every plant in the batch are plotted against each other as an XY scatterplot, three distinct clusters are observed corresponding to the three possible states present among the plants (homozygous AA; homozygous BB; or heterozygous AB). To infer the state of each plant, the clusters must be identified and labeled correctly.  The state of each plant is then inferred from the cluster in which its data point appears.

Clustering may be performed by humans (manual clustering) or by computers (auto-clustering). Manual clustering has proven to be more accurate than auto-clustering, but is much more labor intensive. In order to use auto-clustering effectively it is necessary to identify scatterplots from auto-clustering that have been clustered poorly and submit them for manual review. 

This challenge is seeking a model that can automatically produce a score that reflects the quality of the auto-clustering tool.  As training data, solvers will be provided with numerous example scatterplots with clusters identified by the auto-clustering tool, and a score that reflects how well it approximates manual clustering.  An auto-clustered scatterplot that is identical to its manual clustered counterpart receives a score of 1, while an auto-clustered scatterplot that is completely non-identical to its manual counterpart receives a 0 score.

## Caveats

If all three possible genotypes (AA, BB, and AB) are represented among the 384 plants presented in a scatterplot, three clusters are indeed expected.  There are numerous cases for which only two or one genotypes are represented among the 384 plants, and in these cases only two or one cluster would be expected.

Also, for technical reasons that are well understood but difficult to mitigate, the AB target state will under some circumstances generate two clusters.

## Competition Organizers (alphabetic order)

Ana Carolina Lorena, Computer Science Professor at Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA)
Eduardo de Paula Costa, Associate Research Scientist at Corteva Agriscience 
Elaine Ribeiro de Faria, Computer Science Professor at Universidade Federal de Uberlandia (UFU)
Filipe Alves Neto Verri, Computer Science Professor at Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA)
Ricardo Ara¨²jo Rios, Computer Science Professor at Universidade Federal da Bahia (UFBA)
Ricardo Cerri, Computer Science Professor at Universidade Federal de S?o Carlos (UFSCar)

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the [Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation), which is a measure of difference between two continuous variables. 

## Deadline

The submissions will be closed on October 1st, 2019 (Anywhere on Earth). Therefore, the final results will be based on the solutions posted until October 1st, 2019. Please note that the system will still be able to receive submissions after this deadline, but they will be not taken into account for computing the final results.

## Award Session

As this competition is part of the joint activities of the 2019 editions of the Brazilian Conference on Intelligent Systems (BRACIS) and the Encontro Nacional de Intelig¨ºncia Artificial e Computacional (ENIAC), the top three teams will be invited to present their solutions at a competition award session, during the period 15-18 October, 2019. The actual final positions of the ranking will be disclosed during the conference dinner. 

In this year's edition, we require the models to be made available publicly to be considered in the award session. The competition organizers will get in touch with the top-3 teams once the submission deadline is met.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: scatterplotID and Score. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>scatterplotID,Score<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,0.8<br />9,0.9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,0.1 <br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

## Training Dataset

The complete training dataset comprises 80,000 scatterplots x (up to) 384 datapoints.  There are 7 columns in the training dataset:

1. scatterplotID: unique integer value for each scatterplot. The key to the classifications.
2. datapointID: unique integer value for each datapoint within a scatterplot.  Ranges from 1:384. Datapoints from missing/defective samples are excluded.
3. sampletype: character, either ""Sample"" for samples or ""Negative"" for negative controls.
4. signalX: integer, the X signal value for the datapoint. Values typically range from 0 to 10,000, but values less than 0 and greater than 10,000 occur.
5. signalY: integer, the Y signal value for the datapoint, otherwise like signalX.
6. cluster: character, the cluster to which the datapoint was assigned by auto-clustering.  Values are ""X"", ""Y"", ""XY"", ""E"", ""L"".
7. silhouette, numeric, the silhouette value for each datapoint(Rousseeuw, PJ, 1987, J. Comp. Appl. Math. 20: 53-65).  Values range from -1 ot 1, inclusive. Datapoints assigned to cluster ""L"" receive an arbitrary value of -2.


## Training Data Labels

The training classifications comprises 80,000 rows x 4 columns.  Each row represents an entire scatterplot.  All 80,000 scatterplots provided in the four training datasets described above are present in this one file.  The 4 columns follow:

1. scatterplotID: unique integer value for each scatterplot. The key to the datasets.
2. score: a numeric value ranging from 0 to 1, inclusive, that indicates similiarity between manual clustering and auto-clustering.  An auto-clustered scatterplot that is identical to its manual clustered counterpart receives a 1.  An auto-clustered scatterplot that is completely non-identical to its manual counterpart receives a 0.


## Testing Dataset

The testing dataset comprises 20,000 scatterplots x (up to) 384 datapoints.  This dataset is provided as a single file.  The 7 columns are identical to those in the training dataset.


## Submissions

A sample random submission is provided as example. Is must contain two columns: 

1. scatterplotID: unique integer value for each scatterplot of the test set. 
2. score: a numeric value ranging from 0 to 1 to be predicted in the competion.",True,True
1893,credit-hackathon-2019-test,"# Description

<h2>Teams</h2>
Only one submission and account by team.

<h2>Acknowledgemnts</h2>
<p>We thank Bankia for providing the dataset</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

