<li><strong>StartHour</strong> - Hour at which the task starts</li>
<li><strong>Start_Time</strong> - Time at which the task starts</li>
<li><strong>tat_in_days</strong> - Days to complete Task</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>",True,True
1905,isfog2020-pile-driving-predictions,"# Description

<h1>ISFOG 2020 - Pile driving prediction event</h1>
<h2>INTRODUCTION</h2>
<p>Data science techniques are rapidly transforming businesses in a broad range of sectors. While marketing and social applications have received most attention to date, geotechnical engineering can also benefit from data science tools that are now readily available.</p>
<p>In the context of the <a href=""https://www.isfog2020.org/"">ISFOG2020 conference</a> in Austin, TX, a prediction event is launched which invites geotechnical engineers to share knowledge and gain hands-on experience with machine learning models.</p>
<p>This document outlines the scope of the ISFOG2020 data science prediction event which formulates pile driving predictions as a machine learning problem.</p>

<h2>PROBLEM STATEMENT<h2>
<h3>Prediction problem</h3>
<p>The problem proposed for the prediction event is the prediction of blowcount vs depth for jacket piles in North Sea soil conditions. In current engineering practice, blowcounts are derived using a three-step approach:<p>
<ul>
<li>Predict the soil resistance vs depth from geotechnical data (Alm and Hamre, 2001; Stevens et al., 1982);</li>
<li>Calculate a bearing graph from 1D wave equation analysis for selected depths (Smith, 1960);</li>
<li>Interpolate the bearing graphs to derive blowcounts.</li></ul>
<p>This is a fairly complicated approach to relate soil properties, pile dimensions and hammer settings to the target variable, being the blowcount.</p>
<p>This prediction event will explore whether machine learning models can be trained on a set of pile driving records to provide reliable estimates of blowcount for unseen locations.</p>
<h3>Data set</h3>
<p>The available dataset consists of installation records for 114 piles in the North Sea and contains the following data sources:</p>
<ul><li>CPT profiles with cone tip resistance, sleeve friction and pore pressure measurements;</li>
<li>Pile outer diameter and wall thickness;</li>
<li>Blowcount and hammer energy.</li></ul>
<p>To facilitate participation and keep a focus on the data science techniques, he data has already been processed (Figure 1) and mapped to a regular grid with nodes every 0.5m.</p>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3228850%2F46be9fb26dd6db0d50f087554422b73c%2FScreenshot%202019-08-01%20at%2020.59.50.png?generation=1564686020367643&alt=media)
<center><strong>Figure 1: Results of data processing and grid mapping
routine for one location</strong></center>
<p>For the prediction event, the data set is partitioned into a training dataset of 94 piles and a validation data set of 20 piles. Only the training data will be provided, the validation set will be used to assess the quality of participant¡¯s models.<p>
<h2>GUIDELINES FOR PARTICIPATION</h2>
<h3>Target audience</h3>
<p>This prediction event targets individuals or groups with an interest in data science techniques. Although some knowledge of the techniques can be beneficial, we also invite people who have no prior experience with machine learning.</p>
<p>To facilitate participation, a tutorial which demonstrates the basics of getting started with data science and building a machine learning model for the pile driving problem is made available to participants through an <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/brunostuyts/isfog-2020-linear-model-demo"">example Kernel</a> . The tutorial uses Python and the scikit-learn machine learning library.</p>
<p>Participants are encouraged to use a combination of data science techniques and engineering knowledge to come up with the best possible model.</p>
<h3>Submitting contributions</h3>
<p>Contributions can be submitted via this Kaggle In-class competition.</p>
<p>Participants are also requested to submit a summary of their reasoning for developing the model. A short explanation on which type of model was used and whether any engineering knowledge was used is required</p>
<p>Although participants are not required to submit kernels containing their models, they are invited to do so to further share knowledge and insights.</p>
<p>Submissions will be ranked based on a RMSE metric. Note that the public leaderboard which will be visible during the competition is only based on a part of the test dataset. The final scores based on the full test dataset will be revealed once the competition ends.</p>
<h3>Summary and presentation of results</h3>
<p>All predictions will be evaluated using the validation dataset of 20 piles excluded from the data provided. The lessons learnt from the prediction event and the best models will be discussed in an ISFOG2020 paper.</p>
<h3>Timeframe</h3>
<p>The prediction event runs for a period of 8 months, from April to December 2019. This time frame is long enough to allow teams to build up their models iteratively.</p>
<h3>Communication and support platform</h3>
<p>During the prediction event, there will be frequent communication to encourage contributors, provide links to machine learning tutorials, provide hints for improving models and discuss problems people might be facing.</p>
<p>A discussion can be started via the Kaggle platform.</p>
<h2>REFERENCES</h2>
<ul><li>Alm, T., Hamre, L., 2001. Soil model for pile driveability predictions based on CPT interpretations. Presented at the International Conference On Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering.</li>
<li>Smith, E.A.L., 1960. Pile driving analysis by the wave equation. Soil mechnanics and foundations division - ASCE 24/01/1900.</li>
<li>Stevens, R.S., Wiltsie, E.A., Turton, T.H., 1982. Evaluating Pile Drivability for Hard Clay, Very Dense Sand, and Rock. Presented at the OTC 4205, 14th Annual Offshore Technology Conference, Houston, Texas, pp. 465¨C469.</li></ul>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank <a href=""https://cathiegroup.com/"">Cathie Group</a> for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Scoring metric</h2>
<p>A RMSE scoring metric is used.<p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The submission files should contain two columns: <i>ID</i> and <i>Blowcount [Blows/m]</i>. A sample submission file is provided in the Data section.</p>
----------------------
# Timeline

This competition runs until the end of 2019.
----------------------
# Prizes

The top 3 participants will be commended at the ISFOG 2020 conference in Austin Texas.","# Dataset Description

<p>The pile driving process requires input on the soil, the hammer settings and the pile characteristics. </p<p>The full data set consists of piezocone penetration test data (PCPT), recorded hammer energies and pile characteristics at 114 pile foundation locations. The data is provided vs depth and is resampled to a regular grid with a vertical spacing of 0.5m.</p>
<p>The 114 foundation locations are separated into 94 locations which can be used to train models and 20 locations which will be used to score submissions of the participants.</p>
<p>The pile driving process starts after the pile has penetrated some distance into the ground so blank values are possible at shallow depth. Obviously, no driving data is available beyond final penetration but the soil data is still provided, should participants be interested to create additional features based on the soil data below the pile tip. Data files with blank values removed are provided for the training and test data set.</p>
<p>For the sake of completeness, the PCPT data used for resampling to the regular grid is included at higher depth resolution.</p>
<p>Normalised properties are also provided (Qt, Fr and Bq) as well as the soil behaviour type index Ic according to Robertson and Wride.
</p>
<p>Because the interdistance between pile locations may play a role, a file is provided which classifies the interdistance between each location pair. This file can be used if participants wish to explore spatial correlation between locations.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>training_data.csv</strong> - the training data set with PCPT data, hammer data, pile data and the observed blowcount vs depth (0.5m depth intervals) at 94 pile foundation locations</li>
<li><strong>training_data_cleaned.csv</strong> - the training data set with rows with NaN values removed</li>
<li><strong>validation_data.csv</strong> - the test set with PCPT data, hammer data and pile data vs depth (0.5m depth intervals) at 20 pile foundation locations, blowcounts need to be predicted for these piles</li>
<li><strong>validation_data_cleaned.csv</strong> - the test data set with rows with NaN values removed</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - A sample submission (for the linear model developed in the tutorial) with the required columns <i>ID</i> and <i>Blowcount [Blows/m]</i></li>
<li><strong>full_cpt_training_data.csv.csv</strong> - full PCPT data for the training data locations, this PCPT data has a higher depth resolution</li>
<li><strong>full_cpt_validation_data.csv.csv</strong> - full PCPT data for the validation data locations, this PCPT data has a higher depth resolution</li>
<li><strong>interdistance_data.csv</strong> - Information on the interdistance between pile locations. Each interdistance between each location pair is categorized into an interdistance category</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - a unique ID combining the location name and the depth at which data is provided</li>
<li><strong>z [m]</strong> - depth below mudline</li>
<li><strong>qc [MPa]</strong> - Cone tip resistance</li>
<li><strong>fs [MPa]</strong> - Sleeve friction</li>
<li><strong>u2 [MPa]</strong> - Pore pressure behind the cone</li>
<li><strong>Location ID</strong> - Anonymized location ID</li>
<li><strong>Normalised ENTHRU [-]</strong> - Energy transmitted to the pile. Normalised to be between 0 and 1</li>
<li><strong>Normalised hammer energy [-]</strong> - Energy provided by the hammer. Normalised to be between 0 and 1</li>
<li><strong>Diameter [m]</strong> - Diameter of the pile at the selected depth</li>
<li><strong>Bottom wall thickness [mm]</strong> - Wall thickness at the bottom of the pile</li>
<li><strong>Pile penetration [m]</strong> - Final penetration of the pile below mudline</li>
<li><strong>Blowcount [Blows/m]</strong> - Number of blows required for an additional meter of pile penetration. This describes the rate of pile penetration.</li>
<li><strong>Number of blows</strong> - Total number of blows to reach the selected depth</li>
</ul>",True,True
1906,ds100su19,"# Description

This is the Kaggle competition for UC Berkeley Data 100 Project 2, Summer 2019. Problem statement is available on the project files. There is no private test set; all test data is viewable via Kaggle.

First, join the competition by clicking the ""Join Competition"" button.

To submit predictions, use the blue ""Submit Predictions"" button on the top right.
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will be graded on your accuracy on the test data, as indicated in the project notebook. However, since we do not want students to see the test data labels, we compute your accuracy on the test data in a hidden test. This Kaggle submission is for you to see what your accuracy on the test data is. Note that the accuracy shown by Kaggle is the accuracy on a random 30% of the test data, not all of it.

It is not required for you to submit to Kaggle to receive credit for the project; Kaggle is merely a way for you to check what your test accuracy is, since our project notebook will not tell you.","# Dataset Description

Spam/ham data.

- id : observation ID
- subject : email subject
- email : email text
- spam : 0 (ham) or 1 (spam), not in test.csv",True,True
1907,subscription-of-a-term-deposit-dav,"# Description

<p>In this competition the idea is to predict if a customer would accept an offer of a term deposit or not. For this purpose, the data set contains demographic, social and financial data and the result of similar offers in the past. 
The competition is about making the best model for the classification problem measure by AUC. 
</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>This information is not real and was made for the class.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic""> AUC</a>. 
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and y. 
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,y<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,1<br />3,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">4,1<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>
This collection of data is about a marketing campaign of term deposit to a random set of customers of a bank. Now we want to know how the new customers would behave to the same offer in order to prioritize them.
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>description.txt</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
</ul>",True,True
