1942,ece590cifar,"# Description

## Description
It is exciting to design an image classifier for the CIFAR-10 task. You objective is to build a powerful neural network to achieve high accuracy on the CIFAR-10 classification dataset.

## Memory Limit
3 GB GPU memory limit for each neural network during training.

## Submission Limit
5 submission a day.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation
We will use the top-1 categorical accuracy as the evaluation metric. We will release your test result on 50% of the test dataset on the public leaderboard for you to further improve your model.
Your final result is decided by the remaining 50% of the data in the test dataset.
----------------------
# Submission Deadline

## Submission Deadline
We will accept your submission until 10/02/2019. We will use your best test score as the final result.
----------------------
# Grading & Extra Credit

## Grading
You will receive full credit if you can achieve more than 80\% accuracy.

## Extra Credit

* An extra 5 points will be awarded if your test accuracy goes beyond 85%.
* Another extra 5 points will be awarded if your test accuracy goes beyond 90%.","# Dataset Description

## CIFAR-10 Dataset
The CIFAR-10 dataset consists of 60000 32x32 colour images in 10 classes, with 6000 images per class. There are 50000 training images and 10000 test images. 

The dataset is divided into five training batches and one test batch, each with 10000 images. The test batch contains exactly 1000 randomly-selected images from each class. The training batches contain the remaining images in random order, but some training batches may contain more images from one class than another. Between them, the training batches contain exactly 5000 images from each class. 

We have modified the whole CIFAR-10 dataset a bit for its usage on ECE590 course. We resplitted the dataset and released 50,000 images for training and validation. We hold out 10,000 images for final evaluation on the Kaggle platform. Note that the dataset split is a little different from the original dataset split.

## Reference
Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images, Alex Krizhevsky, 2009.",True,True
