1954,predict-ctcf-binding,"# Description

The data here is [ChIP-seq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChIP-sequencing) of [CTCF](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CTCF) in the [A549](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A549_cell) lung cancer cell line. 

You're provided with training data for all chromosomes apart from chr1, which is reserved as final test data. You can split the training data into train and validation sets however you like, including doing cross-validation. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation will be using the Average Precision (AP) metric, which is identical to auPR (area under the precision-recall curve) assuming constant interpolation of the PR curve. AP is more meaningful (better dynamic range) than auROC (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve) when there is substantial class imbalance. By construction our dataset doesn't have bad class imbalance, but if you took the whole genome as background then it would. 
The submission should be a csv file with two columns: Id (0-7688) and Predicted, a continuous value in [0,1] denoting the probability of CTCF binding (although note since we're using AP any continuous value is actually fine, just the relative rank of the predictions is important).","# Dataset Description

See the Assignment 1 notebook for the data.",True,True
