1998,synthessence-engineer-2019,"# Description

<p>Welcome to Synthessence, the competitive Machine Learning event, hosted as a part of Engineer 2019. Engineer is National Institute of Technology Karnataka's technical fest.</p><b>IMPORTANT</b><p>Please ensure that you fill this form to register for this event: https://forms.gle/vd55sjGXxKQUouAz6 </p>

<h2>About the competition</h2>
<p>You will be provided with historical data, values collected nearly daily across several years. Your goal is to be able to forecast future values for dates given in the testing data.</p>

**For more information regarding the formats of the data files and submission file format, visit the Data tab of the competition.**

**Information regarding the evaluation metric, competition timeline and prizes can be viewed by clicking on the appropriate tab in the pane to the left of this article.**

<h2> A note about the leaderboard </h2>

While the competition is active, you are only evaluated on 30% of the test data, as is the norm for most Kaggle competitions. This is done to prevent any malpractice or manual manipulation in order to improve the score.

Once the submission deadline expires, the private leaderboard will reflect the true standings. **You may choose upto 2 of your submission files from the My Submissions page for final evaluation post the deadline.** In case you do not, your best submissions on the Public Leaderboard are automatically used for the Private Leaderboard

**Final standings will be based on the Private Leaderboard**


----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation""> RMSE</a>.  RMSE is a frequently used measure of the differences between values (sample or population values) predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed. For more information regarding the mathematical equation of RMSE please refer to the link above :p </p>

<p>RMSE is always non-negative, and a value of 0 (almost never achieved in practice) would indicate a perfect fit to the data. In general, a lower RMSE is better than a higher one. However, comparisons across different types of data would be invalid because the measure is dependent on the scale of the numbers used.

RMSE is the square root of the average of squared errors. The effect of each error on RMSE is proportional to the size of the squared error</p>
<br>

<hr />
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: Date and Value. The date column should contain the dates for which you are going to be predicting values and the Value column contains the respective predicted value. Date should be in the format yyyy-mm-dd.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Date,Value<br />2010-09-16,24<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2010-09-17,25<br />2010-09-20,26<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2010-09-21,27<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

Competition Launch: 17th October 12AM IST

Competition End: 20th October 9PM IST
----------------------
# Prizes

1st: Rs 6500 /-
2nd: Rs 2500 /-
3rd: Rs 1500 /-

**Note**: Read the competition rules for prize eligibility.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>date</strong> - Date when the value was obtained</li>
<li><strong>value</strong> - Collected value</li>
</ul>

The **date** field is available to you in both the training set and testing set.

The **value** field depicts the value obtained on each date, basically reflecting the historical time-series data. It is only available in the training set. Your goal is to forecast the value for the test set timestamps (dates)

<h2> Submission Instructions </h2>
Please refer to the sample_submission.csv file for the correct format of your csv file submission. Note that you need to add the column headers and ensure that you have made the correct number of predictions, otherwise your solution cannot be evaluated.",True,True
