2062,buad831fall2019,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the University of Delaware MBA Fall 2019 forecasting competition. Daily sales data for two products sold in two stores are provided for the time period Jan 1, 2012 - May 30, 2013 (note, data is missing for Dec 25, 2012 as the stores were closed that day for the Christmas holiday). The stores are both part of a very large national retailer of grocery products. Your task is to use this historical data and any other information you deem relevant (e.g. US holidays) to forecast daily sales for the period Jun 1, 2013 - July 30, 2013.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanAbsoluteError"" target=""_blank"">Mean Absolute Error</a>. The mean absolute error is a common measure of forecast error in time series analysis and is sometimes known as ""mean absolute deviation"". Mean absolute error (MAE) is defined as.:</p>
<p>\[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left| y_i - \hat{y_i}\right| =\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left| e_i \right|.\]</p>
<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>The error term is \[ |e_i| = |y_i-\hat{y_i}| \]</li>
<li>Actual units sold are \[y_i\]</li>
<li>Predicted units sold are \[\hat{y_i}\]</li>
<li>The number of forecasts made is \[n\]</li>
</ul>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Your submission file must have a header and should be structured in the following format. The id column is a triplet representing a store_nbr, item_nbr, and date. Form the id by concatenating these (in that order) with an underscore. E.g. ""2_1_2013-06-15"" represents store 2, item 1, sold on 2013-06-25. The units column is your submitted forecast. Your csv file should have one header row plus 120 forecast rows (2 stores * 2 products * 30 days = 120 forecasts).</p>
<pre>id, units<br>1_1_2013-06-01,120<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>You have been provided with sales data for 2 products sold in two different retail locations of the same large national grocery chain.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - daily sales data (Jan 2012 - May 2013) for all stores &amp; dates in the training set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format (you will need to change the units from zero to do better in the competition)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>date </strong>- the day of sales</li>
<li><strong>store_nbr</strong> - an id representing one of two stores</li>
<li><strong>items_nbr</strong> - an id representing one of two products</li>
<li><strong>units</strong> - the quantity sold of an item on a given day</li>
<li><strong>id</strong> - a triplet representing a store_nbr, item_nbr, and date. Form the id by concatenating these (in that order) with an underscore. E.g. ""2_1_2013-06-15"" represents store 2, item 1, sold on June 15, 2013.</li>
</ul>",True,True
2063,stat6031-crime-project,"# Description

<p> This is a project assignment for STAT 6031 Applied Statistics I in Fall2019 at University of Cincinnati. Students can work on this project on his/her own or in teams, but each team should consist of NO MORE THAN 2 members.

<p>The dependent variable is the <pre>Street Robbery Percentage</pre> 
That is, you will always predicting the ""Street Robbery Percentage.""</p>

<p> You are encouraged to try the following combinations of predictors and compare the performance of the resulting models:</p>

 1.  Numeric predictors
 2. Qualitative predictors
 3. All predictors
 4. Any predictors: You can also construct new predictors using those in the data, and use all or some of these predictors. The sky is the limit!

There are several **required components** in this project:

 - **Model selection**: Please carefully document the models you have tried and how you decide between them.
 - **Model interpretation**: Interpret the final model you choose, and produce visualizations for diagnostics. It is extremely important to investigate related questions, including but not limited to,
 (i) whether there is statistical evidence to relate adding bus stations to the change of the robbery percentage. 
(ii) whether there is any relationship between the robbery rate and (some) explanatory variables, as you may have expected, such as housing vacancy rate and unemployment rate.
 - **Linear regression**: You are required to build a regression model that give the best RMSE at the test dataset as you can.
*You can later build and apply other type of models such as LASSO, CNN, XGBOOST, but the project score will be deducted if you failed to try, build, present or write in the report a regression model. With this said, if you are able to try another two additional models, you can get up to 15 extra points for your project.* 
 - **Prediction competition**: You are given both a training and testing dataset. You can submit your predictions on the test dataset to Kaggle and compete with others in terms of root mean squared error (RMSE).

 **Deliverables** 

All the following components are **required**.

 - **Code**: You should submit your code and make sure all your results are reproducible! I will setup box folder for you to submit your code. *Without reproducible code submitted, 0 point will be assigned to the team.*
 - **Presentation**: Each team will present its results in the last week of the fall semester. The presentation is expected to be 15min with 3min for F&A. You can (not required) use Google Slides or overleaf to prepare slides for easy collaboration. The slides should be sent to the Instructor before the deadline.
 - **Report**: Each must submit to gradescope a 8-10 page report (single-spaced, font size 11 or 12) on this project. This report should be self-contained. You should explain your team's attempts at building models, model selection, and model interpretation. You can (not required) use Google Docs (or Overleaf if you use Latex) for easy collaboration. The report should be sent to the Instructor before deadline. *For teamwork, the team should also include a **cover page** with description on each member's contributions and with all members' signatures.*

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Professor Lin Liu, Ph.D., from UC Department of Geography for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>training_unchanged.csv</strong> - the training set consisting of data at locations where the bus stops don't change.</li>
<li><strong>training_addition.csv</strong> - the training set consisting of data at locations where a new bus stop has been added.</li>
<li><strong>testing_without_key.csv</strong> - the test data set, where I have masked out the 2013 robbery rate percentage.</li>
<li><strong>Robbery_SampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<p>The dataset contains information about over street robbery rate in some areas in Cincinnati in 2010 and 2013. These data are collected to study whether and how an added bus stop is associated with the local robbery crime rate. There are two datasets, one consisting of data for some locations with a bus stop added between 2010 and 2013, the other consisting of data for locations without added bus stops but within a similar neighborhood (so that we can use them as ""control"" together with the previous dataset as ""treatment""). Both datasets contain the same set of variables. Please note that the variable ""OBJECTID"" is just an index and should not be used directly as a covariate in regression model, since it cannot provide interpretability. 

Your goal is to build a model to explain Street Robbery rate and study how this is related to other variables, including addition of a bus stop. Note that although the testing data I only require you to predict  2013 street robbery rate, such predictions are not the primary or sole goal of this project. So you will need to think about the questions your would like to address and may need to re-organize the data before fitting the models. The CSV files contain self-explanatory columns.</p>

<p> It is OK to perform some transformation. However, when you upload your submission, you need to provide predictions of street robbery pct for 2013 directly without transformation.</p>

</ul>",True,True
