2069,cse512hw4,"# Description

Kaggle page for HW4
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Classification Accuracy.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every test sample in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Category. Id is the same as the image name, and Category is the predicted label, which can take a value between 1 - 5.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Category<br />1-1.jpg,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">1-2.jpg,3<br />3-1.jpg,5 <br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">100-1.jpg,2<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train_Features.pkl</strong> - training set features</li>
<li><strong>Val_Features.pkl</strong> - Validation set features</li>
<li><strong>Test_Features.pkl</strong> - Test Set features</li>
<li><strong>Train_Labels.csv</strong> - training set labels</li>
<li><strong>Val_Labels.csv</strong> - Validation set labels</li>
<li><strong>Train_images96</strong> - Training set images</li>
<li><strong>Val_images96</strong> - Validation set images</li>
<li><strong>Val_Features.csv</strong> - Validation set features</li>
<li><strong>Train_Features.csv</strong> - Train set features </li>
<li><strong>Test_Features.csv</strong> - Test set features </li>
</ul>
<p>
To load the pickle files in Python 2.7, try:
<li>import pickle</li>
<li>TrainFileName = 'Train_Features.pkl' </li>
<li>Train_Features = pickle.load(open(TrainFileName,'rb'))</li> `
</p>
<p> In case you are using Matlab, you can read the csv Feature file as:
<li>name = 'Val_Features.csv'</li>
<li>fid = fopen(name,'r');</li>
<li>while ~feof(fid)</li>
    <li>tline = fgetl(fid);</li>
 <li>%use strplit to split the line, first cell contains the id, rest 512 cells contain the 512-d feature</li>
    <li>disp(tline)</li>
<li>end</li>
<li>fclose(fid);</li>
</p>",True,True
2070,csep546-aut19-kc1,"# Description

# To Spam or not to Spam, that is the question
## Description
Modify the data loading code to load SMSSpamCollection_test.

Build the best model you can using any of the learning algorithms you developed so far. Do any feature engineering you like.

Submit your answers to our competition, check the leader board, and iterate according to the submission rules.
## What is Kaggle?
Kaggle is a platform for hosting and competing in Machine/Deep Learning competitions. We'll be using it to host our own in-class competitions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Criteria
We will be grading on the accuracy of your model, i.e. how many spam and how many ham emails you classify out of all the test emails. 
[IMPORTANT] Keep in mind that only 50% of the test classifications are used for the Leaderboard, but we will be using 100% of the test classification when evaluating the final results.

# Submission attempts
You will be able to submit up to two models a day and your place will be updated on the Leaderboard. (Resets on the next UTC day [5PM PST].)

# Submission instructions
Get [SMSSpamKaggleSupport.py](https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/csep546/19au/assignments/SMSSpamKaggleSupport.py) from the course web site and use that to load and format in your workflow.
 
NOTE: Contrary to what it says in `README_Test.txt`, please submit classifications in the form 
`[ID],[<0/1>]` with `ID,<0/1>` at the very top. NO SPACES OR KAGGLE CRIES. For example:
```
ID,<0/1>
0,1
1,0
2,1
3,1
4,0
...
``` 
----------------------
# Timeline

## Oct 15 - 11:59pm - Start Date
## Oct 27 - 11:59pm - End Date
Daily submissions reset at 5PM PST each day.
Please start early!
----------------------
# Grading

See the course canvas site for [grading details.](https://canvas.uw.edu/courses/1331659/assignments/4924790)","# Dataset Description

# File Description
`SMSSpamCollection` - training data (same as from hw1)

`SMSSpamCollection_test` - unlabeled training data
#Data layout
## Fields
**Classification** - whether it's spam or ham

**ID** - The ID number for the message on the test data

**SMS Message Text** - the message to be classified

## Training data
`[Classification]<tab>[SMS Message Text]`
## Test data
`[ID]<space>[SMS Message Text]`",True,True
