2086,kriti2019cna,"# Description

## Indian Engineering Graduates Employability Rate
> **""More than 75% of the Engineering graduates in India are not fit to be employed.""**

The government of India needs to prioritize higher education and undertake long term policy interventions in the next 5-10 years to ameliorate the low rate of engineering employability.  
<br>
To understand the reasons for such low employability rates, XYZEE, an Analytics company has surveyed a sample of Engineering graduates about their performance history. The company wants to deploy an application for the Engineering graduates to predict what would be their expected salary after Graduation. 
<br>
You, as a Data Scientist have to prepare a predictive model for XYZEE and predict the salary of an Engineer based on some parameters defining their past performance.

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coefficient_of_determination""> R-squared</a>. The R2 socre or Coefficient of determination score, commonly used in regression problems is the proportion of the variance in the dependent variable that is predictable from the independent variable(s).
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Submission files should contain two columns: ID and Salary. Salary should be a real number list. Every ID counts as his/her unique Identification id. There are 999 test points in the testing_dataset. Therefore, there will be 1000 rows (including the header file).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,Salary<br />0,250000<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">1,80000<br />2,97601<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">3,554716<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h1>About the Dataset</h1>

- An anonymized training dataset **training_data.csv** of 3000 engineering students of India consisting of 35 features (incl. Salary). 
- You have to predict the salary of engineers on an unseen **testing_dataset.csv** based on the remaining 34 features.
- The description of the features can be found in the **data_dictionary.xlsx** file provided below.
- The submission csv file should contain the headers **[¡®ID¡¯, ¡®Salary¡¯]**. You can also find the sample submission **sample.csv**
- A portion of IDs in the **test_data.csv** is used to evaluate a Public score while remaining others to evaluate Private score.
- You will be able to see the Public score as soon as you upload a submission on the competition site.

### Do check out the Rules tab before proceeding.
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>training_data.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>testing_data.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>data_dictionary.xlsx</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
Find the same in data_dictionary.xlsx
</ul>

Suggest you to download all files at your local PC",True,True
