2089,nodeb,"# Description

<h2> Overview </h2>
<p>Welcome to Project 2, Node B!</p>
<p>We're going to be predicting which companies will leave a company (1) and which will stay (0). The data for this competition is in the Data tab above!</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every employee (row) in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: id and Attrition. The ""id"" column should correspond to the index number in the testing set¡ªwe'll show you how to do this easily in python using to_csv!</p>
<p> Attrition should be 0 if you are predicting the employee stayed, and 1 if you are predicting the employee quit. Your model should do this for you! </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,Attrition<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,0<br />3,0
<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">411, 0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2> Overview </h2>
<p>On this page are the two data files needed for this competition. </p>
<p> <b> train.csv </b>contains all 34 X variables, as well as the Y variable, employee attrition. Use this to train your model!</p>
<p><b>test.csv</b> contains the same 34 X variables, but without they Y. Use your trained model to predict the Y values! These values are what you will submit ¡ª more details about this on the submission page.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
These are pretty self-explanatory since the columns are intuitive and well-named, but Slack us with any questions :)
</ul>",True,True
2090,homework-3-part-2-11-785-fall-2019,"# Description

<h2>HW3P2</h2>
<p>
This is the Kaggle competition for hw3p2.
The WSJ dataset will be used for this contest. We are going to be using unaligned labels in this contest. That means you are given the list of phonemes for each utterance, but not which frames correspond to which phonemes.
Inference of the alignment will require using CTC loss.
</p>

<h2>What does the data look like?</h2>
<p>
Input data is an array of utterances, each of which is shaped (frames, frequencies).

Labels are simply the list of phonemes in the utterance [0-45]. There are 46 phoneme labels. The phoneme array will be as long as however many phonemes are in the utterance. We provide a lookup mapping each phoneme to a single character for the purposes of this competition. Example below:
</p>

The first sentence in the development set is the following:

<code>THE FEMALE PRODUCES A LITTER OF TWO TO FOUR YOUNG IN NOVEMBER AND DECEMBER</code>

<p>The first item in the dev datafile is an array of ints. If you were to print those integers using our map of phonemes to characters, you would print the following:</p>

<code>.DhfImElpRhdUsizhlitrhvtUtifoR?hGinnOvembrAnddisembr_.
</code>

<p>If you sound it out, you can convince yourself that these are the phonemes for the sentence above.<p>

<h2>The Task</h2>
<p>
Predict the phonemes contained in utterances in the test set. You are not given aligned phonemes in the training data, and you are not asked to produce alignment for the test data.
</p>





----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>
You will be evaluated using Kaggle's character-level string edit distance. Since we mapped each phoneme to a single character, that means you are being evaluated on phoneme edit distance.
</p>

We are using Levenshtein distance, which counts how many additions deletions and modifications are required to make one sequence into another.

Read about Levenshtein Distance [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance)

<h2>Submission Format</h2>

Your submission should be a CSV file. The headers should be ""Id"", and ""Predicted"". - Id refers to the 0-based index of utterance in the test set - Predicted is the phoneme string. See the sample submission for more details","# Dataset Description

<h2>Data Files</h2>
Data files are available for download from this page.

<h2>File Descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li>wsj0_test.npy - test features</li>
<li>wsj0_train.npy - train features</li>
<li>wsj0_dev.npy - development/validation features</li>
<li>wsj0_train_merged_labels.npy - training labels</li>
<li>wsj0_dev_merged_labels.npy - development/validation labels</li>
<li>sample_submission.csv - sample submission in correct format. Submission is a single silence phoneme (""."") for each utterance.</li>
<li>phoneme_list.py - List of phoneme full names, and list of phoneme single-character encodings.</li>

<h2>Feature Files</h2>
Same data layout as previously.

<h2>Label Files</h2>
Array length is the number of utterances. Each item in the array is an array that is the length of the number of phonemes in that utterance. The labels are [0-45], 46 phonemes.",True,True
