2122,pae-fashion-mnist-2019,"# Description

Fashion-MNIST is a dataset that is meant to be a direct replacement of the overused MNIST dataset. It is first introduced by Han Xiao and Kashif Rasul and Roland Vollgraf in their paper Fashion-MNIST: a Novel Image Dataset for Benchmarking Machine Learning Algorithms.

Each data point in the Fashion-MNIST dataset is a 28x28 image flattened out as a vector of length 784 (just like MNIST). If you perform reshape to each vector and visualize the 28x28 image, you will see that the images are one of the following 10 items

T-shirt/top
Trouser
Pullover
Dress
Coat
Sandal
Shirt
Sneaker
Bag
Ankle boot

The above 10 items are labelled with integers from 0 to 9 respectively.
The objective of the task is to come up with an algorithm to assign a label to a given image (a given 784 length vector) that matches with the true label.
----------------------
# Evaluation

As a classification task, the evaluation of the submissions will be based on the percentage of correctly classified samples.

To make a submission, you need to write out your predicted labels for each on the data point in the test_image matrix as a CSV file, in the following format:
Id,Category
0,2
1,3
2,9
Note that the first row is the header. The first column is the index of the data point, which should be 0-4999 since we have 5000 test samples. The second column is the predicted label.",# Dataset Description,True,True
2123,nanoperando-amiens-2019,"# Description

Segment tomography images and have fun!

Competition rules are indicated in the page 'Workflow' below

Contact: arnaud.demortiere@u-picardie.fr
----------------------
# Workflow

**1. Labelling:**
1. Download train1.tif and train2.tif in the page 'Data'
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2Fc3476f5db46e914ee10ee1111cc5dd7a%2Fdown_data.png?generation=1574181372387026&alt=media)

2. Open Fiji --> Plugins --> Segmentation --> Trainable Weka Segmentation (2D) --> Open the Image you start
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2Fc291a32c7531dbb59c88a70cdd7342bd%2Fselect_weka.png?generation=1574181463235283&alt=media)

3. Add one more class by clicking on 'Create new class'
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2F94c1e2f6505fb010bb1d195c5d4a6c39%2Fcreate_class.png?generation=1574181534450685&alt=media)

4. In the dialog of 'Settings', select all the filters
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2Fe244aed9bc44536c30cd140bf3440035%2Ffilters.png?generation=1574181602000953&alt=media)

5. You can change the name for classes (not mandatory, the name will be saved as integer number at the end when you create result)
 (par convention, mandatory) 
**1.NMC 2.carbon 3.vide**
6. Choose a selection tool and start labelling, click 'Train classifier' to train your machine learning model (Random Forest)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2F74e245a0a6e96644e0e78e2bcee34a83%2Fselect_tool.png?generation=1574181976389077&alt=media)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2F68e2adfc5548765ba44b98da241c8997%2Flabelling.png?generation=1574182199336437&alt=media)


7. Use 'Toggle overlay' to help your labelling
8. Save your result by clicking 'create result' then 'File --> save as --> {use your name}1.tif'

**2. Train a Convolutional Neural Network**
1. Go to the page 'Notebooks' and fork the solution notebook
2. Upload your labelling by 'File'-->'Add or upload data'
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2F11c993e23caddc05c35d45989f85e042%2Fupload.png?generation=1574183256270486&alt=media)
2-bis (optional). Or you can relabelling to re-submit (limitation: 20 times per day, server GPU quota 30hours/week)
2-bisbis. (optional). Try different hyper-parameters to improve your score.
3. Post your question/or snapshot in the page 'discussions' **if you encounter a bug or error that you can't resolve**

**3. Submission:**
1. Click on the 'Commit' bottom on the top right corner of the notebook
2. The calculation will be continued even you accidentally close the page
3. Once you see green 'Complete' click on 'Open Version'
4. Scroll down. If your notebook catches no error or bug, there should be a 'submit the prediction' bottom for the submission
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2Fc39d8b25a56ba83711913fccf54b2dc6%2Fsubmit.png?generation=1574183462679696&alt=media)


**Finally**
Make open access your notebook by clicking on the up right corner Access.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1539429%2F63fa5387e4489cfbfd2efa9d0d5c3392%2Fopen_access.png?generation=1574183720902595&alt=media)

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>**Pixel-wise segmentation (semantic segmentation):**<p>
<p> Each pixel has a integer value which indicates its class. By convention, 0 represents NMC, 1 for carbone et 2 for pores. <p>
<p>The **Accuracy / Score** on the 'Leaderboard' is calculated by dividing the number of pixel matched the ground truth which that you can see. The **first place** on the will be rewarded by a gift sponsorized Arnaud. <p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Contents:
 .tif file of tomography for segmenting<p>
<h2>Fichier description</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>trainX.tif</strong> - nano-CT</li>
<li><strong>testX.tif</strong> - for prediction (phase contrast technique)</li>
<li><strong>SEM_X.tif</strong> - SEM images examples</li>

</ul>
<h2>Instructions: different domain to segment</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nickel Manganese Cobalt Oxide (NMC)</strong> - the most absorbent in X-ray. So brighter since a minus log is applied during reconstruction</li>
<li><strong>Carbone-Binder(polymer) Domain (CBD)</strong> - Less absorbent</li>
<li><strong>Pore (vide)</strong> - the weakest</li>

</ul>",True,True
2124,rpi6100,"# Description

The goal of this in-class challenge is to provide the students with hands-on experience (besides the project of their choosing) and also to be introduced to the Kaggle world. 

The main goal is to use all methods learned in class to solve a time series forecasting challenge. 

Keep in mind that a one-page report (template provided in LMS) is expected at the end of the course (11:59pm UTC, December 11th, 2019) detailing the methods employed and which ones seemed to perform better in the challenge.
----------------------
# Evaluation

A file similar to the dataSample.csv should be submitted (do not forget the headers!). Kaggle will compute the mean square error on 30% of the values you provided versus the real ones. Whereas this value will count towards the public leaderboard, the final score will be done only once the competition is closed and it will be the mean square error on the remaining 70%.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File description</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>dataTrain.csv</strong> - the training set</li>",True,True
2125,padhai-hindi-vow-cons-classification,"# Description

<h2>About Us</h2>

<p><a href=""https://padhai.onefourthlabs.in/"">One Fourth Labs</a> is an IIT Madras incubated startup with a goal to make India ready for the AI age. We want to skill Indian workforce in the areas of Artificial Intelligence (AI) at almost one-tenth the industry upskilling price. </p>

<p>Our flagship online school <strong>PadhAI</strong> provides India-specific courses on AI, and is open to all students, faculty, and professionals with a basic background in mathematics and python</p>

<h2>Task</h2>

<p>The goal is to identify the vowel and the consonant of each character image using Convolutional Neural Networks. </p>

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>

<p>Submissions are evaluated on <strong>Accuracy Score</strong> between the predicted and the actual labels on test dataset</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy"">Accuracy Score.</a>
</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every image in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ImageId and Class. ImageId should be the file name in test folder (Eg. 1.png). Class value should be the vowel class respectively followed by a underscore and consonant class (Eg. V0_C0)
</p><p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>

<pre>ImageId,Class<br>5.png,V1_C5<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>

<p>
</p><li><strong>train</strong> - The training set.</li><p></p>

<p>You can find the vowel label (number immediately after V) and consonant label (number immediately after C)  for each image from the image file name.</p>

<p>Eg. <strong>V3_C5_1.png</strong> implies that this image belongs to Vowel 3 and Consonant 5</p>

<p>Refer <a href=""http://bit.ly/vowel-consonant-mapping"" rel=""nofollow"">this sheet</a> (select Hindi Sheet) to know about the vowel consonant mapping. </p>

<p><strong>Path in Kaggle Kernel: </strong>""../input/train/train""</p> 

<hr>

<p></p><li><strong>test</strong> - The test set.</li><p></p>

<p>This folder contains the images to be classified.</p>

<p>Class value should be the vowel class respectively followed by a underscore and consonant class (Eg. V0_C0)</p>

<p>Use image name for ImageId (eg. 23.png )</p>

<p><strong>Path in Kaggle Kernel: </strong>""../input/test/test""</p> 

<hr>

<p></p><li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - A sample submission file in the correct format. All data points are labelled with V0_C0 in this file.</li><p></p>

<p><strong>Path in Kaggle Kernel: </strong>""../input/sample_submission.csv'""</p> 

<hr>",True,True
