2154,ml-f19,"# Description

<p>We often face the problem of searching meaningful emails from thousands of promotional emails. This challenge focuses on creating a multi-class classifier that can classify an email into four classes based on the metadata extracted from the email.</p>
<p>See data description to know more about the data.</p>
<p>Train and test csvs have been removed. Data can be accessed in the new version of the course at https://www.kaggle.com/c/2el1730-machinelearning</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score"">F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every email in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Id and label.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,label<br />0,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">1,0<br />2,1<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">3,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>skeleton_code.py</strong> - a skeleton python script to read train and test data and write output to the submission file.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - the id of the email</li>
<li><strong>date</strong> - date and time at which mail was received</li>
<li><strong>org</strong> - the organisation of the sender</li>
<li><strong>tld</strong> - top-level domain of the sender's organisation</li>
<li><strong>ccs</strong> - number of people cc'd in the email</li>
<li><strong>bcced</strong> - is the receiver bcc'd in the email</li>
<li><strong>mail_type</strong> - the type of the mail body</li>
<li><strong>images</strong> - number of images in the mail body</li>
<li><strong>urls</strong> - number of urls in the mail body</li>
<li><strong>salutations</strong> - is salutation used in the email?</li>
<li><strong>designation</strong> - is designation of the sender mentioned in the email?</li>
<li><strong>chars_in_subject</strong> - number of characters in the email's subject</li>
<li><strong>chars_in_body</strong> - number of characters in the email's body</li>
<li><strong>label</strong> - the label of the email</li>
</ul>",True,True
2155,itmo-spelling-correction-autumn-2019,"# Description

# ITMO Spelling Correction Autumn Contest 2019

It is important for search engine correct misspelled user queries. You'll try to solve a little bit simplified version of this problem. You need to build system for correction individual words.

## Conditions

You can get at most 25 points for this assignment.

* Solutions worse than ?No Fixes At All? gets 0 points.
* There is a limit on the number of submits per day.
* You can¡¯t get points for other students solutions.

## Partial Solutions

* Prefix tree and heuristics from lecture ¡ª 10 points.
* Phonetic algorithms ¡ª 3 points.
* Language model ¡ª 2 points.
* Error model ¡ª 5 points (all levels).
* Cool heuristics ¡ª 5 points (at least two).

To get your earned points you need:

* Get a solution with score higher than ?No Fixes At All? on private dataset.
* Send to me code of your solution, brief description in plain English (or Russian) and the kaggle handle by e-mail.
* If you want to get extra points for cool heuristics, you need to further describe why you think they are really cool.

## Contacts
Send your solution to: InformationRetrievalCourse@gmail.com
Questions to: d.ilvokhin@corp.mail.ru
If I do not answer for a long time (a couple of days) or if you have something urgent, write to [Telegram][1].

  [1]: https://t.me/r3tsky
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Target metric

The target metric is the average [Levenshtein distance] [1] between your correction and the correction that we consider appropriate for this word for all words in the dataset.

You can download script for local evaluation of your submission in section with data.

  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance","# Dataset Description

# Available FIles

* no_fix.submission.csv ¡ª an example of a correctly formed submit file. Not a single word is corrected in this submission. It is expected that the quality metric of any solution will be higher than the quality metric of this submission.
* train.csv ¡ª file with labeled data. Id is the original word, Expected is the expected correction of the word (if correction is not required, the value is equal to value in Id.
* words.csv ¡ª file with the frequencies of all words (labeled and not labeled). Id is the original word, Frequency is the frequency with which the word occurred in X days in the Search logs.",True,True
2156,whiskey-201911,"# Description

## Objective

Your objective is to train a text classification model to analyze textual reviews of certain whiskeys in order to predict the type of whiskey being reviewed.

## Background

The training dataset contains textual reviews of different kinds of whiskeys, and the category of whiskey being reviewed (1: Scotch, 2: Bourbon/Tennessee, 3: Craft, 4: Canadian). 

However the training dataset contains textual reviews, but no categories. So your job is to produce a solutions file that contains your model's predicted category for each of the reviews in the training set.

## Data Dictionary

The competition data has been obtained from the [Whiskey Advocate](http://whiskyadvocate.com/).

### Files

<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

### Columns

<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - the review's unique identifier</li>
<li><strong>description</strong> the review text itself</li>
<li><strong>category</strong> the type of whiskey being reviewed (1: Scotch, 2: Bourbon/Tennessee, 3: Craft, 4: Canadian)</li>
</ul>




----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Files</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data Dictionary</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - the review's unique identifier</li>
<li><strong>description</strong> the review text itself</li>
<li><strong>category</strong> the type of whiskey being reviewed</li>
</ul>",True,True
