2172,f1-boost,"# Description

**NOTICE: The competition is now over.**

----

**Hurry! The competition has started already on 8 Nov 2019 at 12:00 AM and will be over on 14 Nov 2019 at 11:59 PM UTC.**

Register now to participate: https://forms.gle/8rKAgVXu7RSU56kg9

**NOTE:** It is compulsory to fill the aforementioned form to be considered a valid candidate.

**Late registration is allowed. The last date to register is 12 Nov 2019.**

----

Rainfall data is essential for water resource planning and research. The motive of this competitive hackathon is to build data-driven solutions which can predict the amount of rainfall in a district.

Entire datasets created over 100 years for several districts have been provided to participants. The participants must train a model which can predict the amount of rainfall a district is going to receive in future. The best models will be awarded with prizes.

This competitive hackathon is being organised by Developers Student Club, IIT Bhilai during its annual techno-cultural fest Meraz this year, and is being sponsored by C-DAC.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Read carefully the description of the data provided in the [Data Description](https://www.kaggle.com/c/f1-boost/data).

Kaggle Leaderboard will use RMSE evaluation metric.
Please note that Kaggle Leaderboard **is not** the only criteria for deciding winners.

**The entire source code for the operation of model will also be analysed by the judges.**

# Judging Criteria
+ **Innovation:** How unique is the concept of the solution?
+ **Use of data:** How well does the solution use the data provided?
+ **Use of technology:** How well does the solution use technology?
+ **Readability of Code:** How readable and well formatted is the source code?
+ **Feasibility:** How easily can the solution be implemented?
+ **Evaluation Metrics:** RMSE

The decision of the judges will be considered final and binding.
----------------------
# Timeline

| Date, Time | Info |
| --- | --- |
| 1 Nov 2019 | Registration Open |
| 8 Nov 2019, 12:00 AM UTC | Competition Begin |
| 12 Nov 2019, 11:59 PM UTC | Registration Closed |
| 14 Nov 2019, 11:59 PM UTC | Competition End |
| | Evaluation of submissions |
| | Result declaration |
| | Prize distribution |
----------------------
# Prizes

**You must fill this form to be considered a valid participant and to avail prizes**: https://forms.gle/8rKAgVXu7RSU56kg9

Total prize pool worth Rs. 40,000.

**Prizes will be awarded to 1st, 2nd and 3rd ranked teams.**

Note that the prize pool worth can change without any notice.

Note that only Indian students studying in Indian universities can avail prizes. Once you have won, you will be asked to present college ID.
----------------------
# Kernels Requirements

**Programming language must be Python.**

The participants are free to use popular libraries that provide Python API, e.g. TensorFlow.","# Dataset Description

<html>
 <body>
  <h1>Input</h1>
  <p>
   There are four districts:
  </p>
  <ul>
   <li>
    District 1
   </li>
   <li>
    District 2
   </li>
   <li>
    District 3
   </li>
   <li>
    District 4
   </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
   The amount rainfall has been provided for each of these districts for each month from Jan 1901 to Dec 1999, as CSV files:
  </p>
  <table>
   <thead>
    <tr>
     <th>
      District
     </th>
     <th>
      CSV file
     </th>
    </tr>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <tr>
     <td>
      District 1
     </td>
     <td>
      <code>district_1.csv</code>
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      District 2
     </td>
     <td>
      <code>district_2.csv</code>
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      District 3
     </td>
     <td>
      <code>district_3.csv</code>
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      District 4
     </td>
     <td>
      <code>district_4.csv</code>
     </td>
    </tr>
   </tbody>
  </table>
  <h1 id=""output"">
   Output
  </h1>
  <p>
   You have to predict the amount of rainfall for each of these districts for the months from Jan 2000 to Dec 2002.
  </p>
  <p>
   You will have to submit your predictions in the following format:
  </p>
  <table>
   <thead>
    <tr>
     <th>
      ID
     </th>
     <th>
      rainfall
     </th>
    </tr>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <tr>
     <td>
      1
     </td>
     <td>
      Your prediction for the month, year, district mapped to ID 1
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      2
     </td>
     <td>
      Your prediction for the month, year, district mapped to ID 2
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      3
     </td>
     <td>
      Your prediction for the month, year, district mapped to ID 3
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      :
     </td>
     <td>
      :
     </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
     <td>
      144
     </td>
     <td>
      Your prediction for the month, year, district mapped to ID 144
     </td>
    </tr>
   </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
   Where each ID is mapped to a specific district, a specific month and a specific year. These ID mappings have been provided in the file
   <code>
    id_mapping.txt
   </code>
   .
  </p>
  <p>
   <strong>
    Your submission must follow these rules:
   </strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
   <li>
    Must be a valid CSV file
   </li>
   <li>
    Must have exactly two columns, named ¡°ID¡± and ¡°rainfall¡± (case-sensitive).
   </li>
   <li>
    Must have 144 rows, each row representing a prediction for unique ID.
   </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
   An examplary submission file has been provided to take a look at: <code>sample_submission.csv</code>
  </p>
 </body>
</html>",True,True
