2174,priceestimation,"# Description

# The homework assignment 02

## MLDL 2019 Fall @ GIST

Price estimation with linear regressors

**(Note: this is an individual basis assignment!)**

## Task
Given a set of features and their price, please train your linear models to regress the price with given features. The price is a real-valued number.

## Evaluation metric
*Root mean squared error*: Root mean squared errors between your prediction and the true values.

## Submission
* Prerequisite: Accept github classroom invitation for coding assignment 2
* Write a report (latex recommended) about your methods
* Push the final version of your code to the github classroom repo
* Submit your result in kaggle competition in the leader board

## Grading criteria
* Final leaderboard ranking
* Novelty of the tried methods
* Number of tried methods
* Completeness of the report
* Code quality

## Preferred packages
Scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/
PyTorch: https://pytorch.org/

## Reference
1. Jupyter Notebook tutorial: https://www.dataquest.io/blog/jupyter-notebook-tutorial/
2. CoLab tutorial we gave (please find it in the Piazza resource tab)
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is **RMSE**. The Kaggle platform requires you to generate a submission text file that contains each test sample's `id+time` denoted as **id** for simplicity and the corresponding results from your model. We ask you to print the predicted price as the result. Please keep reading about the format of the submission text file.

## Submission Format

Your submission file should be in a csv (column separated value) format. The file extension is `.csv`. It should have two columns; `id` (note that the `id` here is `id`+`time` (concatenation) of the original data) and `price` which you've predicted. The first row of your submission file is a header saying `id,price` and the subsequent rows should contain the id (id+time) that is ordered as in the `price_data_ts.csv`. For example, your sample submission file should look like:

    id,price
    700010085020140926T000000,4028000
    403740028020140923T000000,9389000","# Dataset Description

Use your `price_data_ts.csv` to generate the submission `.csv` file.",True,True
2175,jhbhouseprices,"# Description

Welcome to the Explore Data Science Academy Johannesburg property prices regression challenge!

In this challenge, the goal will be to predict the list prices of properties in JHB and the surrounding areas.

You will probably have come across other similar competitions; however, what makes this one tricky is the limited data available to contestants.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

## Data source
Data was scraped from a variety of web sources.

## Files
The dataset has been split into two separate files, `train.csv` and `test.csv`:

 - `house_prices_train.csv`:  739 different properties, each with 8 variables (includes listing price).
 - `house_prices_test.csv`: 150 different properties, each with 7 variables (excludes listing price)",True,True
2176,resturant-rating-prediction,"# Description

<p>This competition is an in-class competition for the course ""Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (Fall 2019)"" in Renmin University of China.</p>
<p>The competition is to predict the overall rating for a resturant based on its meta information as well as customer reviews. </p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Ramanathan Perumal for providing this dataset. The data was scraped from Zomato. All copyrights for the data is owned by Zomato Media Pvt. Ltd..</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>This data set contains detailed information about resturants in Bengaluru, e.g. name, address, type, cuisines, menu, etc., as well as reviews by customers. The data was scraped from Zomato web site in 2015. The goal is to predict the overall rating of each given resturant using all kinds of related information.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>zomato_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>zomato_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<p>There are 18 variables:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - id of the resturant</li>
<li><strong>url</strong> - url of of the restaurant in the zomato website</li>
<li><strong>address</strong> - address of the restaurant in Bengaluru</li>
<li><strong>name</strong> - name of the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>online_order</strong> - whether online ordering is available in the restaurant or not</li>
<li><strong>book_table</strong> - table book option available or not</li>
<li><strong>votes</strong> - total number of rating for the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>phone</strong> - phone number of the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>location</strong> - the neighborhood in which the restaurant is located</li>
<li><strong>rest_type</strong> - restaurant type</li>
<li><strong>dish_liked</strong> - dishes people liked in the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>cuisines</strong> - food styles, separated by comma</li>
<li><strong>approx_cost(for two people)</strong> - the approximate cost for meal for two people</li>
<li><strong>reviews_list</strong> - list of reviews for the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>menu_item</strong> - list of menus available in the restaurant</li>
<li><strong>listed_in(type)</strong> - type of meal</li>
<li><strong> listed_in(city)</strong> - the neighborhood in which the restaurant is listed</li>
<li><strong>rating</strong> -  the overall rating for the resturant ([0-5])</li>
</ul>",True,True
2177,ds100fa19,"# Description

This is the Kaggle competition for UC Berkeley Data 100 Project 2, Fall 2019. The problem statement is available on the project files. There is no private test set; all test data are viewable via Kaggle.

First, join the competition by clicking the ""Join Competition"" button.

To submit predictions, use the blue ""Submit Predictions"" button on the top right.
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will be graded on the accuracy of your model on the test data, as indicated in the project notebook. However, since we do not want students to see the test data labels, we compute your accuracy on the test data in a hidden test. This Kaggle submission is for you to see what your accuracy on the test data is. Note that the accuracy shown by Kaggle is the accuracy on a random subset of the test data, not all of it.

It is not required for you to submit to Kaggle to receive credit for the project; Kaggle is merely a way for you to check what your test accuracy is, since our project notebook will not tell you.","# Dataset Description

Spam/ham data.

- id : observation ID
- subject : email subject
- email : email text
- spam : 0 (ham) or 1 (spam), not in test.csv",True,True
2178,starcraft-2-player-prediction-challenge-2019,"# Description

<h1>Can you predict who is playing a game of Starcraft 2?</h1>

<p>We consider the video game StarCraft 2. This real-time strategy game is played competitively by cyber-athletes as an electronic sport (esport), and most of the competitions are played online. A major issue for competition organizers is that they need to verify the identify of a player, for being sure that it is not another player (a friend with better chances, ...).</p>

<p>For that matter, we propose to study how it is possible to determine who is playing given a behavioral trace (game events produced by the player) by designing a prediction model using machine learning techniques.</p>

You are provided with a large set of SC2 replay data, from which you need to build features and train a model. Once your model is ready, apply it on the test data and submit your result!

Good luck and happy learning!
The organizer,
Mehdi Kaytoue ,

<h1>Acknowledgments</h1>

Special thanks to
<ul>
<li>GraylinKim for his sc2reader tools (for parsing SC2 replays) (including all contributors and those of the LOTV branch). </li>
<li>Blizzard Entertainment for making awesome games!</li>
<li>All the players of the replays that are used for this competition (these replays are public, but we won't say which to avoid cheating!)</li>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>An example is given</p>","# Dataset Description

<h1>File descriptions</h1>

<ul>
<li>TRAIN - the training set: labelled behavioral traces</li>
<li>TRAIN_LONG - the training set: labelled behavioral traces but with much more information</li>
<li>TEST - the test set: unlabelled behavioral traces (you need to predict the player)</li>
<li>TEST_LONG - the test set: unlabelled behavioral traces (you need to predict the player) but with much more information</li>
<li>SAMPLE_SUBMISSION is the kind of file you have to submit, with your predictions for the test dataset</li>

<h1>Raw input data (TRAIN.CSV and TEST.CSV)</h1>

Binary replay files have been parsed for you to produce an interpretable text file where each line denotes the actions made by one player in a single game. An example is the following:

<pre>http://eu.battle.net/sc2/en/profile/4234852/1/First/,Protoss,Base,s,s,s,s,s,t5,Base,s,hotkey30,hotkey00,t10,t15</pre>

Values are coma separated and denotes :
<ul>
<li>battleneturl: The first field gives the unique ID of the player that generates the behavioral trace (given only for TRAIN.CSV)
<li>played_race: The second field gives the race played (Protoss, Zerg or Terran)
<li>The rest of the fields are actions
<ul>
<li>Selection: a base (nexus, command center, hatchery) has been selected (sBase), a mineral has been selected (sMineral) or anything else (s).</li>
<li>Hotkey: a hotkey has been used. hotkey62 means that the button 6 (0 to 9 values possible) has been used (0=created, 1=updated, 2=used). The principle of hotkeys can be understood thanks to the picture below (Yan et. al, SIGCHI'15)
<img src=""https://perso.liris.cnrs.fr/mehdi.kaytoue/files/control.png"" width=""200px"" /></li>
<li>fields tXX where XX is an integer gives time windows in seconds, that is, all actions before t20 where done before 20 seconds of games, those between t20 and t30 where made in the interval [20;30], ...</li>
</ul>
</ul>

<h1>Long raw input data (TRAIN_LONG.CSV and TEST_LONG.CSV)</h1>

<p>These files describe exactly the same games as before (row wise) but give more information. Several kinds of events of the game are given (not only hotkeys usage!).</p>

<pre>http://eu.battle.net/sc2/en/profile/4234852/1/First/,Protoss,6:CameraEvent:26.0:155.7578125,16:SelectionEvent:Nexus,18:BasicCommandEvent:TrainProbe,21:SelectionEvent:Probe;Probe,...</pre>

<p>As before, first comes the player ID to predict (only for training), the race player (Zerg, Protoss or Terran), then a list of events. Each event is separated with a coma, for each event fields are separated with semi-colons (':'), first the frame at which the event occured (there are 16 frames in one second of game), then the name of the event is given, then its properties. The fields of each event types are given by the following SC2READER code:</p>

<pre>
# A selection with the mouse, parameters are the units/buildings selected
if isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.SelectionEvent):
  line += str(event.frame)+"":SelectionEvent:"" + ("";"".join([o.name for o in event.objects]))
# Hotkey (control group creation, selection, update)
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.ControlGroupEvent):
    line += str(event.frame)+"":ControlGroupEvent:"" + str(event.hotkey)+ "":""+  str(event.update_type)
# Camera moved at position X Y 
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.CameraEvent):
     line += str(event.frame)+"":CameraEvent:"" + str(event.x) + "":"" + str(event.y)
# Message from the player
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.message.ChatEvent):
    line += str(event.frame)+"":ChatEvent:\"""" + smart_str(event.text) + ""\""""
# Ability used (building/unit construction)
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.BasicCommandEvent):
   line += str(event.frame)+"":BasicCommandEvent:"" + event.ability_name
# Target point (attack, move, ... to position X,Y,Z)
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.TargetPointCommandEvent):
   line += str(event.frame)+"":TargetPointCommandEvent:"" + str(event.x) +"":"" + str(event.y) +"":""+ str(event.z) + "":"" + event.ability_name
# Target Unit
elif isinstance(event, sc2reader.events.game.TargetUnitCommandEvent):
   line += str(event.frame)+"":TargetUnitCommandEvent:"" + event.ability.name + "":"" + event.target.name
</pre>

<p>Important note: Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acronyms will I encounter?
Be sure to know these answers, and ask the professor otherwise!</p>",True,True
2179,kzss1,"# Description

Purpose of this task is to build logistic regression model using the provided data.

The evaluation metric is AUC (Area Under the Curve).
Estimate your model on train data set and then score with model test data set to receive prediction for the test data set applications.
The evaluation is done on out-of-time date set.

Gini of your model should be higher than 0.30 and therefore AUC higher than 0.65.
Great model should have Gini higher then 0.35 and therefore AUC higher than 0.675. 



----------------------
# Evaluation

**Gini of your model should be higher than 0.30 and therefore AUC higher than 0.65.
Great model should have Gini higher then 0.35 and therefore AUC higher than 0.675.**

AUC criterion is used as evaluation matrix. Evaluation is done Out-of-time sample sample.","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
