2195,cudlfinance,"# Description


<p>The motivation for this common task framework competition is to evaluate projects on time series prediction as part of the Deep Learning course on an equal footing.  The competition is optional for enrolled students in other projects. </p>

<p><b>Goal: </b> Build an architecture which generates investment decisions across equities on a daily basis. </p>

<p><b>Data: </b> Dataset of daily prices of 505 stocks that currently compose
the S&P 500 and CASH value for the dates 01/01/2015 ¨C 05/31/2018.</p>


<p> <b>Submission: </b> You are required to submit 505+1 entries of weights per day that should be allocated for investments in equities for next 30 days.</p>

* Each weight entry stands for the % allocation of total purchase power for that
day.

* Interpretation of weight entry: if entry < 0 a stock is being sold short; if entry > 0
a stock is being bought; if entry = 0 a stock is excluded from the portfolio.

* The extra entry is for 'CASH' ¨C this adds another degree of freedom to your
investment decision, and is equivalent to 'bias'.

<b>Notes: </b>
* You may choose a method for processing missing data and are allowed to augment the given dataset.

Enjoy, and may the best team win.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For every day in the test data, we have an ideal weight vector that optimizes the gains for that day. Every predicted vector (w_p) is scored with an RMSE loss w.r.t. the optimal weight vector.

Submission with a lower total loss will be placed higher on the leaderboard.","# Dataset Description

This File contains dataset of daily prices of 505 stocks that currently compose the S&P 500 for the dates 01/01/2015 ¨C 05/31/2018. Where each row corresponds to a trading day.",True,True
2196,ruccardinality,"# Description

<p>Cardianlity estimation is a  fundamental task in database query processing and optimization. </p>
<p>In this challenge, you are given a dataset with a set of SQL queries (already extracted into csv format) and the final cardinality of these queries. Further more, execution plans of these queries are also provided.</p>
<p>The dataset is spited into training and testing parts, while the testing data has no labels (i.e. final cardinality of the queries). Our task is to predict the cardinality of testing data using machine learning models learned from the training data.</p>
**Contact sunluming@ruc.edu.cn if you have any problem.**

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>For each query in the testing set, your model should predict the final cardinality.  </p>

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <b> Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)</b>. The RMSLE is a relative error, measuring the factor by which an estimate differs from the actual cardinality:</p>
<p>\[ RMSLE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}  \Sigma_{i=1}^{n} ( \log (actual_{i}+1) - log (predicted_{i}+1))^{2} } \]</p>
<p><strong>Submission Format</strong>
Submission files should be in .csv format and contain two columns: Query ID and Predicted Cardinality. 
<p><b>NOTE: the query id should start from 0</b></p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Query ID,Predicted Cardinality<br />0,92345<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">1,218<br />2,146<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">3,52452<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>training_data.csv</strong> - the extracted SQL training set</li>
<li><strong>testing_data.csv</strong> -  the extracted SQL test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>column_min_max_value.csv</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
<li><strong>training_plans</strong> -  query plans for training data</li>
<li><strong>testing_plans</strong> -  query plans for testing data</li>",True,True
