2239,opencv-pytorch-course-segmentation,"# Description

<p>This competition is about segmentation of images taken from drones. </p>
<p>Evaluation metric is Dice score.</p>
<p>The task is to predict the most accurate mask for each class, taking into account the limited amount of GPU resources you have.</p>
<p>Enjoy the competition!</p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Ishan Nigam for providing the dataset. It could be found <a href=""https://github.com/ishann/aeroscapes""> here</a></p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>This competition is evaluated on the mean <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S?rensen¨CDice_coefficient'>Dice coefficient</a
>. The Dice coefficient can be used to compare the pixel-wise agreement between a predicted segmentation and its corresponding ground truth. The formula is given by: </p>

<p>$$DSC =  \frac{2 |X \cap Y|}{|X|+ |Y|}$$
$$ \small \mathrm{where}\ X = Predicted\ Set\ of\ Pixels,\ \ Y = Ground\ Truth $$ </p>
<p>The Dice coefficient is defined to be 1 when both X and Y are empty. The leaderboard score is the mean of the Dice coefficients for each image in the test set.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p><b>In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values</b>.  Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. <pre>1 3</pre> implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels (1,2,3). </p>
<p>However, since RLE is used for binary masks only, we ask you to convert your predicted mask in binary masks for each class and include <code>ClassID</code> for each binary mask as a tail for corresponding <code>ImageID</code> as follows: <pre>ImageID_ClassID</pre></p>
The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. Note that this competition uses relative pixel positions, meaning that after the first pixel position, the remaining pixel values are simply offsets. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3 are to be included in the mask, as well as 14,15,16,17,18. The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. The pixels are numbered from top to bottom, then left to right: 1 is pixel (1,1), 2 is pixel (2,1), etc.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
<pre>ImageID,EncodedPixels
01_0,1 1 5 1
01_1,2 3 8 1
02_0,1 1
02_1,3 1
03_0,1 1
03_1,4 5
etc.</pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Dataset Description </h2>
<p>The dataset consists of 3,269 images in 12 classes (including background). All images were taken from drones in a variety of scales. Samples are shown below:
<img src=""https://github.com/ishann/aeroscapes/blob/master/assets/data_montage.png?raw=true"" width=""800"" height=""800"">
<p>The data was splitted into public train set and private test set which is used for evaluation of submissions. You can split public subset into train and validation sets yourself.
Images are named with a unique <code>ImageId</code>. </p>
<p> You should segment and classify the images in the test set.</p>
<p>The dataset consists of landscape images taken from drones in a variety of scales.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>imgs/</strong> - folder with images </li>
<li><strong>masks/</strong> - folder with ground truth masks </li>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ImageID</strong> - the id of an image</li>
</ul>",True,True
2240,introduction-to-cross-validation,"# Description

<h1> Welcome to competition 2</h1>

<p> For this competition you guys are going to estimate your test RMSE (root mean squared error) for a regression problem. We are going to predict the overall football ratings for the players in the football game FIFA. </p> 

<p> Before you can submit predictions for this Kaggle, you <b> are required to </b> estimate the test error of your model using your own created K-fold cross validation loop!</p>

<p> You can get started by clicking this link below, or by looking at canvas! </p>
[Kernel](https://www.dropbox.com/s/5r4scg9jq2l9aq1/kernel.html?dl=0)
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use RMSE for this competition, read the kernel for more information.","# Dataset Description

Use the `load()` function to load in .RData files.",True,True
2241,mrclean,"# Description

## Hello there !

Imagine this challenge like something you don't want to do, but **you'll feel better after doing it**. It's not complicated, it's just not as exciting as sentiment analysis or computer vision.

However, it's a **HUUGE** loss of time to not visualize your data before beginning your work.
Did you ever clean your room in complete darkness?
I don't think so!

So, what's the easiest way to clean something ? Shed light on it!

Use visualisation techniques to figure out what's wrong with the dataset. Clean it.
Once done, feed the data in a neural network of your choice.

If done correctly, the cleaning should improve your performance !
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the accuracy</a>. 
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>

<pre>Id, Label<br />1,+<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,-<br />9,-<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,+<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;""></span></pre>

'+"" and ""-"" represent the two classes.","# Dataset Description

Here is our beloved dataset.

The data has been anonymized for privacy concerns.
Each row is positive (""+"") and negative (""-"") attributes of people who's credit application status (accepted or not).

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_set.csv</strong> - the training set with the labels </li>
<li><strong>test_set.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>",True,True
