2247,pda2019,"# Description

<h1 style=""text-align: center"">Recommend movie lists using user preferences and item content data</h1>
<p>Welcome to the page of the <b>Programming for Data Analytics 2019/2020 course: 
Challenge competition. </b></p>
<h3>Description</h3>
<p>Please read carefully till the end of the page!</p>
<ul>
<li style=""text-align: justify"">In this competition you are required to predict and recommend a list of top 10 movies for a set of users in the test set (test users).</li>
<li style=""text-align: justify"">The original un-splitted dataset includes almost 700'000&nbsp;ratings for 5690&nbsp;users and 1824&nbsp;items with visual, tag, and genre features.</li>
<li style=""text-align: justify"">A subset of about 1992 users has been selected as test users.</li>
<li style=""text-align: justify"">The goal is to recommend a list of top 10 items for each test user.</li>
<li>MAP@10 (i.e., Mean Average Precision) is used for evaluation.</li>
<li style=""text-align: justify"">You can use any kind of recommender algorithm you wish (e.g., collaborative-filtering, content-based, hybrid, etc.).</li>
</ul>
<h3>The prize</h3>
<p>(only in exam points, not Euros!).</p>
<p>Each team will receive the final score according to the quality of recommendations computed on a <strong>public leaderboard</strong>:</p>
<ul style=""text-align: justify"">
<li>the position in the final public leaderboard when the competition ends</li>
<li>quality of recommendation in comparison to 5 baseline algorithms (see leaderboard)</li>
</ul>
<p>The final score is computed on a public leaderboard with the following formula:</p>

<p>\[ final\_score  = \begin{cases} \textrm{10} &amp; if\;baseline\_bonus + standing\_points &gt;=10 \\ \textrm{baseline_bonus + standing_points} &amp; if\;baseline\_bonus + standing\_points  &lt; 10  \\ \end{cases} \]</p>

 <p>So <em>final_score</em> will be in the range 0-10:</p>

<h3>Baseline Bonus</h3>
<p>There are 5 baseline algorithms (1 to 5 in leaderboard). Exceeding each of these baselines will add 2 points to your project score (except algorithm 0 which is random and all teams should pass it). Such baselines are computed with five different recommender algorithms.  </p>
<p>Each algorithm is associated with a bonus score that adds to your final score if you exceed it in the final leaderboard:</p>
<p>\[ baseline\_bonus  = \begin{cases} \textrm{Algorithm 5} &amp; +2 \\ \textrm{Algorithm 4} &amp; +2\\ \textrm{Algorithm 3} &amp; +2 \\ \textrm{Algorithm 2} &amp; +2 \\ \textrm{Algorithm 1} &amp; +2 \\ \textrm{Algorithm 0 (random)} &amp; +0 \\ \end{cases} \]</p>
 
<h3>Standing points</h3>
<p>According to the standing in the public leaderboard in the following manner:</p>
<p>\[ standing\_points  = \begin{cases} \textrm{1st&nbsp;team} &amp; +10 \\ \textrm{2nd team} &amp; +9\\ \textrm{3rd team} &amp; +8&nbsp;\\ \textrm{4th-10th team} &amp; +7 \\  \textrm{other teams} &amp; +5 &nbsp;\\ \end{cases} \]</p>
 
 
<h3>Deadline</h3>
<p>Date and time are in UTC (due to Kaggle setting). Deadline will be  the following date:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Sunday, 8 December (11:59 PM UTC)</b></li>
</ul>
<h3></h3>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Evaluation Metric</h3>
<p>The goal is to recommend a list of 10 relevant items for each user&nbsp;</p>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAP@10 (Mean Average Precision)</p>
<h3>MAP@10</h3>
<p>The average precision at 10, for a user is defined as:</p>
<p>$$AP@10 = \sum_{k=1}^{10} P(k) / min(m, 10)$$</p>
<p>where P(k) means the precision at cut-off k in the item list, and m is the number of relevant items in the list. P(k) equals 0 if&nbsp;k -th item is not&nbsp;relevant.&nbsp;If the denominator is 0, the result is set&nbsp;0.</p>
<p>The mean average precision for N users at position 10&nbsp;is the average of the average precision of each user, i.e.,</p>
<p>$$MAP@10 = \sum_{i=1}^N AP@10_i / N$$</p>
<h3>Submission Format</h3>
<p>For every test user (a user with a userID in test set), the submission file should contain two columns: userID and recommended_itemIDs. For every test user, the recommended_itemIDs should contain 10 recommendation of movies. The recommended_itemIDs should be a space-delimited list of exactly 10 items ordered by decreasing relevance (the order counts for MAP!)</p>
<p>The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><b>userID,recommended_itemIDs</b><br>
1, 592 2765 930 1503 102 1537 2646 2602 951 3138<br>
3, 3785 2002 2139 3563 151 3861 1915 836 1935 355<br>
11, 1779 2323 592 3390 3203 616 3327 466 3104 647<br>
29, 2699 1297 2916 2657 1371 3773 2941 3813 3483 1208<br>
etc.</p>
</blockquote>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train-PDA2019.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test-PDA2019.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission-PDA2019.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>content-PDA2019.csv</strong> - supplemental metadata about the movie items (visual features, genre, tags, etc.)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields in Test set</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>userID</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given user</li>
<li><strong>recommended_itemIDs</strong> - the ids of the 10 recommended movies for the user</li>
</ul>",True,True
