2254,statlearning-sjtu-2019,"# Description

**Task 1**: 
You need to predict the category for each *Quick Draw* test image based on the given *Quick Draw* training images.
 &nbsp;

**Task 2**: 
You need to draw 10 Quick Draw images for each category. After collecting the images from all classmates, we have another training set, which is used to further improve your performance in Task 1.

After you draw the Quick Draw, please zoom it to 28*28 pixels (Please ensure your images are still clear after zooming) and save it as .jpg file. Arrange all the images into respective folders named by their categories. Zip all the folders and upload it to our website on Canvas. We will collect all the data and release it as training set to help you to reach higher accuracy.
Before you submit your quickdraw, you can test your quickdraw with your fully trained model. If they are all easily recognized, they may be useless to improve your model. Another aspect, note that this is a competition and the training set may be harmful if your/their images are too strange. You may need some methods to clean the data.
 &nbsp;
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2567931%2F389de83eccda91584a14d12ea3da8d52%2Fpreview.jpg?generation=1574737891765279&alt=media)
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**A-T-T-E-N-T-I-O-N:**
Deadline for submitting your predictions on Kaggle: 2020/1/5.

Deadline for uploading your report and code via our course website: 2020/1/9.

After 2020/1/5, the leaderboard will switch to the rest 70% data(i.e. the private leaderboard).
Your results that mentioned in your reports should be the results on this private leaderboard, instead of your results on public leaderboard.

 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**Before submitting your predictions:**
Please update your name with ""STUDENTID_NAME"" before submitting your predictions. You can update your name in the ""Team"" tab.

 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**About the leaderboard and submissions:**
The public leaderboard only shows 30% results of the dataset. After 2020/1/5, the leaderboard will switch to the rest 70% data(i.e. the private leaderboard). Any extra training data or the code can not reproduce the score is regarded as CHEATING. We will check the results and the submissions after the deadline, PLEASE DO NOT CHEAT. 

There are only two submissions allowed for each person every day. Please carefully arrange the schedule of your submissions.

 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**Requirements for the project report:**
Don't be too nervous about your rank on the leaderboard. The scores are just the proof that your work is valid and sound. We will judge your project **mainly **by the project report.

You have to try at least three different models (we highly recommend the models which are mentioned in the class), and explain the details about your models. Please select the submissions from the valid submissions and put the screenshot of the selected submissions in your project report. You can find all your submissions in ""My Submissions"" tab. Your results that mentioned in your reports should be the results on private leaderboard, instead of your results on public leaderboard.

The length of the project report has no hard limitations. A recommended length is 6 pages for two columns or 10 pages for one column if you write your report in latex with 10pt font size.

The codes for your submissions are required for the project. Please also provide the scripts or instructions to run your codes in a readme.txt.

 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**Requirements for uploading:**
You need to zip and upload the following files before deadline:
¢Ùyour report ¢Úyour codes ¢Ûa readme.txt, which includes the scripts or instructions to run your codes.
The final zip file should be uploaded via our course website.

 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;
 &nbsp;

**Deadline of this project:**
Deadline for submitting your predictions on Kaggle: 2020/1/5
Deadline for uploading your report and code via our course website: 2020/1/9
----------------------
# Evaluation

Metric
This competition is evaluated on the categorization accuracy of your predictions (the percentage of images you get correct).

Submission Format
For every id, submission files should contain two columns: id and categories. 
The id for test image is its filename. 
The categories should be an integer representing the predicted label for id.
```
**Id mapping:**
0.png: 0
1.png: 1
i.png: i
```

```
**Category mapping:**
    {'airplane': 0, 'ant': 1, 'bear': 2, 'bird': 3, 'bridge': 4,
     'bus'     : 5, 'calendar': 6, 'car': 7, 'chair': 8, 'dog': 9,
     'dolphin' : 10, 'door': 11, 'flower': 12, 'fork': 13, 'truck': 14}
```

**The file should contain a header and have the following format:**

```
id,categories
