2332,the-ai-core-mnist,"# Description

<h2>Welcome to the first AI Core competition!</h2>
<p>If you haven't already, please go through the [Neural Networks notebook](https://github.com/AI-Core/Neural-Networks/blob/master/Neural%20Networks.ipynb)
and the [Convolutional Neural Networks Notebook](https://github.com/AI-Core/Convolutional-Neural-Networks/blob/master/Convolutional%20Neural%20Networks.ipynb)<p>The aim of this competition is to sharpen the skills you acquired s so far. Try to improve on the model to increase the accuracy.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The submissions will be scored based on the accuracy of the models </p>

You should submit both your **code** and a CSV file two columns as in the example below;  the first column should be an index and the second should contain the results (labels) of the test dataset produced out of your algorithm.

| index | label |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 7 |
| 2 | 2 |
| 3 | 1 |
| 4 | 0 |

### IMPORTANT NOTE: DO NOT SHUFFLE THE TEST DATASET. USE THE SAME ORDER PROVIDED","# Dataset Description

We are going to use the MNIST dataset just like in the workshop. You can download the dataset using pytorch or download the files attached here and build the tensors yourself. There's no difference between the two. It depends on your preferences.

##Option one (Use pytorch to download the data)
Start a new notebook and type the following code: 

    import torch
    from torchvision import datasets, transforms

    # GET THE TRAINING DATASET
    train_data = datasets.MNIST(root='MNIST-data',        
                            transform=transforms.ToTensor(),          
                            train=True,                               
                            download=True                            
                           )

    # GET THE TEST DATASET
    test_data = datasets.MNIST(root='MNIST-data',
                           transform=transforms.ToTensor(),
                           train=False,
                          )

    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
    import numpy as np
    x = train_data[0]  
    plt.imshow(x[0].reshape(x[0].shape[1],x[0].shape[2]),cmap='gray')
    plt.show()

You should be able to see this image 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2884799%2Fa5233107a62be5749080bda9fee5effb%2Ffirst_MNIST_pytorch.png?generation=1575734378815816&alt=media)



##Option two (Download datafiles from here and build your dataset)

MNIST dataset consists of four files: 

- **train-images.idx3-ubyte:** Traning images 

- **train-labels.idx1-ubyte:** Training labels 


- **t10k-images.idx3-ubyte** : Test images

- **t10k-labels.idx1-ubyte**: Test labels 

Please start by downloading the files in one location. Start a new notebook in the same location and type the following code: 


    import numpy as np
    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
    from torch.utils import data
    import torch

    def loadMNIST( prefix ):
        intType = np.dtype( 'int32' ).newbyteorder( '>' )
        nMetaDataBytes = 4 * intType.itemsize

        data = np.fromfile( prefix + '-images.idx3-ubyte', dtype = 'ubyte' )
        magicBytes, nImages, width, height = np.frombuffer( data[:nMetaDataBytes].tobytes(), intType )
        data = data[nMetaDataBytes:].astype( dtype = 'float32' ).reshape( [ nImages, width, height ] )
        data = torch.stack([torch.Tensor(i) for i in data])
    
        labels = np.fromfile( prefix + '-labels.idx1-ubyte',
                          dtype = 'ubyte' )[2 * intType.itemsize:]
        labels= labels.reshape(labels.shape[0],1)
        labels = torch.stack([torch.Tensor(i) for i in labels])

        return data, labels

    trainingImages, trainingLabels = loadMNIST( ""train"")
    testImages, testLabels = loadMNIST( ""t10k"")

    train_data = data.TensorDataset(trainingImages,trainingLabels) 
    test_data = data.TensorDataset(testImages,testLabels)

    plt.imshow(training_dataset[0][0], cmap='gray')
    plt.show()

You should be able to see this image 


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2884799%2Ffbb8bb2a67e711e2acc527a6b6992792%2Ffirst_MNIST.png?generation=1575733623249386&alt=media)



### Congratulations you are now ready to start building your neural network!",True,True
2333,mldl-competition-1,"# Description

