2386,ietemlcompetition,"# Description

Image recognition competition by IETE STUDENTS' FORUM.
Information about the dataset can be seen on the dataset page.
The test split is divided into 30% public and 70% private. Final standings on the leaderboard will be decided after completion of the competition. In case of any issue feel free to contact in discussion or any IETE mentor.
You are free to use any machine learning/deep learning algorithm for the task.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Categorical accuracy is the evaluation metrics.","# Dataset Description

The dataset comprises of in total 8 classes. These classes are further divided into two categories. 0,1,4,7 belongs to first-class, 2,3,5,6 belongs to second class.

Here the first class is a fight and the second class is under non-fight. 

8 classes are:
{ 'shoot_gun': 0,
 'kick': 1,
 'stand': 2,
 'wave': 3
'hit': 4,
 'ride_horse': 5,
 'push': 6,
 'punch': 7,
}

The images are of dimensions 224 x 224 x 3, where 3 is representing the RGB channels and 224 is depicting the height and the width of the image. The total number of images in the train split is 53098 and in test split is 22757.

**File description:**
Test.csv - It contains 1 column with id name.<br>
Train.csv - It contains 3 columns with label1 comprising of 8 classes and label2 comprising of 2 classes.<br>
These two files are available in main zip file. <br>
Samplesubmission.csv - This is the sample of the file where the second column corresponds to a predicted label.<br>
This file can be obtained using this link - https://drive.google.com/file/d/1dRsStIE0ka21WmmQxI7H8f4fEkuFG6bP/view?usp=sharing
<br>
There is a total of 75855 images in ""ietecompdata"" folder.<br>
**PS:
If you are using kaggle API to download this data, please use this command instead: <br>
kaggle datasets download -d sanchit2843/ietemlcomp <br>
Alternatively, the dataset can be downloaded using this link. <br>
https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1q1O7LI3f8Rncq_6183_Rjj0Xu-kU0s3d<br>
If you are using the kaggle kernel, it will take around 20 minutes to download the data. If you are using google colab or anything else please use the above-mentioned API command for downloading data faster.**",True,True
2387,2401-iris-data-set,"# Description

# Introduction
Following **24 Ultimate Data Science Projects To Boost Your Knowledge and Skills** [[Link]](https://www.analyticsvidhya.com/blog/2018/05/24-ultimate-data-science-projects-to-boost-your-knowledge-and-skills/). We will go through a sequence of classic data science projects to increase our skill

# Source
[archive.ics.uci.edu](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Iris)

# About this data set
Its a simple and classic dataset. A simple dataset with 150 records with 4 attributes. According to wiki it was introduced by the British statistician and biologist Ronald Fisher in his 1936 paper.

# Target
It's a classification project, that you need to predict the class of the flowers
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
1. sepal length in cm
2. sepal width in cm
3. petal length in cm
4. petal width in cm
5. class:
 - Iris Setosa<br>
 - Iris Versicolour<br>
 - Iris Virginica<br>",True,True
