2397,terrassa-buildings-2019,"# Description

Welcome to the GDSA 2019 building visual search competition.

Carefully read the instructions in Evaluation, Data and Rules sections to know how to proceed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The metric used to evaluate the quality of the rankings will be the Mean Average Precision.

**File format** 
The submission files must contain two columns. The first one, Query, must contain the name of each one of the images for which we generated the ranking. The second one, RetrievedDocuments, must contain the sorted ranking separating its entries with spaces. Each ranking must end with an endline ('\n').

You can find a submission example at the Data Description section of this competition.

```
Query,RetrievedDocuments
queryid1, retrievedid1 retrievedid2 retrievedid3 ...
queryid2, retrievedid1 retrievedid2 retrievedid3 ...
...
```","# Dataset Description

**Dataset**
The file `queries.zip` contains all the visual queries you need to compare with each image contained in `database.zip`. For your convenience, all images have been rescaled to 500px width.

**Submission File**
Submission files need to contain a ranked list of all images in the dataset for each query image(together in the same file). Each row corresponds to a ranked list, being the first image ID in a row the one corresponding to the query image.

For your convenience, a file generated with a random ranking (`kaggle_rnd_bl.csv`) is provided as an example of the file format your submissions must follow.

Those queries that belong to the unknown ('*desconegut*') building category will be ignored automatically by the scoring system (you do not need to filter them out).

The public leaderboard will continuously show your performance for a subset of your results. The complete results will be published only after the end of the competition.

When you upload a submission file, please take some time to write a brief description of the used approach.",True,True
2398,snhumat300sp2020,"# Description

This is a friendly competition for you to practice your data science and statistical modeling skills. Consider this a ""playground"" for you to test your modeling metal throughout the semester. Your task is to predict the sale price of a used car given its characteristics. The dataset contains 13 variables to compare used cars and also includes the price for each car. Points are awarded in our class for your participation and performance here. Try to be the best!
----------------------
# Evaluation

One of the prizes awarded this semester will be for the best exploratory data analysis and data visualization. This category will be judged by multiple faculty members.
The remaining two prizes will be awarded for best predictive regression model. Your models will be assessed according to the Residual Standard Error for predictions on the evaluation data (we called this test error in class). The students whose models result in the lowest test error will be awarded prizes.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
<ul>
<li> **train.csv** - the training set
<li> **student_eval.csv** - the evaluation set with unknown sale prices to make your predictions
<li> **sample_submission.csv** - a benchmark submission which naively predicts the average price for every car in the evaluation set -- you should be able to beat this one quite easily
</ul>",True,True
2399,bids-machine-learning,"# Description

<p>Hello and welcome to our first Kaggle class exercise. Here we will practice all what we had learn until today. In this exercise we will practice the algorithms for predicting regression outcomes. You can use R or Python to calculate your predictions.</p>
<p/>
<h2> Acknowledgements </h2>
<p>This dataset.was obtained from the <a href=""http://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Bike+Sharing+Dataset""> UCI Machine Learning Repository </a></p>
<p>The dataset was kindly donated by Hadi Fanaee-T from the Laboratory of Artificial Intelligence and Decision Support (LIAAD), University of Porto, Portugal</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/marknagelberg/rmsle-function"">Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error</a> . Because this value is not only an absolute value, this metric is one of the most resistant metric to outliers that's possible using simple methods. Thus it is the most fair measure to calculate the number of bikes on the given data.</p>

<p>The calculation of this measure is made as follows:</p>

<p>
\[Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error=\sqrt{ {1/n}\sum( { \log(\hat{y_0} + 1)?log(y_0 +1) )}^2 }\]
</p>


<p>In Python you can calculate it as :<p> 
<pre>
import math

#A function to calculate Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)
def rmsle(y, y_pred):
	assert len(y) == len(y_pred)
	terms_to_sum = [(math.log(y_pred[i] + 1) - math.log(y[i] + 1)) ** 2.0 for i,pred in enumerate(y_pred)]
	return (sum(terms_to_sum) * (1.0/len(y))) ** 0.5

rmsle(y, y_pred)
</pre>
<p> In R you can calculate it as :</a>
<pre>
rmsle  < - function(y,ypred) {
    return(sqrt(mean((log(y+1,2) - log(y_pred+1,2))^2))
}
</pre>
<p>Submission Format
For every row in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: id and cnt. It should be a comma-delimited list.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>
id, cnt
1, 0
2, 0
3, 0
4, 0
etc.
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<h1> Bike Sharing </h1>

<p>Bike sharing systems are new generation of traditional bike rentals where whole process from membership, rental and return back has become automatic. Through these systems, user is able to easily rent a bike from a particular position and return back at another position. Currently, there are about over 500 bike-sharing programs around the world which is composed of over 500 thousands bicycles. Today, there exists great interest in these systems due to their important role in traffic, environmental and health issues. </p>
<p>This is a real world dataset obtained from a bike sharing systems, the characteristics of data being generated by these systems make them attractive for the research. </p>
<p>Opposed to other transport services such as bus or subway, the duration of travel, departure and arrival position is explicitly recorded in these systems. This feature turns bike sharing system into a virtual sensor network that can be used for sensing mobility in the city.</p>
<p>Your mission is to try to predict the number of bikes being rented each day using the provided variables.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<p> Please check the field description on the train dataset (click the train.csv link on the ""Data Sources"" and you will see it on ""Columns"").",True,True
2400,cl-cv2020,"# Description

<h1>Problem Statement</h1>

<img style=""float: right;"" src=""https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BZjdkOTU3MDktN2IxOS00OGEyLWFmMjktY2FiMmZkNWIyODZiXkEyXkFqcGdeQXVyMTMxODk2OTU@._V1_.jpg"" height=""35%"" width=""35%"" >

<p>For movie lovers, the movie poster makes up the first impression about the movie contents and its genre. 
<p>Humans can grasp the cues like color, expressions on the faces of actors etc to quickly determine the genre (horror, comedy, animation etc) of a movie. Though it might seem simple, it involves millions of concurrent processes occurring in a less than a jiffy! If humans are able to inherently predict genre of a movie by a glance at its poster, can't we expect the same from a machine ?

<p>It has been studied that the human brain tends to make intuitions based on the color characteristics, local texture based features and structural cues of posters. Hence, the posters possess some characteristics which could be utilized for genre classification</p>

<p>In this competition, your goal is to predict the genres of movies from their posters. </p>

<p><h4><i> * Participants must submit a kaggle kernel which reproduces their selected submission and make the kernel public after the contest end to be eligible for evaluation and prizes.</i></h4> </p>

<p><h4><i> * Please have a look at the competition rules. All rules are to be followed strictly. Any use of unfair means will result in immediate disqualification from the competition. </i></h4></p>

<h4><i> The competition is organized by CyberLabs, the official Coding and Cyber Club of IIT (ISM) Dhanbad, India </i></h4>
----------------------
# Evaluation

 <p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
----------------------
# Timeline

<b>February 3, 2020</b> - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

<b>February 5, 2020</b> - Final submission deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM IST on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

The following prizes will be awarded on the basis of private leader board rank after manually evaluating their submitted final kernels. If any discrepancy is found, then that participant will be not eligible for prize.

1st Prize: ? 5000
2nd Prize: ? 3000
3rd Prize: ? 2000

The top two team with parcipants of 1st year of IIT (ISM) Dhanbad will get a chance for being recruited in AI Division of the Cyber Labs 
----------------------
# Kernels Requirements

<p><h4><i> * Participants must submit a kaggle kernel which reproduces their selected submission and make the kernel public after the contest end to be eligible for evaluation and prizes.</i></h4> </p>
----------------------
# About Cyber Labs

<h1> Overview </h1>

<p> We are a team of individuals from multiple disciplines, driven by the desire of implementing solutions-unbounded by the limits of knowledge or technical know-how. The problem statement address issues ranging from solving social problems and cultural opportunities to building some cool gadgets. It was founded post inspiration on the footsteps of MIT Media Labs by an enthusiastic and ticking learner who discovered a scope beyond rote learning and plain-vanilla implementations of the common problems and sci-fi solutions.
Cyber Labs provides a platform to discuss debate and explore the viable and easy solutions, be it tech or non-tech to problems via a project-based approach.  </p>

<ul>
<li> Website: <a href=""http://www.cyberlabs.club"">http://www.cyberlabs.club</a> </li>
<li> Size: 11-50 members  </li>
<li> Type: Student-run Club </li>
<li> Founded: 2015 </li>
<li> Location: IIT (ISM), Dhanbad </li>
</ul>","# Dataset Description

The training and testing images can be downloaded from <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/sshukla21/clabscvcomp"">here.</a>

* <b><u> Instructions for data downloading</u></b>: <br>
For importing data in Kaggle Kernel, click on add dataset and search by url given above.<br>
For downloading data in colab/cloud use kaggle api given on dataset page (link given above).
                                                                               


The following data files are train.csv and sample_submission.csv

<h2>File Descriptions</h2>

<h2>train.csv</h2>
The files contains 14969 rows with 2 features - id , genres in each row

<i>id</i> - A unique key by which the corresponding image is identified.
<i>genres</i> - The genre of the movie. There are 10 unique genre - Comedy, Documentary, Drama, Horror, Thriller, Action, Romance, Crime, Adventure, Sci-Fi.

<h2>sample_submission.csv</h2>
A valid sample submission.
This files contains 29996 rows with 2 features - id, genres in each row

<i>id</i> - A unique key by which the corresponding image is identified.
<i>genres</i> - Here you have to place predicted genre of the movie.

You must accept the competition's rules to gain access to the competition data.",True,True
