2411,rcmemulators,"# Description

The aim of this problem is to emulate the temperature time series simulated by a regional climate model.

Regional Climate Models are numerical tools built to simulated the long term (hundreds years) climate on a **specific region (Europe)** at **high resolution (12km here)**.  Because they work only on a limited area we *force* them at their boundaries with a Global Climate Model ( low resolution but over the whole planet ). Global Models produce a non reliable information at the local scale and that is why we use Regional Models to downscale this information. 

The aim of this competition is to try to predict the temperature series simulated by the regional model given the simulation from the global model.

The complete presentation of the problem is available at this link : [Intro](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1o0jwqJTcpYJndiYHSpUefnetkh-jY9tx/view?usp=sharing)

As it is mentioned in the presentation, there are 2 different exercises here corresponding to two  different outputs.

In the first one we try to predict only the temperature in one grid point, while in the second one we try to predict over a whole region. The submissions for the second one will be done at this link : [CODALAB]( https://competitions.codalab.org/competitions/22539?secret_key=02c419ef-e4c6-4188-9cca-4a61dc5cb9bc)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the RMSE : 

<p> \[  RMSE =  \sqrt{\frac{\sum( \hat{y_i} - y_i ) }{N}} \] </p>

For the first version one needs to upload a CSV file where the first column corresponds to the date and the second to the temperature serie. 

For the second version (region) we will compute the RMSE on each grid point and then compute the mean and the max. ( for this version the submissions will be done on the codalab competition : [CODALAB](https://competitions.codalab.org/competitions/22539?secret_key=02c419ef-e4c6-4188-9cca-4a61dc5cb9bc))

There you will have to upload a 3D matrix [10220,32,32]. You need to use the function specified in the notebook (First look into the data) to save your file in the right format.","# Dataset Description

Data are simulations from climate models. As it was mentioned in the presentation we use only data coming from the regional climate model even in the input because we focus here on the downscaling problem. To make the input we take the high resolution data and ""upscale"" them to the resolution of the global model (low resolution model). 

The sample is composed of 95 years (2006-2100) of daily data (so 365*95 = 34675 days).

To separate the training sample and the testing sample I put the 3 firsts years of each decades in the test. So I have complete years among the whole period to test the performances. So 67 years are in the train (24455 days) and 28 in the test (10225 days).

The same idea can be applied to build the validation sample to train the network (cf the notebook).


 <h2>The Inputs</h2>

The inputs are 4 dimensionnal : [time , lon , lat , variables ]. 

So for each day we have 19 map of 11*11 points corresponding to different variables simulated over the same region.

The variables are : 

- **Geopotential** at 500hPa, 700hPa, 850hpa ;

- **Temperature** at 500hPa, 700hPa, 850hpa ;

- **Wind component 1** at 500hPa, 700hPa, 850hpa

- **Wind component 2** at 500hPa, 700hPa, 850hpa

- **Humidity** at 500hPa, 700hPa, 850hpa

- **Surface Temperature**

- **Surface Wind 1**

- **Surface Wind 2**

- **Amount of precipitation**


 <h2>Outputs</h2>

<h3>Version 1</h3> : Y_train_mpl.csv

The surface temperature in Kelvin (273.16 + ¡ãC) at the resolution of the regional model on the grid point of Montpellier.

The samplesub.csv file is a template to submit. The prediction should be put in the 'temp¨¦' variable of this file.

<h3>Version 2</h3> : Y_train_box.dat

The surface temperature in Kelvin (273.16 + ¡ãC) at the resolution of the regional model on a small region from south of France.
The size of this box is 32*32 grid points but because it has a (much) higher resolution than the inputs it fits a much smaller region.

For technical reasons the submissions of this exercise should be done on the codelab page at this link :",True,True
2412,doc-class,"# Description

**Goals of the project and Report**

The first goal of this project is to provide a classifier for documents using a neural network with one layer a.k.a. a perceptron, which is a classifier based on a non-linear activation applied to a linear model. 
The second goal is to compare two methods to achieve the first goal: standard gradient descent (GD) and stochastic gradient descent (SGD) on this problem. You should discuss the effectiveness of these methods: which one would you recommend in which situation? 
You report should briefly present the problem, describe these three methods, show the results and discuss them. It should contain three main sections: 1. Introduction, 2. Methods, 3. Comparison and Discussion. 


**Problem description**

For each document x{i} (i=1,2,...,n), you are given its class f(i) in {1,2,...,r} where the function f : {1,...,n} --> {1,...,r} : i --> f(i) gives the topic of document i. 

You want to find the parameters (W,b) that minmize 

sum__i  L( phi(W^T x{i} + b) , y{i} ) [+ lambda ||W||_F^2]

where 

* y_{i}=e_{f(i)}, where e{j} is the jth unit vector (1 in position j, 0 otherwise), 

* L(a,b) is the loss; for example, 
  L(a,b) = ||a-b||^2 (least squares), 
	    
* phi(.) is the non-linear activation of the neural network; for example, 
  phi(x) = max(0, x) (rectified linear unit ReLU), or 
         phi(x)  = 1 / (1 + exp(-a*x) ), where a is a constant 					(logistic function). 


You will be evaluated on the proportion of properly estimated topics for documents for which you do not know the topic. 

In this project, we focus on L(a,b) = ||a-b||^2 (least squares) and phi(x)  = 1 / (1 + exp(-a*x) ) (logistic function). 


**Going further**

If you have time, there are numerous avenues to explore this problem further; in particular analyzing the effect of the different parameters on the performance of the method: 
- the learning rate, 
- the parameter in the sigmoid a, 
- the non-liear activation, 
- the objective function, 
- the number of layers, 
- the initial point chosen for the start the algorithm. 
The use of regularizers (to avoid overfitting) could also be considered. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr />

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the proportion of correctly classified documents. 


<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format in a cvs file:</p>
id,class
1,x
...
5989,y

where x and y are your predictions for the first and last documents; see Data for an example.","# Dataset Description

This file contains, in matlab format, 

- A matrix Xts of dimension 43586x13960 where each row corresponds to a word and each column to a document. 

- A vector yts of dimension 13960x1 that provides the class into which each column of Xts belongs to. There are 20 classes so that yts(i) in {1,2,...20} for all i. 

- A matrix Xvr of dimension 43586x5989 which is the validation test for which you have to provide predictions. 

I have also attached an example of a submission file where all 5989 documents are predicted to belong to the first topic.",True,True
2413,expedia-hotel-ranking,"# Description

In this task, we ask you to train a model that learns to rank properties.

Hotels.com travellers provide their trip information, like destination, holiday dates and number of people in the search. Our search engine then returns a list of properties, ranked by their relevance to that traveller. 
We provide you a data set with this travel information augmented by some features we have found to be predictive, including search-level features like booking window (length of time between booking the trip and taking it), and property-level features, like the country the property is in. 
Please feel free to model with all, some or none of these, or create your own features.
The label in this data set is booking_bool, indicating whether this property was the one that was booked by the customer.
Please provide justification for your metric for this ranking task.

In the leader board, you¡¯ll see the popularity benchmark. This is a useful baseline. It¡¯s the score you¡¯d achieve on the test set if you simply ranked the properties in each search by the number of times it was booked in the training set.

Happy modelling, and good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric is NDCG@50. Relevance is given by propbookingbool
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discounted_cumulative_gain#Normalized_DCG)

submission format 

Suppose that in our test file we only have two searches:

srchid 1234 with propkeys {546,678,678}
srchid 2345 with propkeys {987,849,839,930}

suppose that after scoring this queries you have produced the following scores:

srch\_id, prop\_key, score 
1234, 546, 10.3
1234, 678, 8.3
1234, 890, 3.0
2345, 987, 2.0
2345, 849, 1.0
2345, 839, 3.0
2345, 930, 10.0

the your submission should look like:

srch\_id, prop\_key
1234, 546
1234, 678
1234, 890 
2345, 930
2345, 839
2345, 987
2345, 849","# Dataset Description

In order to solve this exercise you will need the train and test csv files. train.csv contains ground-truth relevance features and the ground-truth relevance (derived from observing consumer's purchase decision) and needs to be used for model training, test.csv will be the basis for your submission.

File descriptions

- a_train.csv - the training set
- a_test.csv - the test set

Data fields

- srch_id: unique identifier for a search
- visitor_id: visitor id used to submit the search 
- visitor_visit_nbr: count of visits for the user who has submitted the search 
- visitor_loc_country: country of the user who has submitted the search 
- visitor_loc_region: region of the user who has submitted the search 
- visitor_loc_city: city of the user who has submitted the search 
- visitor_wr_member: indicates if the user who has submitted the search is part of the hcom loyalty program
- posa_continent: point of sale continent for a partiular search
- posa_country: point of sale country for a particular search
- srch_hcom_destination_id: destination id for a particular search 
- srch_dest_longitude: destination center longitude for a particular search
- srch_dest_latitude: destination center latitude for a particular search
- srch_ci: date of check-in of a search
- srch_co: date of cehck-in of a search
- srch_ci_day: day of check-in of a search
- srch_co_day: day of check-out of a search
- srch_los: length of stay of a search (check-in - check-out date)
- srch_bw: booking window of a search (search-date - check-in date)
- srch_adults_cnt: number of adults for a search 
- srch_children_cnt: number of children for a search 
- srch_rm_cnt: number of requested rooms on the search 
- mobile_bool: indicates if the search was submitted on a mobile browser
- mobile_app: indicates if the search was submitted on a mobile app
- device: identifies the device type type used for this search
- currency: currency relevant for the point of sale the search was submitted form
- travelad_bool: indicates if a porperty in a search was a paid advert
- dotd_bool: indicates if a property in a search was advertised as a special ""daily deal""
- price_without_discount_local: discounted property price in local (posa specific) currency
- price_without_discount_usd: discounted property price in USD
- price_with_discount_local: non-discounted property price in local currency
- price_with_discount_usd: non-discounted property price in USD
- imp_drr: name of the pricing/discount rule relevant for this property
- booking_bool: indicates if property has been booken in a given search
- prop_key: unique property identifier
- prop_brand_bool: indicates if a property has a popular brand
- prop_starrating: conventional hotel star rating (1-5 Stars)
- prop_super_region: the super region this property belongs to (EMEA, APAC, NA, LATAM)
- prop_continent: continent this property is located on (Europe, North-America, South-America, ..... )
- prop_country: country this property is located in
- prop_market_id: higher granularity geo classification
- prop_submarket_id: highest granularity geo classification
- prop_room_capacity: max number of rooms this property has available
- prop_review_score: average guest review score for this property
- prop_review_count: count of customer reviews for this property
- prop_hostel_bool: indicates if a property is a hostel
- local_date: date for a particular search",True,True
2414,dac-iitpkdpetrichor2020,"# Description

<p>According to World Health Organization(WHO), Air pollution kills an estimated seven million people world wide. From smog hanging over cities to smoke inside the home air pollution poses a major threat to health and climate. In 2016, 91% of the world population was living in places where the WHO air quality guidelines levels are not met. </p>
<p> In Delhi, capital territory of india, according to a WHO survey of 1650 world cities, is the worst of any major city in the world. In 2010, the year of the WHO survey, the average PM10 level in Delhi was 286 ¦Ìg/m3. In 2013, the PM2.5 level was 153 ¦Ìg/m3. These levels are considered very  unhealthy. </p>

<p> In this challenge your task is to predict particulate matter concentration (PM 2.5). The dataset is collected from various locations. For data privacy, the locations will not be revealed.
</p>

<h2> Petrichor </h2>
Petrichor, the annual technocultural fest of IIT Palakkad, is a golden opportunity to bring out the artist or techie in you and to meet exponents from various technical, cultural and social fields. For more details visit [Petrichor](https://www.petrichor-iitpkd.com/) website.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2> Evaluation Metric </h2> 
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Squared Logarithmic error. The RMSLE is calculate as </p>
<p>\[ \epsilon = 2\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\log(p_{i}+1)-\log(a_{i}+1))^2} \ \ \ \]</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every row in the test submit PM concentration. For participants convience we have also given sample submission file. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>sampleid,PM<br />LYthQFByHI,1.2<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">DeOEAKFLzf,2.10<br />ngkfVxOpDr,25.124<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">NeKYkKNlNc,124.01<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

Jan 18, 2020 : Competition Deadline.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>sampleid</strong> - an anonymous id unique to each row</li>
<li><strong>timestamp</strong> - when the measurement is taken</li>
<li><strong>sulphur dioxide</strong> - sulphur dioxide concentration in ug/m3</li>
<li><strong>nitrogen dioxide</strong> - nitrogen dioxide concentration in ug/m3</li>
<li><strong>carborn monooxide</strong> - carbon monooxide concentration in ug/m3</li>
<li><strong>ozone</strong> - ozone concentration in ug/m3</li>
<li><strong>temperature</strong> - temperature (celsius) </li>
<li><strong>pressure</strong> - pressure in hecto pascal</li>
<li><strong>dewpoint</strong> - dew point temperature (celsius)</li>
<li><strong>rainfall</strong> - rainfall in mm</li>
<li><strong>wind direction</strong> - wind direction</li>
<li><strong>windspeed</strong> - wind speed in m/s</li>
<li><strong>location</strong> - location of surveillance site (encoded)</li>
<li><strong>PM</strong> - Particulate Matter Concentration (2.5) in ug/m3</li>
</ul>",True,True
2415,optum-ai-racing-league,"# Description

<p>A self-driving car takes measurements using sensors such as camera and predicts values for its actuators such as brake, accelerator, steering, etc. We have a RC car, with a simple camera setup at its forehead. We used the camera to record few snapshots while the car was driven manually. With each snapshot, the throttle and steering values for the car were recorded. Your task is to create a model that produces the values for angle and throttle, given the snapshot of what the car sees.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the Mean Squared Error (MSE) averaged for both the labels. The MSE is calculated for angles and throttle values, and averaged to rank the leaderboard.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For every image in the test dataset, the submission file should contain three columns: <i>tub_image_name</i>, <i>angle</i>, <i>throttle</i>. The file should contain the header line. An example submission file looks like:</p>
<pre>tub_image_name,angle,throttle<br />tub_100_19-11-27/100_cam-image_array_.jpg,0.83,-0.18<br />tub_100_19-11-27/101_cam-image_array_.jpg,0.17,-0.06<br /></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>The data consists of images taken from camera mounted on the top of car, along with the information recorded while the car was being driven manually. Every recording session is grouped in a tub, i.e., all the snapshots taken from the camera while it is being driven is stored in a directory, for example, <i>tub_118_18-01-28</i>, where the number followed by <i>tub</i> indicates the tub number which is incremented every recording session. The tub number is followed by date in <i>YY-MM-DD</i> format corresponding to when the recording was done. </p>

<p>The tubs are divided into train and test datasets. Each images in tub has an associated metadata in CSV files, for example, the <i>milliseconds</i> when the image snapshot was recorded. In addition, the training metadata file, <i>train-tubs.csv</i> has additional metadata information, <i>angle</i> and <i>throttle</i>, which are the labels that needs to be predicted. With each image snapshot, values of angle of the steering wheel and the throttle of the car is recorded. These values are between -1 to 1. 1 indicates the maximum available throttle or steering position, whereas -1 indicates the throttle or steering in reverse. For example, if 1 for angle indicated full right, -1 indicates full left. Each snapshot also contains time in milliseconds when the snapshot was recorded.</p>

<p>Your task is to predict the values of <i>angle</i> and <i>throttle</i> for all the images in <i>test-tubs</i>, whose corresponding <i>millisecond</i> information is recorded in <i>test-tubs.csv</i>.</p>

<p>For leaderboard, average of the MSE values for angle and throttle are used.</p>",True,True
