2451,sirius-january2020-recsys,"# Description

<p>Dear students! This is a little Netflix Prize for you! You are invited to predict user-movie ratings, beeing evaluated by RMSE</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Evaluation metric is RMSE</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_items.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>test_example.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to make a submission</h2>
<p>In the dataset you have file test_items.csv. This is rows from test set. In each row you have fields:
<ul>
<li>Id ¡ª just numeric id of row</li>
<li>user_id ¡ª id of user</li>
<li>item_id ¡ª id of item</li>
<li>ts ¡ª unixtime</li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>
For each row from test set you should output row in format ¡°Id,Prediction¡±. Where Id is row Id from test_items.csv and Prediction is predicted rating for particular user to particular item at particular timestamp.</p>
<p>So you can see example toy solution in file test_example.csv ¡ª for each row  from  test_set.csv it predicts rating 5. This is not very clever solution, but correct in terms of format. You can submit test_example.csv to the system and it should pass (but of course your score would not be high).</p>",True,True
