2540,boolean-retrieval-homework-ir-2-itmo-spring-2020,"# Description

<h2>Finding documents relevant with respect to the search query</h2>
<p>When search engine receives a query, first of all it looks for a set of candidate documents that contain keywords from the query. Later these documents will be ranked and re-ranked so that eventually top-10 best documents will be presented to the user. The implementation of the first retrieval stage is usually based upon inverted index data structure.
</p>
<p>
You need to implement inverted index data structure and find documents matching Boolean search queries.
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For every query and document pair you need to provide a class label: 1 if this document is relevant with respect to this query, 0 otherwise.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>QueryId,DocumentId<br />1,1,0<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">1,2,0<br />2,1,1<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,2,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Query and document texts are lemmatized, without stopwords</p>
<p>Queries are serialized as trees, for example (JEEP|§¥§¨§ª§±) (FORUM|§¶§°§²§µ§®) means (JEEP OR §¥§¨§ª§±) AND (FORUM OR §¶§°§²§µ§®).</p>
<p>In other words:
<ul>
<li>ORs are represented as |-separated lists, i.e. (TERM_A|TERM_B|...)</li>
<li>ANDs are represented as whitespace-separated lists i.e. TERM_A TERM_B ...</li>
</ul>
</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>docs.tsv</strong> - document texts, 3 tab-separated fields: DocumentId, Title Text, Body Text</li>
<li><strong>queries.numerate.txt</strong> - list of queries, 2 tab-separated fields: QueryId, Query Tree</li>
<li><strong>objects.numerate.txt</strong> - mapping of query and document ids to ObjectIds, 3 fields: ObjectId, QueryId,DocumentId</li>
<li><strong>sample.submission.txt</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format (ObjectId,Relevance)</li>
<li><strong>hw2_boolean_search</strong> - an example solution script</li>
</ul>",True,True
