2542,lora-link-quality,"# Description

<p>This competition is for CSE891-004 AIoT of MSU. 
<p>You need to train your own model based on given data of LoRa transceivers. 
The details about data are given in ""Competition Instruction.pdf"" which you can find in ""Data"" page.</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank AIoT for providing this dataset.</p>



----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSE</a>. RMSE, commonly used in regression problem, measures the average error between the predicted values and the actual ones. The RMSE score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^N(x_i-\hat{x}_i)^2}{N}}  \]</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and ESP. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,ESP<br />1,-120<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,-130<br />3,-140<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">4,-150<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">...</span><br />460,-100<br /></pre>","# Dataset Description

#Obejective Model:

In the dataset there are thousands of samples collected by a LoRa system in Tsinghua University, China. Each sample is generated by a LoRa endnode transmitting a packet to a LoRa gateway. In this competition, what you need to do is building a model predicting LoRa signal power captured by the gateway, based on the geographical information of endnodes.

In the following, there are two maps presented:

#1. (x,y) map:

This is the map of the area where the dataset is collected. 

The resolution is 10m(each pixel represents an area of 100 m^2). 

The top-left corner is (0,0). 

The coordinate system is as follows:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4508856%2Fb2ee659e438631e1a1dcf7b236ef90ac%2Fmap.jpg?generation=1581958472783077&alt=media)

#dataset
The features (columns) of data in Xtrain.csv and Xtest.csv:

1. DeviceID: The ID of the LoRa node which transmitted the packet.

2. x-axis coordinate of the endnode.

3. y-axis coordinate of the endnode.

The target value of data in Ytrain.csv:

ESP: expected signal power logged by LoRa gateway.

The training dataset consists of  1841 rows which are released for building your own model. The test dataset consists of 460 rows and their target ESP values are not given.

Gateway coordination is (780,721) with a height of 32 meter. Each LoRa node can be seen as 1-meter-high.

#2. Landcover map:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4508856%2F7314b872b4e3604dbbb43a8b902192fd%2Fclass_map.png?generation=1581968000894393&alt=media)

This is the landcover map obtained by per-pixel classification of the (x,y) map.

Class matrix: class.csv is the landcover matrix.

The value of each entry in the matrix is the landcover class label of one pixel in the class map.

Each pixel in the map is colored according to the actual landcover class in (x,y) map. Those landcover classes attenuate the signal power by varying degrees. For example, trees may obstruct signals more severe than fields. The class labels are defined as following form:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4508856%2F17cba449d6ef148c78b9239396446317%2Flandcover.png?generation=1584506375138040&alt=media)",True,True
2543,dsti-assystemdshack-part-1,"# Description

First competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metaData.csv</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>customer_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given customer</li>
<li><strong>movie_id</strong> - the id of a movie</li>
<li><strong>movie_title</strong> - the title of a movie</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>",True,True
