2558,evohackaton,"# Description

<h2>Welcome to the hackathon!</h2>

<p><strong>This is a Transfer Learning Image Classification competition.</strong></p>
<p>This challenge is dedicated to strengthen your skills in the Transfer Learning area.</p>
<p>You are allowed (and it is highly recommended) to use any open-source pre-trained models in order to predict the classes of images.</p>

<h2>Ideas and hints</h2>
<p>Here are a few options which might be worth a try.</p>

<p>
<strong>1. Similar images might have similar image vectors</strong>
</p>

<li>
download a pretrained `ImageNet` model
</li>

<li>
delete its bottom layer, which is responsible for final classification
</li>

<li>
run the `predict` function for images in `train.zip` and `test.zip` to get the image vectors
</li>

<li>
compare each image vector from `test.zip` versus all image vectors from `train.zip` (using cosine or euclidean distance)
</li>

<li>
use KNN to predict the category of an image
</li>

<p>
<strong>2. Ensemble techniques might improve your results</strong>
</p>

<li>
instead of using one pretrained model, try using five different pretrained models
</li>

<li>
instead of predicting the class of an image directly, try averaging the predictions of rotated/transformed/shifted images
</li>

<p>
<strong>3. Try data augmentation and image preprocessing techniques</strong>
</p>

<li>
download a pretrained image segmentation model
</li>

<li>
use it to separate the content image from the background image
</li>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>
Goal
</h2>

Your goal is to predict the correct classes for images.

Each image belongs to 1 of 100 categories.

For each image in the test set you must predict an integer value in range `[0, 100)`.

<h2>
Metric
</h2>

Submissions will be evaluated based on their <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification"">accuracy</a> score.

<h2>
Submission File Format
</h2>

You should submit a `.csv` file with exactly 16858 rows plus a header row.

Header row should only have two columns called `name` and `category`.

Your submission .csv file should have the following format:

    name,category
    0.jpg,9
    1.jpg,19
    2.jpg,29
    ...etc","# Dataset Description

<h2>
Data description
</h2>

<li>
<strong>train.csv</strong> file has two columns ¨C `name` and `category`.
</li>

`name` is the filename of an image. That image can be found in the `train` folder. 

`category` is the corresponding category of that image. There are 100 different categories.

<li>
<strong>train.zip</strong> file has images mentioned in <strong>train.csv</strong>
</li>

<li>
<strong>test.zip</strong> file has images for which you need to predict a corresponding category.
</li>

<h2>
Goal
</h2>

Your goal is to predict the correct classes for images.

Each image belongs to 1 of 100 categories.

For each image in the test set you must predict an integer value in range `[0, 100)`.

<h2>
Metric
</h2>

Submissions will be evaluated based on their <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification"">accuracy</a> score.

<h2>
Submission File Format
</h2>

You should submit a `.csv` file with exactly 16858 rows plus a header row.

Header row should only have two columns called `name` and `category`.

Your submission .csv file should have the following format:

    name,category
    0.jpg,9
    1.jpg,19
    2.jpg,29
    ...etc",True,True
2559,beer2020,"# Description

<p>The International Bittering Units, or IBU, scale is used to quantify the bitterness of a beer. In this competition, you will build a machine learning model to predict the IBU of a beer, given other attributes about the beer, including its color, alcohol content, and other features.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions will be scored based on root mean-squared error (RMSE) on the test set. If \(y\) represents the actual IBU and \(\hat y\) is the predicted IBU, then RMSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \hat y_i)^2}. \]</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Each beer in the training and test data?is given a unique ID. You should submit your predictions for the test data in the following format. (A sample file, <strong>beer_test_sample_submission.csv</strong>, has been provided for you.)</p>
<pre><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">id,ibu<br />6000,38.2<br />6001,13.1<br />6002,40.4<br />6003,22.0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.<br /></span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>beer_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>beer_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>beer_test_sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - a unique ID for each beer</li>
<li><strong>name</strong> - the name of the beer</li>
<li><strong>description</strong>?- a description of the beer</li>
<li><strong>available</strong> - when the beer is available</li>
<li><strong>isOrganic</strong> - ""Y"" if the beer is organic, ""N"" otherwise</li>
<li><strong>glass</strong> - the type of glass that the beer is intended to be served in</li>
<li><strong>abv</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcohol_by_volume"">alcohol by volume</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>originalGravity</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_(alcoholic_beverage)"">original gravity</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>srm</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Reference_Method"">SRM</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>ibu</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_measurement"">IBU</a>?of the beer</li>
</ul>",True,True
