2562,diamonds-price,"# Description

<p>Hai Data Analyst/Scientist,</p>
<p>Kompetisi ini dibuat untuk latihan bagi kamu yang ingin mencoba dan mengasah kemampuan analisis dan modeling yang kamu miliki.</p>
<p>Objektif dari kompetisi ini sangat sederhana: buat analisis dan model machine learning untuk menduga harga berlian.</p>
<h2>The challenge</h2>
<p>Sudah menjadi rahasia umum bahwa berlian adalah benda berharga yang harganya cukup atau bahkan sangat mahal. Setiap berlian mempunyai karakteristik tertentu mulai dari ukuran, tingkat kehalusan, dan sebgaianya. Data yang digunakan adalah data <code>diamonds</code> dari package R ggplot2.</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>Terima kasih kepada Hadley Wickham and team yang telah membuat package visulisasi yang luar biasa dan menyediakan data ini.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Perhitungan untuk evaluasi pada kompetisi ini menggunakan <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Root Means Sqaure Error (RMSE)</a>.</p>
<p>\[ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i - \hat{y_i})^2}{n}} \]</p>
<h2>Format Submission</h2>
<p><strong>Untuk setiap hasil prediksi yang disubmit</strong>, harus terdiri dari 2 (dua) kolom yang dipisah dengan tanda koma dan format file CSV. Gunakan contoh data submission untuk submit hasil kamu.</p>
<p>Baris pertama pada file submission harus nama kolom dengan format seperti berikut.</p>
<pre>id,price<br />
36,198<br />
56,189<br />
60,190<br />
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Data sudah dibagi menjadi 2 file; training (diamonds_train.csv) dan testing (diamonds_test.csv) set.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>diamonds_train.csv</strong> - untuk training set</li>
<li><strong>diamonds_test.csv</strong> - untuk test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - file sample submission format dengan format yang diinginkan</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data Dictonary</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - row that represent id of the diamond</li>
<li><strong>price</strong> - known price of diamonds</li>
<li><strong>carat</strong> - weight of the diamond </li>
<li><strong>cut</strong> - quality of the cut (Fair, Good, Very Good, Premium, Ideal)</li>
<li><strong>color</strong> - diamond colour, from D (best) to J (worst)</li>
<li><strong>clarity</strong> - a measurement of how clear the diamond is (I1 (worst), SI2, SI1, VS2, VS1, VVS2, VVS1, IF (best))</li>
<li><strong>x</strong> - length in mm</li>
<li><strong>y</strong> - width in mm</li>
<li><strong>z</strong> - depth in mm</li>
<li><strong>depth</strong> - total depth percentage = z / mean(x, y) = 2 * z / (x + y)</li>
<li><strong>table</strong> - width of top of diamond relative to widest point</li>
</ul>",True,True
2563,activitytweeting,"# Description

<p>In this assignment, you will develop a text classifier to classify tweets into two classes:
<ul>
<li> Class '1': If the tweet mentions the tweeter doing a moderate to high physical activity or how they feel about the mentioned activity</li>
<li> Class '0': If the tweet does not refer to the tweeter doing a moderate to high physical activity</li>
</ul>
For this task, all tweets will contain at least one word / term that refers to a moderate to high physical activity. However, the tweet may use the activity term in non-activity context (e.g. ""star wars marathon"") or in the context of someone else actually doing the activity or (e.g. the tweeter is watching a basketball game). In such cases, the label should be '0'. 

<h2> Dataset </h2>
On Canvas, you are provided with a training file of 4000 tweets, and a test file with 1000 tweets. You are expected to train a classifier on train data, and predict labels for each tweet in the test file. The training and test datasets are imbalanced, i.e. the proportion of tweets in the positive and negative classes are not equal in both training and test datasets.?You should follow best practices, such as splitting the train file into train and validation sets, to evaluate out-of-sample performance.

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>The dataset is derived from a current research project on identifying sedentary and physical activity mentions in tweets.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is F1-Score. The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 score weights recall and precision equally, and a good classification algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>

<h2>Output format</h2>
Each line in the output file should contain two fields, separated by a comma as below:
<pre>TweetID,Label</pre>
where TweetID is the id of the tweet from the test file and Label is either 0 or 1. Please refer to the sample_output.txt file for an example. 

<p>Note: The file should contain the column names file above. Please follow the format in the sample_output.txt file provided.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
The canvas folder for this assignment has three files:
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>TweetID</strong> - a project-specific id assigned to the tweets</li>
<li><strong>Label</strong> - 0 or 1, indicating the supervision class label</li>
<li><strong>TimeOfDay</strong> - a number from 0-23 to show the hour of day when the tweet was tweeted (adjusted for time zones).</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>",True,True
2564,data-science-lab-kaggle-sp2020,"# Description

Welcome to the in-class Kaggle competion. 
Do your best to make the best predictions by the closing time of the competition. 
Write a report that indicates what you tried, what worked and what did not. 
You will be scored based on your private and public leaderboard score but also on your creativity on trying various ideas. 

This is a binary classification competition. The meanings of the features were not revealed by the data provider for privacy reasons. 
The metric you are evaluated as is called Area Under the Curve (AUC). It is a binary accuracy metric that works well even for unbalanced problems. We will explain how the metric works in class. 


