2602,ensai-gostatistique-2020,"# Description

The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates.

The competition consist in predicting the Cobalt top soil concentration at the validation locations
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean squared error (MSE). The MSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ MSE = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (Z_i-Z^{\star}_i)^2 \]</p>
<p> where Z_i is the actual value, unknown and hosted here, Z_i* is the predicted value and i is the index.</p> 
<p>The MSE measures the accuracy of the prediction. The lower the better!</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> submission files should contain two columns: the index of the predicted site and the predicted cobalt concentration </p>","# Dataset Description

<p> The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates. 
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
Two of the provided files will be useful
<ul>
<li><strong>jura_pred.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>jura_val_loc.csv</strong> - validation locations and covariables</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong> Xloc </strong> X coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong> Yloc </strong>Y coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong>Landuse</strong> Land use: 1: Forest, 2: Pasture (Weide(land), Wiese, Grasland), 3: Meadow (Wiese, Flur, Matte, Anger), 4: Tillage (Ackerland, bestelltes Land) </li>
<li><strong>Rock</strong> Rock Types: 1: Argovian, 2: Kimmeridgian, 3: Sequanian, 4: Portlandian, 5: Quaternary. </li>
<li><strong>Cd</strong> mg cadmium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Co</strong> mg cobalt kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cr</strong> mg chromium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cu</strong> mg copper kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Ni</strong> mg nickel kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Pb</strong> mg lead kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Zn</strong> mg zinc kg^-1 topsoil</li>
</ul>",True,True
2603,uwmadison-2020spring-stat333,"# Description

<p>Welcome to Spring 2020 - Stat 333's Kaggle page for the class project! </p>

<p>This is where your group will submit your predictions for Yelp ratings in the testing and validation data sets based on the model you built from your training data. For additional details, refer to the project's grading guidelines as well as the data description on Canvas for details about the project.</p>

<p>You can download all the data from Canvas. Kaggle's data section has a sample solution format that you can use as a guide to upload your predictions to Kaggle's website.</p>

<p>Finally, please note that you will be graded based on both test AND the validation data. The public leaderboard only presents your standing based on the validation data set and is a good proxy for your performance in the test data. The private leaderboard, which will be revealed to everyone at the end of the presentations, reveals your standings based on the test data set. </p>

<p>For the technically interested: We are using an root-mean-squared error as our loss function in measuring performance. That is, your prediction error is based on </p>

<p>\[\text{prediction error}=(\text{true rating}?\text{predicted rating})^2 \]</p>
For technical questions about using Kaggle, please contact your TA.<p></p>


<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Yelp for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Submission Format

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should be a .csv file containing two columns: Id and Expected. Id column must contain the ID number from the testing and validation data set. Expected column must contain your predicted Yelp ratings for the corresponding ID number. Expected column should only be numeric numbers. 

<p>For example, the file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id, Expected<br />48861, 1.1 <br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">48862, 3.4<br />48863, 2.7<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">81434, 4.2</span></pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

+ **11:59pm Wednesday Mar 11, 2020**: self chosen group assignment submitted to the instructor by email.
+ **In class Thursday Mar 12, 2020**: random group assignments ready for students without self chosen groups. Group presentation order will be randomly generated in class and posted on Canvas.
+ **11:59pm Wednesday, Apr 22, 2020**: Kaggle submission deadline. Slides and R codes submission deadline (email to TA).
+ **Apr 23 Thursday, Apr 28 Tuesday, Apr 30 Thursday, 2020**: In class presentation.
+ **5pm Apr 30 Thursday, 2020**: Write-up submission deadline (to Canvas).","# Dataset Description

The training, validation, and testing data can be found on Canvas.

The sample solution file gives you the format of the submission file that you should be using.",True,True
