2605,mitsloanprediction2020,"# Description

This is a contest for 15.819 Marketing Analytics Assignment 2. Your goal is to predict with household will purchase weather insurance.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Predictions are evaluated using ""log loss"", which can be used to score probabilistic predictions; lower is better. It is also what `glmnet` with `family=""binomial""` will try to minimize.

A bit more info [here](https://www.kaggle.com/dansbecker/what-is-log-loss).","# Dataset Description


<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>insurance_prediction_training.csv</strong> - Existing data about households that have purchased or not. This is what you fit your model to.</li>
<li><strong>insurance_prediction_to_predict.csv</strong> - New households to get predictions about whether they will purchase.</li>
</ul>",True,True
2606,naive-bayes-imdb,"# Description

<p>Make your best model based on Naive Bayes classifier to predict positive and negative reviews from IMDB dataset.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission file should contain two columns with headers Id and Predicted. The Id column should contain ids from the test dataset, the Predicted column should contain 0 and 1 values. It total there should be 10K predicted values.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p></p>
