2626,faa-2020-cognate-identification,"# Description

<h2>Welcome to the Cognate Identification competition organized as part of the FAA course (year 2019-2020), UdL, FIL, Master Informatique</h2>

This competition is about predicting whether two word forms (or phonetic realizations of these words) that share the same meaning in two different languages are historically related (in the sense that they come from the same ancestor word). Two words that are historically related are called cognates. For instance, the English ""father"" and French ""p¨¨re"" words are cognates, both derived from Latin ""pater"", whereas Latin ""deus"" and Greek ""theos"", despite their apparent sound similarity, are not cognates as they come from different roots.

From a Machine Learning point of view, we are dealing with binary classification problem, having to decide whether two words are cognates (positive class) or not (negative class). The dataset used for this competition contains around 1.4M word pairs, corresponding to 209 original meanings/concepts realized in over 150 languages. This dataset is split into 1.14M training examples (80%) and .29M test examples (20%).  


<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>

This dataset was extracted from the Indo-European Lexical Cognacy Database (IELex), collected at the Max Planck Institute in the Netherlands: http://ielex.mpi.nl/.

<h2>Competition manager</h2>

For any information regarding this competition, please contact Pascal Denis (at inria.fr)
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy, which is computed as follows:</p>
<p>\[ Acc = 1-Err_{test} = 1-\frac{1}{n_{test}}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{test}} |f_{\theta}(\textbf{x}_i)-y_i| \]</p>
<p>where: \\((\textbf{x}_i,y_i)\\) is a test example, \\(n_{test}\\) is the total number of test examples, and \\(f_{\theta}\\) is the prediction function you have learned.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every team in the competition</strong>, submission files should contain exactly two columns: Id and Category. Id is a unique ID per datapoint, while Category indicates whether the example is an instance of cognates (value: 1) or not (value: 0). For example:</p>
<pre>Id,Category<br>540102,0<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>IE2012-lexical-data.train.csv</strong> - the training dataset</li>
<li><strong>IE2013-lexical-data.test.csv</strong> - the test dataset</li>
<li><strong>IE2012-test-maj-baseline.csv</strong> - a sample submission that implements the majority baseline (i.e., the 0 class for all examples)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - id unique for pair of words (i.e., the cognate candidates)</li>
<li><strong>Category</strong> - Class label (0 or 1) indicating whether the 2 words are cognates or not, respectively)</li>
<li><strong>word1</strong> - string or phonetic representation of the first word </li>
<li><strong>language1</strong> - language of the first word</li>
<li><strong>word2</strong> - string or phonetic representation of the second word </li>
<li><strong>language2</strong> - language of the second word</li>
<li><strong>meaning</strong> - concept or meaning associated with the two words</li>
<li><strong>Usage</strong> - field to be ignored</li>
</ul>",True,True
2627,ecs308-658,"# Description

<p> In this assignment, you will be trying to model a real-world time-series data set. </p> 

<p> This contains measurements of a certain quantity at different points in time. The train set contains 110 time points; and the test set contains another 10 points for which the measured value has been removed. In each row, the first value is the date of the measurement (in US format, so month comes before day), and the second value is the actual measurement. Your task is to train a linear regression model (using appropriate basis functions) which can predict the missing values as accurately as possible. </p>

<p> In other words, this task requires you to predict the values on 10 days based on the given information for 110 days. </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> You are allowed to use only linear regression models for this task. Cross-validation, hyper-parameter tuning, and regularisation are encouraged to produce better results. <p>

<p> Evaluation: You will be ranked on your performance on that test set using mean-squared error (MSE) metric. <\p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>


<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - date in (m/d/y format)</li>
<li><strong>value</strong> - real valued (actual quantity will be announced later)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Submission format</h2>
id,value <br>
5/1/10,0.28",True,True
