2637,2020wintermidterm,"# Description

In this project you will draw on your SAS skills to predict assessed values for the city of Boston. I've provided 4 files train, test, predict and zip which you¡¯ll have to put together. You¡¯ll then explore and prepare the data for modeling. Next, you¡¯ll build and document regression models to predict home values (AV_TOTAL), finally you¡¯ll have to predict home values on an out-of-sample predict dataset. I¡¯ve set up this Kaggle competition where you¡¯ll publish your out-of-sample dataset and compare your model against your peers.

## Problem

You now work for a major financial institution as a data scientist, in the mortgage valuation department. You have been asked to build a new regression model that will predict the market value of properties in order to determine how much money to loan a potential buyer. The target that you are attempting to predict is the assessed value column (AV_TOTAL)

You are provided three CSV files:

? Training.csv ¨C this is the data set you¡¯ll train your regression models with

? Validation.csv ¨C this is the data set you¡¯ll use to validate your regression models with

? Prediction.csv ¨C this is the ""test"" data set you¡¯ll predict ¡°assessed value¡±

## You must beat the benchmark submission!

The benchmark RMSE is 115350.9 that means you must have a score LESS than this! 
----------------------
# Evaluation

You'll be evaluated on your model's  root-mean-square error (RMSE). RMSE is a frequently used measure to assess the accuracy of regression models. 

Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) is the standard deviation of the residuals (prediction errors). Residuals are a measure of how far from the regression line data points are; RMSE is a measure of how spread out these residuals are. In other words, it tells you how concentrated the data is around the line of best fit.

SQRT(MEAN(y - y')^2)) 

Square **R**oot of the **M**ean or **S**quared **E**rror (actual minus predicted)","# Dataset Description

What's in the Zip? 

- week_7_data_dictonary.docx - a data dictionary - describing the fields of the datasets
- train.csv - the training data set
- test.csv - the test data set
