# A Competi??o

O Data Science Challenge @ ITA ¨¦ uma competi??o de ci¨ºncia de dados estudantil promovida anualmente no Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA) desde 2019.

Nesta edi??o, o desafio compreende a constru??o de um modelo de aprendizado de m¨¢quina para manuten??o preditiva em aeronaves.  Os competidores ter?o acesso a uma base de dados confidencial fict¨ªcia baseada em dados reais.  Os dados consistem em medi??es de sensores e mensagens da aeronave ao longo do tempo, al¨¦m das datas e hor¨¢rios das
trocas preventivas.

Acesse o regulamento [aqui](https://bit.ly/3baeV3z).

# Inscri??es

Ser?o permitidas inscri??es apenas de alunos atualmente matriculados ou que estiveram matriculados em 2019 em algum curso de gradua??o ou p¨®s-gradua??o das seguintes institui??es de ensino:

- Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA)
- Universidade Federal de S?o Paulo (UNIFESP)
- Instituto Federal de S?o Paulo (IFSP)

Fa?a sua inscri??o [aqui](https://forms.gle/9B7BsJ2ke9dx4gRX7).

# Apoio

- [ITAEx](https://itaex.com.br/)
- [InovaLab](https://www.inovalab.ita.br/)
- [IEAv](http://www.ieav.cta.br/index.php/home) 
- [BCG Gamma](https://www.bcg.com/pt-br/beyond-consulting/bcg-gamma/default.aspx)

# Organiza??o

- Prof. Filipe Verri (coordenador), Divis?o de Ci¨ºncia da Computa??o, ITA
- Prof. Elton Sbruzzi, Divis?o de Ci¨ºncia da Computa??o, ITA
- Prof. Vitor Curtis, Divis?o de Ci¨ºncia da Computa??o, ITA
- Profa. Ana Lorena, Divis?o de Ci¨ºncia da Computa??o, ITA
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

# Importante

Leia o [regulamento](https://bit.ly/3baeV3z) antes de participar da competi??o.

Os dados s?o confidenciais e s¨® ser?o entregues para os inscritos pelo [formul¨¢rio](https://forms.gle/9B7BsJ2ke9dx4gRX7) e ap¨®s assinado o termo de confidencialidade.

# N¨²meros

- 1.352 v?os (901 treinamento e 451 teste) de 9 diferentes aeronaves
- 16.445.612 leituras de 19 diferentes sensores ao longo dos v?os 
- 147.564 mensagens do sistema ao longo dos v?os",True,True
2642,technidus-machine-learning-competition-2-cohrt-3,"# Description

**This is the second competition to test your understanding of machine learning concepts learnt. For this competition, you will be applying your knowledge about algorithms and modelling to predict a customers purchase behavior.
 Use the customer data provided to create a classification model that predicts a customer's likelihood to buy a bike.**

Ensure you apply what you've learned in the resources shared.
Cheers.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Goal</h2> 
It is your job to predict the customers' purchase behaviour.
For each customer, you must predict a 0 or 1 for the BikeBuyer variable. (1 means the customer will buy a bike, 0 means a customer will not)

<h2> Metric</h2> 
Submissions are evaluated on Accuracy comparing the predicted value and the observed BikeBuyer value. 

<h2> Other metrics</h2> 
Your team's python notebook will also be considered for this competition. It will be evaluated outside of Kaggle after the leaderboard is published. Your team's notebook must contain the following sections:

 1. General introduction 
 2. Data preprocessing/cleaning (specifying the techniques used and why) 
 3. Exploratory data analysis (specifying the charts used and why)
 4. Modelling (Algorithms used and why)

The leaderboard will be 80% while the notebook will be 20% of total score

<h2> Submission File Format</h2> 
You should submit a csv file with exactly 3328 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (CustomerID and BikeBuyer) or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

   

 - CustomerID    
 - BikeBuyer (Contains 0's and 1's)","# Dataset Description

This data about a company's customers, including demographic features and information about purchases they have made. The company is particularly interested in analyzing customer data to determine any apparent relationships between demographic features known about the customers and the purchase behaviour. The analysis should endeavor to determine whether a customer's purchase behaviour with the company can be predicted from known customer characteristics.

Check out the description of data fields here:

[Data description][1]",True,True
2643,csci-5622-problem-set-4-2020-spring,"# Description

<h2>About this Activity</h2>
For Problem Set 4, we're stripping away a lot of the structure behind the problem and instead asking you to simply predict income from a series of demographic and employment features.
<h2>About the Dataset</h2>
You have been given two training documents - train.data and train.names - which contain the *features* (in the .data file) and the *outcomes* (in the .names file) in the Data tab above, with rows corresponding to cases. You have also been given test.data with the test case features. Your objective is to provide a test.names file that predicts the outcomes for each case. See the Data page for full details.
<h2>About Submissions</h2>
To submit a set of predictions, upload your properly formatted document in the Submit Predictions section. Use the sample in the Data section for guidance on formatting your submission. Check the Leaderboard section to see how you're doing!

(EDITED TO ADD 3/18 - You have to be logged in to Kaggle to submit, and Kaggle has some tricky rules in place to ensure each human only has one account. If your account is associated with your given name in the course roster or your campus username, I can easily link your activity to your grade. If your Kaggle account is a personal account, under a pseudonym, etc. that's fine - you'll just have to make that link in your brief description. Thanks!)

<h2>Rules</h2>
Full details of how submissions work, etc. can be found in the Rules tab above as well as on Canvas. There are some important details to note here on the main page.
- You may only submit up to two predictions to Kaggle per day. Use internal validation processes (K-fold cross validation, etc) to optimize!
-  You are working on this homework alone. This is not a group assignment!

Otherwise, this homework follows the same rules as other homework assignments - you should be working independently, using Piazza as a resource but not as a coding crutch. There are many approaches to solve this problem, and not everyone has to use the same one!
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
We thank the UCI Machine Learning Repository for access to this dataset.","# Dataset Description

train-features.csv contains the features of the examples / cases, separated by commas, that you will use to train your classifiers. You must associate the features found in each row / line with the output found in the corresponding row / line in train-output.csv to create a full case.

test-features.csv contains the features of the examples / cases that you will need to predict using your trained model.

See train.names for header descriptions of the train and test features. This will be helpful in understanding how you may need to normalize, bin, or otherwise update your data to better suit your classifiers.

You can use test-submission.demo to see what an example submission file should look like. This file is a baseline case - you can even upload it to make sure your Kaggle account is working correctly!",True,True
2644,nicn2,"# Description

