2666,staa578comp2,"# Description

Predict whether or not your bank should approve or deny business a loan based on information  provided in the loan application. The data set is from the U.S. Small Business Administration. Your goal is to estimate whether the loan will be defaulted by analyzing a dataset and then classifying the loan into one of two categories: (a) higher risk¡ªlikely to default on the loan (i.e., be charged off/failure to pay in full) or (b) lower risk¡ªlikely to pay off the loan in full.

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is  Mean F1-Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every entry in the training dataset, submission files should contain two columns: CustomerId and Approve (1=approve, 0 = deny). </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre> CustomerId, Approve <br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,0<br />3,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">4,1<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>The goal is to decide whether or not to approve a loan to a new client based on the predictors that are provided. If you predict a 1, that means that you are predicting that the customer will pay back the laon. The data columns are described in the MetaData.csv file. The response for training is the MIS_Status column, where PIF = paid in full = a successful loan. In homework 4 use only columns 26-31 of the data (for simplicity). In Homework 5, you may use any columns of the data. </p>

<p>You'll evaluate using the mean F1 score (see the Kaggle overview page for more information). Mean F1 scores closer to 1 are better scores. On the leaderboard I've included a benchmark which I created by randomly predicting 0 or 1 for the test data set (see random.guess on the leaderboard). This week, focus more on getting your analyses to work and creating the ROC and PR curves required in the assignment. Your Kaggle score will be the benchmark to see if you can beat your score next week when you use more predictors. </p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>loan_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>loan_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>loan_SampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metaData.csv</strong> - Explains all the data fields in the training data set. Note that in the test data the MIS_Status column is replaced by CustomerID that you'll use in the evaluation step</li>

</ul>",True,True
2667,ubc-mdscl-colx563-ner,"# Description

<h2>UBC COLX 563 Lab 1: Named Entity Recognition</h2>
<p>In this competition, you will compare your generated NER IOB tags against your classmates and the groundtruth for a set of data. You can download the data from this page (Data tab) or use the one on your local storage. Ensure you submit a field comprising two columns with headers Id, Predicted.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submitted files should saved as a CSV and contain 30617 lines. Files should contain a header and subsequent rows of data:</p>
<pre>Id,Predicted<br />1,O<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,O<br />3,B-PERSON<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">4,I-PERSON<br /></span>
----------------------
# Prizes

<h2>Prizes are optional points for the assignment:</h2>
-1st: 2 points
-2nd: 1.8 points
-3rd: 1.6 points
-4th: 1.4 point
-5th: 1.2 point
-6th: 1 point
-7th: 0.8 point
-8th 0.6 point
-9th: 0.4 point
-10th: 0.2 point","# Dataset Description

<p> The kaggle_untagged data is available from GitHub, but it is also available here for download. Ensure you process the filenames alphabetically.</p>",True,True
2668,isu-stat-502602-new-york-real-estate-competition,"# Description

<h2>Generalities</h2>
<p>This is a real estate price prediction competition.  The training data are from 57,382 real estate sales in Queens NYC 2003-2005.  The test set is a related set of 17,573 real esate sales for the same locale in 2006.<p> 

Since, of course, all sales prices are knowable by downloading the original dataset and matching cases, for purposes of this competition

**it is an ethical violation and violation of ISU academic integrity standards to do so** .

You are welcome to read and learn from forums and use kernels related to this problem and similar ones, like material at

https://www.kaggle.com/c/house-prices-advanced-regression-techniques#tutorials

but you **may not** find the actual selling prices for the test set before making your final class predictions. Of course, if you wish to see the prices for the test set after the class competition is over, that is fine. (Examining them is potentially useful for understanding what important aspects of the relationships between property characteristics and price your predictions failed to capture.)

<h2>Training, Testing, and Feedback</h2>

For purposes of this competition, the standard ML paradigm of ""both training and test sets come from exactly the same distribution"" has been broken.  There are undoubtedly some ""time effects"" operating across 2003-2006, and the cases for training and testing are not randomly taken from that time period.  You are welcome to try to account for economic changes (like inflation) in building your predictors.  We'll not make this a fully realistic economic exercise by allowing only information available through 2005 to be used in making the 2006 predictions.  Two data files specifically relevant to Queens real estate price trends 2005-2006 are provided on the ""data"" page and you may look for other US aggregate real estate information to use if you like.

A randomly selected 20% of the test set is used to provide public scores and rankings on the leaderboard. The final scores and rankings are based on the other 80% of the test set.


----------------------
# Evaluation


<hr />

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSLE. This is</p>

<p>\[ \epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N (\log(prediction_i + 1) - \log(actual_i+1))^2 } \]</p>

<p>The 1s in the formula are there because this metric has been used in problems where (while inherently non-negative) ys can be 0.  There seems to be no Kaggle option to remove them (and in this problem their presence is essentially inconsequential).<p>

<h2>Baseline Predictions</h2>
<p> A baseline set of predictions (all prices $379954) derived by exponentiating the sample mean of log(price+1) for the training set has been scored and entered on the leaderboard.  Very substantial improvement on this naive set of predictions should be achieved.<p>  

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: <strong>INDEX</strong> and <strong>SALES PRICE</strong>.  The file should have a header, 17,573 rows of predictions, and the following format:</p>

<pre>INDEX,SALES PRICE<br />1,400000<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2,400000<br />3,500000<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">4,350000<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>


----------------------
# Additional Data And Information Sources

Latitude and Longitude for US Zip Codes are available here:
https://www.kaggle.com/joeleichter/us-zip-codes-with-lat-and-long
(Note that Zip Codes are only ""approximately"" geographically located. But these are better than nothing in terms of saying ""where"" a property is located.  Better locations could be had by getting latitudes and longitudes for NYC ""Blocks"" that are part of the Training and Test Sets.  Those are probably available from the NYU GIS Data Repository, but I don't know how to extract information from GIS files.)

Information on the NYC Building Classification System is here:
https://www1.nyc.gov/assets/finance/jump/hlpbldgcode.html



Since, of course, all sales prices are knowable by downloading the original dataset and matching cases, for purposes of this competition

**it is an ethical violation and violation of ISU academic integrity standards to do so** .

You are welcome to read and learn from forums and use kernels related to this problem and similar ones, like material at

https://www.kaggle.com/c/house-prices-advanced-regression-techniques#tutorials

but you **may not** find the actual selling prices for the test set before making your final class predictions. Of course, if you wish to see the prices for the test set after the competition closes, that is fine. (Examining them is potentially useful for understanding what important aspects of the relationships between property characteristics and price your predictions failed to capture.)","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Queens Training Set.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>Queens Test Set.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>Queens Example.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>Queens Summary 2005.csv</strong> - aggregate information for some 2005 Queens sales</li>
<li><strong>Queens Summary 2006.csv</strong> - aggregate information for some 2006 Queens sales</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
A glossary of terms for NYC proerty sales can be found here:

https://www1.nyc.gov/assets/finance/downloads/pdf/07pdf/glossary_rsf071607.pdf",True,True
