2670,aiml-adult-dataset,"# Description

<p>If you joined this competition it means that you chose to work with the adult dataset, instead of the zoo dataset. Your goal now will be to take the training data and use it to train a KNN and Naive Bayes Classifiers. After training, make predictions and then format your results similar to what is in the example solution file, and submit them. The final submission you make will be counted as your submission for this portion of the assignment. </p>

<p>Test and training data can be read in using the code below. Similar to your in-class assignments, make sure the data files are in the same folder as your notebook file.</p>

<pre>
data_train_df = pd.read_csv(""TrainingFileName.csv"")
data_train_ft = data_train_df.drop('Class', axis=1)
data_train_tgt = data_train_df[""Class""]
</pre>


<p>To format your solution correctly you can use the function below. It will take your prediction data and write it to CSV file, which you can then submit as your solution.</p>
<pre>
//predictions should be the result returned by modelName.fit(test_features)
def writeSubmission(predictions):
    i=1
    submissionList = []
    for prediction in predictions:
        submissionList.append([str(i), str(prediction)])
        i+=1
    
    with open('submission.csv', 'w', newline='') as submission:
        writer = csv.writer(submission)

        writer.writerow(['Id', 'Category'])
        for row in submissionList:
            writer.writerow(row)
</pre>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>
This page has the datafiles that you will be using to do your training and testing for the data. 
</p>
- train.csv has your training data
    - Class 0 is <= 50K
    - Class 1 is >50K 
- test.csv has your testing data which you will use to make your predictions for submission",True,True
2671,edld-654-spring-2020,"# Description

# Statewide testing data
Welcome to the homepage for our class competition. I've uploaded a data dictionary to go along with the data itself, but please don't hesitate to get in contact with Joe or I if you have questions.

As a basic overview, students in every state across the nation are tested annually in reading and math in grades 3-8.  These specific data are simulated from an actual statewide testing administration across the state of Oregon, but the overall distributions are highly similar. The school IDs are real, and you should be able to use these IDs (specifically the `ncessch`) to link the data with other sources (for example, see the [National Center for Educational Statistics](https://nces.ed.gov/ccd/)). Because you have the longitude and latitude, you could also consider linking the data with census data to obtain information on, for example, the economic conditions of the surrounding area around the school (you would have to do this with a spatial join, most likely).
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Root Mean Square Error

This is pretty straightforward. We are in a regression setting and we're using one of the most basic evaluation metrics, the root mean square error.

$$
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \Sigma(y\_i -\hat{ y\_i})^2 }
$$
----------------------
# Timeline

# Due Dates
You are allowed to submit predictions whenever you'd like. However, you MUST submit predictions on the following dates. Please comment these submissions with their purpose.

* Preliminary Fits 1: Due 4/29/20
* Preliminary Fits 2: Due 5/20/20
* Final Fits: Due 6/10/20 (these should be from the model you discuss in your blog post)
----------------------
# Prizes

# Extra Credit
Up to five points extra credit will be awarded to the team providing the most performant model. If there are ties amongst teams, extra credit will be awarded at the discretion of the instructor.","# Dataset Description

Please see the data dictionary for specific column definitions, and the overview tab for an overall overview of the data.",True,True
2672,principios-de-machine-learning-regresion,"# Description

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 100 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas. 

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 20 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 100 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado en base a la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 20 sucursales. 

**?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?**
-------------------------

 - Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 10 p¨¢ginas: 50%.  
 - Predicci¨®n: 50%.

La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en esta parte, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20.

La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros, aqu¨ª o en el curso de Blackboard puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n sera la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE).

Formato de sometimiento
-----------------------

Los sometimientos deben ser archivos .txt de una ¨²nica columna, en la que cada fila represente las ventas de ropa de mujer de las 20 zonas a predecir a partir de la informaci¨®n de la base de validaci¨®n. Las filas deben estar en el mismo orden de los datos presentados en la base de validaci¨®n (ventas_exam_qualify_muj.txt).","# Dataset Description

El archivo ventas_exam_model_muj.txt contiene informaci¨®n sobre 100 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa para mujer que vende por cat¨¢logo.

El objetivo es realizar la predicci¨®n de las 20 zonas contiguas siguientes cuya informaci¨®n est¨¢ contenida en el archivo ventas_exam_qualify_muj.txt, en el cual se ha omitido la variable a predecir (ropamujer).
 
Ambos archivos est¨¢n en formato .txt, separados por tabulador y con l¨ªnea de encabezados entrecomillada.

Campos de datos
---------------

- ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o. 
- correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
- p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
- tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos promedio. 
- impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
- servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
- idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
- tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
- Idloc: ID de la tienda.
- Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
- promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
- nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",True,True
