2688,govinds-kaggle-competition,"# Description

<h2>ML Competition</h2>
<p>You've made it here! I must say I'm super proud of you, and you should be too. You now have an idea of how to use Machine Learning Models, at least the basics. Now, it's time to test them out for real! Lets see how you all do in a friendly compeition!<br>Here's what you need to do:</p>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Remember the Spine Dataset given to you in the Github? It's back. This is your dataset. Go on, process it, and train a model on it, and submit your answers. Remember, this is a friendly competition, lets keep it that way. <br>Feel free to discuss your parameters and tuning on the group. And please dont kill each other for the top spot<br>(its me, and you cannot beat me because I <b>have</b> the correct answers(bwa ha ha ha ha))<br>I really hope you guys enjoy this competition, and had a good time these 3 days! <br>Please stay safe, stay home, and chant with me: <br>""Go Corona, Corona Go!!!""(hope everyone gets the reference). <br>Anyways, <br>Happy coding! &lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<pre>&lt;h2&gt;Evaluation Metric: F-Beta Score&lt;/h2&gt;</pre>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Mr. Ali Hussain for providing this dataset.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>trainGT.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>testGT.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>About the data</h2>
As described in the <a href = ""https://www.kaggle.com/alihussain1993/lower-back-pain-symptoms-datasetlabelled"">dataset</a>,<br>
310 Observations, 13 Attributes (12 Numeric Predictors, 1 Binary Class Attribute - No Demographics)<br>
Lower back pain can be caused by a variety of problems with any parts of the complex, interconnected network of spinal muscles, nerves, bones, discs or tendons in the lumbar spine. Typical sources of low back pain include:<br>
The large nerve roots in the low back that go to the legs may be irritated<br>
The smaller nerves that supply the low back may be irritated<br>
The large paired lower back muscles (erector spinae) may be strained<br>
The bones, ligaments or joints may be damaged<br>
An intervertebral disc may be degenerating<br>
An irritation or problem with any of these structures can cause lower back pain and/or pain that radiates or is referred to other parts of the body. Many lower back problems also cause back muscle spasms, which don't sound like much but can cause severe pain and disability.<br>
While lower back pain is extremely common, the symptoms and severity of lower back pain vary greatly. A simple lower back muscle strain might be excruciating enough to necessitate an emergency room visit, while a degenerating disc might cause only mild, intermittent discomfort.<br>
<b>This data set is about to identify a person is abnormal or normal using collected physical spine details/data.</b>",True,True
2689,qmi-inhouse,"# Description

<p>
This is the in class kaggle competition for QMI Spring 2020. The data needed is in the Data page and you can view your position on the leaderboard. You will only be able to see your position on the public leaderboard. The public leaderboard uses a subset of the testing data that we give you to rank you. You can see your position here. However, there is also a private leaderboard that uses the remaining data in your test set. Please make sure to not overfit. Hopefully, if you do well in the public leaderboard, you will do well in the private leaderboard, but it is still possible to overfit the public leaderboard. Consider using your own validation schemes to get a sense of your generalization error as well.
</p>

<p>
This Kaggle competition doesn't have to be completed individually. Although this is a competition, please collaborate by posting notebooks and or forming teams of 2-3. You're ranking on both the public and private leaderboards is important but understanding is the most important part of this competitions. If you have any questions please reach out to @wesleyklock on Slack. In order to minimize time spent many of the features have been edited. There is no other information on the features, so please explore the data to understand the features and decided how you want to proceed. Credit will be given for creativity as well so try lots of different things (albeit with adequate justification and explanation for your approaches).
</p>

<p>
At the end of the competition we would like everyone to submit a notebook so we can see what you tried. As a result, please sufficiently comment your notebook in markdown and don't remove things that you tried but didn't work.
</p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Data Science Lab EE 460J for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>This specific competition asks you to implement a binary classification model. The metric we are using is ROCAUC. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic). Thus we are trying to minimize false positives and maximize true positives. Feel free to use any objective function or model as long as your submission file is according to the example in the Data tab.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p>For every sample in the test dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Y. You're data should be in .csv format where every line is a data sample and Id and Y are seperated by a comma.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>customer_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given customer</li>
<li><strong>movie_id</strong> - the id of a movie</li>
<li><strong>movie_title</strong> - the title of a movie</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>",True,True
