2746,sasviyaforlearners,"# Description

## Introduction

The SAS Viya for Learners Challenge hosted online through Kaggle InClass is geared towards students who are looking to get into and engage with machine learning problems. Students will walk through the data science process and learn how to build machine learning models on a simulated business problem, with a chance to prove their skills on the live leader board.

SAS Viya for Learners is a full suite of cloud-based software that supports the entire analytics life cycle ¨C from data, to discovery, to deployment ¨C and it also lets you code in SAS, Python or R. Machine learning is about using a combination of programming for model building, as well as visual, interactive interfaces to be able to explore data and test assumptions quickly.

__To enter the challenge:__
* Register for access to the SAS Viya for Learners environment by clicking on the __SAS Viya For Learners Registraton__ tab
* While you wait for email confirmation, please read through the rest of the section for details of the challenge.

## Table of Contents
1. [The Challenge Problem](#introduction)
2. [Challenge Structure](#navigation)
   - [Getting Started](#gettingstarted)
3. [SAS Viya Resources](#resources)
4. [Contact](#contact)

## The Challenge Problem <a name=""introduction""></a>

A simulated telecommunications company, SAS Telecom, is interested in selling more of its services to its existing customers. SAS Telecom is particularly interested in customers that upgrade their services (up-selling) and customers who purchase other services in addition to what they have currently (cross-selling). Ultimately, SAS Telecom want to identify among their new customers, who is likely to be up-sold and/or cross-sold and target those customers with offers.

To that end, they have hired you, who is part of their data science team, to understand the factors that determine whether a customer is likely to be up-sold or cross-sold. To do this, they have provided you a dataset of existing customers who were either up-sold and/or cross-sold services with a list of other information and you must build an analytical model to be able to predict the likelihood of a customer being up-sold and/or cross-sold.

## Challenge Structure <a name=""navigation""></a>

### Getting Started and Entering
To start developing the ML models for this challenge on SAS Viya for Learners, all participants need access to the environment. If you came here from the landing page, you should have an email with a provided credentials for you to access the environment. If you are signing up through Kaggle, please refer to the SAS Viya For Learners Registration section for more details.

Once you have access to the environment, please refer to the Data section to understand and walk-through the data that will be used for the challenge.

### Rules & Evaluation

Please refer to the Evaluation and the Submission Requirements sections for more details.

## SAS Viya Resources <a name=""resources""></a>

To get you started, please refer to the Notebooks section of the Kaggle Competition page. In this Notebook section, are two programming templates in Python and R that walk-through a machine learning pipeline and build a simple model to get you started with the challenge.

For resources on how to use the SAS Viya GUI interfaces, click here

## Contact & Acknowledgements<a name=""contact""></a>
- Please use the Notebooks section for discussion on the challenge
- Feel free to reach out to the SAS Academic team for any further questions (Academic@oz.sas.com)

Thanks for playing!
----------------------
# Evaluation & Submission Requirements

## Submission Requirements

In addition to the Evaluation section, all submissions require accompanying model code to demonstrate that the SAS Viya procedures were used to build and train models. The model code will contain two parts:
* The training script, which is either a Notebook file (Python/R) or a .sas file (SAS).
* The model score code, which is either a .sas file or a combination of .sas & ASTORE. These are scored representations of the model used to score new data.


<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

## Competition Timeline & Dates

- 10AM 13th July 2020 - Competition page launches and participants are able to view the data and familiarise themselves with the problem

- 8AM 20th July, 2020 - Challenge begins. The test dataset will be uploaded onto the Kaggle page and submissions are open to participants for the Leaderboard.

- 6PM 27th July 27, 2020 - Competition ends and submission deadline.

All deadlines will be AEDT based unless otherwise noted.
----------------------
# Prizes

## Prizes

Participants get a chance to hone their skills and receive a SAS Badge for their resume and displayed on LinkedIn.

There will be two badges to recognise:
 - Participants for entering the challenge
 - The final leaderboard winners

These badges will look like:

An email will be sent to the Winner at the top of the Leaderboard from the SAS Academic Team (Academic@oz.sas.com).

----------------------
# SAS Viya For Learners Registration

## Registration 

In order to  to participate in the challenge, you need to register for access to the SAS Viya for Learners environment. This environment is a 24/7, cloud-based environment where you will have access to the SAS Viya analytics platform in order to build models for the challenge.

To register, please visit the following link and sign-up for access:
https://www.sas.com/en_au/events/2020/sas-viya-for-learners-challenge.html

After applying, you should receive an email notice. Please follow the instructions specified in the email notice to navigate into the SAS Viya for Learners environment.
----------------------
# SAS Viya Resource Portal

## Resources

For the SAS Viya for Learners Challenge, we have created a customised learning portal containing resources to learn how to use the SAS Viya platform. 

To access the portal:
1.	Go to support.sas.com/myelearn and log in to your profile. If you do not currently have a profile, please create one using a link provided on support.sas.com/myelearn.
2.	Once you've logged in to your My Training page, locate the ""To Activate a New Product"" box on the right side of the screen.
3.	Enter the activation code, __*FS00613876281*__ and click Submit.
4.	Review and accept the license agreement.
5.	You should now see the training in your list of active courses
6.	Click on the portal link to access the SAS Viya for Learners resources.

Once you have performed this initial activation, visit support.sas.com/myelearn or select My Training from any page on the SAS training web site and log in to your profile to access the portal.","# Dataset Description

## Introduction 
The data used for this competition is simulated customer data for a telecommunications company. This particular telecom is interested in understanding what factors determine whether their existing customers purchase other services. For example, if an individual signs up for a phone plan, what determines whether they also sign up for an internet plan?

The particular target variable is the ""Upsell_Xsell"" flag, along with over a hundred variables that may or may not be useful in building an accurate predictive model. 

## Instructions for the Challenge
The training data is already loaded in the Viya for Learners environment. However, you will have to download the test data and load it into the environment - please do this when you are ready to evaluate your models.

## File Descriptions

*__looking_glass_v4_row10.csv__ - Sampled 10 rows from the training data

*__test.csv__ - Test set containing new customers to evaluate


## Data Fields
Please see description below for the individual data fields.",True,True
2747,recognizance,"# Description

<h1>Update</h1>
Top 21 teams(private leaderboard) have to submit your code here [Click Here](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScY0a52pZ6ZxYyAeG_lg5deOnM_-VOOvHLZlx83Uc4iU_AFYw/viewform?usp=sf_link)
Otherwise, their rank on Kaggle will not be considered valid.

<p>Recognizance 2020 is back with some exciting and challenging tasks. In this year's competition, for the first task, we are providing you the X-Ray Images belonging to 2 classes( COVID and Normal). The task is to classify whether a person has COVID-19 or Not.</p>




<ul>
<strong>Some general guidelines</strong>
<li> There will be a public leader board and a private leader board.</li>

<li>After the competition the top performers will be asked to submit the code of their machine learning algorithm. Final standing will be evaluated only after the code evaluation.</li>


</ul>

<h2>Disclaimer</h2>
<p>This is not an official clinical study and neither we claim that the results produced here can be used directly for medical institutions. This is merely a use of data for educational purposes. </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every image in the test set</strong>,  submission files should contain two columns: image and label. The image field will contain the name of the image on which predictions are being made and label field corresponds to the class to which the image belongs to. A sample submission file is provided with the dataset for reference.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
*<pre>Image, Label<br><br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">test1.jpg,0<br />test2.jpg,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">test3.jpg,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>You are provided a dataset comprises of X-Ray Images. These images belongs to 2 classes. There are a total of 163 training images (83 of Covid patients & 80 of normal person) and 24 test images. The task is to build a classifier which can determine the classes of these images.
1: COVID-19 infected patients , 0: normal person </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train -</strong> contains the training images(two folders with the name as labels).</li>
<li><strong>test -</strong> contains the test images.</li>
<li><strong>train_label.csv -</strong> labels of training images</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv -</strong> a sample submission file in the correct format.</li>


</ul>",True,True
2748,ruccardinality2,"# Description

<p>Cardianlity estimation is a fundamental task in database query processing and optimization.</p>

<p>In this challenge, you are given a dataset with a set of SQL queries (already extracted into csv format) and the final cardinality of these queries. Further more, execution plans of these queries are also provided.</p>

<p>The dataset is spited into training and testing parts, while the testing data has no labels (i.e. final cardinality of the queries). Our task is to predict the cardinality of testing data using machine learning models learned from the training data.</p>

<p>Contact jitao@ruc.edu.cn if you have any problem.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>For each query in the testing set, your model should predict the final cardinality.</p>

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE). The RMSLE is a relative error, measuring the factor by which an estimate differs from the actual cardinality:</p>

<p>$$
R M S L E=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \Sigma_{i=1}^{n}\left(\log \left(a c t u a l_{i}+1\right)-\log \left(p r e d i c t e d_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2}}
$$</p>

<p>Submission Format</p>
<p>Submission files should be in .csv format and contain two columns: Query ID and Predicted Cardinality.</p>

<p>**NOTE: the query id should start from 0**</p>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>

```
Query ID,Predicted Cardinality
0,92345
1,218
2,146
3,52452
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

File descriptions

training_data.csv - the extracted SQL training set

testing_data.csv - the extracted SQL test set

sampleSubmission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

column_min_max_value.csv - supplemental information about the data

training_plans - query plans for training data

testing_plans - query plans for testing data",True,True
