2754,i2a2-peru-bank-credit-risk-evaluation,"# Description

In this competition, you'll be given a dataset with applications for Bank Credit. Each row contains attributes that are used to identify if the customer has good or bad credit risk. Your goal is to create a classification model to predict the correct outcome.

Note that it's worse to predict a bad customer as good than to predict as good customer as bad. Than, the precision is more important the that recall. For that reason, the competitions metric is F-Beta Score 0.5 (the precision is two times more important than the recall).
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score""> Mean FBeta-Score</a>. The F1 score (beta = 1), commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<p>But for this competition, instead of using F1-Score, we're using FBeta-Score with Beta = 0.5. It means that the precision is twice as important as the recall.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every customer in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: id and good_credit_risk. 
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,good_credit_risk<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,1<br />9,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_without_labels.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

Beyond the ""id"", that's an anonymous id unique to a given customer, the data fields are self explanatory.",True,True
2755,stat-380-02-new-species-with-ml-2020,"# Description

