2756,recognizance1,"# Description

<h1>Update</h1>
Top 15 teams(private leaderboard) have to submit your code here [Click Here](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScY0a52pZ6ZxYyAeG_lg5deOnM_-VOOvHLZlx83Uc4iU_AFYw/viewform?usp=sf_link)
Otherwise, their rank on Kaggle will not be considered valid.


<p>Recognizance 2020 is back with some exciting and challenging tasks. In this year's competition, for the second task, we are providing you the food Images belonging to 5 classes. the task will be to classify the images into different classes.</p>




<ul>
<strong>Some general guidelines</strong>
<li> There will be a public leader board and a private leader board.</li>
<li>After the competition the top performers will be asked to submit the code of their machine learning algorithm. Final standing will be evaluated only after the code evaluation.</li>
<li>No external data is allowed. </li>
<li>No pretrained models are allowed.</li>



</ul>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every image in the test set</strong>,  submission files should contain two columns: image and label. The image field will contain the name of the image on which predictions are being made and label field corresponds to the class to which the image belongs to. A sample submission file is provided with the dataset for reference.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Image, Label<br><br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">0.jpg,0<br />1.jpg,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">2.jpg,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>You are provided with a training set and a test set of images. The dataset comprises of 5 classes. There are 1659 training images and 281 test images. The goal of the competition is to create a classifier capable of determining the class of the image from a photo.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train -</strong> contains the training images(5 folders with the name as labels).</li>
<li><strong>test -</strong> contains the test images.</li>
<li><strong>train_label.csv -</strong> csv file containing two fields: image, which corresponding to name of the images (which are present in the train folder and in the subfolders named as their labels) and label which corresponds to the class to which the image belongs to.</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv -</strong> a sample submission file in the correct format.</li>


</ul>",True,True
2757,regression-ecs308-658,"# Description

<p> In this assignment, you will be trying to model a real-world time-series data set. </p> 

<p> This contains measurements of a certain quantity at different points in time. The train set contains 110 time points; and the test set contains another 10 points for which the measured value has been removed. In each row, the first value is the date of the measurement (in US format, so month comes before day), and the second value is the actual measurement. Your task is to train a linear regression model (using appropriate basis functions) which can predict the missing values as accurately as possible. </p>

<p> In other words, this task requires you to predict the values on 10 days based on the given information for 110 days. </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> You are allowed to use only linear regression models for this task. Cross-validation, hyper-parameter tuning, and regularisation are encouraged to produce better results. <p>

<p> Evaluation: You will be ranked on your performance on that test set using mean-squared error (MSE) metric. <\p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>


<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - date in (m/d/y format)</li>
<li><strong>value</strong> - real valued (actual quantity will be announced later)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Submission format</h2>
id,value <br>
5/1/10,0.28",True,True
2758,recognizance3,"# Description

<h1>Update: Deadline Extended till 17th April 9:00 AM</h1>
<h1>About Recognizance</h1>
<p>Recognizance is a Machine Learning event organised by the Department of Electrical Engineering IIT BHU, Varanasi. It has 2 Rounds. Round 2 of the competition would be from 17-19th April. This is Round 1 of Competition in which You are given a basic regression-based  problem.</p>
<h2>Problem Statement</h2>
<p>You are GIven 8 Attributes(quantities calculated) of <a href=""https://www.google.com/search?q=airfoil&oq=airfoil+&aqs=chrome..69i57j69i60l2j69i59l3.5069j1j7&sourceid=chrome&ie=UTF-8"">airfoil</a>(i.e. cross sectional shape of wing,blade(propeller,rotot,turbine etc.)) which are calculated through various tests.The units of different attributes are given.You have to predict  Pressure level(Sound pressure level in decibels) so as to reduce noise.</p>
<ul>
<strong>Some general guidelines</strong>
<li> There will be a public leader board and a private leader board.</li>
<li>After the competition the top performers will be asked to submit the code of their machine learning algorithm. Final standing will be evaluated only after the code evaluation.</li>
<li>No external data is allowed</li>
<li>No pretrained models are allowed.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Time Duration</h2>
<p>Competition will begin on 16th April 9:00 AM to 17th April 9:00 AM</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Submission format should be similar to samplesubmission.csv where the first column should be id and the second column should be pressure level corresponding to id. It should have the same number of rows as that of the test file.
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean square error(MSE).It is calculated by the squared sum of the absolute difference between true and predicted value</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - an anonymous unique id</li>
<li><strong>Attribute1</strong> Attribute with units of (Hz)</li>
<li><strong>Attribute2</strong>Attribute with units of (degrees)</li>
<li><strong>Attribute3</strong> Attribute with units of length in meters</li>
<li><strong>Attribute4</strong> Attribute with units (meters/second)</li>
<li><strong>Attribute5</strong> Attribute in meters</li>
<li><strong>Attribute6</strong>Attribute with units of (Newton)</li>
<li><strong>Attribute7</strong> Attribute with units of (Newton)</li>
<li><strong>Attribute7</strong> Attribute describing type of material</li>
<li><strong>Pressure</strong> The only output describing Pressure level(Sound pressure level in decibels)</li>
</ul>
<h6>Note that you are given quantities calculated for <a href=""https://www.google.com/search?q=airfoil&oq=airfoil+&aqs=chrome..69i57j69i60l2j69i59l3.5069j1j7&sourceid=chrome&ie=UTF-8"">airfoil</a>.You have to predict sound pressure level(in dB) so as to reduce noise.
<h5>Scoring Metric</h5>
<p>Scoring Metric used will Mean Squared error(MSE)</p>",True,True
2759,cu-boulder-image-classification-2,"# Description

<h1> KAGGLE MINI COMPETITION: IMAGE CLASSIFICATION TASK</h1>
<p>This is the Kaggle mini-competition, part of CSCI 4622  that you will be participating. In this competition, you will work with a dataset to classify German traffic signs. This is a simple clean dataset in which each image is 32x32x3 sized which is flattened for convenience purpose to 3072 features. Training and validation data are available in the 'Data' tab. </p>


<h2>Remember: </h2>
<ul>
<li> You should be working in teams of 2-3 people. You are not required to physically code together but you are encouraged to try different things and select the best model you'd like to submit.
</li>
<li> Competition is open from Friday (4/10/2020) at 2 PM to Friday (4/17/2020) at Midnight. 
</li>
<li> Your grade on all the mini competitions account for 20% of your total grade
</li>
</ul>

<h2> How does this work? </h2>
<p> Your goal in this exercise is to build the best model, as a team, to predict the image's class in this dataset (more information in the Data tab). How you code and work as a team is up to you: you can all work on the same code and divide work, or build different models and ensemble amongst them, or any other method you prefer. Once you build the model, you need to upload your submissions. You will have access to labeled training data and unlabeled validation data. Your model will be evaluated based on the log-loss obtained on this validation data. </p>

<p> Based on your log-loss, you will be assigned a rank on the Kaggle Leaderboard. This way you are able to monitor your standing in this competition.  Once the competition is closed, the person at the top of the leaderboard is the winner. But wait, there's a twist. We will hold out an additional subset of the data that your model will be evaluated on. You will be unable to see the scores on this set until the competition closes. This means rankings can change when the competition closes. Therefore, though you try to improve your rankings, remember to keep your model generalizable so that you can have the last laugh. </p>

<h2> Deliverables a.k.a what you will be graded on </h2>

<ul>
<li> Your code(s)
</li>
<li> Short one or two-page report summarizing everything you did to build your best model. It is recommended that you limit it to one page unless there is something you really want to mention that can't be fit. (You must submit a report in order to get a grade)
</li>
<li> Individual's contibutions in brief </li>
</ul>

<h2> Update Regarding Data and Timeline </h2>
<p> There was a problem with how labels were specified in the data. They are now one-hot encoded. If you don't have access to these one-hot encoded data please re-download. </p>

<p> The timeline has now been updated, the competition will run through midnight on <strong> Friday (4/17/2020) </strong> and your reports are due by midnight on <strong>Monday (4/20/2020) </strong></p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2> Goal</h2>

<p>It is your job to predict the correct label of the traffic sign. For each Id in the test set, you must predict the label of the German traffic sign image. </p>

<h2>Metric</h2>

<p>Submissions are evaluated on <a href=""http://wiki.fast.ai/index.php/Log_Loss""> Multi-class-loss </a> between the predicted onehot values and the observed onehot values</p>

<h2> Submission file format </h2>
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
<pre>
id	oh_0    oh_1   oh_2 ... oh_41  oh_42
0	0          0        1       ...   0         0
1	0          1         0      ...   0         0
2	1          0        0      ...   0         0
</pre>

<h4> NOTE: </h4>
<ol>
<li>Ignore or remove the column named Unnamed: 0 in the test data.</li>
<li>Once you create a dataframe containing labels column, name the index column 'id'</li>
<li>Sample code to do this --> data_frame_sol.index.name = 'id'</li>
<li>Please submit the submissions file name as 'sample.csv' to get evaluated.</li>
</ol>
----------------------
# Timeline

Competition opens on Friday (4/10/2020) and goes through Friday midnight (4/17/2020).","# Dataset Description

<p>Recognition of traffic signs is a challenging real-world problem of high industrial relevance. Although commercial systems have reached the market and several studies on this topic have been published, systematic unbiased comparisons of different approaches are missing and comprehensive benchmark datasets are not freely available. </p>


<p>Traffic sign recognition is a multi-class classification problem with unbalanced class frequencies. Traffic signs can provide a wide range of variations between classes in terms of color, shape, and the presence of pictograms or text. However, there exist subsets of classes (e. g., speed limit signs) that are very similar to each other. </p>


<p>The classifier has to cope with large variations in visual appearances due to illumination changes, partial occlusions, rotations, weather conditions, etc. </p>


<p>Humans are capable of recognizing the large variety of existing road signs with close to 100% correctness. This does not only apply to real-world driving, which provides both context and multiple views of a single traffic sign, but also to the recognition from single images.</p>

<p>It's a great dataset for multi-class image classification problem.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_and_test.npz</strong> - the complete dataset
<p><strong> Update:</strong> If you have downloaded the data before 4/14/2020, please re-download the data. The labels in the new dataset are now one-hot encoded </p>
<ol>
<li>Load the data into a variable say 'data' using np.load()</li>
<li>data['X_train'] represents X_train with each image's dimension being (32, 32, 3)</li>
<li>data['y_train'] represents each image's label.</li>
<li>data['X_test'] represents X_test with each image's dimension being (32, 32, 3)</li>
</ol>
</li>

<li><strong>sample_onehot.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format; this file only reflects the format of submissions. Your submissions will likely include probabilities instead of binary values in each row.</li>

<li> *** Please refer to <a href= ""https://stackoverflow.com/questions/35133317/numpy-save-some-arrays-at-once"">https://stackoverflow.com/questions/35133317/numpy-save-some-arrays-at-once </a>  to load using np.load() ***</li>
</ul>",True,True
