2770,koreangenderbiasdetection,"# Description

On the Internet, freedom of speech through anonymity has been considered advantageous in that many people can express their opinions transparently. However, at the same time, there are also negative impacts, such as severely aggressive or insulting threats committed to a specific individual or group.

Recently, in Korean society, it has been publicized that a series of issues were presumed to be caused by target-specific toxic comments. More specifically, after some celebrities appealed mental damage continuously and openly, there were cases leading to a tragic decision. After, hate speech in on-line space has emerged as a critical social issue, a methodology that delicately prevents problematic text has not been immediately carried out by on-line service providers, and the comment system of Entertainment news is currently closed on main news platforms.

Without a doubt, various attempts have been proposed to handle this problem, and the term existence-based detection system is a representative method. The dictionary of publicly available profanity terms for The Korean language was also distributed and some systems are also known to train and deploy the system based on these factors. Notwithstanding the method of identifying hate speech by lexical matching is straightforward, there are limitations. Such terms may be meta-language that mentions only specific content, and profanity terms can appear in a modified format. Even without such terms, bias and sexual offense implicated in the sentence may be aggressive to target figures or other readers.

In this regard, we present the first Korean corpus annotated with gender bias. We collected comments from the Korean entertainment news platform that incorporates a wide range of users in Korea.

We believe that our initial efforts in constructing Korean gender bias dataset that also regards 
 insulting, along with the detection system, will be supportive for both social good and sociolinguistic studies.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
A model is to predict whether comments contain gender bias or not.

## Metric
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is F1-Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>

## Submission File Format
You should submit a CSV file with 975 entries plus a header row. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
comments,label
comment_1,True
comment_2,False
comment_3,False
...
```","# Dataset Description

## Overview
The task is to identify gender bias in the comments of the Korean online entertainment news platform.  Specifically, use `train.gender_bias.csv` and `dev.gender_bias.csv` which are provided `label` for each comment. Your job is to train a model and predict `label` of comments in `test.gender_bias.no_label.csv`. 

We also include `{train, dev, test}.news_title.txt`, to inform where the comments are from.

## Files
- `train.gender_bias.csv`: the training set
- `dev.gender_bias.csv`: the validation set
- `test.gender_bias.no_label.csv`: the test set (w/o label)
- `train.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the training set
- `dev.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the validation set
- `test.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the test set
- `unlabeled_comments.txt`: comments without the label
- `unlabeled_comments.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments without the label

## Fields

- `comments` (`str`) : news comments 
- `label` (`bool`) : gender bias label
  - `True`, `False`

---
`NOTE`: The dataset is under [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/deed.en)",True,True
2771,koreanbiasdetection,"# Description

Online anonymity provides freedom of speech to many people and lets them speak their opinions in public. However, anonymous speech also has a negative impact on society and individuals. 

With anonymity safeguards, individuals easily express hatred against others based on their superficial characteristics such as gender, sexual orientation, and age. Sometimes the hostility leaks to the well-known people who are considered to be the representatives of targeted attributes. 

Recently, Korea had suffered a series of tragic incidents of two young celebrities that are presumed to be caused by toxic comments. Since the incidents, two major web portals in Korea decided to close the comment system in their entertainment news aggregating service. Even though the toxic comments are now avoidable in those platforms, the fundamental problem has not been solved yet.

To cope with the social issue, we propose the first Korean corpus annotated for toxic speech detection. Specifically, our dataset consists of 9.4K comments from Korean online entertainment news articles. Each comment is annotated on two aspects, the existence of social bias and hate speech, given that hate speech is closely related to bias.

We believe that our initial efforts in constructing Korean bias dataset will be supportive for both social good and sociolinguistic studies.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
A model is to predict whether comments contain gender bias, other biases or no bias.

## Metric
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/multi-class-metrics-made-simple-part-ii-the-f1-score-ebe8b2c2ca1""> Macro F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>

## Submission File Format
You should submit a CSV file with 975 entries plus a header row. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
comments,label
comment_1,gender
comment_2,none
comment_3,others
...
```","# Dataset Description

## Overview
The task is to identify biases in the Korean entertainment news comments.  Specifically, use `train.bias.ternary.csv` and `dev.bias.ternary.csv` which are provided `label` for each comment. Your job is to train a model and predict `label` of comments in `test.bias.no_label.csv`. 

We also include `{train, dev, test}.news_title.txt`, to inform where the comments are from.

## Files
- `train.bias.ternary.csv`: the training set
- `dev.bias.ternary.csv`: the validation set
- `test.bias.no_label.csv`: the test set (w/o label)
- `train.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the training set
- `dev.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the validation set
- `test.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments in the test set
- `unlabeled_comments.txt`: comments without the label
- `unlabeled_comments.news_title.txt`: article titles of comments without the label

## Fields

- `comments` (`str`) : news comments 
- `label` (`str`) : bias label
  - `gender`, `others`, `none`

---
`NOTE`: The dataset is under [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/deed.en)",True,True
2772,gene-expression-prediction-cs1850-final,"# Description

<p>
<h2> How do histones relate to gene expression?</h2>
<p>In humans and most eukaryotes, nuclear DNA is often kept repressed by default, bound in the structure of a supercoil. This is a coiled coil of DNA that is wrapped around histones at regular intervals of around 146 base pairs. Each of the histone ""beads"" on the DNA ""string"" is referred to as a nucleosome. </p>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4313577%2Ff3210b6c172ec8462a4b119ffb91308c%2FDNA%20to%20chromosome.jpg?generation=1582061578332733&alt=media)


<p>For genes to be expressed, the DNA to be transcribed into RNA by RNA polymerase. Since most DNA is in the compressed supercoiled state, the promoter sequence is almost completely inaccessible to the polymerase and the transcription factors. However, among the regions where topoisomerases have untwisted the DNA, the properties of the nucleosome determine whether a gene will be expressed.</p>

<h2> Can histone marks predict gene expression?</h2>
<p>Certain modifications to the histone proteins that compose the nucleosome, such as methylation, acetylation, or lactylation of specific places on the protein can change how tightly bound the DNA is to the histone core. These particular modifications are part of the epigenome, which is used by multicellular organisms to allow for cell differentiation. The epigenome is copied to the newly synthesized DNA strands during cell replication, ensuring that descendants of the differentiated cell will be the same type.</p>
<p>Since epigenetic markers like histone modifications affect the accessibility of DNA, in many cases they can serve the determining factor for how much will certain genes be expressed. This competition involves using the frequency and positions of histones in the region of gene promoter sequences in order to predict expression values </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation of the results will be done by Mean Squared Error (MSE) between the predicted expression values and the results. 25% of the submission will be used for the public score, with the other 75% included for the final evaluation.
----------------------
# Prizes

<h5>One</h5>
<h4>single</h4>
<h4>drop</h3>
<h2>of</h2>
<h1>clout</h1>","# Dataset Description

<h2> Dataset Details</h2>
<p>The competition involves using histone mark data to predict gene expression values in a total of 56 cell types across 17447 genes. The data is split into a training set and evaluation set. The training set contains the histone marks and expression values for 50 cell types and 16000 genes. The evaluation set contains **only the histone marks** for the 6 held-out cell types across all genes and the marks for 1447 held-out genes for the 50 cell types.</p>
<p>The histone marks in the data are H3K27me3, H3K36me3, H3K4me1, H3K4me3, H3K9me3. These 5 marks are quantified counting the number of occurrences in bins of length 100 base pairs (bp) selected from regions (+/- 5000 bp) flanking the transcription start site (TSS) of each gene. There are in total 100 contiguous bins for each gene, and 5 read counts of the histone marks per bin. </p>
<p> Gene expression values are quantified via RNA sequencing to identify the amount of gene transcript present. RNAseq provides read counts that can be used to create an expression matrix across all genes in the cell. Since total gene expression in different cell lines are not equivalent, expression values are measured as the difference from a ""reference"" expression matrix produced from the RNA of 10 cell lines combined. </p>

<h2> More Dataset Details</h2>
<p> The data is in a numpy object (.npz) and can be loaded as shown in the provided script. Each cell type has an array of shape (#genes, 100, 7). Thus, each gene corresponds to a 100 by 7 matrix, where each of the 100 rows corresponds to a bin, and the columns are [GeneID, H3K27me3, H3K36me3, H3K4me1, H3K4me3, H3K9me3, Expression Value]. Column 0 gene ID should not be used in training the regression model. </p>
<p>The histone counts have been normalized using ln(1 + counts) in order to account for large deviations in frequency. These values will be >=0. </p>
<p>The expression values were obtained by first calculating the difference from the reference expression matrix ('Cell type-X expression' - 'Ref expression'). If this difference was negative, it was further normalized by -ln(1 - 'expression difference'). If it was positive, it was normalized by ln(1 + 'expression difference'). The final expression values are real numbers that can be both negative or positive. In this way, the prediction task is such that the network learns to identify how the unique cell-line's histone marks result in deviations from a reference expression value. This accounts for the natural variation in the expression amounts between different genes. <\p>

<h2><strong> Final Dataset Details</strong></h2>
seq_data.csv contains the sequence data that corresponds to the region +/- 5000 bp from the transcription start site (TSS), the same sequence window used for the histone marker data. See if you can integrate this information into your model to significantly improve your expression value prediction!

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.npz</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>eval.npz</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>info.py</strong> - example data loading, rearranging into inputs/labels, and submission csv</li>
<li><strong>seq_data.csv</strong> - No header. First column gene id, second column sequence data</li>",True,True
