2803,computervisioncompetitionsc2020,"# Description

<h1> Introduction </h1>
Welcome to the competition. This competition is for SC Students in Computer Vision course at FCIS. The goal of the competition is to build deep learning models that can recognize the action of the video.
<p>
Action recognition task involves the identification of different actions from video clips (a
sequence of 2D frames) where the action may or may not be performed throughout the
entire duration of the video. This seems like a natural extension of image classification
tasks to multiple frames and then aggregating the predictions from each frame.
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>You have to submit a file in CSV format named as 'submit.csv'.</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the overall accuracy </p><p> 

</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong> The file contains 2 column, the first column is Video, which is the name of the test video. The second column is label, which is your predicted label for the video </strong> </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>video,label<br>test_image (1).mpg,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h1> Dataset description </h1>
<p>
UCF is an action recognition data set of realistic action videos, collected from YouTube. The dataset contains 5 categories (Basketball, Diving, Jumping, Tennis, and Walking). The training set contains 5 folders, around 100 video for each vategory. The testing set contains 126 vidoos. The goal is to build a model than can recognize the cateogry of each video in the testing set.
<h2>Files description</h2>
<ul>
<li> train.rar</strong> - https://drive.google.com/file/d/1fu93ErsAOzDf9kK2ws4I2ILcMYr2SZbF/view?usp=sharing</li>
<li><strong>test.rar</strong> - https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tfcRgZrMp8cVJeH-YY2YO0RJEe9dKMVy/view?usp=sharing</li>
<li><strong>submit.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>names.csv</strong> this file contains the IDs of the five categories</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Video</strong> - the name of video </li>
<li><strong>Label</strong> - the predicted label</li>
</ul>",True,True
2804,retencion-en-telefonia-movil,"# Description

<p>Las comunicaciones por tel¨¦fono celular siempre han sido objeto de una fiera competencia, en la que hay, usualmente, un n¨²mero reducido de competidores (operadores) en el mercado que oscila entre cuatro y seis. Los operadores deben hacer fuertes inversiones de entrada para poder operar y prestar un servicio adecuado. El mercado de telefon¨ªa celular est¨¢ usualmente saturado (poca posibilidad de ampliaci¨®n del mercado), y por tanto, el conjunto de consumidores disponibles es reducido, por lo que los operadores usualmente cambian su estrategia de la adquisici¨®n de clientes a la retenci¨®n porque en el mundo se calcula que el costo de adquirir un nuevo cliente es cinco veces superior al de la retenci¨®n, sin contar con el posible CLTV (customer lifetimevalue) del cliente, esto es, el retorno a largo plazo que se genera por un cliente que continua consumiendo los servicios de un operador. Otra cifra clave es que la tasa de deserci¨®n (churn) suele rondar el 2% mensual promedio para un operador de telecomunicaciones. Desde el punto de vista de la inteligencia de negocios, bajo la perspectiva de CRM hay dos esfuerzos fundamentales de Anal¨ªtica: a) predecir la deserci¨®n (churn) de manera adecuada y b) evaluar la manera m¨¢s costo/efectiva de lo que un operador puede hacer para la retenci¨®n (incluyendo no hacer nada).
<p>Un operador de telecomunicaciones les ha contratado para resolver las dos inquietudes anal¨ªticas presentadas anteriormente. Para la primera, desea que, a partir de una base de datos de entrenamiento que es una muestra aleatoria simple de la base de datos total de la compa?¨ªa que contiene el resultado de retenci¨®n en los ¨²ltimos seis meses, se realice la mejor predicci¨®n posible (basada en el ¨¢rea bajo la curva COR) para una base de datos de prueba en un concurso de Kaggle. Para la segunda parte, adicional a un reporte con metodolog¨ªa CRISP-DM (de m¨¢ximo 18 p¨¢ginas) de los modelos realizados, desea que se le presente una estrategia (incluyendo puntos de corte, costos y posibles ganancias) para atacar la retenci¨®n..</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n es el ¨¢rea bajo la curva caracter¨ªstica de operaci¨®n (AUC bajjo la curva COR) en la base de datos de test<p>
<p>Los sometimientos deben ser archivos separados por comas, con un encabezado que contenga las variables ""id"" y ""resultado""</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>La base de datos de entrenamiento tiene una muestra aleatoria simple de los clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.018 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.018.  Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago). </p>

<p>La base de datos de prueba contiene una muestra no aleatoria adicional de clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.018 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.018.  Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago).  La variable de posible churn (resultado) no se incluye, pues es la variable a predecir</p>
<h2>Archivos</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>traintelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de entrenamiento</li>
<li><strong>testelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de prueba</li>
<h2>Campos de la base de datos</h2>
<ul>

<li><strong>id</strong> - id an¨®mino que identifica al cliente</li>
<li><strong> Fecha de nacimiento </strong> - Fecha de nacimiento del cliente, dd/mm/yyyy</li>
<li><strong>tipo cliente</strong> - Tipo de cliente: 1 - Hombre, 2-Mujer, 3-Cliente empresarial</li>
<li><strong>Factura online</strong> - Binario que indica si el cliente recibe ¨²nicamente su factura online</li>
<li><strong>Antiguedad equipo</strong> Meses de antiguedad del equipo</li>
<li><strong>Plan de datos</strong> - Binario. 1=el cliente tiene plan premium de datos.</li>
<li><strong>Facturaci¨®n</strong> - Total facturaci¨®n de los ¨²ltimos seis meses (suma) </li>
<li><strong>Mora</strong> D¨ªas de mora acumulados</li>
<li><strong>Fecha inicio contrato </strong> - Fecha de inicio de su primer contrato de plan pospago en la compa?¨ªa</li>
<li><strong>minutos</strong> - total minutos consumidos en los ¨²ltimos seis meses</li>
<li><strong>resultado</strong> - 0=No hubo deserci¨®n; 1=Hubo deserci¨®n (churn) </li>",True,True
2805,enigma-day-1,"# Description

<p> 

It¡¯s the year 1939, and it¡¯s been less than a year since the infamous Munich Agreement, where British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain agreed to Hitler¡¯s annexation of the Sudetenland, portions of Czechoslovakia with ethnic-German majorities. 
 You being the head of the Polish Army ( also called Wojsko Polskie in Polish) have received information that Adolf Hitler is planning on attacking Poland on September 1st. You also have information about the number of soldiers, kinds of ammunition , etc. being used to attack and conquer each province. You know that Nazi Germany is a strong country and thus devise a plan as to how you would position your armed forces to save your people and land, in case of an attack . 
</p>

<p>
Given, data that contains information on the same and you need to figure out the accurate plan so as to have minimum damage to your country and have major provinces retained under the Polish Government.

Make your own predictive model. Train it on the train dataset and record your reading of the test dataset and upload it.
</p>

<p>
Please make yourselves familiar with the rules of the competition and fill out the form given below.
</p>

<p>
https://forms.gle/XNSrAsPHCt5wZmU49

</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> You will be evaluated based on the number of correct submissions made by you (Accuracy rate) on your test data. Do not submit your notebook as it is available to all other participants. Try to analyse the data well to get a better understanding of the data given.
</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set(labeled)</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set(unlabeled)</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
2806,enigma-day-3,"# Description

On July 16, 1945 the first atomic bomb was exploded in the New Mexico desert. The explosion was massive and the equivalent to 18,000 tons of TNT. This bomb would change the world and could cause mass destruction and death. By this time, Germany had already surrendered and World War II in Europe was over. Japan was defeated as well, but would not surrender. The US was contemplating an invasion of Japan. Army leaders figured that anywhere from 500,000 to 1 million US and Allied soldiers would die in an invasion. You being President Truman decide to use the atom bombs in an effort to bring about Japan¡¯s surrender in the Second World War. You know that Japan would not surrender easily thus devise a plan for the attack.

On August 6, 1945 an atomic bomb named Little Boy is on board Enola Gay which was piloted by Colonel Paul Tibbetts. Months of surveillance and reconnaissance operations, have led to the creation of a dataset, which is used to predict which city should be the target, given the constraints. Three cities are chosen as potential targets-1. Yokohama, 2. Hiroshima, 3. Kyoto.

You are provided with the dataset having all features. Your task is to predict which city should be targeted. The fate of the world is in your hands. Make your choices carefully. 

-train.csv is the training set
-test.csv is the test set to submit your predictions
-sample_submission.csv provides the format for submission.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set(labeled)</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set(unlabeled)</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
