2874,booknew,"# Description

A German book retailer sent out offers to customers. You job is to build predictor variables and a machine learning model to predict how much each customer who received the contact point will spend. The outcome variable is logtarg. The test set is indicated by is.na(logtarg). The objective is to predict logtarg with minimum RMSE on the test set.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.?For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9 10<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">10,10 9<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

You may submit up to two entries a day until 11:59pm on June 8. The winner will be the team with the lowest MSE on the private leader board test set.","# Dataset Description

<P>There are two tables. The target.csv file has one record for every
customer in the training and test sets. The id variable uniquely 
identifies a customer and logtarg is the dependent variable. It is 
set to NA for the test cases. There should be 19,259 records plus a 
header line giving the variale names.
<P>The orders.csv file has one record for every item ordered by a 
customer in the test or training set prior to the contact point. 
There are 775012 records plus a header line. The variables are as 
follows:
<ul>
<LI>id: customer id matching the one in target.csv
<LI>ordnum: uniquely identifies an order
<LI>t: time in days since the order (1 is would be yesterday and 365 
would be one year ago).
<LI>category: category identifier for the item:
1=fiction; 3=classics; 5=cartoons; 6=legends; 7=philosophy;
8=religion; 9=psychology; 10=linguistics; 12=art; 14=music;
17=art reprints;
19=history; 20=contemporary history; 21=economy; 22=politics; 23=science;
26=computer science; 27=traffic, railroads; 30=maps; 31=travel guides;
35=health; 36=cooking; 37=learning; 38=games and riddles; 39=sports;
40=hobby; 41=nature/animals/plants; 44=encyclopedia; 50=videos, DVDs;
99=non books
<LI>qty: Number of items ordered
<LI>price: price per item (a person who orders qty=2 copies of a book 
for 10 Euros would have a total amount of 2*10=20 Euros)
<LI>brandid: the company has three separate brands selling different 
kinds of books. This offer is from brand 1.
</UL>

<P> Here is example code to get your started. Upload testanswer.csv.
<pre>
library(tidyverse)
setwd(""/Users/ecm/teach/data/booknew"")
orders = read.csv(""orders.csv"")
target = read.csv(""target.csv"")

rfm = orders %>%
  group_by(id) %>%
  summarise(tof=max(t), r = min(t), fitem=n(), ford=n_distinct(ordnum), m=sum(price*qty))
head(rfm)
summary(rfm)

all = target %>%
  left_join(rfm, by=""id"")
train=!is.na(all$logtarg)
fit = lm(logtarg ~ r + fitem + m, data=all, subset=train)
yhat = predict(fit, all[!train,])
ans = data.frame(id=all$id[!train], logtarg=yhat)
write.csv(ans, ""testanswer.csv"", row.names=F)
</pre>",True,True
2875,mmm-course-hackathon-2020,"# Description

<p><b>Flatland</b> is a country suffering from rebel islands existin in its teritory, and frequently shoots rockets at its inhabited areas.</p>
<p>There are many radar systems scattered throughout Flatland which detect the rocket trajectories when they are in mid air.
You need to propose a model that given a rocket trajectory, predict the type of rocket: 
<ul>
<li>type 0.</li>
<li>type 1.</li>
<li>type 2.</li>
</ul>
The purpose is to assess the risk posed by a missile (with its type) as soon as possible and to make appropriate assessments.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are now evaluated on the mean column-wise ROC AUC. In other words, the score is the average of the individual AUCs of each predicted column.</p>

<p>Submission File
For each traj_ind in the test set, you must predict a probability for each of the three possible types of missiles (0, 1, 2). The columns must be in that same order. The file should contain a header and the same format as <b>sample_submission.csv</b>.</p>
----------------------
# Timeline

10.5 at 14:00 - 17.5 at 23:55
----------------------
# Prizes

The big prize: Ever Lasting Fame (and maybe 5 points to the final grade)","# Dataset Description

<h3>Data description</h3>
<p>You are provided with a large number of labeled rocket trajectories. Every trajectory is represented as a set of coordinates:
<ul>
<li><b>x</b> coordinate of the rocket.</li>
<li><b>y</b> coordinate of the rocket.</li>
<li><b>z</b> coordinate of the rocket.</li>
<li><b>time_stamp</b> coordinate of the rocket.</li>
</ul>
All the coordinates of the same trajectory have the same <b>traj_ind</b>.
The goal is to predict the rocket type (0, 1, 2) for every <b>traj_ind</b>.</p>

<h3>File description</h3>
<p><ul>
<li>train.csv - the training set, contains rocket trajectories with their labels.</li>
<li>test.csv - the test set, contains rocket trajectories without labels.</li>
<li>sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format.</li>
</ul></p>",True,True
2876,mlp-en-cifar-100,"# Description

<p>Esta competencia consiste en desarrollar una red convolucional para clasificaci¨®n de im¨¢genes. Se utilizar¨¢ el dataset CIFAR-100, que consiste en im¨¢genes fotogr¨¢ficas a color de 32x32 p¨ªxeles, clasificadas en 100 clases distintas. El dataset tiene un set de train de 50.000 im¨¢genes (500 de cada clase) y un set de test de 10.000 im¨¢genes (100 de cada clase)</p>
<p> El objetivo es explorar distintas redes y configuraciones de hiperpar¨¢metros, utilizando el set de train para entrenar los modelos. Para aplicar t¨¦cnicas de validaci¨®n cruzada u otros muestreos de validaci¨®n, deber¨¢ utilizar el set de train. </p>

<p> Se le mostrar¨¢n las im¨¢genes de testeo pero no contar¨¢ con las etiquetas de las clases correspondientes.  </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> Deber¨¢ generar una notebook que corra autom¨¢ticamente en un kernel de Kaggle, que tome como input los archivos de Data de la competencia y que entregue como output un csv an¨¢logo a cifar100_train.csv pero con las predicciones que su modelo obtenga para cada imagen de test. </p>
<p> El submission file debe ser un csv con dos columnas: Image y Label. La columna image debe tener el path relativo a cada archivo del set de test y la columna Label debe tener el nombre de la clase inferida por el modelo.</p>
<p> La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n que se utilizar¨¢ es Categorization Accuracy: el valor de accuracy (proporci¨®n de predicciones correctas sobre totales) promediado a lo largo de todas las clases. </p>","# Dataset Description

<p>En la carpeta 'imgs' hay dos directorios, 'train' y 'test' que contienen las im¨¢genes de cada set respectivamente, en formato PNG. </p>
<p> En el archivo cifar100_train.csv encontrar¨¢n una tabla de dos columnas: la columna 'Image' tiene el path relativo al archivo de imagen, y la columna 'Label' tiene la categor¨ªa a la que pertenece la imagen.</p>",True,True
