2887,house-rent-in-lagos,"# Description

<p>So you have scraped the data. However after you built your first model, you realise the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of the model is quite high between 300k and 500k.

Your boss tells you this is totally unacceptable and the model is useless if it has an error of > 100k.
Based on his experience he tells you that the property details provided in the website are all self reported details provided Agents. Typically self reported figures are subject to errors and missing information.

Armed with this info, your boss wants you to reduce the RMSE of the model to <100k.

Ensure you apply what you've learned in the resources shared.
Cheers.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

# **Goal**

Your boss tells you this is totally unacceptable and the model is useless if it has an error of > 100k.
Based on his experience he tells you that the property details provided in the website are all self reported details provided Agents. Typically self reported figures are subject to errors and missing information.

Armed with this info, your boss wants you to reduce the RMSE of the model to <100k.

# **Metric**

Submissions are evaluated on Root Mean Square Error comparing the predicted price value and the observed price value.

# **Other metrics**

Your kaggle notebook will also be considered for this competition especially for top participants. The RMSE between your test and train in your notebook will be compared to the RMSE on the leaderboard. If there is a huge discrepancy, you will be disqualified from the competition.

# **Submission File Format**

You should submit a csv file with exactly 104 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns other than the id column and price column.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

- id
- price","# Dataset Description

This data is about house prices across specific locations in Lagos. The data contains house features and prices.

This is not a regular competition. In this competition, you are given prices for both test and train. Your goal is to reduce the RMSE on the test data based on the extra information your boss has provided Feel free to take as many logical steps to arrive at a reduced RMSE The price provided for both test and train will give you insights into possible errors in reported figures, missing features etc. Feel free to make logical adjustments to the data In this competition, we will act as your boss and give hints (domain expertise) from time to time. You are expected to make submissions of the prediction on your test data to check whether your RMSE has reduced. At the end of the competition, top participants are expected to submit a Kaggle notebook through Kaggle. Share the private notebook with ¡°The Technidus¡± on Kaggle.

You are not allowed to submit the test data (as it was provided). Only a predicted version of the test data must be submitted on the leaderboard. The test data as it was provided will give an unrealistically low RMSE of <20k and you will be disqualified from the competition.",True,True
2888,limonackathon-2020-gone-wild,"# Description

<p>As you might know, the threat of rockets being shot at our cities and citizens is a major problem IDF deals with. In this task you will analyse this threat and help solving a key question regarding it: the problem of classifying the type of rocket launched at us.</p>
<p>In order to do so we used are tight connections with the country of Talpiland. Sadly, this fractured country has to deal with isles of rebels within its boundaries. Those rebels constantly launch missiles of different types on Talpiland's poor citizens. They agreed to share with us their large datasets regarding those missiles, so we could try solving the question at hand.<p>
<p>You are given the file train.csv. It contains information about the tracks of rockets launched at Talpiland. Talpiland used its many radars to record it. Each record has the followinng properties:</p>
1. traj_ind - an id of a specific rocket's trajectory
2. time_stamp - the time stamp of a point in the trajectory, as recorded in the radar.
3. x,y,z - coordinates of the point in the trajectory, as recorded in the radar.
4. label - the type of the rocket.

<p>Your submission file should look like the example given to you.<p>

<h2>Thrilling update!</h2>
<p>Your grade will be calculated as follow: For each class, the accuracy of your predictions will be calculated, with different weights for each trajectory: as you have heard, it is favorable that you will be able to predict the rocket type as soon as possible. Hence, for right prediction for shorter paths you will get better score. Trajectories with less then 1-20 points will have a score of 5. Trajectories with 20-40 points will have a score of 4, and so on.
Your grade will be the mean of these accuracies.<p>

<p>\[ MeanAcc^*= \frac{1}{\#classes}\sum_{i \in classes} {\sum_{j}{weight_j\cdot 1_{class(j)==pred(j)}}}\]</p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>This challenge is heavily based on a hackathon hosted by Ofek unit.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Your grade will be calculated as follow: For each class, the accuracy of your predictions will be calculated, with different weights for each trajectory: as you have heard, it is favorable that you will be able to predict the rocket type as soon as possible. Hence, for right prediction for shorter paths you will get better score. Trajectories with less then 1-20 points will have a score of 5. Trajectories with 20-40 points will have a score of 4, and so on.
Your grade will be the mean of these accuracies.<p>

<p>\[ MeanAcc^*= \frac{1}{\#classes}\sum_{i \in classes} {\sum_{j}{weight_j\cdot 1_{class(j)==pred(j)}}}\]</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>train_drop.csv</strong> - training set with missing points from trajectories</li>
<li><strong>test_drop.csv</strong> - test set with missing points from trajectories</li>
<li><strong>baseline_0.csv</strong> - baseline</li>",True,True
2889,classifying-the-fake-news,"# Description

<p>Fake news is a common phenomena on internet now a days. It affects every facet of our lives. It influences us in different ways. For example, fake news about the cure of COVID-19 can spread misinformation. To tackle this challenge, you are provided with a dataset to train your model and make predictions for future news if the news is real or fake.

<h3>Acknowledgements</h3>
<p>Ahmed H, Traore I, Saad S. (2017) ¡°Detection of Online Fake News Using N-Gram Analysis and Machine Learning Techniques. In: Traore I., Woungang I., Awad A. (eds) Intelligent, Secure, and Dependable Systems in Distributed and Cloud Environments. ISDDC 2017. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol 10618. Springer, Cham (pp. 127-138).
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Predicted<br />1,1<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">8,8<br />9,9<br /></span>","# Dataset Description

<p>There are two files in the dataset. training.csv will be used to train your model and test will be used to test the model. The results generated by test will be uploaded for evaluation.</p>",True,True
2890,aibiz-2020-spring-task-3-fashion-mnist,"# Description

<p>This is a classical computer vision task.</p>

<p>To complete the challenge, you need to achieve 0.83 in the public testing set. </p>

<p> More instructions will be given in the google document. </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation by accuracy

<h2> Submission Format</h2>

csv file, for example:
---- file start ----
imageID,label
0,1
1,2
2,4
---- file end ----","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>fashion-mnist_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>fashion-mnist_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>fashion-mnist_test_sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>



<h2>About the data set Fashion-MNIST</h2>

<p>Fashion-MNIST consists of a training set of 60,000 examples and a test set of 10,000 examples. Each example is a 28x28 grayscale image, associated with a label from 10 classes. </p>

<p>Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255. The training and test data sets have 785 columns. The first column consists of the class labels (see above), and represents the article of clothing. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.
</p>
<ul>
<li>To locate a pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27. The pixel is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix. </li>
<li>For example, pixel31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Labels</h2>
<p>Each training and test example is assigned to one of the following labels:</p>
<ul>
<li>0 T-shirt/top<\li>
<li>1 Trouser<\li>
<li>2 Pullover<\li>
<li>3 Dress<\li>
<li>4 Coat<\li>
<li>5 Sandal<\li>
<li>6 Shirt<\li>
<li>7 Sneaker<\li>
<li>8 Bag<\li>
<li>9 Ankle boot<\li>
</ul>


<p>In short</p>
<ul>
<li>Each row is a separate image <\li>
<li>Column 1 is the class label.<\li> 
<li>Remaining columns are pixel numbers (784 total). <\li>
<li>Each value is the darkness of the pixel (1 to 255)<\li>
</ul>


<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>label</strong> - an outcome of the image</li>
<li><strong>pixel1</strong> - the 1st pixel of the image</li>
</ul>",True,True
