2922,adversarial-attacks-against-spam-detectors,"# Evaluation

A spamming attack will be evaluated based on how much it can withstand our spam detectors to promote the revenue of the designated target products (yes, we know what products are manipulated, but the evaluation codes will not access that knowledge). We call this metric Practical Effect (PE).

More specifically, the pipeline looks like this:
1. Download the files parameters.csv, review_graph.txt, and target_spammer.pickle to create attacks using your algorithms.
2. An attack is in the form of reviews with time and ratings. There is no review content involved.
3. We add the reviews in each of your attacks to the existing review data and run our detectors.
4. We compute how much the remaining spams can promote the target products.
5. The final performance is averaged over the multiple attacks.

See the Notebooks for a full pipeline from data loading to attack generation and evaluation.

When your algorithm finishes the attacks specified in the file parameters.csv, you should generated 48 'submission_k.txt' files, where k=0,...,47. The format of the submission files are specified in the Notebooks. Zip and submit the submission files into a single file name <team_name>.zip and submit the zip file to the following Google Form:
[https://forms.gle/tF91i5FDGTaQmVEU9](https://forms.gle/tF91i5FDGTaQmVEU9)

Each team will be identified by a unique email address. You need to input the email address along with each submission. All submissions from a team will be linked to that email address. We will contact the winners using these addresses.

We will update the evaluation and the Leaderboard on the workshop website daily:
[https://truthdiscoverykdd2020.github.io](https://truthdiscoverykdd2020.github.io)
----------------------
# Description

##Background
Review spams are prevalent on review platforms such as Amazon and Yelp. They are manipulating items (e.g., books on Amazon and restaurants on Yelp) evaluation and affecting customer decision making. It is of critical importance to remove review spams and make users of the review platforms aware of such threats.

<p align=""center"">
<img align=""center"" height=""100"" width=""500"" src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5118738%2F60469fc13b6bf8928aff33f8fbec08aa%2Ffigures.png?generation=1590372808142530&alt=media"">
</p>

However, these fake reviews are rather challenging to detect due to the following reasons:
1) there is very little labeled data to train accurate machine learning detectors; 2) humans have great difficulty in telling fake reviews from genuine ones; 3) the number of fake reviews are paramount and are overwhelming human inspection bandwidth. Current machine learning and data mining approach to review spam detection is limited by the above factors and is not evaluated using more practical metrics in the business sense. This contest calls for attention to the above limitation of current methods and takes a stab to the above problems.

The contestants will act as spammers to create review spams to evade a grey-box spam detector. Not every review created by the contestants qualifies as spam, though. It is those reviews that can practically manipulate the rating of the target businesses that should be tamed and thus qualify as spams.

##Threat model
- The spammers are interested in promoting a given set of target products.
- The spammers are given a set of spamming accounts on the platform for posting a fixed number of spams towards <it>any</it> products on the platform.
- The spams must be posted in a given period.
- The spammers can choose the ratings of the spams from {1,2,3,4,5}
- The spammers will NOT create the review contents.
- The spammers can NOT create new spamming accounts to post spams.
- No new product can be added to the current review platform.
- The spammers know what detection models are used but don't know the implementation and parameters.

##Detection assumptions
- The detector will use only the rating and post time of the reviews, and the connections between accounts and products for detection. No contents, profiles, and external information of the accounts and products will be used for detection.
- The detector does not know the spammer accounts and the target products.
- The detector detects spams after the spammers post their spams to the existing reviews.
- The given review data are considered non-spams.

##What you will start with
We prepare a startup package for you to get your solution up and running quickly.
In the Notebook tab, you will find a notebook containing Python functions that can:
- Read the input review data as bipartite graph representation.
<p align=""center"">
  <img width=""230"" height=""150"" src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5118738%2F6f9d60f0d3643321ac86c258a26189d3%2Fbipartite.png?generation=1590373739112751&alt=media"">
</p>
- Read attack settings, including selected spamming accounts, target items, attacking time period, attack size, and top-k detection parameter. There will be multiple attacks that need to be created and the evaluation metric will be averaged over these attacks.
- Manipulate the graph by adding or removing reviews. These functionalities are useful for executing and evaluating spam attacks.
- Evaluation codes that compute the metric. As we don't use typical metrics, such as accuracy, AUC, F1 score, we feel necessary to make our evaluation method transparent.
- A simple randomized spamming attack to show you the submission format of an attack.
- A simple detector based on detection features that are used in prior spam detection methods. You can run this detector against your attack and see how much you can evade the detector and achieve high scores!

##Files to download
- parameters.csv: the file contains multiple lines with a header. Each line is an attack scenario. The meaning of the parameters are specified in the header.
- review_graph.txt: this file contains the input clean review graph. See the helper notebook for its format.
- target_spammers.pickle: this file specified the target products and spammer accounts. The detector will not seek spams out of the entire dataset, which is rather large compared to available human inspection efforts. Therefore, it specifies within what range the detector is operating. See the function ""evaluation"" in the Notebooks for more details.

##How to win
The challenge is a grey-box adversarial attack. Your job is to act like a spammer and probe the following main-stream published detectors:

- FRAUDAR: Bounding Graph Fraud in the Face of Camouflage. KDD 2016.
- GANG: Detecting Fraudulent Users in Online Social Networks via Guilt-by-Association on Directed Graphs. ICDM 2017.
- Collective Opinion Spam Detection: Bridging Review Networks and Metadata. KDD 2015
- Suspiciousness prior used in the above paper without MRF and BP.
- Spotting Suspicious Link Behavior with fBox: An Adversarial Perspective. ICDM 2014

We believe this is easier for the contestants compared with a black-box attack challenge, and yet interesting enough that you need to use the detection feedback to guess what the detectors are, simulating a real-world spam attack.",# Dataset Description,True,True
2923,pchs-data-science-covid-19-competition,"# Description

<h2>Who is this competition for?</h2>
<p>The COVID-19 pandemic has had a historic disruption of world events. Everyone is interested in how extensively the virus has spread to different locations. We would like competitors to try to use the data available to us to predict the number of cases and deaths of unknown areas. Anyone can attempt this competition, although knowledge about machine learning and programming is recommended.</p>
<h2>Competition Description</h2>
<p>The dataset we provide has information such as poverty rates, unemployment rates, etc. These factors have been known to correlate to the magnitude of cases and deaths.</p>
<p>Your goal is to correctly predict the number of cases and deaths for the various locations.</p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank the  <a href=""https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19""> CSSE at Johns Hopkins University</a> for providing resources that participants can use.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr />
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>","# Dataset Description


<p>We recommend that you use the dataset found at the Johns-Hopkin's Center for Systems Science and Engineering.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul><a href=""https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19""> CSSE at Johns Hopkins University</a>
<li><strong>Train.csv</strong> - Regional data that might help with prediction</li>
<li><strong>Test.csv</strong> - Data for which you will be predicting the confirmed cases and deaths</li>
<li><strong>Sample.csv</strong> - An example of the format for submission</li>",True,True
2924,sysu-dmml,"# Description

<p>Please use ""train"" data set to train your model. The objective is to predict the class the water quality.</p>
<p>Each student should complete this assignment independently. All algorithms, tools and packages you obtained from Internet can be used.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is F1-score. </p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files in CSV should contain two columns: Id and Category. The Id should be as same as the Id in Test data set, and the Category should be your predictions. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Category<br />200,1<br />201,0 <br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">201,0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>It is very important to control the quality of water for maintaining the ecosystem in the water system. Collect the water sample image, and analyze the data of these images can help us predict the quality of water.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>class</strong> -  the class of water quality, 1: Light green (clear or muddy water),2: Grayish blue, 3: yellowish brown, 4: dark brown (turmeric, dark brown, reddish brown, greenish brown, etc.), 5: Green (yellow-green, oily green, blue-green, dark green, green with brown, etc.)</li>
<li><strong>id</strong> - an anonymous id</li>
<li><strong>R1</strong> - Red channel 1</li>
<li><strong>G1</strong> - Green  channel 1</li>
<li><strong>B1</strong> - Blue channel 1</li>
<li><strong>R2</strong> - Red channel 2</li>
<li><strong>G2</strong> - Green  channel 2</li>
<li><strong>B2</strong> - Blue channel 2</li>
<li><strong>R3</strong> - Red channel 3</li>
<li><strong>G3</strong> - Green  channel 3</li>
<li><strong>B3</strong> - Blue channel 3</li>
</ul>",True,True
2925,sufe-nlp-statisticclass,"# Description

The task is to predict if the semantics of two questions are the same. 
Each piece of data consists of ID, Category, Query1, Query2, and label, which respectively indicate the ID, category, question 1, question 2, and label. The label indicates whether the semantics between the two questions are the same. If they are the same, they are marked as 1, if they are not the same, they are marked as 0.
You are given 20000 sentence pairs for training, 20000 sentence pairs for testing.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use accuracy as the metric.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2981122%2F2a437ea82b66bbee5ab4d66b95abbb2e%2F1589889374(1).png?generation=1589889397803662&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - format: id, category, sent1, sent2, label</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - format: id, category, sent1, sent2</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - format: id, label. (The ids are from the eval.txt.)</li>
</ul>",True,True
