3005,nju-introdm20,"# Description

<p>The data mining challenge is lanuched for course: Introduction to Data Mining 2020 in Nanjing University. </p>

<p>The task is to predict whether two code files are a clone pair. See details in <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/nju-introdm20/data"">Data page</a>. </p>

<p>To participate this competition, the team name should be your student ID. Go to <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/nju-introdm20/team"">Team page</a> to register a team. </p>

<p>Make sure you follows these <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/nju-introdm20/rules"">rules</a> here. </p>


----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is F1-Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: id1_id2 and predictions. 
Each line of submission file should contain two ids separated by a '_' and a prediction of whether files they refer to are a clone pair, separated by a comma. For the prediction, '1' represents 'two files are a clone pair ' and '0' represents 'two files are not a clone pair'. You should use your prediction to replace the default prediction for each pair of file ids in 'sample_submission.csv'. </p>

<p>your submission file should look like:</p>

<pre>id1_id2,predictions<br>5455e4724ba94e3e_214e1f9c567148ff,0<br>fc0cc03a82a64f4b_81db61de85534296,1<br>c80ce53297cd4c6b_84c063b5da214520,0<br>cdc8fb722daa4f64_c7991904666c4525,0<br>11c92d5fd15a4690_50f5207762084568,0<br>...
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>In this challenge you will get files stored in many folders as training set.Files stored in each folder are source codes written in c to solve the same problem, which are considered as clones. Files stored in different folder are not considered as clones.</p>

<p>three files are provided:</p>

<li><strong>train.zip</strong> - source codes of the training set </li>
<li><strong>test.zip</strong> - source codes of the testing set, each file's name is a unique id and will be referred in your submission.csv</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a legal example of submission with all file pairs to be predicted</li>

<p>See <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/nju-introdm20/overview/evaluation"">evaluation page</a> for details of sample_submission.csv.</p>
<p>Train.zip contains lots of subfolders. Files in each folder are clone codes. You need to utilize these supervised information to mining the features of clone codes, and apply to predicting whether those code pairs of test.zip mentioned in sample_submission.csv  are clone ones. </p>",True,True
3006,fired-area-prediction,"# Description

???? ???? ?? ??? ???? ???? ??? ??? ??? ??? ????.


?? ??? ?? http://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Forest+Fires
----------------------
# Evaluation

RMSE","# Dataset Description

The forest Fire Weather Index (FWI) is the Canadian system for rating fire danger.</p>
1. X - x-axis spatial coordinate within the Montesinho park map: 1 to 9</p>
2. Y - y-axis spatial coordinate within the Montesinho park map: 2 to 9</p>
3. month - month of the year: 'jan' to 'dec'</p>
4. day - day of the week: 'mon' to 'sun'</p>
5. FFMC(Fine Fuel Moisture Code) - FFMC index from the FWI system: 18.7 to 96.20</p>
6. DMC(Duff Moisture Code) - DMC index from the FWI system: 1.1 to 291.3</p>
7. DC(Drought Code) - DC index from the FWI system: 7.9 to 860.6</p>
8. ISI(Initial Spread Index) - ISI index from the FWI system: 0.0 to 56.10</p>
9. temp - temperature in Celsius degrees: 2.2 to 33.30</p>
10. RH(Relative Humidity) - relative humidity in %: 15.0 to 100</p>
11. wind - wind speed in km/h: 0.40 to 9.40</p>
12. rain - outside rain in mm/m2 : 0.0 to 6.4</p>
13. area - the burned area of the forest (in ha): 0.00 to 1090.84</p>",True,True
3007,sound-event-classification,"# Description

You need to classify sound events into these 16 categories - 
* alarm (short alert (beep) sound)
* clearthroat (clearing throat)
* cough
* doorslam (door slam)
* drawer
* keyboard (keyboard clicks)
* keys (keys put on table)
* knock (door knock)
* laughter
* mouse (mouse click)
* pageturn (page turning)
* pendrop (pen, pencil, or marker touching table surfaces)
* phone
* printer
* speech
* switch

Each class contains 20 recordings for training. Files in training are named according to the class name, i.e. classXX.wav where XX is a two-digit, non-consecutive number. These files are of different lengths.

`val` folder has recordings for validation, the annotations for the files in this folder are present in `val.txt` . `test` folder has the recordings for the test set. You need to predict the class for these samples and submit. All recordings in `val` and `test` folders have audio recordings of `5s` length.
Your submission should be a csv file, having two columns. For eg.,
`
test_samp_5,keys
`
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorization Accuracy</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every audio file in the test set</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: file_name and class. 
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>file_name,class<br>test_samp_0,pageturn<br>test_samp_1,knock</pre>","# Dataset Description

Each class contains 20 recordings for training. Files in training are named according to the class name, i.e. classXX.wav where XX is a two-digit, non-consecutive number. These files are of different lengths.

val folder has recordings for validation, the annotations for the files in this folder are present in val.txt . test folder has the recordings for the test set. You need to predict the class for these samples and submit. All recordings in val and test folders have audio recordings of 5s length.
Your submission should be a csv file, having two columns. For eg.,
test_samp_5,keys",True,True
3008,dfpmidatascience4,"# Description

## Exercise #5

It is an cryptography exercise. Target values are encrypted with given encryption hash function that you have to guess/reproduce from the train set and apply to test set. Hint: the dataset is large but there's only one variable is used.

train.csv is for training purposes and test.csv to test algorithm. You need to predict target variable in the test.csv.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>There's an anonymized data set with 1 feature. </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - ID of the record</li>
<li><strong>var_0</strong> - the feature</li>
<li><strong>target</strong> - is binary target field</li>
</ul>",True,True
