3034,taller2ann-usm,"# Description

# Rumour Detection

<p>Fake news are shared on social media, spreading misinformation around the globe. Early detection of these posts can be crucial to avoid rumours being spread. Currently, social networks are employing tagging mechanisms to let users know when they are reading false information so they can take action and get informed.</p>

**Given tweets with their associated graph structure, can you propose and implement a mechanism to tag false information?**

*Taking into consideration the tree structure and class unbalancing can be crucial to solve this task.*

## Tips
- Graphs cannot be presented to a neural network as-is and encoding them is suggested:
  - Graph Embedding Techniques, Applications, and Performance: A Survey
http://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02801



----------------------
# Data & Labels

# Dataset
The dataset consists of tweets and contains two different items: First, you are provided with the **tweets' text**, containing what the person tweeted, including hashtags, mentions and emojis. Then you have the **Reply tree**. This tree consists of a reference to the original tweet and the replies that came from it.

## Tree Graph

The filenames of the tree foldesr are the root's tweet ID and are structured as follows:
```
parent node -> child node
Each node is given as a tuple: ['uid', 'tweet ID', 'post time delay (in minutes)']
```
Taking in account the structure of the propagation trees could be crucial to identify different behaviours that arise from sharing fake news.

## Source Tweets
The file `source_tweets.txt` provides the text that was posted by the user, it structure is:
```
source tweet ID \t source tweet content
```

## labels
The `label.csv` files contains the ground-truth labels for the source tweets:
```
label;source tweet ID
```

### Twitter info
* UID stands for user identifier.
* tweet ID stands for the tweet identifier

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> MeanF1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>

# Submission Format
For every group of students, submission files should be a csv that contains two columns: Id and label. The label name should be used and not a number.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```{csv}
label,id
prediction,tweetid
prediction,tweetid
.
.
.
prediction,tweetid
```","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_source_tweets.txt</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_source_tweets.txt</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>train_labels.csv</strong> - the training set labels</li>
<li><strong>train folder</strong> - the training set folder of tree structures</li>
<li><strong>test folder</strong> - the test set folder of tree structures</li>
</ul>",True,True
3035,car-classificationproject-vision,"# Description

<p>Cars are one of the most sought after luxury today. They are mostly admired for their sleek design and features.</p>
<p>In this task, you are given 45 classes(cars) with 100 images each.Train a CNN on the given dataset and submit the predictions on the test set in a csv file. Download the dataset from data tab and read further instructions. </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h1>Public-Private Leaderboard</h1>
<p>
The test set has been further split into a private and a public set on a 30-70 basis. On submission, the score as seen on the public leaderboard is based on the performance set on the public test set, the final scores post deadline will be based on the performance on the private test set. This split has been adopted in order to prevent any participants from overfitting on the public leaderboard. 
</p>
<hr>
<h1>Evaluation Metric</h1>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorization Accuracy i.e. the percentage of correct predictions on total submissions. 
This will be given by,</p>
<h2>$$x = {t \over n}\cdot 100$$</h2>
<p>where t is the number of correct classification and n is the total number of samples</p>
<hr>
<h1>Submission Format</h1>
<p>You must predict the class no. for each <b><i>image_id</i></b>. Here, the predicted class must belong to one of the 45 classes of cars as mentioned in the training set. Here the class number is mapped to the classes of cars as mentioned in the car_map.csv file.
The submission is to be made in a csv file format, must have a header and should look like the following:</p>
<pre>ImageId,Class<br>image1.jpg,42<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h1>Overview</h1>
<p> The data has been split into two groups:</p>
<ul>
<li>training set(train folder)</li>
<li>test set(test folder)</li>
<p>The training set contains 45 different classes(cars) and there are 100 images for each car. These images are stored in separate folders according to names of the cars. The labels of the images are the folder names.</p>
<p>The test set contains 450 pictures of different cars.</p>
<p>Train your model on the training set. Then use that model to generate predictions and store them in a csv file</p>
<p> The csv file must contain two columns. The first column will have the string ""image""+image_no. The second column will have the predictions in the form of integers(0-44). You have to assign an integer to each car when the car names are sorted without considering the case</p>
<p> If you have any problem regarding the label encoding of the car names, refer car_map.csv</p>
<p> If you have any problem regarding the format of the file to be submitted, refer sample_submission.csv</p>",True,True
