3039,datamites-sos-tweets-classification,"# Description

<p>Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is an infectious disease caused by a newly discovered coronavirus. At this time, there are no specific vaccines or treatments for COVID-19. However, there are many ongoing clinical trials evaluating potential treatments.</p>

<p>Coronavirus has had a large-scale impact across the globe. There are thousands of tweets in a day in which people are asking for various kinds of help and assistance. Due to the sheer volume of tweets on an everyday basis, the majority of these SOS tweets go completely unnoticed and don't get required help.
</p>



----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>This competition is evaluated on the accuracy score of your predictions.</p>
<hr>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For each index in the test set, you must predict a whether it is a SOS request or not. The file should contain a header and have the following format:</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>index,help<br>0,1<br>1,0<br>2,1<br>3,0<br>4,0<br>5,1<br></pre></strong>
----------------------
# End Result

In this data set we have labelled these tweets into two categories and we want the participants to train a model which can automatically label the tweets using machine learning with highest accuracy. 
An accurate working model will have far-fetched advantages and will help to get to the right kind of support and answers . 
On the other hand, this model will not only assist the people in need but also will help brave individuals, who are working in the forefront and doing a phenomenal job in saving people¡¯s lives.

----------------------
# Inspiration

To fight this global pandemic as one community, by reaching out to all the people out there who are in need of help. We want this model to help people in need to connect with Covid relief providers, NGOs, various other institutions, private companies  and all forms of Government bodies.","# Dataset Description

# **What should I expect the data format to be?**


You will **get three** columns in **training dataset** i.e index, tweet and help i.e.<br>
- **'index'** identifier within each file.<br>
- **'tweet'** column will contain the tweets<br>
- In **'help'** column, you will get SOS tweets classified as 0 or 1 i.e <br>
0: non SOS tweet <br>
1: SOS tweet

<br>
You will **get two** columns in **test dataset** i.e index, tweets.

<br><br>
# **What am I predicting?** <br>
You are predicting the probability that a tweet is SOS help. A SOS tweet would receive a 1. A non-SOS comment would receive a 0. In the test set, all tweets are classified as either a 1 or a 0.

<br><br>
# **Files**
- **datamites_kaggle_tweets_train.xlsx**: training dataset
- **datamites_kaggle_tweets_test.xlsx**: testing dataset",True,True
3040,pitch-recog,"# Description

In this assignment, you are required to recognize the scale (ÒôëA) of music sounds. From each music sound,  we have extracted the mel-cepstrum feature, which can be represented as a 2-D image, for you. You can get an image dataset of the extracted mel-cepstrum features from [http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music\_train.zip](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music_train.zip) for training your model. For each image, its corresponding scale (ÒôëA) is labeled with a number ranging between 0 and 87. The file [http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/truth.txt](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/truth.txt) gives you the labels for all training images. You can use the labels in your model training. To test your network model, here is the test dataset ([http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music\_test.zip](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music_test.zip)). All you need to do is to build a CNN to learn the training data and test the CNN using the testing data. You have to output the recognized scale for each testing image and prepare the outputs as a 'output.csv' file by following the sample format given in the ""Data"" page ([https://www.kaggle.com/c/pitch-recog/data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/pitch-recog/data)). Once the 'output.csv' is ready, submit it to the Kaggle through [https://www.kaggle.com/c/pitch-recog/submit](https://www.kaggle.com/c/pitch-recog/submit). Then, Kaggle will evaluate your recognition accuracy immediately. You can resubmit your result when you get an improved one. Eventually, only the submission with the best accuracy among your submissions will be counted as your credit for this assignment.  

You can design a convolutional neural network (CNN) to do the recognition task. To attain a higher recognition accuracy, you can design any network architecture you like for the CNN. 

P.S.: You may need to learn how to read each image as a numpy array and then convert it into a PyTorch tensor. To this end, the OpenCV package (https://pypi.org/project/opencv-python/) is recommended.

