3089,codeasylums-asynamite-101,"# Description

**CodeAsylums Community**

A Learning Ride, which imports you to the zone of Programming Affinity!

**Learning** - lively, interactive, adaptive, collaborative with Mentorship & Sustainability 

**Ride** - An amusement ride full of fun, adventure & experience with care, which can help you to knockout your fears

**Imports** - helps a file to become a part of existing package 

**Zone **- a player moves along various challenging paths with the team mindset 

**Programming** - CodeAsylums Community 

**Affinity** - Oops! We have a recursive error in the above line ??

*Affinity to anything in long run becomes desire which can leads to greed and gambling!*

And we are here for everything!

**Let¡¯s Play and Predict**


**Asy~namite **

Looking for a 201 GET **response** for a thanksgiving community **request**
  
Challenges for mixed mindsets including Analyser, Coders, Developers, Predictor, Debugger, Designer and Hacker. Come and join our community for brainstorming, problem solving, predictive analysis  and building competitive and interesting challenges.

Connect at www.codeasylums.com

CodeAsylums Community
community@codeasylums.com
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For each ID in the test set, you must predict Industry. The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,industry<br>1,Insurance<br>2,BPO / Call Centre / ITES<br>3,IT-Software / Software Services</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>You have been given a job posting dataset. Your task is to classify the dataset into one of the industries (Ex - IT-Software / Software Services, Recruitment / Staffing, Real Estate / Property etc). However, You have given a training dataset. Using Machine Learning, find the pattern and predict industries on the given test dataset.</p>
<h2>Data Description</h2>
<h4><b>Id</b> : Unique Id</h4>
<h4><b>company</b> : Company name which has listed the job post</h4>
<h4><b>education</b> : Education required for given job post</h4>
<h4><b>experience</b> : Experience required for given job post </h4>
<h4><b>jobdescription</b> : Job Description for job post </h4>
<h4><b>jobid</b> : Job Id for job post</h4>
<h4><b>joblocation_address</b> : Job location for given job post</h4>
<h4><b>jobtitle</b> : Title for given job post</h4>
<h4><b>numberofpositions</b> : No of positions for given job post </h4>
<h4><b>payrate</b> : Salary for given job post</h4>
<h4><b>postdate</b> : Job Post Date</h4>
<h4><b>skills</b> : Skills required for given job post</h4>
<h4><b>industry</b> : Industry</h4>

<h1> What am I predicting? </h1>
<p>Predict industries based on your learned model</p>

<h1>Files : </h1>
<li> trainData.csv</li>
<li> testData.csv</li>
<li> sample_submission.csv</li>",True,True
