3129,summerschool2020,"# Description

With the ever-increasing volume of transported goods worldwide an enhanced efficiency in logistics processes is getting more and more important. Typically, this results in a higher level of automation for inter- and intra-logistics. The chair for material flow and warehousing at the TU Dortmund University hosts an intra-logistics campus as a testbed for evaluating automatization solutions.
For controlling robots in a warehouse scenario, knowledge of their positions is necessary for centralized control of material flows.
Your task will be to turn the raw sensor output into position predictions.

This competition is part of the summer school on resource-aware machine learning, further information can be found here: https://www-ai.cs.tu-dortmund.de/summer-school-2020/

##Scenario

A robot is driving in a hall on a predetermined path. The hall floor is equipped with a sensor array with each node delivering the following information: magnetometer, accelerometer, gyroscope, and received signal strength indicator (RSSI) of a received message from a wireless sensor node under the robot.
The sensor floor collects the data while the robot is driving on top of the floor and saves it in a CSV file. A motion capturing system is used to track the robot and save the robot¡¯s position. Both datasets are merged into a dataset, containing the information as documented in [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/summerschool2020/data)

![sensor floor](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akrv/sensorfloor/master/static/img/InoLab_Boden.jpg)
Vicon 0,0 is located 18.5cm from strip 12 towards strip 13.

Using this training data, your team¡¯s task is to predict the robot¡¯s position based on the sensor floor data (ex. regression). For evaluating your prediction model, you upload your predictions for test data with unknown robot positions. Based on your model's performance on the test set, your team will be ranked compared to the competing teams' performance.

### Second task

This first task on Kaggle provides a localization algorithm for controlling the robot, which will be used in the next task where you will be provided with documentation for controlling a robot's movement and access to live sensor floor data. Using both, you will control the robot to drive through predefined waypoints.
Up to five of the highest ranking teams of this Kaggle prediction competition will be invited for live control of the robot using your algorithm on Friday, 4th of September. This task will be performed outside of Kaggle and the API for controlling the robot published on the summer school homepage.

## Evaluation

For evaluation purposes you are to provide the predicted x and y values separately. The overall rating is the sum of the difference between prediction and the actual value based on the overall mean squared error (MSE) treating the x and y columns independently. We are aware that a measure like euclidean distance would provide a more accurate score, but this evaluation metric is currently not included in Kaggle.

The score is based on the test set. 50% of the test data is used to determine the public score, which is visible to all participants. The other 50% is used to calculate the private score, which will be used for the final ranking. The data is split into two sets to eliminate the possibility of an unfair advantage by trying different values to improve the score.

----------------------
# Sensor Floor

The sensor floor is a sensor array made up of 345 nodes in a matrix made up of 23 x 15 rows and columns. Each column is called a strip with 15 nodes attached to a flat cable strip equidistantly 1 meter apart. The cable connects the 15 nodes to power and a data line. Each node is a sensor tag CC1350 STK with onboard sensors for measuring different physical phenomena. Some of the sensors used for this event are the nine-axis IMU with accelerometer, gyroscope, and magnetometer. The radio interface is used to measure the received signal strength indicator (RSSI). 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F343357%2Fdec924bda1c13e8f3d9bd20519a89ca8%2FRobot%20Paths.png?generation=1596630072046678&alt=media)


Each of the strips ends with a raspberry pi which connects the RS422 bus and the bus controller for the 1wire communication bus. At any given time, only a single node can talk using the wired bus and to control the medium access. The 1wire bus is used to switch the bus interface controllers on and off. The time required for communicating with the 1wire bus is the overhead in reading the sensor values using the wired bus. All the raspberry pis are time-synchronized using an NTP server, and they publish the sensor values to an MQTT broker. These published topics and their data can be consumed in any other application depending on requirements.

The hall also has a ROS-based, pick-and-place robot that can freely navigate the hall using a long-range LiDAR and SLAM algorithm. The lab also has a Vicon motion capturing system, a marker-based object tracking system that provides millimeter level precise location information of the tagged objects. All of these systems are connected to the same network, and a messaging service like MQTT is used to interconnect the motion capture system. The robot's necessary ROS commands are abstracted as MQTT topics, and finally, the live data delivered by the sensor floor is also available as MQTT topics.

In this hackathon, we will be using the 9 axis IMU and the RSSI from each of the 345 sensor nodes to localize a robot driving on the floor. There are two tasks in this hackathon, where the first one is about predicting the robot's location using the provided training data set. This is the task of this competition here on Kaggle. Once a model is developed, a localization service is then used in task 2 to develop a robot controller using the live data to drive the robot through a predefined path in the hall.","# Dataset Description

<p>We have two systems that asynchronously record data (Vicon motion capturing and sensor floor). The sensor floor consists of different sensor units (IMU, Magnetometer, RSSI), providing observations for these features at about 4 per second, buffered and flushed with a round trip time of 5 seconds. (The sensors for each strip are read sequentially). The Vicon system precisely tracks the robot in a 3D-Coordinate system and provides a timestamp, the robot¡¯s XYZ-position, and rotation.</p>

<p>Creating the training data is done by matching the Vicon and sensor floor data by time. Each sensor floor observation is matched to the single closest Vicon data point according to their timestamp. We obtain a merged CSV file containing the sensor observations and position and time information for each observation as the sensor floor operates at roughly 2 Hz (2 Observations per second). We match the Vicon data to the sensor floor as the Vicon system creates observations at a higher frequency.</p>

<p>Some sensors such as the magnetometer or accelerometer, measure constant values such as the earth¡¯s gravity or magnetic field. We calibrate the data by normalizing each sensor with constants obtained from gathering sensor data on an empty research hall. We eliminate possible differences between sensors based on spatial positioning.
This is done for all nodes individually for each feature.</p>

<p>Finally, we arranged so-called frames of sensor values. Each frame contains a single measurement for each sensor and feature along with the current Vicon position and timestamp. The measurements of each frame are approximately within 0.25 seconds of each other. Each frame is encoded as a JSON string in the data field of the csv files. The frame is identified by a frame number and each frame is associated with a vicon position in x- and y-direction. We provide 10 low-level features: RSSI, magnetometer (x,y,z), accelerometer (x,y,z) and gyroscope (x,y,z), timestamps, vicon position and an identifier for the sensor in the form of two columns strip- and node-id.</p>

<h1>CSV Files</h1>
<p>The provided CSV files contained in the zip-archive for training and test data always contain a frame number and a data column, while the training data additionally contains the ground truth for the robot position.</p>

<table style=""width:100%"">
    <tr>
        <th>frame_number</th>
        <th>vicon_X</th>
        <th>vicon_y</th>
        <th>data</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Integer</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>String (JSON)</td>
    </tr>
</table>


<h1>JSON Data-String</h1>
<p>The JSON-String in the data-column of the csv-files is composed of identifiers for each sensor and the values measured at each sensor on the time indicated by the timestamp column.
Each strip id and node id pair is further identified by an index in the json file ranging from 0 to 340, with one entry for each sensor.</p>


<p> Here we read all available frames for each training data file and store it in a list. For convenience we create dictionaries containing the data alongside the labels and frame numbers.</p>

<h2>Identifiers</h2>
The identifiers in the JSON file are used to associate each set of measurements to the measuring sensor and additionally to assign the proper timestamp when the measurement was taken. 
The index column, that can be found in the JSON string is a unique identifier for each row, that is, each sensor in the data.
<table style=""width:100%"">
    <tr>
        <th>index</th>
        <th>strip_id</th>
        <th>node_id</th>
        <th>timestamp</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Integer</td>
        <td>Integer</td>
        <td>Integer</td>
        <td>DateTime</td>
    </tr>
</table>
<h2>Features</h2>
<table style=""width:100%"">
    <tr>
        <th>ax</th>
        <th>ay</th>
        <th>az</th>
        <th>gx</th>
        <th>gy</th>
        <th>gz</th>
        <th>mx</th>
        <th>my</th>
        <th>mz</th>
        <th>r</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
        <td>Float</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>Reading the JSON-portion of the csv can be for example done in python like this: </p>

<code>
import os <br>
import pandas as pd<br>

data_dir = ""path/to/training/data""<br>

train = []<br>
for file in os.listdir(data_dir):<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;df = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(data_dir, file))<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;frames = []<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;for index, row in df.T.items():<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;frame = {col: row[col] for col in df.columns if col != 'data'}<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;frame['data']  = pd.read_json(row['data'])<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;frames.append(frame)<br>
&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;train.append(frames)<br>
</code>

<h1>Column Description</h1>
<p>The columns in the data set are divided into three categories: <b>Identifiers</b>, <b>Features</b>  and <b>Label</b></p>
<h2>Identifiers</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Frame Number: ID of the current frame in chronological order.</li>
    <li>Timestamp: Timestamp with [ns] precision when the sensor values in the corresponding row were gathered </li>
    <li>StripID: ID of the Strip where the Sensor is located in</li>
    <li>NodeID: Numbering of the Node inside a given Strip</li>
</ol>
<h2>Features</h2>
<ol>
    <li> [ax,ay,az]: Accelerometer values in [g] (with 1g = 9,81 m/s?2) in x,y,z direction (az is always facing towards the ground (1g ~ Earth¡¯s gravity)</li>
    <li>[gx,gy,gz| Gyroscope values in degrees per second [dps] in x,y,z direction</li>
    <li>[mx,my,mz] Magnetometer values in micro Tesla [¦ÌT]</li>
    <li>[r] Received Signal Strength Indication [RSSI], 0 indicates a timeout/ no package received</li>
</ol>
<h2>Labels</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Vicon_x: Actual Robot Position tracked by the vicon system in x-direction in meters [m] (distance from vicon(0,0))</li>
    <li>Vicon_y: Actual Robot Position tracked by the vicon system in y-direction in meters [m] (distance from vicon(0,0))</li>
</ol>",True,True
3130,stroke-trial-prediction,"# Description

<h2>General Guidelines</h2>
<p>This is a competition to predict deaths in the International Stroke Trial. The training set includes information about 9,762 patients within the trial. The test set includes related information about 9,558 additional patients. The goal of this competition is to predict whether each patient was still living or not six months after the initial event.</p>

<p>The public scores on the leaderboard are determined by 20% of the test set. Final scores will be determined by including the other 80% of the test set.</p>

<h2>Acknowledgments</h2>
<p>This dataset comes from the International Stroke Trial Database found in journals published by BioMed Central. The data collection was funded primarily by the UK Medical Research Council, the UK Stroke Association, and the European Union BIOMED-1 program. The data was collected and coordinated with the help of the Neurosciences Trials Unit, the Clinical Trial Service Unit in Oxford, and thousands of patients and their doctors.</p>


----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Classification Accuracy. A score is calculated by determining the number of correct predictions and dividing this number by the total number of predictions made.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submissions should be CSV files. There should be two columns in a submission file: ID and PatientDied. A Y in this column indicates that the patient died within the first six months after the initial event. An N indicates the patient was still living at six months. The first row should be a header, followed by 9,558 rows of predictions in the following format:</p>
 <pre>ID,PatientDied<br>9826,N<br>9827,N<br>9828,Y<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File Descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train1.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>Test1.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>SampleSubmission1.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Variables</h2>
<p>Look here for variable names and descriptions:</p>
<p>https://trialsjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1745-6215-12-101/tables/2</p>",True,True
3131,computervisionpracticum,"# Description

Welcome to the In-class CV competition! This is an in-house competition curated to give you a hands-on experience of Computer Vision techniques and models. Through the current competition, we would like you to apply the methods and techniques taught in class on the data-set provided. Through the course, we give you the opportunity to challenge and test the skills you pick up.

Please note that this is a limited-participation competition. Only invited users may participate.

Have fun!

if you attended the CV Practicum course and you are facing any problem in this competition,
Please contact:
Abhijeet Biswas (abhibiswas@deloitte.com)
Ankur Shukla ( ankushukla@deloitte.com)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Accuracy will be used as the evaluation criteria

 $$ Accuracy =  \frac{TP+TN}{(TP+TN+FN+FP)} $$

Please submit the files in the same format as mentioned in ""submission.csv""
For example,
Submission file:
Images, Label
0.jpeg, NORMAL
1.jpeg, PNEUMONIA
....
....","# Dataset Description

The dataset is organized into 2 folders (train, testl) and contains subfolders for each image category (Pneumonia/Normal) in train. There are 5841 X-Ray images (JPEG) and 2 categories (Pneumonia/Normal).


Chest X-ray images (anterior-posterior) were selected from retrospective cohorts of pediatric patients of one to five years old from Guangzhou Women and Children¡¯s Medical Center, Guangzhou. All chest X-ray imaging was performed as part of patients¡¯ routine clinical care.


For the analysis of chest x-ray images, all chest radio graphs were initially screened for quality control by removing all low quality or unreadable scans. The diagnoses for the images were then graded by two expert physicians before being cleared for training the AI system. In order to account for any grading errors, the evaluation set was also checked by a third expert.",True,True
3132,silero-audio-classifier,"# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F851584%2F6da18c3fcfcf39ad7e079cbebdeeff89%2Fheader.png?generation=1595528284928601&alt=media)

# TLDR;

You need to classify the provided 3-second audio samples into 3 categories: **voice, music and noise**.
This task is similar to a voice activity detector (VAD), but the task is simplified a bit:

- You do not need to solve a sequence-to-sequence task or classify each audio frame;
- Data is representative of real ""in-the-wild"" data, but audio formats / lengths are unified;
- Data is curated to minimize the engineering challenge and let the participants explore various ML algorithms;

# Data

- 3 classes - voice, music, noise;
- 30 GB of data in 3 classes, about 328k audio files;
- All files are in `.wav` format, sampling rate is 16 kHz;
- We provide roughly 30 GB of data, 10 GB per each class;
- All files have the same length - 3 seconds or 3 * 16 samples;
- A very diverse selection of data sources and domains;
- All samples are anonymized and stored in the on-disk DB with their hashes as paths;

# Folder Structure
 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F851584%2F1c69045b78f3de2c3d0902140d0f01f9%2F2020-07-23_19-18-23.jpg?generation=1595521135670936&alt=media)

# Starter Code

We provide some useful utilities and some starter boilerplate code.
----------------------
# Metric

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F851584%2F6da18c3fcfcf39ad7e079cbebdeeff89%2Fheader.png?generation=1595528284928601&alt=media)

The metric is just a plain vanilla **classification accuracy**.
Classes are balanced, i.e. there are 33% of each class in train / val / test.
You can see the submission example in the provided data files.
A simple random submission would predictably get a 33% accuracy.","# Dataset Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F851584%2F1c69045b78f3de2c3d0902140d0f01f9%2F2020-07-23_19-18-23.jpg?generation=1595521135670936&alt=media)

- `train.csv` contains ground truths for train data;
- There are 3 types of audio files: music, noise and speech;
- All 3 classes are balanced, i.e. there is ~33% of each type of files;
- Please see some starter code and helper utils in the provided notebook;",True,True
3133,dl-course-final-competition,"# Description

# Introduction
This is the final competition for NCTU Deep Learning Course.
In this competition, you need to do the classification problem for the Chinese news title in different categories. 

There has 10 different categories for the Chinese news title. You need to use the model to predict the category based on the news title or some keywords.

* ŠÊ˜· news_entertainment
* ówÓý news_sports
* ·¿®a news_house
* ÆûÜ‡ news_car
* ½ÌÓý news_edu 
* ¿Æ¼¼ news_tech
* ÜŠÊÂ news_military
* ‡øëH news_world
* Þr˜I news_agriculture
* ëŠ¸‚ news_game


# Upload
1. You need to upload the **answer.csv** file for your result of prediction
2. The detail of the format, please check the [Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/c/dl-course-final-competition/overview/evaluation) and [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/dl-course-final-competition/data)
3. You need to upload your code and README to New E3 system

# Rules
1. Only one person as a team
2. The team name should name as your student ID, i.e. 08XXXXX, AXXXXXX, BXXXXXX
3. You can submit the answer **20** times every 24 hours
4. You can reference the code and architecture for your model on the internet
5. Do not share your result with your classmate
6. The score will base on the result of the private leaderboard

# Hint
You should tokenize the Chinese word to create the dictionary
* [Jieba](https://github.com/fxsjy/jieba)
* [CKIP](https://github.com/ldkrsi/ckip_python)
* [NLTK](https://www.nltk.org/)

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision).
The Accuracy = (TP + TN)/(TP + TN + FP + FN)
# Submission Format
Submission files should contain two columns: id and label ( label should be a [integer](https://numpy.org/devdocs/user/basics.types.html) between 0 and 9)

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,label<br>0,2<br>1,1<br>   .<br>   .<br>   .<br>59907,5<br></pre>

You can use any name but with file type **.csv**
For more details, you can reference the [sample.csv](https://www.kaggle.com/c/dl-course-final-competition/data)","# Dataset Description

# File description
* **train_data.csv** - the training set for your model training
* **test_data.csv** - the testing set for the leaderboard ranking and final scoring
* **sample.csv** - the sample submission file in the correct format

# Data fields
* **id** - an anonymous id unique to a given news title
* **label** - the label category of the news title
* **label_name** - the category name of the news title
* **title** - the title of the news
* **keyword** - the keywords from the news",True,True
