3145,sasviyaforlearners2020,"# Description

## Introduction

The SAS Viya for Learners Challenge hosted online through Kaggle InClass is geared towards students who are looking to get into and engage with machine learning problems. Students will walk through the data science process and learn how to build machine learning models on a simulated business problem, with a chance to prove their skills on the live leader board.

SAS Viya for Learners is a full suite of cloud-based software that supports the entire analytics life cycle ¨C from data, to discovery, to deployment ¨C and it also lets you code in SAS, Python or R. Machine learning is about using a combination of programming for model building, as well as visual, interactive interfaces to be able to explore data and test assumptions quickly.

### To Enter The Challenge:

* Register for access to the SAS Viya for Learners environment by clicking on the __SAS Viya For Learners Registraton__ tab
* While you wait for email confirmation, please read through the rest of the section for details of the challenge.
* Please click on the Rules tab and accept the competition rules in order to participate.

## Table of Contents
1. The Challenge Problem
2. Challenge Structure & Getting Started
3. SAS Viya Resources
4. Contact & Acknowledgements

## The Challenge Problem

A simulated telecommunications company, SAS Telecom, is interested in selling more of its services to its existing customers. SAS Telecom is particularly interested in customers that upgrade their services (up-selling) and customers who purchase other services in addition to what they have currently (cross-selling). Ultimately, SAS Telecom want to identify among their new customers, who is likely to be up-sold and/or cross-sold and target those customers with offers.

To that end, they have hired you, who is part of their data science team, to understand the factors that determine whether a customer is likely to be up-sold or cross-sold. To do this, they have provided you a dataset of existing customers who were either up-sold and/or cross-sold services with a list of other information and you must build an analytical model to be able to predict the likelihood of a customer being up-sold and/or cross-sold.

## Challenge Structure
### Getting Started and Entering
To start developing the ML models for this challenge on SAS Viya for Learners, all participants need access to the environment. If you came here from the landing page, you should have an email with a provided credentials for you to access the environment. If you are signing up through Kaggle, please refer to the __SAS Viya For Learners Registration section__ on the left for more details.

Once you have access to the environment, please refer to the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/sasviyaforlearners2020/data) section to understand and walk-through the data that will be used for the challenge.

### Rules

Please refer to the Evaluation and the Submission Requirements sections for more details.

## SAS Viya Resources 

To get you started, please refer to the Notebooks section of the Kaggle Competition page. In this Notebook section, are two programming templates in Python and R that walk-through a machine learning pipeline and build a simple model to get you started with the challenge.

For resources on how to use the SAS Viya graphical user interfaces, please refer to the __SAS Viya for Learners Resources Portal__.

## Contact &amp; Acknowledgements
- Please use the Discussion and Notebooks section for discussion on the challenge 
- Feel free to reach out to the SAS Academic team for any further questions (Academic@oz.sas.com)

Thanks for playing!
----------------------
# Evaluation & Submission Requirements

# Submission to Kaggle

## Submission Requirements

All submissions require the following components to be eligible:

- A CSV containing your model predictions on the test set labelled as submission.csv. This submission.csv is entered through the Kaggle competition page.

- Model code of your SAS model (built using SAS code, or the Python/R APIs). This is your model score code (.sas or .sas + ASTORE) and must be emailed to Academic@oz.sas.com

- Please also link your training notebook as part of your submission!

## Submission CSV Evaluation
All entries are evaluated on a final submission CSV file that contains two columns, CustomerID and upsell_xsell. CustomerID represents the unique CustomerID of the testing set and upsell_xsell is the prediction whether they will upsell and/or xsell to another service (1 = yes, 0= no).

```
CustomerId, I_upsell_xsell
3932693,	0
3932880,	1

```

The scoring metric for the Submission CSV is the Categorization Accuracy.

## SAS Model Code
Along with your submission CSV, you must submit and provide accompanying model files so your submission can be replicated and you demonstrated that you used the SAS Viya procedures to build your model.

The model score code, which is either a .sas file or a combination of .sas &amp; ASTORE. These are scored representations of the model used to score new data.

These files can be emailed to Academic@oz.sas.com with an email titled ""SAS Viya for Learners Submission ###"" where ### is your Kaggle user or your team-name. 
----------------------
# Timeline

## Competition Timeline & Dates

- 10AM 13th July 2020 - Competition page launches and participants are able to view the data and familiarise themselves with the problem

- 8AM 20th July, 2020 - Challenge begins. The test dataset will be revealed on the Kaggle page and activated on the environment. Participants can then submit to the Leaderboard. Notebook and modelling templates will also be shared to help get participants started.

- 6PM 27th July 27, 2020 - Competition ends and submission deadline.

All deadlines will be AEDT based unless otherwise noted.
----------------------
# Prizes

## Prizes

Participants get a chance to hone their skills and receive a SAS Badge for their resume that can also be displayed on LinkedIn. These are digital badges through Acclaim and help not only display your skills, but link you to prospective employers looking for SAS skills.

There will be two badges offered:
 
A Participants for entering the challenge and making a submission.
![Participants Badge](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1570418%2F2e74bf1c45d23b8ede6da5f9c5998d28%2FParticipantBadge.png?generation=1593741499565967&alt=media)


A Winner's Badge for the final Top 3 participants or team on the Leaderboard at the end of the challenge.
![Winner's Badge](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1570418%2Fc14af378f7d501afe09cf97fda8550e6%2FWinnerBadge.png?generation=1593741514430259&alt=media)


An email will be sent to the Top 3 Winners on the Leaderboard from the SAS Academic Team (Academic@oz.sas.com) on Tuesday the 28th. If entering as a Team, all members will receive a Winner's Badge. Note to be eligible, the participant(s) must have registered their Kaggle account with an Australian university student email. Please refer to the Rules for more detail.

----------------------
# SAS Viya For Learners Registration

## Registration 

In order to  to participate in the challenge, you need to register for access to the SAS Viya for Learners environment. This environment is a 24/7, cloud-based environment where you will have access to the SAS Viya analytics platform in order to build models for the challenge.

To register, please visit the following link and sign-up for access:
https://www.sas.com/en_au/events/2020/sas-viya-for-learners-challenge.html

After applying, you should receive an email notice. Please follow the instructions specified in the email notice to navigate into the SAS Viya for Learners environment.
----------------------
# SAS Viya For Learners Resources Portal

## Resources

For the SAS Viya for Learners Challenge, we have created a customised learning portal containing resources to learn how to use the SAS Viya platform. 

To access the portal:
1.	Go to [support.sas.com/myelearn](https://support.sas.com/edu/viewmyelearn.html) and log in to your profile. If you do not currently have a profile, please create one using a link provided on support.sas.com/myelearn.
2.	Once you've logged in to your My Training page, locate the ""To Activate a New Product"" box on the right side of the screen.
3.	Enter the activation code, __*FS00613876281*__ and click Submit.
4.	Review and accept the license agreement.
5.	You should now see the training in your list of active courses
6.	Click on the portal link to access the SAS Viya for Learners resources.

Once you have performed this initial activation, visit [support.sas.com/myelearn](https://support.sas.com/edu/viewmyelearn.html) or select My Training from any page on the SAS training web site and log in to your profile to access the portal.

## Kaggle Introduction

Follow the ""Step-by-Step Guide to Using Viya for Learners for 2020 Kaggle Challenge"" to familiarise yourself with using the Viya platform.","# Dataset Description

## Introduction 

A simulated telecommunications company, SAS Telecom, is interested in selling more of its services to its existing customers. SAS Telecom is particularly interested in customers that upgrade their services (up-selling) and customers who purchase other services in addition to what they have currently (cross-selling). Ultimately, SAS Telecom want to identify among their new customers, who is likely to be up-sold and/or cross-sold and target those customers with offers.

To understand this behaviour, SAS Telecom have captured data for its current customers containing various metrics as well as whether that particular customer up-sold/x-sold given by the target variable, _Upsell_xsell_, where 1 indicates that customer did upsell/xsell and 0 indicates that they did not.

As a data scientist, SAS Telecom have tasked you with building a model on this target variable in order to predict whether new customers are likely to be up-sold or x-sold. Along with the target variable, the dataset contains over a hundred variables detailing information such as the amount time a customer has spent making calls, the age of their account to even how what sales channel they used with SAS Telecom to purchase their services.

__However,__ though there are a lot of variables, not all of these variables might be useful in building an accurate predictive model for upsell/xsell. On top of that, some variables might be useful predictors but are messy and have data quality issues and require cleaning and processing before they can be used as input into the model.

As the data scientist, it's up to you to figure out and understand which variables make sense to be accurate predictors of the target, and which ones would be best used to build the best predictive model.

## Data Overview 

There are two datasets used for this challenge, the training data and evaluation data:

- The training data, __looking_glass_v4__, which contains the customer data that the predictive model will be trained on. This dataset is already loaded in the Viya for Learners environment so you can start exploring it once you have access but a sample of the data has also been loaded onto Kaggle for you to explore as well.

- The testing data, which contains new customer data that the predictive model will be evaluated on. This dataset is loaded onto the environment as __kaggletest_looking_glass_1_v3__. It contains the exact same columns as the training data, but without the Upsell_Xsell target column that your predictive model will attempt to predict. An example submission file has been uploaded to Kaggle so you can see what your final submission CSV will look like, called __submissionExample.csv__.

## Instructions for the Challenge

### Access the Training Data using SAS Viya for Learners Interface

Since the training data is already loaded into the environment, once you have registered into SAS Viya for Learners, go into the __DLUS34__ Caslib to start accessing the data.

### Coding ML models on training data using SAS/Python/R

Using SAS through SAS Studio or Python/R with the SWAT package in JupyterLab, the data must first be loaded in the __DLUS34__ Caslib. See the Notebooks section for more snippets to get started.

### Generating Submission CSV on Evaluation Data

Kaggle expects the submission CSV to have the CustomerId column to a specific order, when you evaluate your model you need to follow the below steps to generate your submission CSV.

1. After you have trained your model, you must score it on the test data and generate your prediction column, __I_Upsell_xsell__. Once this column is generated, output the column as a CSV and save it.

2. Download the __submissionId.csv__ file. This is a CSV containing the CustomerIds in the test set in the specified order. 

3. Append your prediction column to the __submissionId.csv__ and save as a new CSV - your predictions should be ready to submit. To see what this file should look like, please refer to the __submissionExample.csv__ file. 

## File Descriptions

* __looking_glass_v4.csv__ - Training data, already loaded into the DLUS34 Caslib on the environment.

* __kaggletest_looking_glass_1_v3.sas7bdat__ - Test data, already loaded into the ACADCOMP Caslib on the environment.

* __submissionExample.csv__ - Example submission 

* __submissionId.csv__ - Submission Id columns required to make the submission",True,True
