</ul>",True,True
3154,pris20-covithon,"# Description

<h2>Background</h2>
**Covithon Competition** is part of this year's Phi's Research &amp; Innovation *Virtual* Summit, an annual gathering of more than 3000 Arab youth under one goal and united by one passion: to advance and enable scientific research in the Arab world. Under this year¡¯s concept: ¡°Taking Action!¡±, we aim to enable researchers and innovators at a very concrete level, and connect them with worldwide top scientists, innovators, and industry experts.



<h2>Eligibility</h2>
<p>This competition is accessible to everyone regardless of their level of experience in machine learning. you may use any programming language (Python, R,.. etc) or tool you feel comfortable with (you can literally only use EXCEL or Spreadsheet to predict the results). if you are planning to use code we recommend using the following libraries: PyTorch, Scikit-learn, Numpy, Pandas. The data is a single table, pre-cleaned .CSV format.

<h2>The Challenge</h2>
The Challenge suggests a scenario where a new pandemic has hit earth in 2025, the symptoms are not typical and the method of spread is unusual. The Data consists of a single table, a structured dataset made of 28 features, and 8000 records. Your job is to predict whether each person should be labeled as infected or not infected based on your prediction model (Binary Classification, Supervised Learning Problem).</p>

Now, the real challenge is to gain the highest prediction *Accuracy* in comparison to all other contestants during the 3-day competition, you may view a real-time leaderboard that is constantly updated with the latest submissions and highest prediction results, so you can see what others have achieved so far in terms of predictions accuracy.

The submission format is a simple double column table that consists of ""id"" & ""prediction"" with [1 or 0] values of 2000 Test records (.CVS format).

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We'd like to especially thank Hamza Zagha & Mohammad Al-Salti for the great efforts they have put in designing the challenge and putting together the dataset for the challenge. </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation page describes how submissions will be scored and how contestants should format their submissions.</p>

<h2>Evaluation metric </h2>
<strong>CategorizationAccuracy:</strong> The percentage of total correct classification from the test data.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
| Submission Column | Expected Column | Validator |
| --- | --- |
| id | Id (String) | (No Validation) |
| prediction | Category (String) | Behavior Prediction = [0, 1] |

<strong>You can review the ""sampleSubmission.csv"" file in the ""Data"" tab for your reference as a submission template.</strong>","# Dataset Description

<p>The train data consists of a structured, precleaned dataset made of 28 features and 8000 records, while the test data of 2000 records. Your job is to predict whether each person should be labeled as infected or not infected based on your prediction model (Binary Classification, Supervised Learning Problem).</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
