3167,kddbr-2020,"# Description

This competition is part of the 4th?[KDD-BR](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/kdd.html)?(Brazilian Knowledge Discovery in Databases competition) event, one of the joint activities of the 2020 editions of?the Brazilian Conference on Intelligence Systems ([BRACIS](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/)),?Encontro Nacional de Intelig¨ºncia Artificial e Computacional ([ENIAC](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/eniac.html)) and Symposium on Knowledge Discovery, Mining and Learning ([KDMiLe](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/kdmile.html)), promoted by the Brazilian Computer Society ([SBC](https://www.sbc.org.br/)).

*Due to the COVID pandemic and the sanitary and economical related issues, BRACIS 2020 will be a virtual (online) conference.*

## Challenge objective

The objective of this competition is to predict the unavailability of cars in a car rental agency. 
When a car is unavailable, its status can be either ¡°in maintenance¡± or ¡°being washed¡±. The goal is to predict the number of cars entering and leaving each of the two status, for each of the four shifts in a day, for one week ahead.

## Background information

Nowadays, with great advances on transportation solutions and the evolution of car rental companies, it has become very important to be able to predict the behavior of demand and availability of their car fleet in order to provide maximum efficiency on fleet management on their branches. Without such efficiency, a company won¡¯t be able to provide high quality and agile services for their customers.

In order to have a great fleet management, there are two very important pieces of information that must be predicted: demand and availability. Consider a car rental branch that has such a high demand on weekends and that there are not enough cars available for all customers, mainly because a great part of the fleet is under maintenance or washing due to customers¡¯s previous adventures. This is a big loss of opportunity for the branch, and therefore it is important to be able to predict not only demand, but also the availability of its fleet to avoid such kind of event.

Consider the simplified car journey to be as follows: 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F210371%2Fa725239bd0154225ea57089b917ea9ae%2Flocaliza_exemplo.png?generation=1594062202781499&alt=media)

## Competition Organizers (alphabetic order)

- Ana Carolina Lorena, Computer Science Professor at Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA)
- Angelo Ciarlini, Data Science Director at Localiza Hertz
- Daniel Almeida, Data Scientist at Localiza Hertz
- Caio Gomes, Pricing Coordinator at Localiza Hertz
- Elaine Ribeiro de Faria, Computer Science Professor at Universidade Federal de Uberlandia (UFU)
- Filipe Alves Neto Verri, Computer Science Professor at Instituto Tecnol¨®gico de Aeron¨¢utica (ITA)
- Gustavo Borges, Pricing and Fleet Management Director at Localiza Hertz
- Jo?o Maciel, Pricing Intern at Localiza Hertz
- Ricardo Cerri, Computer Science Professor at Universidade Federal de S?o Carlos (UFSCar)
- Ricardo Stary, Senior Pricing Analyst at Localiza Hertz
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the [Weighted Root Mean Squared Error (WRMSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation), which is a measure of the difference between two continuous variables.  For each tuple, multiple outputs must be predicted. The weight of the predicted output in the error calculation is inversely proportional to the number of days ahead.

| Output's days ahead | Weight |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | 1.00 |
| 1 | 0.75 |
| 2 | 0.60 |
| 3 | 0.50 |
| 4 | 0.43 |
| 5 | 0.38 |
| 6 | 0.33 |

## Deadline

The submissions will be closed on October 1st, 2020 (Anywhere on Earth). Therefore, the final results will be based on the solutions posted until October 1st, 2020. Please note that the system will still be able to receive submissions after this deadline, but they will be not taken into account for computing the final results.

## Award Session

As this competition is part of the joint activities of the 2020 edition of the Brazilian Conference on Intelligent Systems ([BRACIS](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/)), the Encontro Nacional de Intelig¨ºncia Artificial e Computacional ([ENIAC](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/eniac.html)) and the Symposium on Knowledge Discovery, Mining and Learning ([KDMile](http://bracis2020.c3.furg.br/kdmile.html)), the top three teams will be invited to present their solutions at a competition award session, during the period 20-23 October, 2020. **Finalists must register at the conference to be considered in the award session**. The actual final positions of the ranking will be disclosed after presentations. 

In this year's edition, competitors must share with the organizers all information to reproduce the achieved solution, including sharing source code of all parts of the solution under [The zlib/libpng License (Zlib)](https://opensource.org/licenses/Zlib), to be considered in the award session. The competition organizers will get in touch with the top-3 teams once the submission deadline is met.

Please also note that, this year, the maximum size of the competing teams is of **3** members.

## Submission 

Submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `value`. The column `id` contains the tuple identifier and the output name concatenated together (separated with `_`). The column `value` should contain the predicted value.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

<pre>id,value<br />45958_output_1_0,0.0<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">45958_output_1_1,0.1<br />45958_output_1_2,0.2<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">45958_output_1_3,0.3 <br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>

A sample submission (`sample.csv`) is provided in the [Data section](https://www.kaggle.com/c/kddbr-2020/data).
----------------------
# Prizes

## Award session

As this competition is part of the joint activities of the 2020 edition of the Brazilian Conference on Intelligent Systems (BRACIS) and the Encontro Nacional de Intelig¨ºncia Artificial e Computacional (ENIAC), the top three teams will be invited to present their solutions at a competition award session, during the period 20-23 October, 2020.  **Finalists must register at the conference to be considered in the award session**. The actual final positions of the ranking will be disclosed after presentations. 

In this year's edition, competitors must share with the organizers all information to reproduce the achieved solution, including sharing source code of all parts of the solution under [The zlib/libpng License (Zlib)](https://opensource.org/licenses/Zlib), to be considered in the award session. The competition organizers will get in touch with the top-3 teams once the submission deadline is met.

## Prizes

The top 3 teams will be awarded with a single use three-day car rental for each team member (limited to three members per team) with coverages and additional driver included to be used in any Localiza Hertz branch until November 30th, 2020.  

1st place: Car group GX (Automatic SUV)

2nd place: Car group FX (Automatic Sedan)

3rd place: Car group C (Economic with air)

The car rental will be available for individual usage and it is not cumulative.

**Only teams presenting at the award session will be rewarded!**","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of daily tuples identified in the ¡°date¡± column. Each tuple has an ""id"" column that uniquely identifies it. The training set, built using real data from 2017 and 2018, contains both input and output columns. In the test set, containing data from 2019, only the input columns are provided.

The input variables are 557 features that may be correlated to the target variables. They are labeled from 0 up to 556, with the first four being categorical variables, and the rest being numerical. For the numerical variables, all the previous fourteen (14) values are provided (one for each day in two weeks before the tuple date). The input column names follow the syntax below:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F210371%2Faf05e6e353863f45c6ce70e4618be43f%2Fdatakdd1.png?generation=1594062355235890&alt=media)

The output variables consist of a set of 16 (sixteen) columns, representing each of the 7 (seven) days ahead of the tuple date, 4 (four) shifts a day and 4 (four) status transitions a day, two for each status (entering or leaving). 
The column names follow the syntax detailed below:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F210371%2F9cf037d978a6ff49919c3c1e6cadafbd%2Fdatakdd2.png?generation=1594062372260850&alt=media)",True,True
3168,fundamentos-ml,"# Description

<p>Bienvenidos al desaf¨ªo de clasificiaci¨®n autom¨¢tica para los alumnos del curso en Fundamentos de Aprendizaje Autom¨¢tico. El desaf¨ªo se abre el 9 de julio de 2020 y termina el 30 de julio de 2020.  Trabajar¨¦is en los grupos de clase y podr¨¦is hacer hasta 4 env¨ªos al d¨ªa.</p>

<p>El nombre del equipo se formar¨¢ con los c¨®digos NIA (pod¨¦is consultarlos en Aula Global) de los participantes.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluaci¨®n



<p>La m¨¦trica que vamos a usar para evaluar vuestros clasificadores es  <strong>F1</strong>. Si llamamos TP al n¨²mero de verdaderos positivos (observaciones de la clase 1 etiquetadas en la clase 1), FP al n¨²mero de falsos positivos (observaciones de la clase 0 etiquetadas en la clase 1) y FN al n¨²mero de falsos negativos (observaciones de la clase 1 etiquetadas en la clase 0), la m¨¦trica F1 se calcula como</p>
<p>\[
F1 = 2\frac{P\times R}{P + R} \mbox{ where } P = \frac{TP}{TP+FP} \mbox{ and } R = \frac{TP}{TP+FN} 
\]</p>

<h2>Formato de los env¨ªos</h2>
<p>Cada fichero enviado deber¨ªa tener <strong>predicciones de etiquetas binarias para cada observaci¨®n del conjunto de test</strong>. Los ficheros enviados deber¨¢n tener dos columnas: ¨ªndice de la observaci¨®n y predicci¨®n.</p>
<p>El fichero tiene que llevar cabecera, y seguir este formato:</p>
<pre>ID,target<br>0,1<br>1,0<br>2,0<br>3,0<br></pre>

<p>En este ejemplo la observaci¨®n con ID 0 se asigna a la clase 1, la observaci¨®n con ID 1 se asigna a la clase 0, igual que las observaciones con ID 2 e ID 3.</p>
----------------------
# Calificaciones

<p>La calificaci¨®n del desaf¨ªo es competitiva y supone un 20% de la nota final del curso.
- Para puntuar es necesario que el env¨ªo final de cada grupo alcance un F1 superior a 0,35 (el que se obtiene con un env¨ªo que declare como positivas todas las observaciones). Por tanto, aquellos grupos con F1 inferior a 0,35 obtendr¨¢n 0 puntos.</p>
- El grupo vencedor obtendr¨¢ 100 puntos.
- El resto de grupos obtendr¨¢n una puntuaci¨®n proporcional a la diferencia entre su F1 y el F1del vencedor. Si el grupo *i* obtiene un F1 igual a *Ai* y el vencedor obtiene un F1 igual a *Ag*, entonces la puntuaci¨®n del grupo *i* viene dada por
<p>\[
100 \times \frac{A_g-A_i}{A_g - 0.35}
\]</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>El conjunto de datos</h2>
<p>
En este desaf¨ªo ten¨¦is que resolver un problema de clasificaci¨®n binaria. Os vamos a proporcionar una base de datos de *origen desconocido* que hemos transformado de varias maneras:</p>
- Hemos a?adido caracter¨ªsticas ruidosas<br>
- Hemos a?adido como caracter¨ªsticas algunas combinaciones lineales y no lineales de las caracter¨ªsticas originales y de las ruidosas<br>
- Y por si esto fuera poco, hemos perdido algunos valores de las observaciones durante las transformaciones. ?Lo sentimos!<br>

<h2>Ficheros disponibles</h2>
- <strong>train_set.csv</strong> - el conjunto de entrenamiento<br>
- <strong>test_set.csv</strong> - el conjunto de test. Ten¨¦is que enviar una etiqueta para cada fila de este fichero<br>
- <strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - un ejemplo de fichero de env¨ªo en el formato correcto<br>

<h2>Estructura de los ficheros de datos</h2>
- <strong>feat_XX</strong> - columna con una variable<br>
- <strong>target</strong> - etiqueta de la observaci¨®n de esta fila<br>",True,True
