3218,wecrec2020,"# Description

<h1> Web Enthusiasts' Club Recruitment Test 2020 </h1>
<p>This contest is being conducted as a part of the recruitment process for the Intelligence Group at Web Enthusiasts' Club NITK.</p>
<p>Participants who can improve on the baseline will be shortlisted for interviews.</p>
<hr>
<h2> Getting Started </h2>
<ul> 
<li><a href=""https://numpy.org/doc/""> Numpy Docs </a></li>
<li><a href=""https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/""> Pandas Docs </a></li>
<li><a href=""https://scikit-learn.org/""> Scikit-Learn Docs </a></li>
<li><a href=""https://matplotlib.org/contents.html""> Matplotlib Docs </a></li>
<li><a href=""https://jupyter.org/install""> Jupyter Notebook Installation </a></li>
<li><a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/random-forest-and-its-implementation-71824ced454f""> Random Forest Regressor Explanation </a></li></ul>
----------------------
# Evaluation


<h1> Metric </h1>
<p> The metric of evaluation is <b> Root Mean Squared Error. </b>RMSE is a measure of how spread out the residuals are. In other words, it tells you how concentrated the data is around the line of best fit. </p>
<p> The formula for Root Mean Squared Error : </p>
<p><b>RMSE = sqrt((1 / n)(sigma((Actual_output - Predicted_output)^2))).</b></p>
<p>RMSE is widely used in regression problems because it gives a non-negative measure of error and larger errors are more severely penalised.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Submission format</h2>
<p> The submission should have 2 fields - The ID and the PredictedValue. The Predicted output is the 'O/P' field of the training dataset. The format should be as follows : </p>
<table>
  <tbody><tr>
    <th>ID</th>
    <th>PredictedValue</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>14001</td>
    <td>355.22</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>14002</td>
    <td>424.11</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>14003</td>
    <td>476.23</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>14004</td>
    <td>288.54</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>14005</td>
    <td>322.45</td>
  </tr>
</tbody></table>
<p> ...and so forth.</p>
----------------------
# Getting Started

<p>To write and execute your code, you could either use the Kaggle kernel or do it locally.</p>
<h2>Kaggle kernel</h2>
<ol>
<li>Go to Kernels -&gt; Baseline</li>
<li>Fork the kernel</li>
<li>Write your code in the forked kernel</li>
<li>Click on 'Commit' button at the top to save your work</li>
<li>To view/download output file, click on double arrow button at the top right. Click on the 'Output' tab on the top bar. And to make a submission click on the 'Submit to contest' button.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h2> Local Kernel </h2>
<p>You could use Jupyter Notebook to write your code. To know more check out this <a href=""https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LrMOrMb8-3s"">tutorial.</a></p>
<p><b>Submission: </b>To make a submission, make sure your output file follows the correct format mentioned in Evaluation. Click on 'Submit Predictions' in the top bar of the contest's main page. Upload your file.
</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File Description : </h2>
<ul> 
<li> Train_data.csv </li>
<li> Test_data.csv </li>
<li> output.csv (Sample Submission) </li>
</ul>

<h2> Data Description : </h2>
<ul>
<li> F1 : Index </li>
<li> F2 : Datestamp </li>
<li> F3 : Categorical (4 Categories) </li>
<li> F4 : Categorical (2 Categories) </li>
<li> F5 : Categorical (12 Categories)</li>
<li> F6 : Continuous </li>
<li> F7 : Categorical (24 Categories) </li>
<li> F8 : Categorical (2 Categories) </li>
<li> F9 : Categorical (7 Categories) </li>
<li> F10 : Continuous </li>
<li> F11 : Categorical (2 Categories)  </li>
<li> F12 : Categorical (4 Categories)  </li>
<li> F13 : Continuous  </li>
<li> F14 : Continuous  </li>
<li> F15 : Continuous </li>
<li> F16 : Continuous </li>
<li> F17 : Continuous </li>
<li> O/P : Output field (Continuous vales) </li>",True,True
