3235,machine-learning-for-business-regression,"# Description

Within the context of Eleme delivery service, at every moment the command platform of ""Smart Logistics"" sends orders from customers to delivery-men for instant delivery. The decision being made by the delivery-men are mainly two-fold: pick-up in store, and delivery to customers. At one specific moment, the delivery-man may receive orders assigned to him, while he has unfinished orders which are previously assigned. He would need to make a decision as to what to do next, based on his order status and geographical location. The goal is to build a model to predict the delivery-man's next move, based on his historical decision and current status.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is MAE-Score.  The MAE score is given by:


$$MAE = \sum_{i=0}^{n}{\left|predict\_time_i - expect\_time_i\right|}$$

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every item in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain one column called ID, with a sequence from 0 to 22393. and a column of predicted expected time. The file should have a header, ""ID"" and ""expect_time"" for the two columns, and the file should have 22395 rows in total. The submission should be in CSV format</p>","# Dataset Description

### Data Overview

The data was stored in four separate folders: action, courier, distance, order. Each folder contains a dataframe, labeled by date. Here's some brief overview of some of the attributes, the rest can be explored on your own.

#### Action

The ActionStep attribute records the delivery-man's action, you may group by wave based on courier_id and wave_index. the expect_time is the timestamp when the action was completed, and can be sorted to give the courier's sequence of actions. 'courier_wave_start_lng' and 'courier_wave_start_lat' records the geographic locations. 

#### Distance

'source_type' and 'target_type' are categorical values containing three values: 'Assign', 'PickFood', and 'DeliverFood'. When type is 'Assign', the location attributes represent the courier's location; When type is 'PickFood' the location attributes represent the vendor's location; When type is 'DeliverFood' the location attributes represent the customer's location.

#### Order

| attribute     | explanation                      |

| ------------- | -------------------------------- |

| create_time   | The time the order was created   |

| confirm_time  | The time the order was confirmed |

| assigned_time | The time the order was assigned  |

| aoi_id        | Unique ID indicating an area     |

| shop_id       | Unique ID indicating a vendor    |

### Prediction

For each 'action', group by courier and wave, and check 'expect_time' attribute. The non-zero values are the actual timestamps of completed actions. Your job is to predict the timestamps of zero values in this column. Ideally, after you've predicted all timestamps, ordering by the timestamps gives you the chronological order of the actions.

The available data is from 2.1 to 2.28, and we want you to predict for 2.29",True,True
3236,machine-learning-for-business-classification,"# Description

Within the context of Eleme delivery service, at every moment the command platform of ""Smart Logistics"" sends orders from customers to delivery-men for instant delivery. The decision being made by the delivery-men are mainly two-fold: pick-up in store, and delivery to customers. At one specific moment, the delivery-man may receive orders assigned to him, while he has unfinished orders which are previously assigned. He would need to make a decision as to what to do next, based on his order status and geographical location. The goal is to build a model to predict the delivery-man's next move, based on his historical decision and current status.
----------------------
# Evaluation


<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every item in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain one column called ID, with a sequence from 0 to 22393. and a column of predicted action_type . There are two types of actions: ""DELIVERY"" and ""PICKUP"". We want you to label ""DELIVERY"" as 1 and ""PICKUP"" as 0.
The file should have a header, ""ID"" and ""action_type"" for the two columns, and the file should have 22395 rows in total. The submission should be in CSV format</p>","# Dataset Description

### Data Overview

The data was stored in four separate folders: action, courier, distance, order. Each folder contains a dataframe, labeled by date. Here's some brief overview of some of the attributes, the rest can be explored on your own.

#### Action

The ActionStep attribute records the delivery-man's action, you may group by wave based on courier_id and wave_index. the expect_time is the timestamp when the action was completed, and can be sorted to give the courier's sequence of actions. 'courier_wave_start_lng' and 'courier_wave_start_lat' records the geographic locations. 

#### Distance

'source_type' and 'target_type' are categorical values containing three values: 'Assign', 'PickFood', and 'DeliverFood'. When type is 'Assign', the location attributes represent the courier's location; When type is 'PickFood' the location attributes represent the vendor's location; When type is 'DeliverFood' the location attributes represent the customer's location.

#### Order

| attribute     | explanation                      |

| ------------- | -------------------------------- |

| create_time   | The time the order was created   |

| confirm_time  | The time the order was confirmed |

| assigned_time | The time the order was assigned  |

| aoi_id        | Unique ID indicating an area     |

| shop_id       | Unique ID indicating a vendor    |

### Prediction
Predict the actions of each row. There are two types of actions: ""DELIVERY"" and ""PICKUP"". We want you to label ""DELIVERY"" as 1 and ""PICKUP"" as 0

The available data is from 2.1 to 2.28, and we want you to predict for 2.29",True,True
3237,test-2020-08-18,"# Description


----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>",# Dataset Description,True,True
3238,mnist-for-clothes,"# Description

# Introduction
The MNIST dataset is one of the most common datasets -- maybe *the* most common -- used in classification / computer vision projects; the dataset description states that ""if it doesn't work on MNIST, it won't work at all."" The original dataset consists of 60,000 grayscale images of handwritten digits, but our dataset consists of 60,000 grayscale images of clothes. Fun!

Our task is to **design a classifier** (maybe multiple) to output the correct label (sock, shirt, ...) given an input image. The goal is to achieve the best performance with respect to a given metric, such as overall accuracy. There are many different classification algorithms out there: some to consider include logistic regression, support vector machines, decision trees / random forests, and convolutional neural networks.

# Resources
 * [more background on MNIST](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MNIST_database)
 * [intro to classification in machine learning](https://towardsdatascience.com/machine-learning-classifiers-a5cc4e1b0623)
 * [intro to CNNs](https://victorzhou.com/blog/intro-to-cnns-part-1/)

Other things to explore:
 * data augmentation
 * [add more here]
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <strong>overall accuracy</strong>. 

</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>Submission files should contain two columns: <strong>Id</strong>, corresponding to which test entry the label is, and <strong>Prediction</strong>, which is the predicted label of your classifier. That's it! </p>","# Dataset Description

#Content 
Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255. The training and test data sets have 785 columns. The first column consists of the class labels (see above), and represents the article of clothing. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image. 

 * To locate a pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27. The pixel is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix. 
 * For example, pixel 31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top. 

#Labels 
Each training and test example is assigned to one of the following labels: 

0: T-shirt/top 

1: Trouser 

2: Pullover 

3: Dress 

4: Coat 

5: Sandal 

6: Shirt 

7: Sneaker 

8: Bag 

9: Ankle boot 

#TL;DR 
 * Each row is a separate image.
 * Column 1 is the class label. 
 * Remaining columns are pixel numbers (784 total). 
 * Each value is the darkness of the pixel (1 to 255) 

#Acknowledgements 
Original dataset was downloaded from [here](https://github.com/zalandoresearch/fashion-mnist).",True,True
