3262,trialcompetitiontime,"# Description

<p> Eval </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Nothing to type</p>",True,True
3263,mapping-the-impact-of-policy-on-covid-19-outbreaks,"# Description

Several months into the COVID-19 pandemic, we can more clearly observe the diverging actions that each state in the US has made to combat the virus as well as the results that followed. The spread of the virus likely has geopolitical influences which luckily are observable through a large amount of available data. 

In this competition, we're interested in analyzing the outcomes of public policy changes, such as state lockdowns and stay-at-home orders, on the behaviors of the public and the course of the pandemic. How can we quantify, analyze, or visualize shifts in numbers of cases and deaths as local governments and their people react?  What interesting distinctions can be found on a county-by-county or state-by-state level? Leverage the data provided to produce your own tools or findings. 

This competition is hosted by the Purdue Libraries and School of Information Studies for students of Purdue University. If you have questions about the competition, please contact us directly. 

<h2>Timeline</h2>
This competition will be run from Oct. 12 through Nov. 2. For rules about timelines, please see the Rules page.

<h2>Goal</h2>
Your objective will be to use data analysis techniques of your choice as well as the provided data to make predictions about the spread of COVID-19. You should study the data provided under the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/mapping-the-impact-of-policy-on-covid-19-outbreaks/data) section. 

<h2>Submission and Evaluation</h2>
You must read the [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/c/mapping-the-impact-of-policy-on-covid-19-outbreaks/rules) section for submission information before you begin competing to get familiar with the judging format and what results are expected. 


----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The submission on Kaggle need the prediction of daily confirmed cases in the level of whole states, i.e the total confirmed cases through out US. </p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/residentmario/model-fit-metrics""> Mean Squared Error(RMSE)</a>. The MSE is calculated by the mean of the sum of the differences between predictions and the true values. The RMSE is given by:</p>
<p>\[ MSE =\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n{(y_i-\hat{y_i}})^2\]
where\[y_i\]is the observed values,\[\hat{y_i}\] is the predicted value.</p>
<p>The predicted results of yours must be converted to the format, confirmed cases per thousand people and only keep 2 digits. For example, if the result is 44527, you should keep the result as 44.53. </p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>In the dataset submission files should contain two columns: Date and #Confirmed. #Confirmed should be in the format stated above.Date is from Jul. 21 to Sept. 21. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Date,#Confirmed<br>07/21/20,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>There are six files provided. Three or them are datasets and the other three are data dictionaries. </p>
<p>All files will be able to be accessed once the competition starts.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>us-m50.csv</strong> - Daily confirmed cases.</li>
<li><strong>us-m50_README.txt</strong> - Data dictionary for us-m50.csv.</li>
<li><strong>us_covid19_daily.csv</strong> - Daily tested cases. </li>
<li><strong>us_covid19_daily_README.txt</strong> - Data dictionary for us_covid19_daily.csv.</li>
<li><strong>us_covid19_states_policies.csv</strong> - Polices pronounced by the governments.</li>
<li><strong>us_covid19_states_policies_README.txt</strong> - Data dictionary for us_covid19_states_policies.csv.</li>
</ul>",True,True
3264,pine-real-estate-ii,"# Description

<p>Pine is a Real Estate company with a long experience in the real estate market and is immersed in a digital transformation to value its historical data</p>

<p>Pine challenges us to predict the sale price of houses based on historical sales</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Evaluation Metric</h3>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error.</p>
<p>The RMSLE score is calculated:</p>

<p>\[ RMSLE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\log(p_i + 1) - \log(a_i+1))^2 }\]</p>

Where:
n: the total number of observations in the (public/private) data set
ai: the actual price for the i-th observation 
pi: your prediction of the price for the i-th observation
log: the natural logarithm

<h3>Submission Format</h3>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,price<br>1,170480.2775<br>2,320350.3892<br>3,417730.4446<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>The data correspond to thousands of houses sold on a year in a city in the United States</p>
<p>Like any real-world dataset, you could encounter noise and missing values</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file with the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong>: an unique id to a given house</li>            
<li><strong>city</strong>: an anonymous city in the United States</li>  
<li><strong>postalcode</strong>: postal code</li>          
<li><strong>coord_X</strong>: distance (meters) (X axis: positive East, negative West) from the city center</li>  
<li><strong>coord_Y</strong>: distance (meters) (Y axis: positive North, negative South) from the city center</li>        
<li><strong>dist</strong>: shortest distance (meters) from the house to the city center (direct distance or ""flying distance"")</li>       
<li><strong>bearing</strong>: angle (degrees) from North (Y axis). Vertex is city center, one ray is North and the other ray is the house. Clockwise is positive</strong>: 90o=East, -90o=West, -180o or +180o = South. </li>      
<li><strong>house_quality_index</strong>: An overall evaluation of the quality of the house: 1 to 10 (10 is highest quality)</li>
<li><strong>house_state_index</strong>: An overall evaluation of the condition of the house: very poor, poor, fair, good, very good</li>
<li><strong>age_since_construction</strong>: years since construction</li>           
<li><strong>age_since_renovation</strong>: years since renovation (if there were no renovation age_since_construction=age_since_renovation)</li>  
<li><strong>floors</strong>: number of floors</li> 
<li><strong>basement</strong>: if the house has (""Y"") or doesn't have (""N"") basement</li>           
<li><strong>bedrooms</strong>: number of bedrooms</li>      
<li><strong>baths</strong>: number of bathrooms (decimals for bathrooms with partial services)</li>   
<li><strong>living_m2</strong>: house interior living squared meters</li>    
<li><strong>lot_m2</strong>: house lot squared meters</li>     
<li><strong>living_vs_neighbors</strong>: ratio living_m2 of the house vs living_m2 of neighbourhood</li>
<li><strong>lot_vs_neighbors</strong>: ratio lot_m2 of the house vs lot_m2 of neighbourhood</li>           
<li><strong>viewsToPOI</strong>: if the house has views (""Y"") to a point of interest or not (""N"")</li>          
<li><strong>view_quality</strong>: view quality: very poor, poor, fair, good, very good</li> 
<li><strong>dow</strong>: the day of week the house was sold</li>         
<li><strong>month</strong>: the month the house was sold</li>
<li><strong>price</strong>: the price the house was sold</li>
</ul>",True,True
