3273,pasc-data-quest-warm-up,"# Description

<h1>DataQuest (Warm up) - Organized by PASC</h1>
<hr>
<h3>It's the end of the world :/</h3>
<p>The year is 2035. Climate change has destroyed the planet. A strong presence of oxygen has been discovered on a planet named Keruroid. On observing the planet through satellites, it is seen that many explosions occur here regularly with variable intensities. An expedition has been planned to explore these regions, but to ensure the safety and survivability of the expedition, we need to be able to predict the nature of these explosions. Given is the satellite data of Keruroid captured at different times of the day. <strong>Your job is to use this data to predict the intensity of explosions.</strong></p>
<p>The fate of humanity lies in your hands. And kaggle GPUs.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error"">Mean Squared Error</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Labels</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Latitude</strong> - Center of explosion pixel</li>
<li><strong>Longitude</strong> - Center of explosion pixel</li>
<li><strong>Brightness_t21</strong> -  Channel 21 brightness temperature of the explosion pixel measured in Kelvin</li>
<li><strong>Brightness_t31</strong> -  Channel 31 brightness temperature of the explosion pixel measured in Kelvin</li>
<li><strong>Scan</strong> -  Along Scan pixel size: Scan and track reflect actual pixel size.</li>
<li><strong>Track</strong> -  Along Track pixel size: Scan and track reflect actual pixel size.</li>
<li><strong>Date</strong> -  Acquisition Date</li>
<li><strong>Time</strong> -  Acquisition Time</li>
<li><strong>Region</strong> - Regionof planet being observed</li>
<li><strong>frp</strong> -  Fire Radiative Power: Depicts the pixel-integrated fire radiative power in MW (megawatts).</li>
<li><strong>Day/Night</strong> -  Indicates Daytime/ Nighttime</li>
<li><strong>Explosion Intensity</strong> -  Value of intensity on a scale of 1-100</li>
</ul>",True,True
