3287,lendo-machine-learning-workshop,"# Description

<h2> Welcome to the first Lendo Machine Learning workshop! </h2>

In this workshop we will classify breast cancer tumours using Machine Learning. You can read more about the dataset in the data tab. 

<h2> How does it work? </h2>

Everyone has the same dataset and we will try to get as accurate classifications as possible. There is a notebook called *guide* under the *Notebooks* tab above that is a great place to get started if you are not that familiar with Machine Learning or Python. Even if you want to create your own notebook from scratch, the guide notebook has some code that can be nice to copy for loading the dataset and making the submission file.

<h2> Submissions and Leaderboard </h2>

When you upload a submission you will see where you end up on the public leaderboard. There is also a private leaderboard that is only revealed at the end of the competition. It is optional to submit your solutions, only do it if you think it's fun, no pressure!

<h2> Useful links </h2>

- 8-minute introduction video to Machine Learning: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ukzFI9rgwfU
- More thorough presentation of Machine Learning concepts: https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1kSuQyW5DTnkVaZEjGYCkfOxvzCqGEFzWBy4e9Uedd9k/preview?imm_mid=0f9b7e&amp;cmp=em-data-na-na-newsltr_20171213&amp;slide=id.g168a3288f7_0_58
- Quick guide to ML lingo: https://www.databriefing.com/concepts/lingo-and-concepts/
- Getting started with Kaggle: https://elitedatascience.com/beginner-kaggle
- Book about statistical learning: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KCCF1b9R7tPr04wGK_-JCicvDI2Y5DIj/view?usp=sharing

<h2> How to get started </h2>

Check out the guide notebook and read up on the stuff that confuses you using the links above. Also write in the channel about anything that confuses you!","# Dataset Description

<h2> Dataset </h2>

Each point in this dataset holds some information about cell nuclei in breast cancer tumours. For each nucleus, the following ten measurements were taken several times:

- radius (mean of distances from center to points on the perimeter)
- texture (standard deviation of gray-scale values)
- perimeter
- area
- smoothness (local variation in radius lengths)
- compactness (perimeter^2 / area - 1.0)
- concavity (severity of concave portions of the contour)
- concave points (number of concave portions of the contour)
- symmetry
- fractal dimension (""coastline approximation"" - 1)

Each datapoint holds the mean value for each measurement, along with the standard error (SE) and the largest/worst value for the given measurement.

<h2> Do I need to read up on breast cancer for this competition? </h2>

Absolutely not! The goal is to create a Machine Learning model that will become an expert on breast cancer tumours, so that we don't need to! :-) 

<h2> Training and Testing data </h2>

The training dataset is for tuning your Machine Learning model, for each data point it has a label 1 for malignant tumour and 0 for benignant tumour. This way our model can practice classifying tumours, and learn from its mistakes.

When we are with our trained model, we go the testing data. This data does not have labels. We will classify the data points in this dataset and then upload the result as a submission file. If you are unsure of how to do this there is some code at the end of the *guide* notebook in the *Notebooks* tab.",True,True
