3298,petrol-price-forecasting,"# Description

<p>This competition is for Time Series L0 forecasting.</p>
<h2>Problem statement</h2>

<p> ONGCF is an oil and natural gas organization. It has data available for price at a weekly level. It wants to predict the price for crude oil for the next 16 months, starting from 1st Jan 2019 to April 2020. Forecast the prices based upon the best model as per your choice. Check for outliers and missing values.</p>

<p>Train.csv has training data available and sample submission.csv describes the file format to be submitted. </p>

<p>MAPE is the evaluation metric that will be used in this case to evaluate your output. You are expected to make the submission within 180 minutes of your examination. Submissions post that won't be accepted. A maximum of 20 submissions can be made. </p>

Questions:

1. Are there any outliers or missing values? If yes, treat them with appropriate measures. Describe the steps taken and why was it taken.

2. What type of time series is present? Whether it's additive or multiplicative or none? Explain.

3. Decompose the time series components without using any external package(using ground mathematical formula for this)

4. Forecast the values for time indexes given in the test.csv file and upload the output as a CSV file in the Kaggle competition platform itself.

5. Apart from MAPE, what other metrics could have been used to calculate the forecasting error? Write a function to calculate MAPE which takes in predicted and original values as an array, and returns MAPE as the final output.
 

Once done, put in all the output files and notebook(R/Python) as a zip file in the following format:

EmpoyeeID_Name_Forecasting_L0.zip and send out in mail to Anindita, Soumya, Ashmita Sengupta and Shubham
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAPE. The dscription for MAPE can be found here: <a href=""https://www.statisticshowto.com/mean-absolute-percentage-error-mape/#:~:text=The%20mean%20absolute%20percentage%20error,values%20divided%20by%20actual%20values.e""> MAPE</a></p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Date and Predicction. Date should be in correct indexed order.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p> The data contains petrol prices(Petrol (USD)) and Date column in train_data.csv. The test data has the dates for which the predictions are to be made(column names Date and Prediction), corresponding to which Prediction is blank. You are supposed to make the prediction and submit the file in that form.",True,True
3299,predict-dna-methylation,"# Description

# DNA Methylation
[DNA methylation (DMeth)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/dna-methylation) is an epigenetic modification of DNA that happens in all cells of your body. DMeth levels across the genome have been successfully used to predict all kinds of diseases from [cancers](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123808660600022) to [Alzheimer's](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5804548), your [biological age](https://genomebiology.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13059-019-1824-y) and even your [time to death!](https://www.eurekalert.org/pub_releases/2019-02/rp-dmg021219.php#:~:text=Named%20after%20the%20grim%20reaper,journal%20Aging%20publishes%20the%20findings).  

# The Problem
Unfortunately, we still don't know what causes the changes in DMeth. In a typical DMeth assay, there are two many (around 500,000) DMeth measures and we don't know which ones are important and which ones are garbage, and using all of them together in models result in overfitting or memorizing that particular data set (doing great within the sample but not that great in new datasets).

# The Solution
To avoid this issue, we take a different approach here. We engineer smarter features using DNA methylation data and subsequently use them in predicting cancer status. In particular, we are looking for genomic features that underlie DNA Methylation states at different DNA sites. We focus on normal tissues for now, as they are easier to start with.

# Prediction
The goal of this project is to predict DNA methylation state (Beta 0= unmethylated, Beta 1=methylated), at any given [CpG site](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cpg-site). DNA samples are collected from more than 400 individuals and averaged to calculated the methylation state at each CpG site. See [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/c/predict-dna-methylation/data) for data dictionary.

# Getting Started
1. We know that CpG islands are usually unmethylated ([reference](https://www.nature.com/articles/321209a0)). A simple ML model incorporating this information should achive AUC&gt;0.5. ([sample implementation](https://www.kaggle.com/mahdimoqri/cpg-island))
2. Create a continuous measure of CpG density instead a binary measure of CpG island (e.g. count number of CpGs bases around the given CpG, using Forward_Sequence (short) or seq (long) features in the data - [sample implementation](https://www.kaggle.com/mahdimoqri/cpg-density)). 
2. We know that DNA sequence surrounding a CpG influence its methylation state ([reference](https://cancerres.aacrjournals.org/content/70/4/1398)). A DL model incorporating this information should further increase the AUC (e.g. CNN).
 
----------------------
# Evaluation","# Dataset Description

# Data Dictionary
We have pre-selected around 50,000 CpG sites stable methylation states. For each site, the following attributes are provided:

* <b>CHR</b>: CpG (chromosome number)
* <b>MAOINFO</b>: CpG Location (base location on the chromosome)
* <b>UCSC_CpG_Islands_Name</b>: [CpG island](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cpg-island#:~:text=CpG%20islands%20(CGIs)%20are%20regions,40%25%20of%20mammalian%20gene%20promoters.) information
* <b>UCSC_RefGene_Group</b>: CpG position relative to genes
* <b>Relation_to_UCSC_CpG_Island</b>: CpG position relative to CpG islands 
* <b>Regulatory_Feature_Group</b>: CpG position relative to regulatory elements
* <b>Forward_Sequence</b>: DNA sequence surrounding the CpG (~120 bases)
* <b>seq</b>: DNA sequence surrounding the CpG (~2000 bases)
* <b>Beta</b>: CpG methylation state: (0= unmethylated, 1=methylated)

See the train (~30,000 observations) and test (20,000 observations) data 
Source: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE103657 (averaged)",True,True
3300,desafio-worcap-2020,"# Description

# Desafio WorCAP 2020

Este desafio foi criado com o objetivo de estimular os alunos a realizar Data Science e aplicar t¨¦cnicas de Machine Learning. No exerc¨ªcio proposto, a ideia ¨¦ que os participantes da competi??o busquem formas de melhorar a acur¨¢cia de classifica??o do conjunto de dados gerado no trabalho de [Johnson *et al.*, (2012)](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01431161.2011.629637?scroll=top&amp;needAccess=true). Nesse trabalho, os autores apresentaram acur¨¢cia de **85.9%** ?? ?? 

&gt; Ser¨¢ que voc¨º ¨¦ capaz de ultrapassar essa acur¨¢cia ?

Desta forma, para que voc¨º ven?a o desafio ¨¦ necess¨¢rio aplicar uma t¨¦cnica de classifica??o, utilizando [R](https://www.kaggle.com/oldlipe/ml-curso-de-verao-r/) ou [Python](https://www.kaggle.com/phelpsmemo/ml-curso-de-verao-python), e obter o melhor resultado da competi??o (N?o necessariamente melhor que o resultado apresentado no artigo). Feito isso, voc¨º vence! ?? 

## Sobre o conjunto de dados

Neste desafio foi selecionado para uso o conjunto de dados de Sensoriamento Remoto sobre tipos de florestas. Os dados foram publicados por [Johnson *et al.*, (2012)](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01431161.2011.629637?scroll=top&amp;needAccess=true), no artigo denominado ""Using geographically weighted variables for image classification"". Os dados foram gerados a partir de imagens de Sensoriamento Remoto das florestas de Ibaraki Prefecture, no Jap?o. As imagens utilizadas s?o apresentadas abaixo.

![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dataAt/ml-aplicado-dados-espacial/master/src/img/study_area.png)

As imagens apresentadas acima foram imageadas pelo sensor ASTER a bordo do sat¨¦lite Terra, nos per¨ªodos de Setembro de 2010, Mar?o de 2011 e Maio de 2011, respectivamente. 

## Agradecimentos

Durante a cria??o do material recebemos muitas ajudas, uma fundamental foi a do professor. Dr. Rafael Santos. Obrigado!
----------------------
# Objective

# Objetivo

Seu trabalho ¨¦ fazer a predi??o de diferentes tipos de florestas:
-  **s** - Abrevia??o de `Sugi` - Plantio de Florestas
- **h**  - Abrevia??o de `Hinok` - Plantio de Florestas
- **d**  - Abrevia??o de `Mixed deciduous`- Floresta natural mista de folhas largas 
- **o**  - Abrevia??o de `Others` -  Tipo de  dados n?o-floresta, como ruas, constru??es, etc

Cada um dos tr¨ºs tipos de floresta tem diferentes usos econ?micos e valores de conserva??o ambiental, sendo assim, um mapeamento eficiente ¨¦ importante para a conserva??o do meio ambiente.

Certifique-se que, para cada linha do conjunto de dados `test.csv`, h¨¢ um r¨®tulo que represente um desses tipos de florestas em seu arquivo de submiss?o.

## M¨¦trica: 

A pontua??o da sua submiss?o ¨¦ a porcentagem de acertos que seu modelo de classifica??o foi capaz de predizer. Isso ¨¦ conhecido como **[acur¨¢cia](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score)**:

![](https://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Ctexttt%7Bacuracia%7D%28y%2C%20%5Chat%7By%7D%29%20%3D%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn_%5Ctext%7Blinhas%7D%7D%20%5Csum_%7Bi%3D1%7D%5E%7Bn_%5Ctext%7Blinhas%7D%7D%201%28%5Chat%7By%7D_i%20%3D%20y_i%29)

Em que:

![](https://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Chat%7By_i%7D) = ¨¦ o valor predito para linha ***i***; 
![](https://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?y) = ¨¦ o valor verdadeiro;

## Submiss?o

Para realizar a submiss?o ¨¦ necess¨¢rio que envie um arquivo `.csv` com o mesmo n¨²mero de linhas que o arquivo de teste (**315 linhas**). Caso seu arquivo tenha mais ou menos linhas do que esperado, vai dar erro de submiss?o. 


<p>O arquivo deve conter um cabe?alho e ter o seguinte formato:</p>
<pre>id,label<br>1,d<br>2,o<br>3,h<br>4,s<br>etc<br></pre>
----------------------
# Tips

# Dicas

Esta se??o tem algumas dicas que podem te ajudar a come?ar a manipular os dados.

## Classifica??o

O problema proposto pelo desafio ¨¦ relacionado a classifica??o de dados. Para obter um bom resultado ¨¦ preciso verificar a importancia dos atributos dentro do conjunto de dados. ?? 

## Leitura dos arquivos de treino e teste

- Em `Python`
```python
import pandas as pd

treino = pd.read_csv(""treino.csv"")
teste = pd.read_csv(""teste.csv"")
```

- Em `R`
```R
treino <- read.csv(""train.csv"")
teste <- read.csv(""test.csv"")
```
## Escrita do arquivo de submiss?o

- Em `Python`

```python
# df ¨¦ o DataFrame com os dados
df.to_csv(""submission.csv"", index = False)
```

-  Em `R`

```R
write.csv(dado_predicao, ""submission.csv"")
```

# Links ¨²teis

- D¨²vidas na manipula??o e limpeza dos dados, consulte o material de [introdu??o ¨¤ an¨¢lise de dados](https://dataat.github.io/introducao-analise-de-dados/)

- D¨²vidas no uso dos algoritmos de Machine Learning, veja nosso exemplos: 
 - [Cluster hierarquico](https://www.kaggle.com/oldlipe/intro-ml-r-hiererquico-worcap2020)
 - [Classifica??o com ¨¢rvore de decis?o](https://www.kaggle.com/phelpsmemo/intro-ml-python-decisiontree-worcap2020)
 - [Regress?o linear](https://www.kaggle.com/lordadriano/intro-ml-python-linear-regression-worcap2020)","# Dataset Description

# Descri??o dos dados

Os dados s?o dividos em duas partes:

- `treino.csv`
- `teste.csv`

## Atributos:

- **id:** Identifica??o ¨²nica da linha (n?o use este atributo para treinar seu modelo)
- **label:**  's' ('Sugi' floresta), 'h' ('Hinoki' floresta), 'd' ('Mixed deciduous' floresta), 'o' ('Other' n?o floresta) 
- **b1:** Banda do verde corresponde ao m¨ºs de setembro de 2010
- **b2:** Banda do vermelho corresponde ao m¨ºs de setembro de 2011
- **b3:** Banda do infravermelho pr¨®ximo corresponde ao m¨ºs de setembro de 2011
- **b4:** Banda do verde corresponde ao m¨ºs de Mar?o de 2011
- **b5:** Banda do vermelho corresponde ao m¨ºs de Mar?o de 2011
- **b6:** Banda do infravermelho pr¨®ximo corresponde ao m¨ºs de Mar?o de 2011
- **b7:** Banda do verde corresponde ao m¨ºs de Maio de 2011
- **b8:** Banda do vermelho corresponde ao m¨ºs de Maio de 2011
- **b9:** Banda do infravermelho pr¨®ximo corresponde ao m¨ºs de Maio de 2011
- **pred_minus_obs_S_b1 at¨¦ pred_minus_obs_S_b9**: Valores espectrais previstos (Com base na interpola??o espacial) subtraido dos valores  espectrais reais para a classe 's'
- **pred_minus_obs_S_b1 at¨¦ pred_minus_obs_H_b9**: Valores espectrais previstos (Com base na interpola??o espacial) subtraido dos valores  espectrais reais para a classe 'h'

## O que fazer com os dados?

O conjunto de treinamento `treino.csv` deve ser usado para construir os modelos de aprendizagem de m¨¢quina. Para o conjunto de treinamento, fornecemos o resultado (tamb¨¦m conhecido como a ""verdade em terra"") para cada classe. O seu modelo dever¨¢ fazer a identifica??o de tipos de ¨¢rvores.

O conjunto `teste.csv` deve ser usado para verificar a capacidade de generaliza??o do modelo criado. Com base nos resultados obtidos atrav¨¦s deste modelo, voc¨º dever¨¢ submeter seus resultados.
- Fique atento ao formato de envio. O documento deve conter as colunas `Id` e `label`.",True,True
3301,fake-data-hackathonv2,"# Description

# Fake Data Hackathon

Fake data is every where, but instead of resiting it we will embrace it, use it to our own advantage.
You can use it however you feel like. Only restriction is you use it.


### Problem Statement : 
L&T Finance provides vehicles loans to its customers . However they do want to find out which customers are trustworthy and will not default. You are asked to create a customer default model (binary classification) to predict which customer will default or not.
The dataset and competition details can be found [in this Kaggle competition](https://www.kaggle.com/c/fake-data-hackathonv2/overview)

### Data : 
You have the following datasets:
train.csv - Training data
test.csv - Test data
data_dictionary - Dictionary containing the explanations of various data columns. 

As this is fake data challenge, you are required to use fake/generated data in the model creation process. No feature generation does not count as fake/generated data !

You can use the [CTGAN library](https://github.com/sdv-dev/CTGAN) or any other library of your choice for generating fake tabular data. You should use at-least X rows of fake data while training your models.

Some ideas for including fake data include : 

1. Augment your training data with generated data.

2. Create stacked classifier with fake/ generated data.

3. Use generated data in your test set as [mentioned in this presentation](https://gitlab.com/ing_rpaa/hackathons/fake-data-hackathon/-/blob/master/resources/model_uncertainty_slides.pdf)

4. [Data augmentation using GAN to improve Risk Models] (https://confengine.com/odsc-india-2019/proposal/11560/data-augmentation-using-gan-to-improve-risk-models-for-new-credit-card-customers) by American express. They also [published a paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.02271.pdf) about it.

5. [Improve class imbalance using GAN](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334409523_Generative_adversarial_fusion_network_for_class_imbalance_credit_scoring)

Let your creative juices flow.

### Environment

The datasets are small enough to use on your laptops, however if you want you can use the RPAA GCP environment as well. 

**Please shut-down the instance after use to avoid high costs**

### Evaluation Metric : 
Log loss will be used as the evaluation metric . You can read more about it [here](http://wiki.fast.ai/index.php/Log_Loss)

### Rules : 

- You need to use atleast 3000 rows of generated data in your model training process.
- The winner will not only be the one with the best score, but also the one with the most innovative approach to the problem. This will be decided during the presentation.
- 20 submissions max.
- 2-3 people max per team
- deadline 11th Sept 2020, 16:00 CEST

### Submission : 
You need to create a `submission.csv` file with the `uniqueid`'s from `test.csv`, and add a column `loan_default` which as the probabilities.
Here is the [sample notebook](https://gitlab.com/ing_rpaa/hackathons/fake-data-hackathon/notebooks/) to create baseline model and submit the results.

## Heroes

> Heroes: Dan, Tingting, Anil","# Dataset Description

train - Training data

test - Test data

data_dictionary.csv - Explaination of data.

The target column is loan_default.",True,True
