3303,shared-bikes-demand-prediction,"# Description

<h1>Folk Developers Community presents DataScience Hackathon</h1>
<h2>Problem Statement:</h2>
<p>A US bike-sharing provider `XBikes` has a daily dataset on the rental bikes based on various environmental and seasonal settings. It wishes to use this data to understand the factors affecting the demand for these shared bikes in the American market and come up with a mindful business plan to be able to accelerate its revenue as soon as the ongoing lockdown due to corona pandemic comes to an end. <br><br>

Essentially, the company wants to know ¡ª<br>

- Which variables are significant in predicting the demand for shared bikes.<br>


- How well those variables describe the bike demands<br>
- Predict the count of Bikes used.<br>

<br>


Essentially, the company wants to know ¡ª<br>

Which variables are significant in predicting the demand for shared bikes.
How well those variables describe the bike demands <br>
<b>So interpretation is important!</b> <br><br>

<b>Winners will be decided based on the analysis done in the Jupyter notebook and R2 score, So please submit your notebooks</b>

</p>
<br>
<p>
<b>Acknowledgements</b><br>
we are hosting this competition for the machine learning community to use for fun and practice. This dataset was provided by Hadi Fanaee Tork using data from Capital Bikeshare. We also thank the UCI machine learning repository for hosting the dataset. If you use the problem in publication, please cite:

Fanaee-T, Hadi, and Gama, Joao, Event labeling combining ensemble detectors and background knowledge, Progress in Artificial Intelligence (2013): pp. 1-15, Springer Berlin Heidelberg.
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is R2 </p><p> </p>","# Dataset Description

<p>day.csv have the following fields:<p>
	
	<li>instant: record index</li>
	<li> dteday : date</li>
	<li>season : season (1:spring, 2:summer, 3:fall, 4:winter)</li>
	<li> yr : year (0: 2018, 1:2019)</li>
	<li>mnth : month ( 1 to 12)</li>
	<li>holiday : weather day is a holiday or not (extracted from http://dchr.dc.gov/page/holiday-schedule)</li>
	<li>weekday : day of the week</li>
	<li>workingday : if day is neither weekend nor holiday is 1, otherwise is 0.</li>
	<li>weathersit : 
                <ol>
		<li>1: Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy</li>
		<li>2: Mist + Cloudy, Mist + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist</li>
		<li>3: Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds</li>
		<li>4: Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog</li>
               </ol>
       </li>
	<li>temp : temperature in Celsius</li>
	<li>atemp: feeling temperature in Celsius</li>
	<li>hum: humidity</li>
	<li>windspeed: wind speed</li>
	<li>casual: count of casual users</li>
	<li>registered: count of registered users</li>
	<li> cnt: count of total rental bikes including both casual and registered</li>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>",True,True
3304,cs5242,"# Description

<p>Your task is to build a classification model to label lung CT images and decide whether there is any kind of disease shown in the image.  </p>


The dataset is generated from several medical lung CT datasets with simple modifications for ease of use.<p>
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use accuracy for evaluation. Accuracy = R / N, where R is the number of test instances with correct label and N is the total number of test instances.","# Dataset Description

This is the file for training and testing.

You may find all images zipped file train_image and use them for your model training with train_label.csv.

After you finish model training, you may run classification on the test images and submit your classification result in a .csv file with the instance ID and instance label.",True,True
3305,pubg,"# Description

Battle Royale-style video games have taken the world by storm. 100 players are dropped onto an island empty-handed and must explore, scavenge, and eliminate other players until only one is left standing, all while the play zone continues to shrink.

PlayerUnknown's BattleGrounds (PUBG) has enjoyed massive popularity. With over 50 million copies sold, it's the fifth best selling game of all time, and has millions of active monthly players. 

We challenge the IIITB students to predict final placement from final in-game stats and initial player ratings. You are given over 65,000 games' worth of anonymized player data, split into training and testing sets.

What's the best strategy to win in PUBG? Should you sit in one spot and hide your way into victory, or do you need to be the top shot? Let's let the data do the talking!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is used as evaluation metric for this challenge. 

#Submission File
For each Id in the test set, you must predict their placement as a percentage (0 for last, 1 for first place) for the winPlacePerc variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:  

    Id,winPlacePerc
    47734,0
    47735,0.5
    47736,0
    47737,1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

<p>In a PUBG game, up to 100 players start in each match (matchId). Players can be on teams (groupId) which get ranked at the end of the game (winPlacePerc) based on how many other teams are still alive when they are eliminated. In game, players can pick up different munitions, revive downed-but-not-out (knocked) teammates, drive vehicles, swim, run, shoot, and experience all of the consequences -- such as falling too far or running themselves over and eliminating themselves.
<br><br>
You are provided with a large number of anonymized PUBG game stats, formatted so that each row contains one player's post-game stats. The data comes from matches of all types: solos, duos, squads, and custom; there is no guarantee of there being 100 players per match, nor at most 4 player per group.
<br><br>
You must create a model which predicts players' finishing placement based on their final stats, on a scale from 1 (first place) to 0 (last place). </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<p>For more details on the dataset, please refer to the original <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/pubg-finish-placement-prediction/data"">PUBG Finish Placement Prediction</a> challenge.</p>",True,True
