3307,imc2021la1,"# Description

# Source of data

The data set is an altered part of another database. It will be used specifically for this competition.

The database has fourteen independent variables and one dependent variable which is the one to be predicted.

The dependent variable is real and therefore we are facing a **regression problem**.
In the training and generalization set there are 3,500 and 3,500 patterns, respectively. A subdivision can be made on the training set to create a validation set (highly recommended strategy). Both sets have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model.

Finally, a set called *testkaggle* is provided which only contains the data of the fourteen independent variables (input).

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest accuracy (lowest MSE) the dependent variable (output) from the *testkaggle* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate.

#Participants

In this competition, only students of IMC subject can participate. To participate, students must use an identifier of the type ""`IMC2021{UCOuser}`"". For those who already have a user account on kaggle they will have the options of: changing their Username and Display Name fields in their user profile, or creating a new account.

We recommend the option of creating a new account to avoid confusion with name changes.

#Rewards

Guided by the maxim ""*the important thing is to participate*"", we, the teachers and collaborators of the subject, would like the majority of students enrolled in the course to be involved.

Therefore, the qualification of the practice will be increased by 0.5 points for all those people (or teams, in this case the points would be distributed as indicated by the members) who are among the 75% of the best private scores of the *LeaderBoard* at the end of the competition.

Three competitions will be held throughout the course. Those who obtain the best scores in the private *LeaderBoard* of each competition will obtain an extra score in the final grade of the practical part of the course according to the following list:
- First place in the final ranking: 1.5 extra points in the practical part of the course.
- Second place in the final ranking: 1.0 extra points in the practical part of the subject.
- Third place in the final ranking: 0.75 extra points in the practical part of the course.

The final ranking is constructed as follows: points will be awarded according to the position obtained in each competition and according to the number of participants in that competition. So, in a competition where 20 people have participated, the person who has occupied the first position in the private ranking will get 20 points, the second person 19 points, the n person will get 20-n+1 points.

The sum of the points from the competitions held during the four-month period will be the way to rank the participants in the final ranking.

# Acknowledgements

All thanks go to the members of the AYRNA research group for supporting and making this type of project possible.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition will be the [MSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error). The MSE metric is commonly used in many regression applications.

#Format of the file to be uploaded
The file that is uploaded to the platform can only have two columns: 'ID' and 'Category'.
- The 'ID' is a column with the values {0, 1, 2, ..., 2999}.
- As for `Predicted`, it is a column with the predictions obtained, in real format.

The length of the ID column and the Predicted column must be the same. Therefore, and in summary, the file must contain the following format:

```
Id,Predicted
0,0.249267
1,0.0648722
2,0.0657131
3,0.00304894
4,0.00260159
5,0.00386107
6,0.000461663
7,0.0848175
8,0.0298513
9,0.560343
10,0.00931308
```

Finally, the name of the delivery you make, must follow the following pattern: `.csv'.","# Dataset Description

# Source of data

The data set is an altered part of another database. It will be used specifically for this competition.

The database has fourteen independent variables and one dependent variable which is the one to be predicted.

The dependent variable is real and therefore we are facing a **regression problem**.
In the training and generalization set there are 3,500 and 3,500 patterns, respectively. A subdivision can be made on the training set to create a validation set (highly recommended strategy). Both sets have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model.

Finally, a set called *testkaggle* is provided which only contains the data of the fourteen independent variables (input).

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest accuracy (lowest MSE) the dependent variable (output) from the *testkaggle* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate.",True,True
