3332,codeop-predicting-home-credit-default-risk,"# Description

This kaggle-in-class is a simpler replica of [this](https://www.kaggle.com/c/home-credit-default-risk) other competition. It only uses one of the sources of data, the one present on the 'application' csv file. The data contains a bunch of information from conceded loans. The target 'default' is a binary variable that takes value 1 if the person was not able to repay the loan and 0 otherwise. For more detailed information, you can check their website.

This particular competition will be evaluated computing the [accuracy score](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy) 

The objective of this competition is to get familiar with kaggle competitions, the end-to-end flow of machine learning projects and apply the content we see in class while we see its impact on a real dataset.

Up until now we've already seen the basics of supervised learning and classification problems, so you should be ready to already apply those.

Have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition will be evaluated computing the AUC score - same as the original one. You can compute it using sklearn's function:
https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html

You can take a look at the samplesubmission.csv file to check how the submission should look like. It should have a column with SKID_CURR (ID) and default (target).","# Dataset Description

This competition has two main data files: - train.csv: this file contains all the data where the target is present. - test.csv: this file contains only the feature values that you will need to generate predictions and create a submission file. I've also added two auxiliary files: - sample_submission.csv: this file contains an example of how a submission should look like for Kaggle to take it. - HomeCredit_columns_description.csv: this file contains information on the columns of the train and test csv files.",True,True
3333,edld-654-fall-2020,"# Description

# Statewide testing data
Welcome to the homepage for our class competition. I've uploaded a data dictionary to go along with the data itself, but please don't hesitate to get in contact with Joe or I if you have questions.

As a basic overview, students in every state across the nation are tested annually in reading and math in grades 3-8.  These specific data are simulated from an actual statewide testing administration across the state of Oregon, but the overall distributions are highly similar. The school IDs are real, and you should be able to use these IDs (specifically the `ncessch`) to link the data with other sources (for example, see the [National Center for Educational Statistics](https://nces.ed.gov/ccd/)). Because you have the longitude and latitude, you could also consider linking the data with census data to obtain information on, for example, the economic conditions of the surrounding area around the school (you would have to do this with a spatial join, most likely).
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Root Mean Square Error

This is pretty straightforward. We are in a regression setting and we're using one of the most basic evaluation metrics, the root mean square error.

$$
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \Sigma(y\_i -\hat{ y\_i})^2 }
$$","# Dataset Description

Please see the data dictionary for specific column definitions, and the overview tab for an overall overview of the data.",True,True
3334,codeboss-cat2-bitcoin-test,"# Description

This is a Kaggle competition set up for Question 1 of CodeBoss 2020. A maximum number of 10 submissions are allowed for each team.

**The Challenge**

Given Bitcoin transaction graph data, predict which ransomware family a Bitcoin address belongs to.

**The Story**

Ransomware attacks encrypts sensitive information to exchange for a ransom. Attacks have been shown by studies to have caused significant economic losses and the usage of cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin have made it easier for ransomware developers to attack.

Bitcoin transaction data are permanently recorded and publicly available. However, current methods to detect ransomware relies only on a couple of heuristics. The idea of exploring data modelling techniques is not widespread as of now.

This challenge proposes to use data modelling techniques for a data driven method to detect new Bitcoin address from a ransomware family.

**What Data Will I Use in This Competition?**

In this competition, you will have access to two similar datasets that include attributes and labels. One dataset is titled `train.csv` and the other is titled `test.csv`. The datasets are anonymized.
Each of these train and test have the same features, and only the train.csv file will include the correct labels, being the ransomware family.

You will have to model the train data, to the best of your ability and predict the corresponding labels in the test data. 

Check out the ¡°Data¡± tab to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you have created a competitive model, submit it to see where your model stands on our leader board against other contestants.

**Tips**

While the score your model achieves is the most important factor in determining the Kaggle ranking, if two models achieves the same results, the earlier submission will rank higher!
A tip from us is to do a quick baseline and submit periodically after improvements to the model. Use your 10 submissions well!

----------------------
# Evaluation

**Goal**
It is your job to predict which ransomware family the Bitcoin address belongs to.
For each row in the test set, you must predict a class.

**Evaluation**

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score""> Macro F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score of a target class is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other. The Macro F1-score is calculated by averaging the F1-Score of every class</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
You should submit a csv file with the same number of rows as Test.csv plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have a different number of columns or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>address,label<br>17ujHj7k5sVXcL1JUd9WUv8nVfwuR1C4n1 ,0<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

**Data Fields**

<p>Address ¨C The Bitcoin address </p>
<p>Feature 1 ¨C 6  - Different measurements of the Bitcoin transaction graph. They have been anonymized.</p>
Label ¨C The class of ransomware family. There are 8 classes in total, so the label ¡Ê [0,7].

<p>The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the correct outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) which is the ransomware family class. Your model will be based on the features in the given training dataset. </p>
<p>The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each row in the test set, use the model you trained to predict the ransomware family the Bitcoin address is from.</p>
The sample_submission file is provided to the contestants as an example submission to see how the submission works in Kaggle.",True,True
