3356,sysu-dmml-1,"# Description

<p></p>The competition data was extracted from an online dataset about Danish craft beer breweries. A critical challenge faced by most breweries is to match their supply with the market demand, while both supply and demand are volatile. In terms of operations, this challenge translates to the requirement on responsive supply chain and inventory strategies that can react fast enough to the market change. When the supply fails to match the demand, unsatisfied demand will be back-ordered. However, the costs associated with backorders can be difficult to evaluate since they would include lost sales, annoyed or disappointed customers, and additional paperwork, etc. Therefore, it is the breweries¡¯ interest to avoid backorder as much as possible. A good predictive model that can raise a timely warning for products that are likely to stock out in the future can help the breweries be more intelligent on stocking and become more proactive. Your task is to apply your knowledge learned in this course to develop a predictive model that can accurately predict future stock-out events. For this competition, we will use the accuracy to rank the quality of predictions. <p></p>

<p></p>**Note**: Each student should complete this assignment independently. Please edit your team name as ""student id-student name"". All algorithms, tools and packages you obtained from Internet can be used.<p></p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

For this competition, we will use the Accuracy to rank the quality of predictions.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>The dataset is historical data, which contains information of an anonymous company¡¯s products for the 8 weeks prior to the week that is going to be predicted. <h2>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong> sku </strong> - Stock keeping unit, a unique product identifier
</li>
<li><strong> national_inv</strong> -  Current inventory level for the product</li>

<li><strong> lead_time</strong> -  Transit time for the product</li>

<li><strong> in_transit_qty</strong> -  Amount of the product in transit from source</li>

<li><strong> forecast_3_month</strong> -  Forecast sales for the next 3 months</li>

<li><strong> forecast_6_month</strong> -  Forecast sales for the next 6 months</li>

<li><strong> forecast_9_month</strong> -  Forecast sales for the next 9 months</li>

<li><strong> sales_1_month</strong> -  Sales quantity for the prior 1 month</li>

<li><strong> sales_3_month</strong> -  Sales quantity for the prior 3 months</li>

<li><strong> sales_6_month</strong> -  Sales quantity for the prior 6 months </li>

<li><strong> sales_9_month</strong> -  Sales quantity for the prior 9 months</li>

<li><strong> min_bank</strong> -  Minimum recommend amount to stock </li>
<li><strong> potential_issue</strong> -  Source issue for part identified; 1 indicates that some issues are identified, and 0 indicates no issue</li>

<li><strong> pieces_past_due</strong> -  Amount overdue from source</li>

<li><strong> perf_6_month_avg</strong> -  Source performance for the prior 6 months </li>

<li><strong> perf_12_month_avg</strong> -  Source performance for the prior 12 months</li>

<li><strong> local_bo_qty</strong> -  Amount of stock orders overdue</li>

<li><strong> deck_risk</strong> -  Part risk flag; 1 indicates risk identified, and 0 indicates no risk</li>

<li><strong> oe_constraint</strong> -  Part risk flag; 1 indicates risk identified, and 0 indicates no risk</li>

<li><strong> ppap_risk</strong> -  Part risk flag; 1 indicates risk identified, and 0 indicates no risk</li>

<li><strong> stop_auto_buy</strong> -  Part risk flag; 1 indicates risk identified, and 0 indicates no risk</li>

<li><strong> rev_stop</strong> -  Part risk flag; 1 indicates risk identified, and 0 indicates no risk</li>

<li><strong> went_on_back_order</strong> -  Product actually went on backorder. This is the target value. </li>



</ul>",True,True
3357,gis-iii-machine-learning-lab-2020,"# Description

Car sharing service allows car renting and returning via a network of stations located throughout the city. The data generated by these systems is explicitly recorded and could be used for analysis. Car sharing systems, therefore, function as a sensor network, which can be used for studying mobility in a city. Now, we have collected the historical usage patterns and decided to use it for forecasting car rental demand for building a new station in the city.
<hr>

###Acknowledgements
The dataset used in this lab was provided by Gad Benram using the data from AutoTel car sharing (https://www.kaggle.com/gidutz/autotel-shared-car-locations).

<div>Icons made by <a href=""https://www.flaticon.com/authors/surang"" title=""surang"">surang</a> from <a href=""https://www.flaticon.com/"" title=""Flaticon"">www.flaticon.com</a></div>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
Submissions are evaluated one the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). You could find your submission scores in **public leaderboard** (calculated with 40% of the test data) and **private leaderboard** (calculated with 60% of the test data after the deadline of this competition).

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Your submission file must have a header and should be structured in the following format:</p>
<pre> Index,daily_car_demand<br> 0,0<br> 1,0<br> 2,0<br> ... <br> ... </pre>","# Dataset Description

<h3>Data Sources </h3>
The location of the parked cars was extracted from the AutoTel website, every two minutes for several weeks. The raw data was saved in CSV format. In this lab, there are 4 csv files provided: 
</br></br>
- <b>car_location_training.csv</b> : Contains the training data that you need. 
</br>
-  <b>car_location_testing_daily_withoutLable.csv </b> : Contains the data that you need to predict. It has more column then the training set as we have aggrated the data as daily records and provided the number of parking spots in the new car station. The prediction results need to be submitted for the in-class competition.  
</br>
- <b>tel_aviv_neighborhood.csv</b> : Contains the data that you need for visualization.
</br>
-  <b>sample_submission.csv </b>: A sample of what you should upload for the competition. The sample submission file contains the results from a dummy model.",True,True
3358,iiitb-ga-customer-revenue-prediction,"# Description

The 80/20 rule has proven true for many businesses¨Conly a small percentage of customers produce most of the revenue. As such, marketing teams are challenged to make appropriate investments in promotional strategies.

In this competition, you¡¯re challenged to analyze a Google Merchandise Store (also known as GStore, where Google swag is sold) customer dataset to predict revenue per customer. Hopefully, the outcome will be more actionable operational changes and a better use of marketing budgets for those companies who choose to use data analysis on top of GA data.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)

Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as the following
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1942116%2F7c87cecdff8d00fdecaba22b0cc01068%2FScreenshot%202020-09-21%20at%2012.34.49%20PM.png?generation=1600671910935817&alt=media)


where y hat is the natural log of the predicted revenue for a customer and y is the natural log of the actual summed revenue value plus one.

## Submission File

For each fullVisitorId in the test set, you must predict the natural log of their total revenue in PredictedLogRevenue. The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:  

    fullVisitorId,PredictedLogRevenue
    0000000259678714014,0
    0000049363351866189,0
    0000053049821714864,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## What should I expect the data format to be?

Both ga_trainV2.csv and ga_testV2.csv contain the columns listed under Data Fields. Each row in the dataset is one visit to the store. Because we are predicting the log of the total revenue per user, be aware that not all rows in ga_testV2.csv will correspond to a row in the submission, but all unique `fullVisitorIds` will correspond to a row in the submission.

**IMPORTANT** : Due to the formatting of fullVisitorId you must load the Id's as strings in order for all Id's to be properly unique! There are multiple columns which contain JSON blobs of varying depth. In one of those JSON columns, totals, the sub-column transactionRevenue contains the revenue information we are trying to predict. This sub-column exists only for the training data.

## What am I predicting?

We are predicting the natural log of the sum of all transactions per user. For every user in the test set, the target is:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1942116%2F7602b575f66450a8957034a9ef731414%2FScreenshot%202020-09-21%20at%204.24.38%20PM.png?generation=1600685709042594&alt=media)

**NOTE** - The transactions data is bundled inside the JSON columns, you need to open them. 

## How are the Public & Private Leaderboards calculated?
The public and private leaderboards are calculated through partitioned solutions of the test data, with the public leaderboard being calculated using 30% of the solution set and the private leaderboard (final leaderboard) is through 70% of the solution set. 

## Data
* **ga_trainV2.csv** - the updated training set
* **ga_testV2.csv** - the updated test set
* **sample_submissionV2.csv** - a updated sample submission file in the correct format. Your submission's PredictedLogRevenue column should make forward-looking predictions for each of these fullVisitorIds for the entire timeframe. Review ""What am I predicting?"" above for details.
**NOTE** - Uploading this sample submission file will throw an error due to mismatch in the number of rows. Use this sample submission file to note the column names and formatting ONLY. 

## Columns
* **fullVisitorId** - A unique identifier for each user of the Google Merchandise Store.
* **channelGrouping** - The channel via which the user came to the Store.
* **date** - The date on which the user visited the Store.
* **device** - The specifications for the device used to access the Store.
* **geoNetwork** - This section contains information about the geography of the user.
* **socialEngagementType** - Engagement type, either ""Socially Engaged"" or ""Not Socially Engaged"".
* **totals** - This section contains aggregate values across the session.
* **trafficSource** - This section contains information about the Traffic Source from which the session originated.
* **visitId** - An identifier for this session. This is part of the value usually stored as the _utmb cookie. This is only unique to the user. For a completely unique ID, you should use a combination of fullVisitorId and visitId.
* **visitNumber** - The session number for this user. If this is the first session, then this is set to 1.
* **visitStartTime** - The timestamp (expressed as POSIX time).
* **hits** - This row and nested fields are populated for any and all types of hits. Provides a record of all page visits.
* **customDimensions** - This section contains any user-level or session-level custom dimensions that are set for a session. This is a repeated field and has an entry for each dimension that is set.
* **totals** - This set of columns mostly includes high-level aggregate data",True,True
