<h1>Welcome to the TWIMLfest AI for Good Hackathon!</h1>
<h2>Contribute to Modern Slavery Research</h2>
<p>In 2018, the Global Slavery Index found that there were 40.3 M people in modern slavery, of whom 25M were in forced labor producing computers, clothing, agricultural products, raw materials, etc and 15M were in forced marriage. In order to facilitate measures to end modern slavery the United Nations asks companies to take immediate measures and state clear policies. The Future Society is an independent nonprofit think-and-do tank curating an up-to-date repository of >16K of those statements. Use your NLP-Skills predict whether those statements meet the required standards!</p>

<p>The data for this competition consists of 981 of those statements from different companies with an expert-reviewed annotation. This annotation is used to determine whether the company is providing training to their employees and supply chain workers regarding addressing modern slavery and human trafficking. Training can be provided to
<ul>
  <li>Employees (all)</li>
  <li>Procurement / purchasing</li>
  <li>Recruitment / HR</li>
  <li>Leadership</li>
  <li>Suppliers</li>
  <li>or it might not be mentioned who exactly</li>
</ul>

You can use any NLP-Method on the text of those statements and try to predict if the training is sufficiently addressed or not.</p>

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
<p>From the 981 data points of the data set 288 are used for evaluating your models. These 288 statements are evenly split into a public and a private leaderboard. For this binary classification problem we're evaluating with binary cross entropy/ logloss:</p>

<p>
\[
\text{score} = - \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \left[ y_{i} \log(\hat{y}_{i}) + (1 - y_{i}) \log(1 - \hat{y}_{i})\right]
\]
</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Your task is to provide <b>probability estimates (NOT final classifications)</b> for the test set whether they classify as ""Training Provided"" or not. All statements in the test set have a unique ID. The predictions on the statements of the test set should be submitted in the following format as a comma separated csv file:
<pre>
ID, LABEL_MSA_training_binary
1, 0.0
2, 0.0
3, 0.0
...
</pre>

<h2>Timeline</h2>
<p>The hackathon starts on Wednesday, October 14th after the introduction session and we will finish on Wednesday, October 28th at 4:30 PM CEST (Register <a href=""https://twimlai.com/twimlfest/sessions/ai-for-good-hackathon-end-modern-slavery/"" target=""_blank"">here</a>). During TWIMLfest we will have a weekly checking, you can find the dates <a href=""https://twimlai.com/twimlfest/sessions/ai-for-good-hackathon-end-modern-slavery/"" target=""_blank"">here</a>.
</p>

<h2>Prizes and Karma</h2>
This Hackathon is intended to help the global research community improve their work on screening Modern Slavery Statements to hold global corporations accountable. As a small incentive there will be the following prizes:
<ul>
  <li>1. Place: $250</li>
  <li>2. Place: $100</li>
  <li>3. Place: $50</li>
</ul>
<p>The Prizes can be taken as credit for your favourite cloud provider or as a credit for TWIML swag. </p>
<p>You are not allowed to use external data sources for this competition than what is provided through this page. Your solution should also be able to scale over tens of thousands of modern slavery statements so manually reading and classifying the statements does not qualify here. We ask you to be fair and not cheat. Cheating at an AI for Good Hackathon probably brings some pretty bad karma :-) As a winner you should be prepared to showcase your approach, share your code so it can be reproduced!</p>","# Dataset Description

There are two files that you need:

- **train.csv** - containing the 650 examples of training data
- **test.csv** - containing the 331 examples of competition data, with the LABELs that you need to predict.

The sample_submission.csv is an example of correctly formatted submission file.

The training data contains the extracted texts from companies' modern slavery statements. These statements are originally published in PDF (both scanned and digital) and HTML formats. A variety of methods has been used to get the raw text including OCR and HTML scraping techniques which is why sometimes you might see some noise or misaligned titles.

The target metric LABEL_MSA_training_binary essentially answers the question: Does the company provide training specifically to address modern slavery and human trafficking? 

This training can take place as a standalone course on modern slavery, or be embedded in a wider training programme on supply chains or human rights. It can cover many different groups- procurement specialists, and those who work in recruitment or in leadership positions.

Training can be provided to:
- Employees (all)
- Procurement / purchasing
- Recruitment / HR
- Leadership
- Suppliers

The train.csv includes the ground truth 1/0 values for each statement and your task is to estimate the probability for all the documents in the test set whether they contain information about the provision of training.

It's possible that the statement mentions the provision of training but:
a) it's not specified to whom or about what, or;
b) some training methodology is only being created, that is, it's ""in development""
in those instances the target value is 0 because companies can provide any types of training while we care about specifically modern slavery related training programs. Additionally, training programs that are in development are not creating any impactful change in the world.",True,True
3383,rock-paper-scissors,"# Description

Rock, Paper, Scissors (sometimes called roshambo) has been a staple to settle playground disagreements or determine who gets to ride in the front seat on a road trip. The game is simple, with a balance of power. There are three options to choose from, each winning or losing to the other two. In a series of truly random games, each player would win, lose, and draw roughly one-third of games. But people are not truly random, which provides a fun opportunity for AI.

Studies have shown that a Rock, Paper, Scissors AI can consistently beat human opponents. With previous games as input, it studies patterns to understand a player¡¯s tendencies. But what happens when we expand the simple ¡°Best-of-3¡± game to be ¡°Best-of-1000¡±? How well can artificial intelligence perform?

In this simulation competition, you will create an AI to play against others in many rounds of this classic game. Can you find patterns to make yours win more often than it loses? It¡¯s possible to greatly outperform a random player when the matches involve non-random agents. A strong AI can consistently beat predictable AI.

This problem is fundamental to the fields of machine learning, artificial intelligence, and data compression. There are even potential applications in human psychology and hierarchical temporal memory. Warm up your hands and get ready to Rock, Paper, Scissors in this challenge.

Image acknowledgements:
Photos from The Noun Project: [Rock](https://thenounproject.com/term/rock/899808/), [Paper](https://thenounproject.com/term/paper/661463/), [Scissors](https://thenounproject.com/term/scissors/3582625/)





----------------------
# Evaluation

Each day, your team is able to submit up to 5 agents (bots) to the competition. Each submission will play episodes (games) against other bots on the ladder that have a similar skill rating. Over time, skill ratings will go up with wins or down with losses. Every bot submitted will continue to play games until the end of the competition. On the leaderboard, only your best scoring bot will be shown, but you can track the progress of all of your submissions on your Submissions page.

Each Submission has an estimated Skill Rating which is modeled by a Gaussian N(¦Ì,¦Ò2) where ¦Ì is the estimated skill and ¦Ò represents our uncertainty of that estimate which will decrease over time.

When you upload a Submission, we first play a Validation Episode where that Submission plays against copies of itself to make sure it works properly. If the Episode fails, the Submission is marked as Error. Otherwise, we initialize the Submission with ¦Ì0=600 and it joins the pool of All Submissions for ongoing evaluation.

We repeatedly run Episodes from the pool of All Submissions and try to pick Submissions with similar ratings for fair matches. We aim to run ~8 Episodes a day per Submission, with an additional slight rate increase for the newest-submitted Episodes to give you feedback faster.

After an Episode finishes, we'll update the Rating estimate for all Submissions in that Episode. If one Submission won, we'll increase its ¦Ì and decrease its opponent's ¦Ì -- if the result was a draw, then we'll move the two ¦Ì values closer towards their mean. The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous ¦Ì values and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty ¦Ò. We also reduce the ¦Ò terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result. The score by which your bot wins or loses an Episode does not affect the skill rating updates.

At the submission deadline, additional submissions will be locked. One additional week will be allotted to continue to run episodes. At the conclusion of this week, the leaderboard is final.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **February 1, 2021**&nbsp;- Entry Deadline, Team Merger Deadline, Final Submission Deadline

* **February 2, 2021 - February 8, 2021**&nbsp;- Final episodes are played

* **February 8, 2021**&nbsp;- Winners announced

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Environment Rules

## Episode Objective

Use your Agent to throw a symbol to defeat your opponent's chosen symbol. Rock defeats Scissors. Scissors defeats Paper. Paper defeats Rock. Best of 1000 rounds wins the match.

## How To Play

Player's will choose their symbol simultaneously without knowledge of the other player's choice. You may instruct your agent to select `rock`, `paper`, or `scissors`. 

- Rock defeats Scissors. 
- Scissors defeats Paper. 
- Paper defeats Rock.
- If two identical symbols are selected, the round results in a draw.

This episode continues for 1000 rounds.

## Writing Agents

An Agent will receive an observation containing the Id (see table below) of the opponent's last symbol (None on first step).

| Id | Symbol |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | Rock |
| 1 | Paper |
| 2 | Scissors |

An Agent should return the Id of the symbol they wish to use on the current step.

Here¡¯s what that looks like as code: 

```
def copy_opponent_agent(observation, configuration):
    if observation.step > 0:
        return observation.lastOpponentAction
    else:
        return 0
```

## Agent Rules

1. Your Submission must be an ¡°Agent¡±.
2. An Agent may only use any module from the Kaggle Kernels notebook image.
3. An Agent¡¯s sole purpose is to generate an action. Activities/code which do not directly contribute to this will be considered malicious and handled according to the Rules.
4. An Agent can have a maximum file size limit of 100 MB.
5. An Agent must return an action within 1 second (61 seconds on the first turn) of being invoked. If the Agent does not, it will lose the episode and may be invalidated.
6. An Agent which throws errors or returns an invalid action will lose the episode and may be invalidated.

### Evaluation
Each Submission has a rating which is modeled by a Gaussian \\(\mathcal N (\mu, \sigma^2)\\) where \\(\mu\\) is the estimated skill and \\(\sigma\\) represents our uncertainty of that estimate.

When you upload a Submission, we first play a validation episode where that Submission plays against itself to make sure it works properly.  If the validation episode fails, the Submission is marked as ¡°Error¡±.  Otherwise, we initialize the Submission with \\(\mu_0 = 600\\) and it joins the pool of ¡°All Submissions¡± for ongoing evaluation.

We run episodes with Submissions from the pool of All Submissions, and try to pick Submissions with similar ratings. We usually try to have each Submission participate in an episode a day, but the frequency may vary. After an episode finishes, we'll update the rating for both Submissions.  If one Submission won the episode, we'll increase its \\(\mu\\) and decrease its opponent's \\(\mu\\) -- if the result of the episode was a draw, then we'll move the two \\(\mu\\) values closer towards their mean.  The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous \\(\mu\\) values, and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty \\(\sigma\\).  We also reduce the \\(\sigma\\) terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result of the episode.

So all valid Submissions will continually play more matches and have dynamically changing scores as the pool increases.  The Leaderboard will show the rating of each team's or participant¡¯s (if that participant is not part of a team) best Submission.


----------------------
# Getting Started

## TL;DR

Create `submission.py` with the following source and [submit here!](https://www.kaggle.com/c/rock-paper-scissors/submit)

```
def copy_opponent_agent (observation, configuration):
    if observation.step > 0:
        return observation.lastOpponentAction
    else:
        return 0
```

See [the Connect X getting started notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/ajeffries/connectx-getting-started) for more tips on working with kaggle-environments, or checkout some of [the other agents included with the rock paper scissors environment](https://github.com/Kaggle/kaggle-environments/blob/master/kaggle_environments/envs/rps/agents.py).

You can also install and run kaggle-environments locally via pip:
`pip install kaggle-environments`

## Kaggle Environments Source Code

If you'd like to read more about kaggle-environments, please check out the links below:
* [Kaggle agent execution source](https://github.com/Kaggle/kaggle-environments/blob/master/README.md)
* [Rock Paper Scissors environment source code](https://github.com/Kaggle/kaggle-environments/blob/master/kaggle_environments/envs/rps/rps.py)
