</ul>",True,True
3452,tjhsst-cv-club-data-augmentation-contest,"# Description

## Overview
As mentioned in our club meeting on October 14th, we are going to run a data augmentation contest until Sunday, October 25th. For those of you who missed the meeting, data augmentation is the practice of altering images in a dataset to increase classification accuracy. For example, if you look at the image below, you will see the same kitten in several different orientations. If a model is trained on a single image of the same kitten, it might learn to only classify that image. However, if you train it on the same image but slightly modified, it will result in a more robust model.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2374783%2Face1552420202cd0e9ca09ccac5255a6%2Funtitled.png?generation=1603055807799622&amp;alt=media)

## Setup
To start off, download the three files under the ""Data"" tab. Two of these files are shellcode - as a Jupyter Notebook and as a raw .py file. You can use whichever one you prefer, but I strongly advice you use the Jupyter Notebook one (it makes life much easier). On that note, if you don't have Jupyter Notebook installed, you can install it with:
```
pip install jupyter
```
then run it and open a web terminal with:
```
python jupyter notebook
```
The next Python package you need to install is PyTorch. To install the version of PyTorch without GPU support, run 
```
pip install torch==1.6.0+cpu torchvision==0.7.0+cpu -f https://download.pytorch.org/whl/torch_stable.html
```
If you do have a GPU, you can check out the PyTorch website for different installation instructions. Besides PyTorch, the remaining packages can be installed with:
`pip install opencv-python matplotlib numpy`

## Data Augmentation
Once you have all the necessary packages installed, you can open up the shellcode in Jupyter Notebook or your favorite editor. Most of the code is PyTorch stuff for building and training the model - you can ignore this.

The only function you need to modify is `transformImage` (found under Step 5). As discussed in club, you should only use OpenCV and Numpy functions to apply image transformations. The function should return a numpy array containing the transformed images, as well as the original. In the shell code, the `transformImage` function applies a rotation 10 degrees to the left and right. Add some transformations in addition to this one!

If you need a refresher on how to use OpenCV for transformations, here's the [Jupyter Notebook Demo](https://activities.tjhsst.edu/computervision/lectures/transform_demo.ipynb) from our last club meeting.
## Submissions
Once you run the code, the file `results.csv` should be generated. This is all you need to upload to Kaggle. Also, to ensure that results are reproducible, we will ask to see the code of the winner(s) - aka, make sure you didn't cheat. 
## Other Stuff
This contest is fairly intensive in terms of familiarity with Python and machine learning libraries such as PyTorch. As such, it is completely normal for there to be things you don't understand. If you need help with anything at all, feel free to reach out to any of the club officers.",# Dataset Description,True,True
3453,rnd-42-welcome,"# Description

<p>This is an educational competition for the members of the RnD-42 team and all other participants are welcome also.</p>
<p>We think it is quite easy for experienced teams, so please participate personally.</p>
<p>The competition is about solving a binary classification problem over a dataset without a description of its columns. </p>
<p>Train any binary classification model you like on the data from the training dataset and submit the result of the prediction for the test dataset. Please beat the baseline at least and then do your best to improve the ROC AUC score.</p>
<p>Good luck!</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <strong>ROC AUC</strong></p><p> 
</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission file should contain two columns delimited by comma: ID (each row in the test dataset has unique ID) and Y (the result of the classification, 0 or 1).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,Y<br>123,1<br>321,0<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Dataset description</h2>
<p>We solve a binary classification problem over a dataset without a description of its columns. 
The only known things about data are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Each row in both train and test datasets has a unique ID.
<li>Each row in the dataset represents an event that occurred at the time represented by ""DT"" column and we try to predict: if the same event occurs at least one more time until the end of the day. ""1"" in the target means that it will occur, ""0"" - no.
<li>Columns with the ""N_"" prefix represent numeric features, with the ""C_"" prefix - §ãategorial features. All these features describe somehow the person, who raised the event or the previous history of such events raised by this person or environmental conditions at the time of the event.
</ul>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>r42_welcome_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>r42_welcome_train_res.csv</strong> - the targets for the training set</li>
<li><strong>r42_welcome_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>r42_welcome_test_sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - an anonymous unique id of the event</li>
<li><strong>DT</strong> - datetime of the event</li>
<li><strong>N_*</strong> - numeric features</li>
<li><strong>C_*</strong> - categorial features</li>",True,True
