3463,ads5035-01,"# Description

<p>Deepfake is an algorithm that allows you to express a person's face by changing it to someone else. Due to recent advances in deepfake technology, high-level deepfake images are being produced. More and more attempts are being made to harm people by using these images with malicious intent.</p>

<p>

</p>

<p>The problem situation given is as follows: You are a security officer at an IT company that actively utilizes face recognition technology. Recently, you have heard that there is a loophole in your company's facial recognition technology. While you were investigating technology loopholes, some malicious people used the Neural Texture model to create fake faces, and you found an attempt to bypass your company's facial recognition technology.

</p>
<p>The company has assigned you to solve this problem after hearing that you have taken a security class.</p>


<p>3 Submission</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are scored by categorization accuracy.
You need to be as accurate as possible. There are no other restrictions.</p>
<p> 3 Submission is available per day and ONLY ONE SUBMISSION WILL BE CALCULATED AS FINAL</p>","# Dataset Description

Dataset Description
=============
* Dataset Copyright
  * All data is generated from the dataset provided by [Faceforensics](https://github.com/ondyari/FaceForensics).
  * We don't have any ownership of this data, nor do we have participants in this contest.
* Format of Dataset
 * Inside the dataset, **the images have different sizes**. You should manage the input image by yourself.
 * Every image in this dataset has channel order as follows
    * RGB

![RGB format image](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nkmk/python-snippets/7b135319354f28ed55244fae00a7695a2c14e372/notebook/data/dst/lena_bgr_cv.jpg)    
    * BGR

![BGR format image](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nkmk/python-snippets/7b135319354f28ed55244fae00a7695a2c14e372/notebook/data/dst/lena_bgr_pillow.jpg)

* Dataset ratio
    * Training dataset
         * Total 10000 (Real:5000 counts ,Fake:5000 counts )
         * Real: Real image from Faceforensics dataset
         * Fake: Synthetic image that generated by Neural Texture and input was Real Faceforensics dataset    
    * Validation dataset
        * Total 5000 ( Real: 2500 counts, Fake:2500 counts)
        * Real: Real image from Faceforensics dataset
        * Fake: Synthetic image that generated by Neural Texture and input was Real Faceforensics     
    * Test dataset
        * Total 2000 (Unknown Real, Fake ratio)
        * Real: Real image from Faceforensics dataset
        * Fake: Synthetic image that generated by Neural Texture and input was Real Faceforensics",True,True
3464,retencin-en-telefona-celular,"# Description

Las comunicaciones por tel¨¦fono celular siempre han sido objeto de una fiera competencia, en la que hay, usualmente, un n¨²mero reducido de competidores (operadores) en el mercado que oscila entre cuatro y seis. Los operadores deben hacer fuertes inversiones de entrada para poder operar y prestar un servicio adecuado. El mercado de telefon¨ªa celular est¨¢ usualmente saturado (poca posibilidad de ampliaci¨®n del mercado), y por tanto, el conjunto de consumidores disponibles es reducido, por lo que los operadores usualmente cambian su estrategia de la adquisici¨®n de clientes a la retenci¨®n porque en el mundo se calcula que el costo de adquirir un nuevo cliente es cinco veces superior al de la retenci¨®n, sin contar con el posible CLTV (customer lifetimevalue) del cliente, esto es, el retorno a largo plazo que se genera por un cliente que continua consumiendo los servicios de un operador. Otra cifra clave es que la tasa de deserci¨®n (churn) suele rondar el 2% mensual promedio para un operador de telecomunicaciones. Desde el punto de vista de la inteligencia de negocios, bajo la perspectiva de CRM hay dos esfuerzos fundamentales de Anal¨ªtica: a) predecir la deserci¨®n (churn) de manera adecuada y b) evaluar la manera m¨¢s costo/efectiva de lo que un operador puede hacer para la retenci¨®n (incluyendo no hacer nada).

Un operador de telecomunicaciones les ha contratado para resolver las dos inquietudes anal¨ªticas presentadas anteriormente. Para la primera, desea que, a partir de una base de datos de entrenamiento que es una muestra aleatoria simple de la base de datos total de la compa?¨ªa que contiene el resultado de retenci¨®n en los ¨²ltimos seis meses, se realice la mejor predicci¨®n posible (basada en el ¨¢rea bajo la curva COR) para una base de datos de prueba en un concurso de Kaggle. Para la segunda parte, adicional a un reporte con metodolog¨ªa CRISP-DM (de m¨¢ximo 18 p¨¢ginas) de los modelos realizados, desea que se le presente una estrategia (incluyendo puntos de corte, costos y posibles ganancias) para atacar la retenci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n es el ¨¢rea bajo la curva caracter¨ªstica de operaci¨®n (**AUC** bajo la curva COR) en la base de datos de test

Los sometimientos deben ser archivos separados por comas, con un encabezado que contenga las variables ""id"" y ""resultado""","# Dataset Description

<p>La base de datos de entrenamiento tiene una muestra aleatoria simple de los clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.019 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.020). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.019.  Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago). </p>

<p>La base de datos de prueba contiene una muestra no aleatoria adicional de clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.019 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.020). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.019. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago).  La variable de posible churn (resultado) no se incluye, pues es la variable a predecir</p>
<h2>Archivos</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>traintelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de entrenamiento</li>
<li><strong>testelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de prueba</li>
<h2>Campos de la base de datos</h2>
<ul>

<li><strong>id</strong> - id an¨®mino que identifica al cliente</li>
<li><strong> Fecha de nacimiento </strong> - Fecha de nacimiento del cliente, dd/mm/yyyy</li>
<li><strong>tipo cliente</strong> - Tipo de cliente: 1 - Hombre, 2-Mujer, 3-Cliente empresarial</li>
<li><strong>Factura online</strong> - Binario que indica si el cliente recibe ¨²nicamente su factura online</li>
<li><strong>Antiguedad equipo</strong> Meses de antiguedad del equipo</li>
<li><strong>Plan de datos</strong> - Binario. 1=el cliente tiene plan premium de datos.</li>
<li><strong>Facturaci¨®n</strong> - Total facturaci¨®n de los ¨²ltimos seis meses (suma) </li>
<li><strong>Mora</strong> D¨ªas de mora acumulados</li>
<li><strong>Fecha inicio contrato </strong> - Fecha de inicio de su primer contrato de plan pospago en la compa?¨ªa</li>
<li><strong>minutos</strong> - total minutos consumidos en los ¨²ltimos seis meses</li>
<li><strong>resultado</strong> - 0=No hubo deserci¨®n; 1=Hubo deserci¨®n (churn) </li>",True,True
3465,msmk-machine-learning,"# Description

El objetivo es predecir el precio de las viviendas. Son datos de ventas reales producidas en el Condado de King, EEUU.
----------------------
# Evaluation

El error se medir¨¢ mediante la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio del logaritmo de los precios.","# Dataset Description

# Descripci¨®n de los conjuntos de datos

## `houses_train.csv`

Se trata del conjunto de datos con el que deb¨¦is entrenar los modelos. Las variables que contiene este conjunto de datos son:

- `id`: identificador ¨²nico de cada casa vendida.
- `date`: fecha de venta.
- `price`: **precio de venta (en d¨®lares)**. **Variable que hay que predecir**.
- `bedrooms`: n¨²mero de habitaciones.
- `bathrooms`: n¨²mero de ba?os donde $0.5$ significa una habitaci¨®n con aseo (ba?o sin ducha).
- `sqft_living`: ¨¢rea habitable de la casa (en pies cuadrados).
- `sqft_lot`: ¨¢rea de la parcela (en pies cuadrados).
- `floors`: n¨²mero de plantas de la vivienda.
- `waterfront`: variable binaria (*dummy*) indicando si la vivienda tiene vistas al mar o no.
- `view`: ¨ªndice (0-4) de las vistas.
- `condition`:  ¨ªndice (1-5) del estado de la vivienda.
- `grade`: ¨ªndice (1-13) de la calidad de la construcci¨®n y dise?o.
- `sqft_above`: ¨¢rea de la parte que est¨¢ por encima del nivel del suelo (en pies cuadrados).
- `sqft_basement`: ¨¢rea del s¨®tano (en pies cuadrados).
- `yr_built`: a?o de construcci¨®n.
- `yr_renovated`: a?o de la ¨²ltima reforma.
- `zipcode`: c¨®digo postal.
- `lat`: latitud.
- `long`: longitud.
- `sqft_living15`: ¨¢rea de vivienda media de las 15 casas m¨¢s pr¨®ximas (en pies cuadrados).
- `sqft_lot15`: ¨¢rea de parcela media de las 15 casas m¨¢s pr¨®ximas (en pies cuadrados).

## `houses_test.csv`

La estructura es id¨¦ntica a la de `houses_test.csv` a excepci¨®n de que no aparece la variable `price`. Este conjunto de datos es sobre el que deb¨¦is aplicar los modelos entrenados para obtener una predicci¨®n del precio para cada observaci¨®n. Durante el desarrollo de la asignatura iremos viendo c¨®mo hacerlo.

## `sample_submission.csv`

Este archivo es un ejemplo de la estructura que deber¨ªa tener el archivo que gener¨¦is con las predicciones del modelo, es decir, la predicci¨®n que hay¨¢is dado para cada observaci¨®n del conjunto `houses_train.csv`, y que ser¨¢ el archivo que subamos a Kaggle.

Tiene solamente las variables `id` y `price`. Nosotros conocemos cu¨¢l es el precio real al que se ha vendido cada casa y lo compararemos con vuestros resultados para poder calcular el error cometido.",True,True
3466,uwec-cs-491-midterm-2020,"# Description

This Kaggle competition will assess the accuracy of your predictions for the midterm project.

Click on the ""Data"" link above to get the data.  Click ""Rules"" to read the rules for the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean Absolute Error.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Submission files should contain two columns: id and price\_dollars. The ids should match the values in the Online\_courses\_test.csv file.  The prices should be listed to 2 decimal places of accuracy, and should not have the ""$"" sign or the word ""dollars.""</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,price_dollars<br>937678,1.00<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

The goal of this competition is to use the data in the training set to build one or more models to predict price_dollars.  To check the accuracy of your model, you will use your model to predict price_dollars for each row in the file Online_courses_test.csv.
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Online_courses_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>Online_courses_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>Online_courses_sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
The data come from a company that sells online courses about business, data analytics, and other topics.
<ul>
<li><strong> id</strong> - course identification number</li>
<li><strong>name</strong> - the name of the course</li>
<li><strong>url</strong> - the URL of the course on the company's website</li>
<li><strong>is_paid</strong> - equals TRUE if the course is paid, and FALSE otherwise</li>
<li><strong>num_subscribers</strong> - the number of people who have subscribed to the course</li>
<li><strong>avg_rating</strong> - the course's average rating</li>
<li><strong>avg_rating_recent</strong> - the course's average rating over a more recent time span than avg_rating</li>
<li><strong>rating</strong> - I don't know how this differs from avg_rating.</li>
<li><strong>num_reviews</strong> - The number of people who have rated the course.</li>
<li><strong>is_wishlisted</strong> - Equals TRUE if the course is on a wishlist.</li>
<li><strong>num_lectures</strong> - The number of lecture videos in the course.</li>
<li><strong>num_practice_tests</strong> - The number of practice tests in the course.</li>
<li><strong>created</strong> - When the course was created.</li>
<li><strong>published_time</strong> - When the course was published.</li>
<li><strong>discount_price</strong> - The discounted price of the course, if any.  Measured in US dollars.</li>
<li><strong>price_dollars</strong> - The usual (non-discounted) price of the course, in US dollars.</li>
</ul>",True,True
3467,bads2021,"# Description

<h2> Assignment  </h2>

<p> This assignment will allow you to apply your skills in business analytics on real-world data from the field of e-commerce and customer targeting and practice the scientific methods for rigorous testing and documentation. Passing the assignment is required to participate in the exam for the class *Business Analytics and Data Science*.
Do not use the ""Kernels"" feature to make your code public. You must not share large code chunks or complete scripts with other participants! You are required to solve the assignment individually, but we encourage discussing issues and code pieces and sharing insights both offline and on the Kaggle forum.</p>

<p>For the assignment, you are highly encouraged to go beyond the standard methods taught in class, make use of the scientific literature and conduct and document your own experiments with the data. Make sure to consider all stages of a typical modeling process:
</p>

- research the relevant technical and task-related knowledge in the literature
- gather, clean and preprocess the relevant data
- select the best model and model parameters
- deploy and assess the model in terms of performance and plausibility
with possibly revision of any step or the whole process.


<h2> Setting </h2>

<p> Customers send back a substantial part of the products that they purchase online. Return shipping is expensive for online platforms and return orders are said to reach 50% for certain industries and products. Nevertheless, free or inexpensive return shipping has become a customer expectation and de-facto standard in the fierce online competition on clothing, but shops have indirect ways to influence customer purchase behavior. For purchases where return seems likely, a shop could, for example, restrict payment options or display additional marketing communication.</p>

<p>For this assignment, you are provided with real-world data by an online retailer. Your task is to identify the items that are likely to be returned. When a customer is about to purchase a item, which is likely to be returned, the shops is planning to show a warning message. Your task is to build a targeting model to balance potential sales and return risk in order to optimize shop revenue. The data you receive is artificially balanced (1:1 ratio between returns and non-returns).</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the <b>Area-under-the-ROC-curve (AUC)</b> based on the probability scores of your model. The model will be evaluated on the<i> class</i> dataset. To pass the assignment and qualify for the exam, a final AUC score of<b> at least 0.68 </b>on the public leaderboard is required. You can submit multiple predictions and improve your score until the end date of the competition.</p>

<p>For every order-item combination in the dataset, submission files should contain two and only two named columns: order_item_id and return. A higher probability score denotes a higher chance of return (1).</p>
You can open .csv files in a text editor (*not* Excel) to check that everything is exactly right.</p>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>

order\_item\_id ,return
100001,0.4
100002,0.05
etc.","# Dataset Description

<p>The data contains real orders by customers of a clothing store. It contains 14 columns that describe the order and customer. The column names should be self-explanatory. In compliance with the shop's wishes, we are unable to answer any further questions regarding the data.<br>

<p>
<b> File descriptions</b> <br>
BADS_WS_2021_known.csv - The training data for which the returns are known<br>
BADS_WS_2021_unknown.csv - The final test data for which predictions are required<br>
example_prediction - An example of what your final prediction should look like </p>


<p>
<b>Data fields</b> <br>
order_item_id - The elements for which a prediction is required<br>
return - The target column: If a customer has returned the item (1) or kept it (0)<br>
+ columns describing the order</p>",True,True
