3471,ucsd-neural-data-challenge,"# Description

# Competition Description
Welcome to the first-ever UCSD Kaggle competition put together by the Data Science Student Society and NeuroTechX. This is a competition for **UCSD Students of all skill levels** to create data visualizations and/or machine learning models for binary classification using Electroencephalography data. We are looking forward to seeing what you come up with!

Electroencephalography (EEG) is a simple non-invasive way to collect electrical signals from the scalp. Motor Imagery (MI) for left vs right hand imagined movement is one common neural pattern Cognitive Neuroscience has identified is detectable through EEG data. In this dataset competition, you will explore continuous and epoched neural data to try to predict if someone was thinking left vs right-hand movement. Read more about the experiments used to generate this data here: http://www.bbci.de/competition/iv/desc_2b.pdf

# Helpful Workshops
This competition is done alongside a 3-part workshop series by NeuroTechX in Fall 2020 on: 
1. Cracking open the dataset - 10/22
2. EEG Feature Extraction 1 - Week 5
3. EEG Feature Extraction 2 - Week 7
4. Competition Wrap-up - TBA (Winter quarter)

If you would also like to learn more about the types of models you can use for this competition please check out the following workshops from DS3:
1. Introduction to Deep Learning - Week 5
2. Introduction to Sklearn Pipelines and Models - Week 5
3. Introduction to Transfer Learning - Week 6

# Social Media
This competition is put together by the Data Science Student Society and NeurotechX at UCSD.
We have created a discord to handle any and all questions related to the competition!
https://discord.gg/Ekcqezr
 
Please also check out DS3's and NTX's FB pages for the upcoming events! 
https://www.facebook.com/DS3UCSD
https://www.facebook.com/ntxucsd 

If you have any technical or competition questions feel free to reach out to Geeling Chau, Tanveer Mittal, or Simon Fei via Facebook or Discord!
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Competition Evaluation
This page details how participants will have their submissions evaluated. This competition has a test set with labels that are hidden from the public. Participants will use their models to compute predictions on the test set and submit these predictions to be evaluated on the hidden labels as well as submit a notebook of their methods. If you would also like to compete just in data visualization you may choose to do that and not participate in the modeling portion of the competition!

## Judging Categories
- Most accurate model - $75
- Best Neuroscience Backed Methods for Feature Extraction - $50
- Best Data Science/ML Methods - $50
- Best Data Visualization - $25

## Submission Format
Before the competition deadline, participants must also submit a notebook detailing their methods for judging on the Notebooks tab. Judging criteria for this portion of the competition TBA. Make sure to include teammate names and their ucsd emails as well! Include this information at the top of your submission notebook.
```
Name(s):
Email(s):
UCSD PID:  (starts with A or U - put NA if not a UCSD student, you will be considered for honorable mention, but not a cash prize reward)
```

To make a modeling submission, you must also submit a csv that contains the columns: ""trial\_id"" and ""event\_type"". This will allow us to match your predictions against our answer key to score your predictions.

## Evaluation Method
We will be evaluating submissions by computing a F1 score on predictions. This will allow for evaluation of model accuracy that equally weighs precision and recall. Read more about the F1 Score here: [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score)","# Dataset Description

Simplified data originally from BCI Competition IV 2b (more details: http://www.bbci.de/competition/iv/desc_2b.pdf)  

There are two full sets of data: (1) raw EEG data in train, test, and y_train_only folders, and (2) epoched data to 4s trials of all sessions concatenated. 

## Electrode Setups
10/20 system: 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2479259%2F9b34adc2ec7bd2c485f541cfb8e35614%2F10-20.png?generation=1603408618153915&alt=media)

EOG setups for this dataset: 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2479259%2F6c5f4d0a6c463b52518facb5d53acd98%2FEOG-montage.png?generation=1603408641588949&alt=media)

## File descriptions
- **epoched_train.pkl** - Pickled dataframe of training set that is epoched for easy training setup
- **epoched_test.pkl** - Pickled dataframe of testing that is epoched for easy testing setup
- **train** - Folder of raw patients' data for the training set
- **test** - Folder of raw patients' data for the test set
- **y_train_only** - Folder of event labels for patients' trials in the training set

## Data fields
- **patient_id:** an anonymous id unique to a given subject. e.g. ""B0102T"" means subject 1, session 2, T=Training. E=Evaluation. (only in epoched data, but also found as filenames for raw data) 
- **C3:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from channel in 10/20 system
- **Cz:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from channel in 10/20 system
- **C4:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from channel in 10/20 system
- **EOGch01:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from EOG setup
- **EOGch02:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from EOG setup
- **EOGch03:** Electrode voltages (in uV) from EOG setup
- **start_time:** time in ms since start of recording when this trial started (only in epoched data) 
- **event_type:** Trial Type - 0 means Left, 1 means Right. (only in epoched data)
- **time:** time in ms since start of recording (only in raw data) 
- **EventStart:** Whether or not the timepoint is the start of a trial (only in raw data) 
- **EventType:** Trial Type - 0 means Left, 1 means Right. (only in training data)(what you must attempt to predict)
 


## Loading the data
Feel free to use the following lines of code to load in the epoched training and testing data:

`import pickle`

`train_df = pickle.load(open(""data/epoched_train.pkl"", ""rb""))`

`test_df = pickle.load(open(""data/epoched_test.pkl"", ""rb""))`",True,True
3472,caiis-dogfood-day-2020,"# Description

Nothing ruins the thrill of buying a brand new car more quickly than seeing your new insurance bill. The sting¡¯s even more painful when you know you¡¯re a good driver. It doesn¡¯t seem fair that you have to pay so much if you¡¯ve been cautious on the road for years.

<p><img src=""https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1489824904134-891ab64532f1?ixlib=rb-1.2.1&ixid=eyJhcHBfaWQiOjEyMDd9"" alt=""beep beep"" width=""250"" style=""float: right""></p>

Inaccuracies in car insurance company¡¯s claim predictions raise the cost of insurance for good drivers and reduce the price for bad ones. But Kaymo &trade;  wants to stop that!

In this competition, you¡¯re challenged to build a model that predicts the probability that a driver will initiate an auto insurance claim. While Kaymo &trade; has used machine learning for the past 10 years, they¡¯re looking to the CAIIS machine learning community to explore new, more powerful methods. A more accurate prediction will allow them to further tailor their prices, and hopefully make auto insurance coverage more accessible to more drivers.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a probability for the `target` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,target
    3,0.1
    9,0.7
    11,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Each row in this dataset represents an insurance policy. You must?predict the value for the `target` column, whether a policy resulted in a claim `1` or not `0` over a fixed time period. Variables prefaced with `cat` are categorical (nominal or ordinal, but not specified), while those prefaced with `cont` are continuous.

*   **train.csv** - the training data
*   **test.csv** - the test data; you must predict a `target` for each row (`id`)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
3473,enpm808w-fall-2020-hw3-question-answering,"# Description

The evaluation format is accuracy. Because there are more wrong answers than correct answers, you should always get above 0.53 accuracy. This is the simplest baseline you should do better than.

Submission Format

For row in the test file, submission files should contain two columns: row and corr. You should provide for each row whether the guess is correct or not.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

row,corr
31,False
32,False
33,False
88,False
91,False
139,False
140,False
141,False
142,False
188,True
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

Each line has a guess (page) and a correct answer (answer) given some fraction of the question revealed so far (text). Your goal is to predict whether they match. Each guess is the title of a Wikipedia page. To get you started, you have the following columns

row A unique ID for every guess

body_score A measure of the confidence for how well the text of the Wikipedia page matches the question text

page The guess produced

answer The correct answer

text The text so far revealed (randomly chosen)

category The category (could be noisy) of the question

tournaments The tournaments the question was used in

answer_type The kind of answer of the question (e.g. person, place, work)

corr Whether the guess (page) was correct or not (i.e., whether it matched ¡°answer¡±)

inlinks The number of inlinks on the guessed Wikipedia page

File descriptions

qb.train.csv - the training set

qb.test.csv - the test set

qb.guess.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

features.R - R code to generate some additional features (described in the lecture)",True,True
