3497,neural-computation-test,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the UoB School of Computer Science Neural Computation coursework challenge! Full details about this coursework can be found on the Neural Computation canvas page where you will find a tutorial notebook to help you get started. </p>
<h2>Datasets</h2>
<p>Datasets for this coursework are also found on canvas, <strong>NOT</strong> in the data tab here on Kaggle.</p>
<h2>Teams</h2>
<p>By navigating to the teams tab you can merge into teams with other students. Please use the team name assigned to your team on canvas.</p>
<h2>Questions and Queries</h2>
<p>Please post and questions, queries and problems on the Neural Computation teams page. The discussion tab on this Kaggle competition will not be checked by staff.</p>


<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
Thanks to the ACDC challenge organisers for letting us use the ACDC dataset for this competition.

O. Bernard, A. Lalande, C. Zotti, F. Cervenansky, et al.
""Deep Learning Techniques for Automatic MRI Cardiac Multi-structures Segmentation and
Diagnosis: Is the Problem Solved ?"" in IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging,
vol. 37, no. 11, pp. 2514-2525, Nov. 2018
<p></p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Private and Public Scores</h2>
When you make your submission, Kaggle will only display your score on 20% of the test set on the public leader board. When the competition ends, the scores for each team on the remaining 80% of the test set will made visible and used to determine the overall winner. 

<strong> Why does Kaggle do this? </strong> 

The reason for this approach is that if your scores on the entire test set were visible on the leader board, you could tune your networks to overfit the entire test set which defeats the purpose of creating a generalizable model.
<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition will be the dice score. The general form of the dice score for two sets is as follows: 
</p>
<p>\[Dice(A,B)=\frac{2|A\cap B|}{|A|+|B|}\]</p>
<p>Where | | denotes the cardinality of a set. However as we are interested in the segmentation of the three anatomical features of the heart we will take a dice score for each feature, with an average of these three scores taken as the final score for each mask . The total dice score you will see displayed on Kaggle will be the average dice score across all test set samples. More details about the evaluation metric can be found in the tutorial notebook.
</p>

----------------------
# Submission

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> The output for your network should be a 80 segmentation masks corresponding to the 80 test samples. To ensure that your submissions are in the correct format, we have made a helper function to convert your masks to a csv file that Kaggle will accept for submission you can find this function in the tutorial notebook availible on canvas. Please follow these steps:
</p><ul>
  <li>Create a new directory to save your masks</li>
  <li>Save all 80 predicted masks as png files in this directory. This directory should contain <strong>ONLY</strong> these 80 masks. The name of each mask file <strong>MUST</strong> be same as the test set sample it relates to. E.g for an MRI scan from the test set named patient013_ED.png, the associated mask png file must also be named patient013_ED.png.</li>
  <li>Call the <strong>submission_converter</strong> helper function with the path to your mask directory as the first argument, and the path to the directory you wish to save your csv to as the second.</li>
  <li>Submit the submission.csv file produced by this function to Kaggle by clicking ""Submit Predictions"".</li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p>The below diagram details the process for further clarity.</p>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6001231%2F20cf68d54adac7de7a78d03cf66859ed%2Fconverter%20(1).png?generation=1603362921441959&amp;alt=media)","# Dataset Description

<p><strong>All data provided for this coursework is available from the Neural Computation canvas page</strong>. The csv listed below is simply a placeholder to meet the requirements of a Kaggle competition.<p>",True,True
3498,cosmic-research-ml-lab03,"# Description

<p>Dota 2 is a computer game in the MOBA (Multiplayer Online Battle Arena) genre. It is played by two teams, called Radiant and Dire which consist of five players each. The main goal of the game is to destroy other team's ¡°Ancient"", located at the opposite corners of the map. Matches are generated from a queue, taking into account the level of the game all players, known as MMR (Match Making Rank).</p>

<p> Each player earns gold and experience during the game, gets items and performs kills. Information about each match includes statistics of each player and of the whole team.</p>

<p>Your goal is to predict an outcome of the match: whether radiant win or lose</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html""> ROC-AUC</a>.</p>


<p>Submission file must contain two columns:</p>
<pre>
<ul>
<li>match_id - [int] identifier of the match</li>
<li>radiant_win - [float] probability that team radiant wins the game</li>
</ul>
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_db.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_db.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>hero_stats.json</strong> - supplemental information about heroes that are used by the players</li>
<li><strong>lab03_baseline.ipynb</strong> - a sample notebook that includes sample solution</li>
<li><strong>baseline_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission</li>
</ul>

<h2>Train and Test Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>match_id</strong> - an identifier of a match</li>
<li><strong>radiant_win</strong> - outcome of the match (train set only)</li>
<li><strong>hero</strong> - hero chosen by player (goes with prefix r1-r5 for radiant and d1-d5 for dire)</li>
<li><strong>level</strong> - level of a player</li>
<li><strong>xp</strong> - xp gained by the player</li>
<li><strong>gold</strong> - amount of gold acquired by the player</li>
<li><strong>lh</strong> - number of neutral creatures killed by the player</li>
<li><strong>kills</strong> - number of kills performed by the player</li>
<li><strong>deaths</strong> - times the player died</li>
<li><strong>items</strong> - number of items obtained by the player</li>
</ul>
<p> Also there are some columns that reflect team statistics: first_blood, courier_time, etc.</p>",True,True
