Use the `housedata2.csv` file to train your model.  Use your model with the data in `housedataTEST2.csv` to obtain your predictions.  The format to upload your `*.csv` file is shown in `Yourlastname_yourfirstname2.csv`.",True,True
3513,109-1-ntut-dl-app-hw2,"# Description

In Homework 2 we are going to train models that can recommend hashtags based on the content of an image. We will use the dataset ""HARRISON"". Please refer to the paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.05054) for more details about the dataset.

The original data of HARRISON can be downloaded here: (https://drive.google.com/file/d/1B9NZf42J_GpslRNlTTAxvM5c9WRVl_1Z/view?usp=sharing). You can download the images and take a look, but this is not mandatory. We already extracted the image features for you and upload to Kaggle as ""harrison_features.npz"".

The demo code separates the training process into two stages: word2vec training and image-to-word2vec training. The notebook of last semester can be found here: https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-devise
You can export and import the code to this competition.

To evaluate the model performance, the top 10 hashtags (KNN=10) recommended from your model will be used to calculate the hamming loss of ground truth.  Get your computer ready and have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

We will use the Hamming loss function to evaluate the performance:
<p> \[ \frac{1}{|Y|} \sum_{i=1}^{|N|} \sum_{j=1}^{|H_i|} XOR(y'_{ij}, y_{ij}) \] </p>

<p> where \\(  |N| \\) is the total number of images, \\( |Y| \\) is the total number of labels, \\(  |H_i| \\) is the number of labeled hashtags of image  \\( i \\). </p>
<p> The \\(  y'_{ij} \\) is a binary output (True or False ) that predicts the existing of a hashtag  \\( h_{ij}  \\). The \\(  y_{ij} \\) is the ground truth. </p>","# Dataset Description

The files are briefly described below:

<ol>
<li> Code files
<p> There are three iPython notebooks that show you how to train a model and create a prediction output in Kaggle format. Please run the code to understand the concepts, and modify them to build your own model. For functions of each file please read the ""README.txt"". The ""utility.py"" is used the demo code. </p>
</li>

<li> Data files
<p>
<ul>
<li>
""harrison_features.npz"" - Pre-extracted features of images in harrison dataset. We extracted ImageNet and places365 CNN features.
</li>
<li>
""tag_list.txt"" - The list of hashtags for all image files.
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</li>
</ol>",True,True
3514,deep-learning-thu-2020,"# Description

Tiny ImageNet Challenge is the first course project of Introduction to Deep Learning. It runs similar to the [ImageNet challenge] and [Stanford CS231n].
[ImageNet challenge]:http://www.image-net.org/challenges/LSVRC/2014/
[Stanford CS231n]:https://tiny-imagenet.herokuapp.com/

## Dataset
The Tiny ImageNet dataset has 100 classes. Each class has 1,000 training images, 100 validation images. The test set contains 10,000 images in total. We have released the training and validation sets with images and labels. The test set is released without labels. You are asked to predict the class label of each test image.

In detail, all training and validation images are from ImageNet training set. For test set, 5,000 images (id 0~4999) are selected from ImageNet validation set, while another 5,000 images (id 5000-9999) from [ImageNet-A]dataset, which called **Natural Adversarial Examples**. Modern deep CNNs achieve very low accuracy on these natural adversarial examples. ImageNet-A examples cause mistakes due to occlusion, weather, and other complications encountered in the long tail of scene configurations. You are recommended to read the [original paper] for more information.
[ImageNet-A]:https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.07174.pdf
[original paper]:https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.07174.pdf

For this course project, you need to consider how to achieve high classification accuracy on both general ImageNet images and natural adversarial examples.

## Submission
We use the test set accuracy to measure the performance. To submit your predictions on the test set, you need to upload a CSV file, which should be a two-column file with 10,001 lines (the first row is a header). Each line contains a pair of test image filename and its predicted class id (0-99). An example might look like:
```
Id,Category
0.jpg,0
1.jpg,1
2.jpg,2
...
9999.jpg,99
```
After the deadline, you are required to submit all codes and a short report (no less than one page). Plagiarism and labeling test images manually will be penalized.

##Evaluation
Competition scoring and ranking are based on two leaderboards : public and private. These two leaderboards are created by randomly splitting the test dataset. Public leaderboard is based on a subset of 40% of observations in test dataset you are submitting while the private leaderboard is based on the remaining 60% of data. The private leaderboard remains secret until the end of the competititon. It will determine the final competition ranking!
 
So far, all your submissions are based on the public leaderboard dataset. Using it, you are receiving a feedback about your performance. It can tell you how good you model is and how it is generalizing on unseen data.
 
However, the final leaderboard (60% of test dataset) will determine final result. Before the end of competition ranking (Submissions dead line : 08 November 2020 23:59, UTC+8), you will have to select your three favorite submissions. By default, Kaggle chooses the three submissions that have performed the best on public leaderboard. However you are free to select the three submissions you want.
 
Finally, these three submissions will result in the private leaderboard and so final ranking. In a Kaggle competition, the purpose of this division is to prevent people from winning by overfitting to the public leaderboard. Participants then are motivated to make sure their models will generalize well to the private leaderboard test set.
 
Therefore, your results on public and private leaderboard could be slightly different. 

## Grading Policy
Total marks for this project is 20:
- 14 marks based on rankings
   - 1st place: 14 marks
   - 2nd - last place: between 7 and 14 marks, in descending order
last place: 7 marks
- 6 marks based on the final report
   - correctness
   - novelty

Members in the same group get the same grade.

## References
Natural Adversarial Examples. https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.07174.
ImageNet LSVRC challenge. http://www.image-net.org/challenges/LSVRC.
Tiny ImageNet Visual Recognition Challenge. https://tiny-imagenet.herokuapp.com/.
----------------------
# Tips

You are highly recommended to follow the [PyTorch ImageNet classification] example to start your project.
[PyTorch ImageNet classification]:https://github.com/pytorch/examples/tree/master/imagenet
There are some useful tips for you:

- Forget all codes dealing with 'distributed'/'dist' in the official example.
- Note the image resolution (64\*64) and number of classes (100), since [torchvision models] often use 224\*224 (e.g. resnet) for 1000 classes. You need to modify the model architecture properly.
[torchvision models]:https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/torchvision/models.html
- We provide an example of Tiny ImangeNet Dataloader, note that [ImageFolder] may lead to label mismatch.
[ImageFolder]:https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/torchvision/datasets.html#imagefolder
- ResNet-18 model can achieve about 30% accuracy on test set. Random guess is about 1%.
```
# Tiny ImangeNet Dataloader
import numpy as np
from PIL import Image
def default_loader(path):
    return Image.open(path).convert('RGB')
class TinyImageNetDataset(torch.utils.data.Dataset):
    def __init__(self, root, data_list, transform = None, loader = default_loader):
        # root: your_path/TinyImageNet/
        # data_list: your_path/TinyImageNet/train.txt etc.
        images = []
        labels = open(data_list).readlines()
        for line in labels:
            items = line.strip('\n').split()
            img_name = items[0]

            # test list contains only image name
            test_flag = True if len(items) == 1 else False
            label = None if test_flag == True else np.array(int(items[1]))

            if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, img_name)):
                images.append((img_name, label))
            else:
                print(os.path.join(root, img_name) + 'Not Found.')

        self.root = root
        self.images = images
        self.transform = transform
        self.loader = loader

    def __getitem__(self, index):
        img_name, label = self.images[index]
        img = self.loader(os.path.join(self.root, img_name))
        raw_img = img.copy()
        if self.transform is not None:
            img = self.transform(img)
        return (img, label) if label is not None else img

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self.images)
```","# Dataset Description

You can download the Tiny ImageNet dataset from the link below or [BaiduYun].
[BaiduYun]:https://pan.baidu.com/s/1YwxH5DBqkZv_6EeIVT_1ZQ

After unzip the TinyImageNet.zip, there are several files and folders:

- `train`: training set, each subfolder contains 1000 images.
- `val`: validation set, each subfolder contains 100 images.
- `test`: test set, contains 10,000 images in total.
- `train.txt`: training list, contains `image_name label` pairs.
- `val.txt`: validation list, contains `image_name label` pairs.
All images has been resized to 64\*64 pixels.",True,True
3515,sysu-dmml-2,"# Description

This competition data is similar to the one we used in class. The training data set is exactly same while the testing data set is following the training data set. 

Please use ""train"" data set to train your model. The objective is to predict the ""Playoffs"".

Although the team name is blinded in the data set, for better prediction, you may use some information about MLB, such as the changes of rules etc.

Each student should complete this assignment independently. Please edit your team name as ""student id-name"". All algorithms, tools and packages you obtained from Internet can be used.
