3517,tsma-202021-music-genre-classification,"# Description

Estimate genre of each musical audio file.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation for this challenge metric is Categorical Accuracy:

TruePositive / (TruePositive + FalsePositive)

for all categories (genres).","# Dataset Description

<p>The data provided consists of two archives of audio files (MP3 format) and csv files with metadata.</p>
<p> test.zip and train.zip are the audio files composing the train dataset and the test dataset (about 4000 tracks in each set, about 3.6Go for each set). Genre information is given for train set but not for test set. One unique genre has been indicated for each track. The objective of the challenge is to retrieve the genre indicated for the audio files of the test set.</p>
<p> train.csv is the csv file which indicates the genre (genre_id) of each track of the train set.</p>
<p> test.csv is the csv file which gives all the track_id of the tracks whose genre has to be estimated.</p>
<p> genres.csv is a file which indicates the possible genre and the corresponding id. Eight genres are considered here.</p>
<p> sampleSubmission.csv is an example of submission file which can be evaluated according to category accuracy. This example has been randomly computed. </p>
<p> For example, file 000002.mp3 is an audio file from the train set. Its genre is indicated in train.csv. Genre_id 1 is associated to the track_id 000002. In genres.csv, it is indicated that the genre_id 1 corresponds to Classical genre. </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format with constant (incorrect) genre</li>
<li><strong>genres.csv</strong> - information about genres</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>genre_id</strong> - an id unique to a given genre</li>
<li><strong>track_id</strong> - the id of a music track</li>
</ul>
<h2>Audio Files</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.zip</strong> - archive with audio files of the train set</li>
<li><strong>test.zip</strong> - archive with audio files of the test set</li>
</ul>",True,True
3518,edm-upv-2020,"# Description

Predice el precio de una casa a partir de una serie de caracter¨ªsticas.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) .","# Dataset Description

##File descriptions

train.csv - the training set

test.csv - the test set

data_description.txt - full description of each column, originally prepared by Dean De Cock but lightly edited to match the column names used here

sample_submission.csv - a benchmark submission from a linear regression 


##Data fields

Here's a brief version of what you'll find in the data description file.

* SalePrice - the property's sale price in dollars. This is the target variable that you're trying to predict.
* MSSubClass: The building class
* MSZoning: The general zoning classification
* LotFrontage: Linear feet of street connected to property
* LotArea: Lot size in square feet
* Street: Type of road access
* Alley: Type of alley access
* LotShape: General shape of property
* LandContour: Flatness of the property
* Utilities: Type of utilities available
* LotConfig: Lot configuration
* LandSlope: Slope of property
* Neighborhood: Physical locations within Ames city limits
* Condition1: Proximity to main road or railroad
* Condition2: Proximity to main road or railroad (if a second is present)
* BldgType: Type of dwelling
* HouseStyle: Style of dwelling
* OverallQual: Overall material and finish quality
* OverallCond: Overall condition rating
* YearBuilt: Original construction date
* YearRemodAdd: Remodel date
* RoofStyle: Type of roof
* RoofMatl: Roof material
* Exterior1st: Exterior covering on house
* Exterior2nd: Exterior covering on house (if more than one material)
* MasVnrType: Masonry veneer type
* MasVnrArea: Masonry veneer area in square feet
* ExterQual: Exterior material quality
* ExterCond: Present condition of the material on the exterior
* Foundation: Type of foundation
* BsmtQual: Height of the basement
* BsmtCond: General condition of the basement
* BsmtExposure: Walkout or garden level basement walls
* BsmtFinType1: Quality of basement finished area
* BsmtFinSF1: Type 1 finished square feet
* BsmtFinType2: Quality of second finished area (if present)
* BsmtFinSF2: Type 2 finished square feet
* BsmtUnfSF: Unfinished square feet of basement area
* TotalBsmtSF: Total square feet of basement area
* Heating: Type of heating
* HeatingQC: Heating quality and condition
* CentralAir: Central air conditioning
* Electrical: Electrical system
* 1stFlrSF: First Floor square feet
* 2ndFlrSF: Second floor square feet
* LowQualFinSF: Low quality finished square feet (all floors)
* GrLivArea: Above grade (ground) living area square feet
* BsmtFullBath: Basement full bathrooms
* BsmtHalfBath: Basement half bathrooms
* FullBath: Full bathrooms above grade
* HalfBath: Half baths above grade
* Bedroom: Number of bedrooms above basement level
* Kitchen: Number of kitchens
* KitchenQual: Kitchen quality
* TotRmsAbvGrd: Total rooms above grade (does not include bathrooms)
* Functional: Home functionality rating
* Fireplaces: Number of fireplaces
* FireplaceQu: Fireplace quality
* GarageType: Garage location
* GarageYrBlt: Year garage was built
* GarageFinish: Interior finish of the garage
* GarageCars: Size of garage in car capacity
* GarageArea: Size of garage in square feet
* GarageQual: Garage quality
* GarageCond: Garage condition
* PavedDrive: Paved driveway
* WoodDeckSF: Wood deck area in square feet
* OpenPorchSF: Open porch area in square feet
* EnclosedPorch: Enclosed porch area in square feet
* 3SsnPorch: Three season porch area in square feet
* ScreenPorch: Screen porch area in square feet
* PoolArea: Pool area in square feet
* PoolQC: Pool quality
* Fence: Fence quality
* MiscFeature: Miscellaneous feature not covered in other categories
* MiscVal: $Value of miscellaneous feature
* MoSold: Month Sold
* YrSold: Year Sold
* SaleType: Type of sale
* SaleCondition: Condition of sale",True,True
3519,prediccin-de-estancia-hospitalaria-2020-1,"# Description

<p>Para enfrentar los retos de las instituciones hospitalarias, ¨¦stas deben dise?ar diferentes estrategias que les permitan ser m¨¢s eficientes con el uso de los recursos, ser m¨¢s efectivos en la prestaci¨®n de sus servicios y mejorar la satisfacci¨®n de los clientes. Una variable fundamental para lograrlo, es el tiempo de estancia hospitalario y la predicci¨®n del mismo. Esta variable es de gran inter¨¦s en la administraci¨®n hospitalaria y por esta raz¨®n ha sido un foco de estudio de investigadores afines con el tema, sin embargo, su predicci¨®n no ha sido un problema sencillo debido a diferentes caracter¨ªsticas que tiene el fen¨®meno de estancia hospitalaria y por esta raz¨®n sigue siendo un tema de investigaci¨®n vigente.
</p><p>La predicci¨®n de esta variable es de gran inter¨¦s debido a su versatilidad, ya que al predecir una variable se pueden definir estrategias que apalanquen el cumplimiento de los objetivos de la administraci¨®n hospitalaria; por un lado, permite la planeaci¨®n de servicios de hospitalizaci¨®n programados con base en la liberaci¨®n de capacidad por las altas, de tal manera que la capacidad no se desborde y tampoco est¨¦ subutilizada, impactando en la eficiencia en el uso de los recursos. Por otra parte, tambi¨¦n se ha utilizado el LOS para generaci¨®n de alertas tempranas en pacientes que tienen complicaciones, bas¨¢ndose en la desviaci¨®n entre el LOS estimado y el LOS real del paciente, lo que permite evitar que la condici¨®n de un paciente se agrave y por ende, se mejore la efectividad de los servicios prestados. Finalmente, se ha identificado que el tiempo de hospitalizaci¨®n y el conocimiento del mismo, por parte de los pacientes, afecta la experiencia que ellos tienen durante su estancia, la cual mejora cuando dicho tiempo es informado oportunamente.

 </p><p>En el presente trabajo se tiene el objetivo de predecir el tiempo de estancia hospitalario, en d¨ªas, de los pacientes de un hospital con base en diferentes variables que se van registrando en las bases de datos</p>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1678947%2F93410b5bbab1ca1e7ab70b9da6ca0b62%2Fcama_hospitalaria.jpg?generation=1585596590559902&amp;alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

## M¨¦trica

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n para esta competencia es el MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error). El MAPE es el promedio del error absoluto o diferencia entre el tiempo de estancia hospitalaria real y la predicci¨®n del tiempo de estancia hospitalaria. Por lo general,  se expresa como una relaci¨®n definida por la siguiente expresi¨®n:<p></p>

<p>\[  MAPE=\sum_{i=1}^{n} \left | \frac{y_{i} -\widehat{y}_{i}}{y_{i}} \right |  \]</p>
<p></p> donde
<p>\[ y_{i}\]</p>
<p></p> es el verdadero valor del tiempo de estancia hospitalaria y 
<p>\[\widehat{y}_{i} \]</p>
<p></p>es el valor predicho del tiempo de estancia hospitalaria. 
<p></p>El MAPE se puede presentar rambi¨¦n como un porcentaje.

## ?C¨®mo someter a evaluaci¨®n una predicci¨®n?

Antes de someter las predicciones realizadas, ajuste su modelo de regresi¨®n en `R`. Importe los datos:

```
datos_los_train &lt;- read.csv(""datos_los_train.csv"", header=TRUE)
datos_los_test &lt;- read.csv(""datos_los_test.csv"", header=TRUE)
```

A continuaci¨®n se ajusta un modelo:

```
modelo &lt;- lm(lengthofstay ~ respiration + pulse + dialysisrenalendstage, 
                       data = datos_los_train)
```

Las predicciones se deben realizar con los datos de prueba:

```
prediccion &lt;- predict(modelo, newdata = datos_los_test)
```

Finalmente, se deben guardar las predicciones en un marco de datos:

```
eval_data &lt;- data.frame(eid=datos_los_test[,""eid""], ""lengthofstay""= prediccion)
```

¨¦ste marco de datos deber¨¢ ser guardado en un archivo en formato CSV. Si por ejemplo, se nombra el archivo como  `sample_eval.csv`, se debe usar el siguiente comando:

```
write.csv(eval_data, file='sample_eval.csv', row.names=FALSE)
```


V¨ªnculo para ver como hacer un env¨ªo a Kaggle: https://www.screencast.com/t/oRdhlucJ","# Dataset Description

<p>Los datos provienen de un proyecto de Microsoft para predicci¨®n LOS en el que se utiliza una base de datos para evaluar varios modelos. Los datos fueron aportados por una instituci¨®n que trabaja con diferentes hospitales en Estados unidos. A la base de datos se les realiz¨® un remuestreo de las variables rcount y lengthofstay que son el n¨²mero de readmisiones en los ¨²ltimos 180 d¨ªas y el LOS resepectivamente. Tambi¨¦n se realiz¨® un remuestreo de las variables continuas para hacer que sus distribuciones fueran normales y se realizaron correcciones de problemas que se ten¨ªa con las fechas de altas y n¨²mero de documentos<p>
<h2>Descripci¨®n de archivos</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>datos_los_train.csv</strong> -Es el conjunto de datos que se utiliza para entrenar modelos</li>
<li><strong>datos_test_nooutput.csv</strong> - Es el conjunto de datos para los que se deben hacer las predicciones, con base en las predicciones de este conjunto de datos se eval¨²an los desempe?os de los modelos. ESte conjunto de datos no cuenta con la variable respuesta, el resultados del desempe?o de las predicciones solo se conocer¨¢ al subir <ensp>las predicciones a la plataforma</li>
<li><strong>sample_Submission.csv</strong> -Este archivo tiene un ejemplo de como se deben enviar las predicciones de los modelos</li>
<li><strong>descripcion_campos.csv</strong> - En este archivo se tiene una descripci¨®n de cada uno de los campos en la base de datos</li>",True,True
3520,stats202-autumn-2020,"# Description

This data set is derived from the South African Heart data set in Elements of Statistical Learning

Our goal is to predict the binary Status (a simulated response) from the other features, with particular interest in seeing if our new assay is predictive of disease or not. A good model here will both predict well and give your colleague an effect of the predictive power of this new assay. You will be given a second followup dataset for a final estimate of the predictive power of this new assay.

Our features (which have all been standardized) are:

<ul>
<li>old_assay: a previous biological measurement used to predict this disease;
</li><li>gold_standard: standard-of-care predictive score for this disease;
</li><li>assay: a new assay developed in lab you are working with.
</li><li>BP: a measure of blood pressure;
</li><li>smoking: a measure of cumulative tobacco use;
</li><li>alcohol: a measure of alcohol consumption;
</li><li>cholesterol: a measure of cholesterol;
</li><li>behavior: a behavioral measure measuring risk for this disease;
</li><li>BMI: standardized body mass index;
</li><li>age: subject age
</li></ul>
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is CategorizationAccuracy.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
A valid submission will be a CSV file with predicted label called Category having value TRUE or FALSE, for each of the IDs in the test data. Your file must have columns Category and ID.


<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Category,ID<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2> File descriptions </h2>
<ul>
<li>train_data.csv - the training set -- CSV file
<li>test_data.csv - the test set (minus the outcome Status) -- CSV file
<li>sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format -- CSV file
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li>Status: disease status -- 0 or 1
<li>old_assay: a previous biological measurement used to predict this disease;
<li>gold_standard: standard-of-care predictive score for this disease;
<li>assay: a new assay developed in lab you are working with.
<li>BP: a measure of blood pressure;
<li>smoking: a measure of cumulative tobacco use;
<li>alcohol: a measure of alcohol consumption;
<li>cholesterol: a measure of cholesterol;
<li>behavior: a behavioral measure measuring risk for this disease;
<li>BMI: standardized body mass index;
<li>age: subject age
</ul>",True,True
3521,imc2021la3,"# Description

# Source of data

The data set is an altered part of another database. It will be used specifically for this competition.

The database has 51 independent variables and one categorical dependent variable which is the one to be predicted and which takes value from a total of 5 labels or categories.

The dependent variable is categorical and therefore it should be faced as a **classification problem**.
There are 11893 and 13875 patterns in the training and generalization sets, respectively. These two sets are labelled. A subdivision can be made on the training set to create a validation set (highly recommended strategy). Both sets have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model.

Finally, an unlabelled set called *kaggle.dat* is provided which only contains the data of the 51 independent variables (input).

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest certainty (highest CCR) the dependent variable (output) from the *kaggle* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate.

#Participants

In this competition, only students of IMC subject can participate. To participate, students must use an identifier of the type ""`IMC2021{userUCO}`"". For those who already have a user account on kaggle they will have the options of: changing their Username and Display Name fields in their user profile, or creating a new account.

We recommend the option of creating a new account to avoid confusion with name changes.

#Rewards

Guided by the maxim ""*the important thing is to participate*"", we, the teachers and collaborators of the subject, would like the majority of students enrolled in the course to be involved.

Therefore, the qualification of the practice will be increased by 0.5 points for all those people (or teams, in this case the points would be distributed as indicated by the members) who are among the 75% of the best private scores of the *LeaderBoard* at the end of the competition.

Three competitions will be held throughout the course. Those who obtain the best scores in the private *LeaderBoard* of each competition will obtain an extra score in the final grade of the practical part of the course according to the following list:
- First place in the final ranking: 1.5 extra points in the practical part of the course.
- Second place in the final ranking: 1.0 extra points in the practical part of the subject.
- Third place in the final ranking: 0.75 extra points in the practical part of the course.

The final ranking is constructed as follows: points will be awarded according to the position obtained in each competition and according to the number of participants in that competition. So, in a competition where 20 people have participated, the person who has occupied the first position in the private ranking will get 20 points, the second person 19 points, the n person will get 20-n+1 points.

The sum of the points from the competitions held during the four-month period will be the way to rank the participants in the final ranking.

# Acknowledgements

All thanks go to the members of the AYRNA research group for supporting and making this type of project possible.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

# Source of data

The data set is an altered part of another database. It will be used specifically for this competition.

The database has 51 independent variables and one categorical dependent variable which is the one to be predicted and which takes value from a total of 5 labels or categories.

The dependent variable is categorical and therefore it should be faced as a **classification problem**.
There are 11893 and 13875 patterns in the training and generalization sets, respectively. These two sets are labelled. A subdivision can be made on the training set to create a validation set (highly recommended strategy). Both sets have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model.

Finally, an unlabelled set called *kaggle.dat* is provided which only contains the data of the 51 independent variables (input).

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest certainty (highest CCR) the dependent variable (output) from the *kaggle* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate.",True,True
