3524,docu-class,"# Description

**Goals of the project and Report**

The first goal of this project is to provide a classifier for documents using a neural network with one layer a.k.a. a perceptron, which is a classifier based on a non-linear activation applied to a linear model. 
The second goal is to compare two methods to achieve the first goal: standard gradient descent (GD) and stochastic gradient descent (SGD) on this problem. You should discuss the effectiveness of these methods: which one would you recommend in which situation? 
You report should briefly present the problem, describe these three methods, show the results and discuss them. It should contain three main sections: 1. Introduction, 2. Methods, 3. Comparison and Discussion. 


**Problem description**

For each document x{i} (i=1,2,...,n), you are given its class f(i) in {1,2,...,r} where the function f : {1,...,n} --&gt; {1,...,r} : i --&gt; f(i) gives the topic of document i. 

You want to find the parameters (W,b) that minmize 

sum__i  L( phi(W^T x{i} + b) , y{i} ) [+ lambda ||W||_F^2]

where 

* y_{i}=e_{f(i)}, where e{j} is the jth unit vector (1 in position j, 0 otherwise), 

* L(a,b) is the loss; for example, 
  L(a,b) = ||a-b||^2 (least squares), 
	    
* phi(.) is the non-linear activation of the neural network; for example, 
  phi(x) = max(0, x) (rectified linear unit ReLU), or 
         phi(x)  = 1 / (1 + exp(-a*x) ), where a is a constant 					(logistic function). 


You will be evaluated on the proportion of properly estimated topics for documents for which you do not know the topic. 

In this project, we focus on L(a,b) = ||a-b||^2 (least squares) and phi(x)  = 1 / (1 + exp(-a*x) ) (logistic function). 


**Going further**

If you have time, there are numerous avenues to explore this problem further; in particular analyzing the effect of the different parameters on the performance of the method: 
- the learning rate, 
- the parameter in the sigmoid a, 
- the non-linear activation, 
- the objective function, 
- the number of layers, 
- the initial point chosen for the start the algorithm. 
The use of regularizers (to avoid overfitting) could also be considered. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The quality is evaluated using the percentage of correctly classified documents within the 20 categories.","# Dataset Description

This file contains, in matlab format, 

- A matrix Xts of dimension 43586x13960 where each row corresponds to a word and each column to a document. 

- A vector yts of dimension 13960x1 that provides the class into which each column of Xts belongs to. There are 20 classes so that yts(i) in {1,2,...20} for all i. 

- A matrix Xvr of dimension 43586x5989 which is the validation test for which you have to provide predictions. 

I have also attached an example of a submission file where all 5989 documents are predicted to belong to the first topic.",True,True
3525,cs-43615361-in-class-competition,"# Description

<h2>Goal</h2>
<p> The goal of this competition is to predict the flux in next 30 minutes. </p>
<p> Have fun :) </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean Square Error (MSE)</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submissions to the Kaggle leaderboard must take a specific form. Submissions should be a csv with two columns, ""ID"" and ""Prediction"" and 55822 rows corresponding to the test observations. Including the header row, there will be 55823 rows in the submission file. The ""ID"" is a unique numeric identifier from ""1"", ""2"", ... to ""55822"". The ""Prediction"" is your predicted flux for the test set.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2> Data Description </h2>
<p> Each row of x_train (and x_test) represents fluxes for 1 hour and y_train represents the maximum flux in next 30 minutes. </p>",True,True
3526,uni-ml-course-competition,"# Description

<p>One of your friends owns a hairdressing salon. He struggles with customers who make an appointment but then do not show up. Since he knows that you recently attended a machine learning class, he asks for your help.</p>

<p>Task: </p>
<p>In your study group, set up a ML model to predict ¡±no-shows¡± at your friend's hairdressing salon. 
Develop algorithms to identify customer churn probabilities. Train your data with the train.csv file provided and find the best algorithm to classify ""no-shows"" in terms of AUC by testing your selected algorithm(s) on the provided test.csv file. </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the AUC score. The group that comes up with the best AUC score wins this competition.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Predicted<br>134,0.56<br></pre>
<p>You can find an exemplary submission file in the ""Data"" section.</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>
<p>For your submission file it is important that</p>
<li> the column names are specified exactly as in this example</li>
<li>the column ""Id"" is in factor format</li>
<li>the column ""Predicted"" is in numeric (double) format.</li>
</ul>","# Dataset Description

<p><p>The dataset includes appointments at a hairdresser salon of a friend of yours. The data is already split into training and test dataset.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>hairdresser_training.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong> hairdresser_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - Unique ID for each customer</li>
<li><strong>Gender</strong> - Customer gender</li>
<li><strong>Age</strong> - Customer age (standardised)</li>
<li><strong>Neighbourhood</strong> - Location of the customer</li>
<li><strong>ScheduledDay</strong> -Scheduled day of hairdresser appointment</li>
<li><strong>AppointmentDay</strong> - Actual day of hairdresser appointment</li>
<li><strong>TimeBetweenScheduleDayAppointment</strong> - Time between day when appointment was scheduled and actual day of the appointment</li>
<li><strong>WeekdayAppointmentDay</strong> - Week day of the appointment</li>
<li><strong>Student_Discount</strong> - If customer can request a student discount (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>LoyaltyCard</strong> - If customer owns a loyalty card (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>BeautyPackage</strong> - If a beauty package have been requested (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>DiscountOffer</strong> - If it is possible to apply a discount offer (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>HairDye</strong> - If customer requested a hair dye treatment (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>SMSsent</strong> -If SMS have been sent (yes -1/ no - 0) </li>
<li><strong>No_show</strong> - outcome variable, if customer showed up at the appointment date</li>
</ul>",True,True
