3536,utkml-2016-president,"# Description

<p>In this challenge, you will try to predict the winner of the popular vote during the U.S. 2016 presidential election by county based on demographic information.</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We got the original dataset from another source, and we will mention where it came from after the competition is over</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthews_correlation_coefficient""> MCC</a>
</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The submission format will be a csv file that has 2 columns, id and Winner. id corresponds to sample id in test set and Winner is a 0 or 1 for each sample</p>","# Dataset Description

<p> You are given a csv file called train.csv which you will use to model. The feature outputs vary from ratios and percentages to absolute counts (number of people who voted for democrats in 2008, etc.). There will also be NaN (Not a Number) values, or blanks for some of the samples. Not all features will necessarily be useful. It's up to you to decide how to deal with those.</p>
<p>The label column is the Winner column. 0 represents Democrats and 1 represents Republicans. The goal is to create a submission file exactly like the sample submission file so you have a 0 or 1 for every row in the test.csv file. The leaderboard you see is not the true ranking, but it should be close.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
