3560,ml-competition-43isg3301v,"# Description

# Machine Learning Competition

Hello! 
Your goal is to predict the interest rate, the column labelled `int_rate`, as best as possible. How you go about this is entirely up to you, whether you want to go with algorithms from `sklearn`, build a neural network (NN) with `tensorflow` or `pytorch`. 

## Challenges  
While the task of prediction is quite simple in principle, this is complicated by a few things:

### A Large Set of Features
If you've done any machine learning before, you might be used to using every feature available, however you might have a tough time If you are to use all 144 features available on this dataset. Spending some time with the dataset and determining what you believe to be useful/non-useful is a challenge of its own. 

### Raw Data
Unfortunately, the data is very *dirty* and so one of the first goals should be to get in a nice clean form that your algorithm or NN can be trained with. Again, you are free to do this as you please as there is no correct way to clean data, what matters is that you can train something on it. For example, what's an algorithm meant to do to train on a string?

### Lots of Data (A bad thing?)
While you might hear that data is gold and the more the better, that relies on having the compute power to use it all. You may want to quickly iterate on ideas, train new architectures or try different models, waiting 2 hours to each time is going to be time prohibitive. 

## Some pointers
* While you are free to use whatever programming language and libraries you want, `sklearn`, `keras`, `tensorflow` and `pytorch` are some of the typical python machine learning libraries used. They all rely heavily on `numpy` for vector and matrices operations, have a look there as well!
* Data cleaning and processing can be tedious, especially if you try to implement it yourself. Tools such as Excell can help or if you prefer a more programmatic approach, `pandas` is an excellent python library for dataset management.
* Preprocessing data (as opposed to cleaning data) can drastically improve the performance of certain kinds of models, `sklearn` provides a lot of this [see here](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html).
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation
As described on the **Data** page,the full \\( \mathcal{D}\_{full} \\) has been split into a training set \\( \mathcal{D}\_{train} \\) and a test set \\( \mathcal{D}\_{test} \\).

The \\( n \\) instances in \\( \mathcal{D}\_{test} = \\{ x\_1, \ldots, x\_n \\} \\) have the labels \\( \\{ y\_1, \ldots, y\_n\\} \\) (these labels are the `int_rate`). 

Your goal is to generate the set of predictions \\( \\{p\_1, \ldots, p\_n \\} \\) as close as possible to these true labels.

The final score is the **Mean Absolute Error** (MAE) of your solution. Intuitively this is just the average distance between each of your predictions and the true label, it is calculated as
\\[ \text{MAE} = \frac{1}{n}(\,\left|  y\_1 - p\_1 \right| + \ldots + \left|  y\_n - p\_n \right|\,) = \frac{1}{n}\sum\_{i = 1}^{n}\left| y\_i - p\_i \right| \\]

where \\( \left| y\_i - p\_i \right| \\) can be interpreted as the absolute difference between the prediction \\( p\_i \\) and the true label \\( y\_i \\).
----------------------
# Resource Links

### Sklearn Algorithms:
Some sklearn algorithms you can use:
* https://scikit-learn.org/stable/supervised_learning.html

### Sklearn Preprocessing:
Any preporcessing of the dataset you might need:
* https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html

### Anaconda
A great way to manage Python and the libraries:
* https://www.anaconda.com/products/individual

### Pandas
For processing the csv file or reading in the .h5 format:
* https://pandas.pydata.org/getting_started.html

---
*If there are any other good links you think should be added, feel free to send an email to EddieBergmanHS@gmail.com*","# Dataset Description

# Data format

The data has `145` columns, `144` are the features and the goal is to predict the label column `int_rate`, (interest rate, located in column `6`). Of course you don't have to use all `144` features and some are unusable anyway which is why some data cleaning is required before training any model.

The data has been split into two main files, `p1_train.csv` and `p1_test.csv`. 

If you use Python, the library [pandas](https://pandas.pydata.org/) has a special compressed format it can use `.h5`. The data is also available in this format as `p1_train.h5` and `p1_test.h5`. This is much faster for loading and storing data than the plain-text `csv` format but feel free to use whichever you prefer, the data contained is the same in both.

## `p1_train`
This is the dataset you can use in whichever way you see fit to train a model to predict the `int_rate` column. It has `904267` rows and it is made from `80%` of the full data.

## `p1_test`
The remaining `20%` of the data has been reserved as test data. This consists of `144` columns with the column `int_rate` removed. Your submission should contain the predicted `int_rate` for each of the `226067` rows present.

## `p1_test_mean_preds`
This is a sample submission in which the predicted `int_rate` is just the mean `int_rate` of the training data.
It contains two columns `id | int_rate`.
You can see from the file that the submission contains the id's `0, ... ,226067` along with the corresponding `int_rate` in the second column. You need to use the same structure for your own submissions.",True,True
3561,geostatistics-lutetia-week-2020,"# Description

The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates.

The competition consist in predicting the Cobalt top soil concentration at the validation locations
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean squared error (MSE). The MSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ MSE = \sum_{i=1}^n (Z_i-Z^{\star}_i)^2 \]</p>
<p> where Z_i is the actual value, unknown and hosted here, Z_i* is the predicted value and i is the index.</p> 
<p>The MSE measures the accuracy of the prediction. The lower the better!</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> submission files should contain two columns: the index of the predicted site and the predicted cobalt concentration </p>","# Dataset Description

<p> The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates. 
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
Two of the provided files will be useful
<ul>
<li><strong>jura_pred.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>jura_val_loc.csv</strong> - validation locations and covariables</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong> Xloc </strong> X coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong> Yloc </strong>Y coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong>Landuse</strong> Land use: 1: Forest, 2: Pasture (Weide(land), Wiese, Grasland), 3: Meadow (Wiese, Flur, Matte, Anger), 4: Tillage (Ackerland, bestelltes Land) </li>
<li><strong>Rock</strong> Rock Types: 1: Argovian, 2: Kimmeridgian, 3: Sequanian, 4: Portlandian, 5: Quaternary. </li>
<li><strong>Cd</strong> mg cadmium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Co</strong> mg cobalt kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cr</strong> mg chromium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cu</strong> mg copper kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Ni</strong> mg nickel kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Pb</strong> mg lead kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Zn</strong> mg zinc kg^-1 topsoil</li>
</ul>",True,True
