3573,aimdatathon,"# Description

-![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F366544%2Fa92c218a1d6213ff15bd8c77d2e917e3%2FDBBR%20Roswell%20banner.PNG?generation=1604631049515387&amp;alt=media)


### What is the AI in Medicine Datathon?

48-hour datathon that focuses on cancer patient's health through the dataset from the Roswell Park DataBank and BioRepository Shared Resource. The challenge is to create a model that uses a dataset of various cancer cases which includes multiple clinical variables such as cancer stage and morphology to predict patient survival.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

- **Tumor Information_train.csv** - Train dataset with the label ( PatientStatus ).

- **Tumor Information_test.csv** - Unlabelled test dataset.",True,True
3574,limited-data-deep-learning,"# Description

<p>In this competition, you have to train a model that recognize food objects in an image. Your goal is to output bounding boxes for objects.
</p>
<br>
<p>
For each image in the test set, you must predict a list of boxes describing objects in the image. 
Submissions are evaluated by computing mean Average Precision (mAP). The participants will be ranked on this final metric.
</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>
The ranking on the leaderboard is the 50% of the testing packs. Your final score will be decided by the rest 50% after the end of the competition.
</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<pre>Id,Expected<br>imagename,[food,confidence, x1, y1, x2, y2]<br></pre>
<h2>Example Submission</h2>
<pre>Id,Expected<br>2020-04-30_199.jpg, [food, 1.0, 935, 478, 1277, 762]<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>
Images folder contain the images <br>
Labels folder contain the annotations
</p>",True,True
3575,slregression2020,"# Description

<p>What predicts an article popularity? In this competition, you'll be using data from Mashable (mashable.com), a digital media website, defined (by themselves) as a leading source for news, information &amp; resources for the Connected Generation.</p>
<p>Time magazin noted Mashable as one of the 25 best blogs in 2009, and has been described as ""one stop shop"" for social media. As of November 2015, it has over 6,000,000 Twitter followers and over 3,200,000 fans on Facebook. In this problem, you'll use data from thousands of articles published by Mashable in a period of two years to see which variables predict the popularity of an article.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated on the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). The RMSE is calculated as:</p>
<p>\[RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum^n_{i=1}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i})^{2}}\]</p>
<p>Where y_i denotes the shares of an article and yhat_i denotes the corresponding prediction.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Shares. The submission should be a csv file. The Id should just be the corresponding observation Id column from the dataset. The shares column should be the predicted shares for that article Id.</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,shares<br>1,300<br></pre></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>",True,True
3576,slclassification2020,"# Description

<p>El proyecto consiste en predecir los diferentes perfiles de sus clientes de tarjetas de cr¨¦dito,
con el prop¨®sito de poder implementar las pol¨ªticas de mitigaci¨®n del riesgo apropiadas para el
producto. En este orden de ideas, el inter¨¦s principal del proyecto es poder modelar la probabilidad
de que un cliente entre en default (imposibilidad para pagar la deuda adquirida), teniendo en
cuenta su informaci¨®n financiera, comportamiento crediticio e informaci¨®n demogr¨¢fica. El banco
cuenta con la siguiente informaci¨®n para sus clientes del a?o 2017 La variable de respuesta es
default.payment.next.month, que es igual a 1 si existe default y 0 de lo contrario.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is AUC. The AUC is a commonly used evaluation metric for binary problems like this one. The interpretation is that given a random positive observation and negative observation, the AUC gives the proportion of the time you guess which is which correctly. It is less affected by sample balance than accuracy. A perfect model will score an AUC of 1, while random guessing will score an AUC of around of 0.5.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Probability. The submission should be a csv file. The Id should just be the corresponding observation Id column from the dataset. The Probability column should be the predicted probability of the outcome 1 for that observation Id.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:.</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Probability<br>1,0.8<br></pre></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>TrainClas.xlsx</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>TestClas.xlsx</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>DataDes.txt</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>",True,True
