3671,codeml-challenge4,"# Description

<h1> ????? Hello here! welcome to Kaggle!</h1>
<p> Difficulty: Medium (Less guidance) </p>
<h2> New Intro </h2>
<p> ** For those new to Kaggle ** watch the video below to see how a competition goes normally <br>

<a href=""https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CM2VskTenJc""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2F062953624781141846d43dbfbe50870a%2FCapture.PNG?generation=1605797218139781&amp;alt=media""> </a> <br>

Once you are ready to start the competition, click on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4""> Join competition </a> button to create an account and access the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/data""> competition data </a>. Next, check out the tutorial <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/alexisbcook/titanic-tutorial""> Titanic by Alexis Cook </a> which takes you step by step on how to make your first submission!  and also that of <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/yassineghouzam/titanic-top-4-with-ensemble-modeling""> Notebook Yassine Ghouzam </a>


</p><h2> The challenge: </h2>
<p> Consumers have left comments (texts) of the last product they just used. We would like to know if it is possible to determine if the comments are positive or negative in the future for other products. </p>
<p> This challenge is simple: use machine learning techniques to create a model that predicts what the positive or negative feedback is on using old feedback collected </p>
<p> So the data here is text and what you will do is close to the branch of machine learning language processing </p>.
<p> A notebook is given in the Notebook section and it contains a start of the word processing that you can do before sorting the text (it is just a track not the solution) 
</p> <p> This model that you will allow us in the future to have an automatic positive or negative feedback because the comments will immediately go through your algorithm </p>

<h2> Presentation of how Kaggle competitions work </h2>

<h3> 1.Join the competition </h3>
<p> Read the challenge description, accept the contest rules and access the contest dataset. </p>

<h3> 2.Start working </h3>
<p> Download the data, create models on it locally or on Kaggle Kernels (our Jupyter Notebooks environment without configuration and customizable) and generate a prediction file. You can also work locally on your computer directly, we will only ask for the prediction files </p>

<h3> 3. Make a submission </h3>
<p> Upload your prediction as a quote on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score. </p>

<h3> 4. Look at the Leaderboard </h3>
<p> See how your model ranks against other Kagglers in our rankings. </p>

<h3> 5. Increase your score </h3>
<p> Check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/discussion""> discussion board </a> for lots of tutorials and ideas from other competitors. 

</p><h2> What data will I use in this contest? </h2>
<p> In this competition you will have access to 3 similar datasets which include the text comment to classify. One dataset is titled `train.txt` and the other titled` label.txt` and the last one `test.txt`. <br> <br>

</p><li> <strong> train.txt </strong> - training data, separate comments with newlines \ n </li>
<li> <strong> train_label.txt </strong> - training responses, class comments in the same order as the texts in separate train.txt with newlines \ n </li>
<li> <strong> test.txt </strong> - data for the test always comments separated by newlines </li>
Using your models and the train.txt data, predict whether any comments in test.cv are positive or not. <br> <br>

Visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/data""> ¡°Data¡± </a> competition tab to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you've created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your model stands in our rankings against other Kagglers. <p> </p>

<h2> How to submit your prediction to Kaggle </h2>
<p> Once you are ready to submit an application and be in the leaderboard: <br> <br>
1. Click on the ""Submit predictions"" button <br>
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2Fcbd6642aa72d7264b912eb7b192ef4d5%2Fscreen1.png?generation=1600955792312915&amp;alt=media"" alt=""Media""><br>
2. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You can submit 10 submissions per day.
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2F4e5c05145c265cab77bf2f7349803ea1%2Fscreen2.png?generation=1600955792270010&amp;alt=media"" alt=""Media""><br>

</p>
<h3> Format of the submission file </h3>
<p>
You must submit a csv file with exactly the number of lines as test.csv and more than one header line. Your submission will display an error if you have additional columns or rows. <br> <br>

The file must have exactly 2 columns: <br> <br>

- TextId (sorted in any order) <br>
- Rating (Positive or negative) (contains your binary predictions: 1 for positive, 0 for negative)
</p>
<h2> Ok! I am ready to begin. Where can I get help if I need it? </h2>
<p>
For help with the contest: <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/discussion""> Discussion forum </a> of the competition <br>
Technical help: page <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact""> Contact us Kaggle </a> <br> <br>

Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team, so you will generally find that you receive a response more quickly by posting your question in the appropriate forum. The forums are full of useful information on data, metrics, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you will find that others will share a lot too!
</p>

<h2> A final word on Kaggle Notebooks </h2>
<p>
As we mentioned earlier, Kaggle Notebooks is our Jupyter Notebooks environment with no customizable configuration and a large repository of community published data and code. <br> <br>

In each competition, you will find many publicly shared notebooks with incredible information. It is an invaluable resource that is worth familiarizing yourself with. Check out the notebook from this competition <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/notebooks""> here </a>.
</p>
<h2> ???¡â Ready to compete? Join the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4""> competition here! </a> </h2>
<h2> Acknowledgments </h2>
<p> We thank Kaggle Team for providing this dataset, and for his introduction page Alexis Cook </p>.

