</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2> (Fran?ais plus bas)</h2>
<h2> Objective </h2>
<p> It's your job to predict whether a feedback is positive or not. <br>
For each element of the test set, you must predict a value of 0 or 1 for the associated text and put the results in the same order as the texts. </p>

<h2> Metric </h2>
<p> Your score is the percentage of error you make in the classification (Mean Absolute Error)</p>

<h2> Submission file format </h2>
<p>
You must submit a csv file with exactly one header called testLabels and the same number of predictions as the number of comments in test.txt. Your submission will display an error if you have additional columns or rows. <br>

The file must have exactly 2 columns: <br>

id: which will determine which example this corresponds to <br>
testLabels (any order) <br>
</p>
<pre> id, testLabels <br> 10,0 <br> 1900,1 <br> 204,0 <br> Etc. </pre>
<p> You can download a sample submission file (all_positive_submission.csv) from the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/data""> Data </a> page. </p>

___________

<h2> Objectif </h2>
<p> C'est votre travail de pr¨¦dire si un retour d'information est positif ou non. <br>
Pour chaque ¨¦l¨¦ment de l'ensemble de test, vous devez pr¨¦dire une valeur de 0 ou 1 pour le texte associ¨¦ et mettre les r¨¦sultats dans le m¨ºme ordre que les textes. </p>

<h2> m¨¦trique </h2>
<p> Votre score est le pourcentage d'erreur que vous faites dans le classement (Erreur absolue moyenne)</p>

<h2> Format du fichier de soumission </h2>
<p>
Vous devez soumettre un fichier csv avec exactement un en-t¨ºte appel¨¦ testLabels et le m¨ºme nombre de pr¨¦dictions que le nombre de commentaires dans test.txt. Votre soumission affichera une erreur si vous avez des colonnes ou des lignes suppl¨¦mentaires. <br>

Le fichier doit avoir exactement 2 colonnes : <br>

id : qui d¨¦terminera l'exemple correspondant ¨¤ <br>
testLabels (n'importe quel ordre) <br>
</p>
<pre> id, testLabels <br> 10,0 <br> 1900,1 <br> 204,0 <br> Etc. </pre>
<p> Vous pouvez t¨¦l¨¦charger un exemple de fichier de soumission (all_positive_submission.csv) ¨¤ partir de la page <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge4/data""> Data </a>. </p>","# Dataset Description

<h2> (Fran?ais plus bas) </h2>
<h2> The data in detail: </h2>
The data was divided into 3 groups:
<li> <strong> train.txt </strong> - training data, separate comments with newlines \ n </li>
<li> <strong> train_label.txt </strong> - training responses, class comments in the same order as the texts in separate train.txt with newlines \ n </li>
<li> <strong> test.txt </strong> - data for the test always comments separated by newlines </li>
The training data should be used to create your machine learning models. When it comes to training data, we provide results to predict for each feedback. In our case here, your model must use the reviews you have pre-processed and the text class to predict whether a comment is positive or not </strong>.

Test data should be used to see how your model is performing on data it has not yet seen. For the test set, we do not provide predictable results for each feedback. It's your job to predict those outcomes. For each text in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether the product received a positive review or not. <br> <br>

We also include <strong> all_positive_submission.csv </strong>, a set of predictions that assume all reviews are positive. This <strong> all_positive_submission.csv </strong> file is an example of what your submission file should look like. <br> <br>

<p> Notes Variables (Reminder) </p>
<p> The different comments in train.txt are separated with newlines \ n '</p>
<p> Your results must be in csv format and contain a column with a header named 'testLabels' and the rest of the rows of prediction values ??in any order(Kaggle take care of the order). They should be values ??between 0 or 1 (0: Negative, 1: Positive) </p>

_____________________________

<h2> Les donn¨¦es en d¨¦tail : </h2>
Les donn¨¦es ont ¨¦t¨¦ divis¨¦es en 3 groupes :
<li> <strong> train.txt </strong> - donn¨¦es relatives ¨¤ la formation, commentaires s¨¦par¨¦s avec des nouvelles lignes \ n </li>
<li> <strong> train_label.txt </strong> - r¨¦ponses de formation, commentaires de classe dans le m¨ºme ordre que les textes dans un train.txt s¨¦par¨¦ avec des nouvelles lignes \ n </li>
<li> <strong> test.txt </strong> - les donn¨¦es pour le test sont toujours des commentaires s¨¦par¨¦s par des nouvelles lignes </li>
Les donn¨¦es de formation doivent ¨ºtre utilis¨¦es pour cr¨¦er vos mod¨¨les d'apprentissage sur machine. En ce qui concerne les donn¨¦es de formation, nous fournissons des r¨¦sultats ¨¤ pr¨¦voir pour chaque retour d'information. Dans notre cas ici, votre mod¨¨le doit utiliser les commentaires que vous avez pr¨¦trait¨¦s et la classe de texte pour pr¨¦dire si un commentaire est positif ou non </forte>.

Les donn¨¦es de test doivent ¨ºtre utilis¨¦es pour voir comment votre mod¨¨le se comporte sur des donn¨¦es qu'il n'a pas encore vues. Pour l'ensemble des tests, nous ne fournissons pas de r¨¦sultats pr¨¦visibles pour chaque commentaire. C'est ¨¤ vous de pr¨¦dire ces r¨¦sultats. Pour chaque texte de l'ensemble de tests, utilisez le mod¨¨le que vous avez form¨¦ pour pr¨¦dire si le produit a re?u un avis positif ou non. <br> <br>

Nous incluons ¨¦galement <strong> all_positive_submission.csv </strong>, un ensemble de pr¨¦dictions qui supposent que tous les avis sont positifs. Ce fichier <strong> all_positive_submission.csv </strong> est un exemple de ce ¨¤ quoi doit ressembler votre fichier de soumission. <br> <br>

<p> Notes Variables (rappel) </p>
<p> Les diff¨¦rents commentaires dans train.txt sont s¨¦par¨¦s par des nouvelles lignes \ n '</p>
<p> Vos r¨¦sultats doivent ¨ºtre au format csv et contenir une colonne avec un en-t¨ºte nomm¨¦ ""testLlabels"" et le reste des lignes de valeurs de pr¨¦diction dans n'importe quel ordre (Kaggle va s'en occuper tant que les ids sont l¨¤). Les valeurs doivent ¨ºtre comprises entre 0 et 1 (0 : N¨¦gatif, 1 : Positif) </p>",True,True
3672,upgrad-rise-hackathon-for-data-science-and-tech,"# Description

Imagine you are sitting at your home and watching your favourite netflix show or busy completing your course work. Suddenly you receive a message saying that ¡°Your purchase of a ¡®X¡¯ product is successful¡± . Wouldn¡¯t that be frightening when you have no knowledge of the transaction in the first place?

Can you use artificial intelligence to decide the fate of the transaction and proactively predict the fraudulent transaction(and decline them), saving the customers millions of dollars every year.

The task of this competition is to build machine learning models to predict the probability of a transaction being fraudulent, denoted by the binary target - fraud_flag.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>In this competition you are predicting the probability that a transaction is fraudulent, as denoted by the binary target - fraud_flag </p>


<h2>Features and Description</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Amt_Transaction </strong> - transaction payment amount in USD</li>
<li><strong>ProductCD  </strong> - product code, the product for each transaction</li>
<li><strong>card1 - card6  </strong> - payment card information, such as card type, card category, issue bank, country, etc.</li>
<li><strong>addr </strong> - address</li>
<li><strong>dist  </strong> - distance</li>
<li><strong>C1-C14 </strong> - counting, such as how many addresses are found to be associated with the payment card, etc. The actual meaning is masked.</li>
<li><strong>D1-D15  </strong> - timedelta, such as days between previous transactions, etc.</li>
<li><strong>M1-M9  </strong> - match, such as names on card and address, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Vxxx,Wxxx  </strong> - Engineered rich features, including ranking, counting, and other entity relations.¡°For example, how many times the payment card associated with a IP and email or address appeared in 24 hours time period, etc. - Numerical Features¡±</li>
<li><strong>P_ and (R__) emaildomain  </strong> - purchaser and recipient email domain</li>
<li><strong>The Transactiondatetime_insec feature is a timedelta from a given reference datetime (not an actual timestamp) </strong></li>
<li><strong> id01 to id11 are numerical features for identity, such as device rating, ip_domain rating, proxy rating, etc. Also it recorded behavioral fingerprint like account login times/failed to login times, how long an account stayed on the page, etc</strong></li>

</ul>

<h2>Categorical Features</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>P_emaildomain</strong> </li>
<li><strong>R_emaildomain</strong> </li>
<li><strong>M1 - M9</strong> </li>
<li><strong>ProductCD</strong> </li>
<li><strong>card1 - card6</strong> </li>
<li><strong>addr1, addr2</strong> </li>
<li><strong>DeviceType</strong> </li>
<li><strong>DeviceInfo</strong> </li>
<li><strong>id_12 - id_38  </strong> </li>
</ul>

<p> Please note transactionid is unique column representing transaction ( Not to be used in Prediction) </p>


<h2>Files </h2>
<li><strong>train.csv  </strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv  </strong> - the test set (you need to predict the fraud_flag value for these observations)</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv   </strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>",True,True
3673,codeml-challenge5,"# Description

<h2>Fran?ais plus bas</h2>
<h1> ????? Hello here! welcome to Kaggle!</h1>
<p> Difficulty: Medium (Less guidance) </p>
<h2> New Intro </h2>
<p> ** For those new to Kaggle ** watch the video below to see how a competition goes normally <br>

<a href=""https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CM2VskTenJc""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2F062953624781141846d43dbfbe50870a%2FCapture.PNG?generation=1605797218139781&amp;alt=media""> </a> <br>

Once you are ready to start the competition, click on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5""> Join competition </a> button to create an account and access the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5/data""> competition data </a>. Next, check out the tutorial <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/alexisbcook/titanic-tutorial""> Titanic by Alexis Cook </a> which takes you step by step on how to make your first submission!  and also that of <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/yassineghouzam/titanic-top-4-with-ensemble-modeling""> Notebook Yassine Ghouzam </a>


</p><h2> The challenge: </h2>
<p> You have a zip file of images of 10 differents objects: **'airplane', 'automobile', 'bird', 'cat', 'deer', 'dog', 'frog', 'horse', 'ship', 'truck' **</p>
<p> This challenge is simple: use machine learning techniques to recognize these 10 different classes </p>
<p> So the data here is a zip file containing multiple images in PNG format and labels for training in text file format. </p>.

<h2> Presentation of how Kaggle competitions work </h2>

<h3> 1.Join the competition </h3>
<p> Read the challenge description, accept the contest rules and access the contest dataset. </p>

<h3> 2.Start working </h3>
<p> Download the data, create models on it locally or on Kaggle Kernels (our Jupyter Notebooks environment without configuration and customizable) and generate a prediction file. You can also work locally on your computer directly, we will only ask for the prediction files </p>

<h3> 3. Make a submission </h3>
<p> Upload your prediction as a quote on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score. </p>

<h3> 4. Look at the Leaderboard </h3>
<p> See how your model ranks against other Kagglers in our rankings. </p>

<h3> 5. Increase your score </h3>
<p> Check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5/discussion""> discussion board </a> for lots of tutorials and ideas from other competitors. 

</p><h2> What data will I use in this contest? </h2>
<p> In this competition you will have access to 4 files. Read the data part for more precisions.<br> <br>

</p> - train__images.zip  - training data, multiple images in PNG format each. The images are ordered with an index number (Image_00.png , Image_01.png, Image_1090.png, ....) <br> <br>
- train_label.txt - training responses, class comments in the same order as the images index separated with newlines \ n <br> <br>
- test__images.zip - data for the test containing images in png format but no answers.<br> <br>
Using your models and the train_images.zip data, predict each class for any images in test_images.zip<br> <br>

Visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5/data""> ¡°Data¡± </a> competition tab to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you've created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your model stands in our rankings against other Kagglers. <p> </p>

<h2> How to submit your prediction to Kaggle </h2>
<p> Once you are ready to submit an application and be in the leaderboard: <br> <br>
1. Click on the ""Submit predictions"" button <br>
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2Fcbd6642aa72d7264b912eb7b192ef4d5%2Fscreen1.png?generation=1600955792312915&amp;alt=media"" alt=""Media""><br>
2. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You can submit 15 submissions per day.
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5344680%2F4e5c05145c265cab77bf2f7349803ea1%2Fscreen2.png?generation=1600955792270010&amp;alt=media"" alt=""Media""><br>

</p>
<h3> Format of the submission file </h3>
<p>
You must submit a csv file with exactly the number of lines as the number of images in test_images.zip. You can see a sample submission file also in the data part named 'all_five_submission.csv' . Your submission will display an error if you have additional columns or rows. <br> <br>

The file must have exactly 2 columns: <br> <br>

- ImageId (sorted in any order) <br>
- Class of image (0 to 9)
</p><h2> Ok! I am ready to begin. Where can I get help if I need it? </h2>
<p>
For help with the contest: <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5/discussion""> Discussion forum </a> of the competition <br>
Technical help: page <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact""> Contact us Kaggle </a> <br> <br>

Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team, so you will generally find that you receive a response more quickly by posting your question in the appropriate forum. The forums are full of useful information on data, metrics, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you will find that others will share a lot too!
</p>

<h2> A final word on Kaggle Notebooks </h2>
<p>
As we mentioned earlier, Kaggle Notebooks is our Jupyter Notebooks environment with no customizable configuration and a large repository of community published data and code. <br> <br>

In each competition, you will find many publicly shared notebooks with incredible information. It is an invaluable resource that is worth familiarizing yourself with. Check out the notebook from this competition <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5/notebook""> here </a>.
</p>
<h2> ???¡â Ready to compete? Join the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/codeml-challenge5""> competition here! </a> </h2>
<h2> Acknowledgments </h2>
<p> We thank Kaggle Team for providing this dataset, and for his introduction page Alexis Cook </p>.

