3686,mock-competition-2-ai-funaab,"# Description

*This challenge was designed by Data Science Nigeria specifically for the DSN Bootcamp 2019, which takes place 19-24 November 2019, and it was originally hosted on Zindi [here](https://zindi.africa/competitions/data-science-nigeria-2019-challenge-1-insurance-prediction)*

### Description of the challenge:

Recently, there has been an increase in the number of building collapse in Lagos and major cities in Nigeria. Olusola Insurance Company offers a building insurance policy that protects buildings against damages that could be caused by a fire or vandalism, by a flood or storm.

You have been appointed as the Lead Data Analyst to build a predictive model to determine if a building will have an insurance claim during a certain period or not. You will have to predict the probability of having at least one claim over the insured period of the building.

The model will be based on the building characteristics. The target variable, Claim, is a:

- `1` if the building has at least a claim over the insured period.
- `0` if the building doesn¡¯t have a claim over the insured period.

**You are strongly encouraged to go over to the [original competition's page](https://zindi.africa/competitions/data-science-nigeria-2019-challenge-1-insurance-prediction) and check up the discussions forum to have a strong understanding of what is required of you and to improve your models' performances.**

[Check this out](https://www.kaggle.com/rkkaggle2/logistic-regression-0-72-accuracy-score-eda) if you need a decent guide notebook to get started.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the Area Under the ROC curve (AUC).

Your sample submission should look like:

```
Customer Id Claim
H0             1     
H10000         0
H10001         1
```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions 

- train.csv** - the training set.
- **test.csv** - the test set.
- **sampleSubmission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.
**VariableDescription.csv** - supplemental information about the data.",True,True
3687,ifest-demo-3,"# Description

<h1>This is an example Kaggle InClass competition setup. The problem data is generated using a simple linear model with Gaussian noise on the predictors only, as with sensing noise.

Your challenge is to best estimate the noisy linear relationship between independent (a.k.a. input, predictor) variables x1, x2, x3 and the dependent (a.k.a. output, target) variable y. Additionally, there is an identifier ""id"" for each row. Sample training data (train.csv) is given. You'll compute a linear model from it, apply that model to the testing data (test.csv), and submit that data according to the given submission format (submissionExample.csv).</h1>


----------------------
# Evalution

<p>Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean Squared Error.
Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

id,y

5001,

5002,

...

10000,

</p>","# Dataset Description


<h2>File descriptions</h2>",True,True
