3707,sufenlp-20fall,"# Description

1. The task is to predict the label of a given sentence. For example, if the input sentence is ""ÂÒÍ¿Ð´ºÓÎ÷Â·ÇàÁúÇÅÎ÷ÄÏ²àÂÒÍ¿Ð´"", it's related label is apparently the ""·Ç·¨Ð¡¹ã¸æ"". 
2. Each piece of data consists of ID, Sent, and a related label. 
3. You are given 20000 sentence pairs for training, 20000 sentence pairs for testing.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use accuracy as the metric.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2981122%2F2a437ea82b66bbee5ab4d66b95abbb2e%2F1589889374(1).png?generation=1589889397803662&amp;alt=media)","# Dataset Description

# Encoding is UTF-8

**train and test csv files' delimiter is '\t' while sample_submission.csv is ',' you need also use ',' as delimiter for your submission csv file.**


- train.csv - format: idx \t sent \t label
- test.csv- format: idx \t sent \t label
- sample_submission.csv - format: idx \t label. You need to output a similar csv file whose columns are idx and label.
- label2idx.json: you can read the json file as a python dict object whose key is the label and idx is its related idx.",True,True
3708,cc4018-2021-forestfires,"# Description

Forest fires are a very important issue that negatively affects climate change. Typically, the causes of forest fires are those oversights, accidents and negligence committed by individuals, intentional acts and natural causes. The latter is the root cause for only a minority of the fires.

Their harmful impacts and effects on ecosystems can be major ones. Among them, we can mention the disappearance of native species, the increase in levels of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, earth¡¯s nutrients destroyed by the ashes, and the massive loss of wildlife. 

Data mining techniques can help in the prediction of the cause of the fire and, thus, better support the decision of taking preventive measures in order to avoid tragedy. In effect, this can play a major role in resource allocation, mitigation and recovery efforts. 

The ICFN - Nature and Forest Conservation Institute has the record of the list of forest fires occurred in Portugal for several years.  For each fire, there is information such as the site, the alert date/hour, the extinction date/hour, the affected area and the cause type (intentional, natural, negligent, rekindling or unknown).

In the files fires2015_train.csv and fires2015_test.csv, you have data on reported forest fires during 2015, for which the cause is known.

**The goal of this practical assignment is to build a machine learning model to predict the cause type of a forest fire**.

Further details on this practical assignment can be found [here](https://www.dcc.fc.up.pt/~rpribeiro/aulas/DMI2021/material/DMI-PracticalAssign_2021.pdf).
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorization Accuracy. </p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every id there should be a label in the target variable cause_type.
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,cause_type<br>1,label<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. </p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>fires2015_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>fires2015_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>fires2015_baseline.csv</strong> - a sample submission file</li>
</ul>

<h2> Data Fields</h2>

The attributes have information regarding the forest fire's alarm point and the total affected area:
<ul>
<li>id</li>
<li>region</li>
<li>district</li>
<li>municipality</li>
<li>parish</li>
<li>lat</li>
<li>lon</li>
<li>origin</li>
<li>alert_date</li>
<li>alert_hour</li>
<li>extinction_date</li>
<li>extinction_hour</li>
<li>firstInterv_date</li>
<li>firstInterv_hour</li>
<li>alert_source</li>
<li>village_area</li>
<li>vegetation_area</li>
<li>farming_area</li>
<li>village_veget_area</li>
<li>total_area</li>
<li>cause_type</li>   
</ul>",True,True
3709,matrixcompletion223,"# Description

The data consists of a subset of the entries of a low-rank matrix. Your goal is to fill in the entries marked NaN with numerical values. NaN stands for ""Not-a-Number"".

The Kaggle data comes in the form of an ordered list, but in the ""Data"" tab you will also find programs in Python and Matlab to re-shape this into a matrix to play with, and than to reshape a matrix back into the ordered list to submit. Instructions for the Matlab version below, the Python version is similar.

In order to take the Kaggle data and put it into matrix form, use the Matlab program csvReadFromKaggle.m. It takes as input the name of the csv file.

Example: M =csvReadFromKaggle('Mobserved50by200').

The matrix M now consists of numerical values for observed entries and NaN's for unobserved entries. Now (somehow) make a matrix Mhat which has as entry (i,j) your estimate of the corresponding entry the original matrix.

In order to upload your estimate to Kaggle, use the Matlab function csvWriteForKaggle. Upload the resulting .csv file to Kaggle.

Example: csvWriteForKaggle('mySubmission', Mhat).D. for providing this dataset.s dataset.<p></p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Let Mhat be the matrix in your submission and M be the original low-rank matrix.  The score is the ""Hilbert-Schmidt"" norm of the difference (this is the Euclidean norm of the vectorized matrix), multiplied by a certain scalar.  A low score is better.  A score of 0 would mean perfect matrix completion, but that's not possible, or is it?","# Dataset Description

Mobserved50by200 is a sub-sampled low-rank matrix, represented as an ordered list.  You can put this is matrix form using the included Matlab or Python files.  See ""overview for more instructions"".",True,True
3710,roadrunner-v2,"# Description

Welcome to roadRunner. Submit predictions here.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Predict test_log.csv

Prediction csv should have an ""id"" column and a ""prediction"" column with integer class values

Predictions are compared to road quality labels that were determined from experimentally collected road roughness data",# Dataset Description,True,True
3711,bio322a,"# Description

<blockquote>In vision and hearing, the wavelength of light and frequency of sound are highly predictive of color and tone. In contrast, it is not currently possible to predict the smell of a molecule from its chemical structure. This stimulus-percept problem has been difficult to solve in olfaction because odors do not vary continuously in stimulus space, and the size and dimensionality of olfactory perceptual space is unknown. Some molecules with very similar chemical structures can be discriminated by humans, and molecules with very different structures sometimes produce nearly identical percepts. Computational efforts developed models to relate chemical structure to odor percept, but many relied on psychophysical data from a single 30-year-old study that used odorants with limited structural and perceptual diversity. (<a href=""https://science.sciencemag.org/content/355/6327/820.full"">Predicting human olfactory perception from chemical features of odor molecules</a>)
</blockquote>

<p>In this challenge, you are given access to a database of responses from a subject who had sniffed a large number of molecules and been asked to rate the pleasantness of the smell. You are also given a comprehensive list of the physical and chemical features of the molecules smelled. Your task is to produce algorithms to predict the correspondence between the pleasantness of each smell and a given molecule.</p>

<p>The data set and the task is derived from the publication <a href=""https://science.sciencemag.org/content/355/6327/820.full"">Predicting human olfactory perception from chemical features of odor molecules</a>.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated with the root of the mean squared error (RMSE) over the test data:</p>
<p>\[\mathrm{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \hat y_i)^2}\]</p>
<p> where \\( y_i \\) is the ""VALENCE.PLEASANTNESS"" reported by the subject and \\( \hat y_i \\) is your prediction.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: ""Id"" and ""VALENCE.PLEASANTNESS"" that contain your predictions of the pleasantness of the molecule with the corresponding Id in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/epfl-bio-322a/data"">test_data.csv</a> file.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>""Id"",""VALENCE.PLEASANTNESS""<br>1,0<br>2,11<br>3,91<br>...<br></pre>
<p>You may want to use the R function `write.csv` to generate this file.</p>","# Dataset Description



<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>training_data.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_data.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - molecule identity</li>
<li><strong>Intensity</strong> - intensity of the odor</li>
<li><strong>VALENCE.PLEASANTNESS</strong> - how pleasant the odor is</li>
<li><strong>SWEETORSOUR</strong> - whether the odor is perceived as more sweet than sour</li>
<li><strong>other columns</strong> - physicochemical features of each of the molecules smelled by the subjects (including atom types, functional groups, and topological and geometrical properties that were computed using Dragon chemoinformatic software)</li>
</ul>",True,True
3712,bio322b,"# Description

<blockquote>In vision and hearing, the wavelength of light and frequency of sound are highly predictive of color and tone. In contrast, it is not currently possible to predict the smell of a molecule from its chemical structure. This stimulus-percept problem has been difficult to solve in olfaction because odors do not vary continuously in stimulus space, and the size and dimensionality of olfactory perceptual space is unknown. Some molecules with very similar chemical structures can be discriminated by humans, and molecules with very different structures sometimes produce nearly identical percepts. Computational efforts developed models to relate chemical structure to odor percept, but many relied on psychophysical data from a single 30-year-old study that used odorants with limited structural and perceptual diversity. (<a href=""https://science.sciencemag.org/content/355/6327/820.full"">Predicting human olfactory perception from chemical features of odor molecules</a>)
</blockquote>

<p>In this challenge, you are given access to a database of responses from a subject who had sniffed a large number of molecules and been asked to rate the pleasantness of the smell. You are also given a comprehensive list of the physical and chemical features of the molecules smelled. Your task is to predict if a new molecule smells more sweet or more sour..</p>

<p>The data set and the task is derived from the publication <a href=""https://science.sciencemag.org/content/355/6327/820.full"">Predicting human olfactory perception from chemical features of odor molecules</a>.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> Your submission is evaluated with the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC).</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: ""Id"" and ""SWEETORSOUR"" that contain your predictions of the probability that molecule with the corresponding Id in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/epfl-bio-322a/data"">test_data.csv</a> file is perceived as more sweet than sour.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>""Id"",""SWEETORSOUR""<br>1,0.1<br>2,0.99<br>3,0.31<br>...<br></pre>
<p>You may want to use the R function `write.csv` to generate this file.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Please download the training and test data <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/epfl-bio-322a/data"">here</a>.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
