3767,biws2020,"# Description

In this competition, you are required to classify various products sold in an online store into 9 classes depending on 100 features that describe each product. You may use any algorithm, tool, or software you like, but you may be asked to provide your code, which must be verifiable and reproducible.

Your predictions for the test set will be used to award marks.

The better you score on the test set (in the final private leaderboard), the higher mark you receive.

Good luck!


<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Vasily Tolkachev for creating the challange</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions will be evaluated using multiclass log-loss, based on the probabilities which you supply on each product class:</p>
<p>\[ logL = -\dfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{K} \textbf{1}_{y_{i} \in j} \log(p_{ij}) \]</p>
<p>where \(N\) is the number of observations in the test set,  \(K\) is the number of classes, \(\textbf{1}_{y_{i} \in j}\) is an in
dicator function, which is 1 if a product \(i\) is in class \(j\) and 0 otherwise. \(p_{ij}\) is the predicted probability of a product \(i\) to belong to class \(j\) </p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set
<p>Contains an <strong>id</strong> variable for a product, the features(variables) <strong>x_1, ..., x_100</strong> describing the products, each line/row being a single product. The class variable <strong>y</strong> contains the product class 1, 2, ..., 9. The classes and features have been anonymized to avoid advantages of background knowledge.</p>

<strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set
<p>Contains an <strong>id</strong> variable for a product, the features(variables) <strong>x_1, ..., x_100</strong> describing products, each line being a single product, similarly to the training set. Does not have the class variable <strong>y</strong>, which needs to be predicted for each product.",True,True
3768,previsions-de-consommation-electrique,"# Description

Le but de ce challenge est de pr¨¦voir la courbe de consommation ¨¦lectrique sur une semaine en fonction de diff¨¦rentes variables. Dans cette optique, vous ¨ºtes encourag¨¦s ¨¤ :

- S¨¦lectionner et construire de nouvelles variables explicatives ¨¤ partir des variables fournies dans les fichiers train et test. Vous pouvez aussi ajouter vos propres donn¨¦es d¨¨s lors qu'elles sont publiques et que vous en mentionnez la source dans le rapport.
- Tester diff¨¦rents mod¨¨les de pr¨¦visions (GAM).
- V¨¦rifier leur validit¨¦ en analysant des sorties visuelles et en proposant un protocole robuste de validation (type CV).
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Le crit¨¨re d'¨¦valuation est l'erreur MAPE:</p>

<p>\[ MAPE = \frac{100}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{|Y_i-\hat{Y}_i|}{|Y_i|}\,.\]</p>","# Dataset Description

-  **data_train.csv**: electricity consumption, nebulosity and temperature from January 8. 2012 to December 31. 2016 

-   **data_test. csv**: nebulosity and temperature from January 1. 2017 to December 31. 2017

-  **benchmark.csv**: submission example -- electricity consumption  from January 1. 2017 to December 31. 2017",True,True
