<h2>Data Description</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Donations.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
</ul>",True,True
3813,moroccan-darija-trigger-word-classification,"# Description

In this challenge, you have to build a model to recognize two spoken words of Moroccan Darija: ""yallahbda"" and ""safi7bess"". The data was collected in 2018 at the LIMIARF Laboratory of Mohammed V University of Rabat during a project within the IOSM Master at ENSIAS. For more details, please see this report [PFE document](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nktSKHnBdmAUk22J1-M-a6rsXzzqr7OF/view?usp=sharing).","# Dataset Description

<p>The data can be downloaded as a zip file. It contains audio files of two trigger words spoken in Moroccan Darija : ""yallahbda"" and ""safi7bess"". The speakers are of different ages and genders and speaks in different environments and speed. The audio files are in .wav format and are of different lengths. The train folder contain 144 audio files and the test folder contains 82 audio files.<p/>

<p>The train.csv file contains two columns: the id column as the filename of the files in the train folder, the label column with 0 if the trigger word is ""yallahbda"" and 1 if it is ""safi7bess"". The test.csv file contains the filenames of the audio files in the test folder without the labels column.<p/>

<p>Your submission file should be in the format of the sample_submission.csv file: For each audio file in the test set, output a single line containing the id and the Moroccan Darija word's label you predict.<p/>

<p>The evaluation metric for this challenge is the categorization accuracy, or the proportion of test audio files that are correctly classified. For example, a categorization accuracy of 0.97 indicates that you have correctly classified all but 3% of the audio files.<p/>",True,True
3814,petro-bytes,"# Description

<p> This competition expects the teams to develop an algorithm to identify Olivine grains which can take into account its PPL and XPL visible properties via feature engineering as well as using an appropriate ML pipeline for the same. Apart from this, taking into consideration the concepts behind the optical properties and their relation with crystallographic orientation is also imperative when carrying out feature engineering.</p>
<p> Olivine, a neso-silicate mineral, belongs to the 2/m 2/m 2/m crystal class of the Orthorhombic crystal system. This mineral is ubiquitous in ultramafic, ultrabasic rocks. Identification of Olivine under the petrographically microscope becomes the first and foremost step for studying it further.</p>
<h2>Problem Statement</h2>
<p> Data augmentation and preparing a dataset is key to solving challenging tasks using machine learning for identification of minerals in thin section. The dataset made available to participants contains more than 200 images of thin sections containing Olivine and other minerals. The goal is to develop an algorithm to identify/locate olivine grains from the grains of other minerals. Essentially it is a localisation task which requires the participants to first create a tool to generate appropriate dataset and further design an appropriate machine learning pipeline which is capable of solving the said localisation/segmentation task. </p><p> This competition expects the teams to develop an algorithm to identify Olivine grains which can take into account its PPL and XPL visible properties via feature engineering as well as using an appropriate ML pipeline for the same. Apart from this, taking into consideration the concepts behind the optical properties and their relation with crystallographic orientation is also imperative when carrying out feature engineering.</p>
<p> Hence, this problem statement requires the participants to make a GUI based tool to create appropriate dataset required (from the given images) for an image segmentation/localisation task to find olivine minerals amongst others in a thin section. Now, in the latter part, the participants are required to propose a detailed ML pipeline which can effectively solve the the localisation problem. Due to time constraints, participants are only required to propose the ML algorithm and not code it. However coding  the data generation tool is required.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation will be done on the basis of ease of use of the GUI interface. As a final marking criteria, the students must submit appropriate labelled data in one submission file. The labelled data must be in the form of X and Y matrices.</p>
<p> X being the inputs (images of thin sections) i.e. images in RGB format . Hence the dimensions of X should be (m,a,b,3) where m stands for number of examples (number of images in this case), a and b stands for the resolution of each image (number of pixels) and '3' stands for the number of channels which are three viz. R, G and B.  </p>
<p> Y being the outputs should contain corresponding masks for each input image. Hence, Y is a one hot encoded vector. The mask should highlight the area where olivine mineral is present i.e. should have a value '1'. It should have a value '0' at all other places. The dimensions of Y should be (m,a,b,1). 'm', 'a' and 'b' bearing the same definitions as for the input matrix X. Since, the only class of minerals to be identified is olivine, the number of channels are 1.
</p><hr>Submission Format
<p>The participating teams must submit the final version of their code (preferably in python or matlab). The teams must also submit the labelled dataset matrices 'X' and 'Y'. All of this must be in one single zip file bearing the name in the format:- 'TeamName_PetroBytes_2020.zip'</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>The dataset contains an unlabelled data of images of  thin sections containing different minerals including olivine. It is not necessary that all images will contain the olivine minerals and it is left out to the participants to discern the same.</p>",True,True
3815,uncover-mysterious-underlying-data-structure,"# Description

<p>
Data contains 77 numerical predictors, 1 numerical response.
</p>
<p>
Your objective is to build a predictive model (i.e., mapping the inputs to the lone output) using the training data, then use this model to predict (secret) responses from the test data.  
</p>
<p>
This is an exercise to test your ability to build models to detect subtle underlying structure.
</p>
<p>
IMPORTANT:  Do not include any quotes (either for the variable values or variable names) in your submission CSV file.
</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

Lowest test RMSE wins.  Simple as that.","# Dataset Description

<p>
Data contains 77 numerical predictors (aka, inputs, explanatory variables), the names start with ""x""; and 1 numerical response (aka, output, outcome), ""y"".  These data are synthetically created especially to test your ability to build models to detect subtle underlying structure.
</p>
<p>
IMPORTANT:  Do not include any quotes (either for the variable values or variable names) in your submission CSV file.
</p>",True,True
