3820,bgu-recsys-ctr-prediction,"# Description

In this challenge, we provide a data set from our large-scale recommendation system, which serves billions of recommendations per day.
Each row represents an event of recommendation and contains information about the user, the context, and the recommended item.
The rows are labeled as clicked/ not clicked.

Your task is to build a model that predicts if a user will click on an item.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Area Under the Curve (AUC)](https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5). AUC - ROC curve is a performance measurement for the classification problem at various threshold settings. ROC is a probability curve and AUC represents the degree or measure of separability. It tells how much the model is capable of distinguishing between classes. Higher the AUC, the better the model is at predicting 0s as 0s and 1s as 1s.

<h1>Submission Format</h1>
For every index in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Predicted probability between [0,1].

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
Id, Predicted
1,0.3
2,0.8
3,0.4
4,0.7
etc.","# Dataset Description

<h1>Taboola Notice</h1>
All Datasets and other materials provided by Taboola.com Ltd., (the ¡°Content¡±) are provided only for educational and informational purposes. The Content confidential, privileged and is the property of Taboola.com Ltd, its subsidiaries, and affiliates (collectively, ¡°Taboola¡±). You may not copy, reproduce, distribute, publish, display, perform, modify, create derivative works, transmit, or in any way exploit any such Content, nor may you distribute any part of this Content over any network, including a local area network, sell or offer it for sale, or use such Content to construct any kind of database other than as explicitly permitted by Taboola. Copying, storing or disseminating any Content except as provided above, intentionally or unintentionally, is expressly prohibited without prior written permission from Taboola. For permission to use the Content, please contact Legal@taboola.com. The information contained in the Content is provided on an ¡°as is¡± basis with no guarantees of completeness, accuracy or usefulness and Taboola shall not be liable for any actions taken in reliance thereon.

<br>
The train data contains supervised data for CTR prediction of specific time, user and item.

<h2>Your submission should follow the format of the sample submission file: Id , Predicted. </h2>
<ul>
<li>Id - you do not suppose to change/filter/sort.
<li>Predicted - Predict the click probability [0-1].
</ul>

<br>
The train data contains supervised data for CTR prediction of specific time, user and item.

<h2>Your submission should follow the format of the sample submission file: Id , Predicted. </h2>
<ul>
<li>Id - you do not suppose to change/filter/sort.
<li> Predicted - Predict the click probability [0-1].
</ul>

<h3>Available Features</h3>
<h4>Target item features</h4>

<ul>
<li>target_id_hash - a unique identifier of a specific item
<li>syndicator_id_hash - sponsored items are provided by syndicators (also called advertisers), and a syndicator_id is a <li>unique identifier of the syndicator of the current item.
<li>campaign_id_hash - syndicators promote campaign which may include a set of items. So each item has a single campaign, and the campaign_id_hash is a unique identifier of the item¡¯s campaign.
<li>empiric_calibrated_recs - the number of views of the current item calibrated
<li>empiric_clicks - the number of clicks on the target item
<li>target_item_taxonomy - 3rd party hierarchical taxonomy of the target
<li>placement_id_hash - Identifies the location in the publisher site in which the ad has been viewed
<li>page_view_start_time
</ul>

<h4>User features</h4>
<ul>
<li>user_id_hash - a unique identifier of a specific user
<li>user_target_recs - the number of times the user saw this target
<li>user_recs - total number of items presented to the user so far
<li>user_clicks - total number of items clicked by the user so far
</ul>

<h4>Context features</h4>
<ul>
<li>publisher_id_hash - a unique identifier of the source¡¯s publisher
<li>source_id_hash -a unique identifier of the current source
<li>source_item_type - the type of the source item - Text / Photo Gallery / Video etc.
<li>browser_platform - Desktop / Mobile / Tablet etc.
<li>os_family - Windows / Linux / IOs / Android etc.
<li>country_code
<li>region
<li>day_of_week
<li>time_of_day
<li>gmt_offset
</ul>

<h2>Note:</h2>
At the test file, there is a column named 'Id'. This column represents the original Id of the record within the original dataset (not only cold users).
You may use it if you want to. 
However, when you are submitting your prediction use the index of the test file as the Id.

<h2>In other words:</h2>
<ol>
<li> pred = clf.predict_proba(test_df)[:,1]
<li>pred_df = pd.DataFrame(pred)
<li>pred_df.reset_index(inplace=True)
<li>pred_df.columns = ['Id','Predicted']
<li> pred_df.to_csv('my_submission.csv',index=False)

</ol>",True,True
3821,mat300-prediction,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the MAT300 modeling competition (Spring 2021)! You will have the opportunity to put your regression skills to the test by predicting the logarithm of the medical costs of a customer based on their age, sex, body mass index, how many children they have, whether they smoke or not, and the region that they live in.</p>
<p>There will be prizes (and bragging rights) for the student with the best exploratory data analysis and data visualizations and the student with the most accurate model!</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the root mean squared error (RMSE) of logcharges. </p>  
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every customer in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: id and logcharges.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,logcharges<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p> There are two datasets provided to you. The file training_data.csv contains demographic information and the logarithm of the number of dollars charged to the insurance company for 936 insurance customers. You will use this data set to train your regression model.  The dataset competition_data.csv contains information for 402 additional customers, but without the logarithm of the charges to the insurance company. Use the model that you built on the first dataset to predict the cost to the insurance company for each of these 402 customers. </p> 

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given customer</li>
<li><strong>age</strong> - the age of the customer (years)</li>
<li><strong>sex</strong> - the biological sex of the customer (male or female)</li>
<li><strong>bmi</strong> - the body mass index of the customer</li>
<li><strong>children</strong> - the number of children that the customer has</li>
<li><strong>smoker</strong> - whether the customer is a smoker or not</li>
<li><strong>region</strong> - the region that the customer lives in</li>
<li><strong>logcharges</strong> - the logarithm of the amount of money charged to the insurance company for the medical care of the customer (US dollars)</li>
</ul>",True,True
3822,dauphine-datascience-ecommerce-2021-1,"# Description

<h2>Predict conversion rate</h2>

The goal of the competition is to predict the **conversion rate** of a product on a given day.

The training dataset contains data on all products of our top 10 categories for January 2020.

The test dataset contains data for February 2020 (until Feb 11).
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Weighted Root Mean Squared Error (WRMSE)**
<p>Evaluation will be performed using Weighted Root Mean Square Error:</p>
<p>\[ \epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n v_i (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2 }{\sum_{i=1}^n v_i} } \]</p>
<p>where v is the number of views (m_total_vu) of the product page, y is the conversion rate of the product and y hat its predicted value.</p>


**Submission File**
For every (s\_date, s\_product) in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: 
id and conversion_rate

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

id,conversion_rate
0,0.03
1,0.03
2,0.03
etc...","# Dataset Description

# [train|test]

One row = one product on one given day

 - `s_date`, `s_product` : primary key
 - `s_brand`: brand of the product
 - `s_family`: highest level category  on the website (Gardening, Tools, ...)
 - `s_lowest_category`: lowest level category on the website (Drills, swimming pools, ...)
 - `s_provider`: seller of the product
 - `s_retail_price`: observed retail price
 - `s_shipping_time`: estimated shipping time
 - `s_weight`: weight of the product
 - `m_nb_ratings`: number of ratings of the product
 - `m_avg_rating`: average rating of the product
 - `m_has_pdf`: product has a pdf documentation
 - `m_length_description`: length of the product description
 - `m_unit_price_vat_inc`: product price including VAT
 - `m_nb_images`: number of images of the product on the website
 - `m_total_vu`: number of views of the product page (not present in test set)
 - `conversion_rate` : `m_total_transaction / m_total_vu` (our target not present in test set)

Note that you can access the number of orders by multiplying `m_total_vu` and `conversion_rate`",True,True
3823,aws-challenge-2020-phase-2,"# Description

This Kaggle competition is for participants of the NUS AWS Challenge.

This is Phase 2, where participants are required to predict the time to death in patients with type 2 diabetes or hypertension
or hyperlipidaemia from the time of first diagnosis.

Further details of the challenge statement can be found in Challenge-Statement.pdf under Data.

Participants will have access to AWS computing tools and resources. You can get more details at:

http://medicine.nus.edu.sg/dbmi/aws-challenge.html

### Please check under rules to see how to register your team and participate.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is *Mean Squared Error (MSE)* of 
**Time\_To\_Death**

The time to death is defined as the number of years to death after the first diagnosis of type 2 diabetes or hypertension or hyperlipidaemia. If there is no death since the first diagnosis, then time to death is 0. For the train set, the death year (if any) can be found in *patientdemo\_phase2\_train.csv* under the column **Death\_Date**. For the test set, this column has been taken out.

Special Case 1: If the time to death is the same year as the diagnosis, then time to death is 1.
Special Case 2: If there is no first diagnosis, then time to death is 0.

- Example 1: If the patient develops type II diabetes on 9 Jan 2019, and dies in 2019, then number of years to death is 1
- Example 2: If the patient develops hypertension on 13 Jan 2019, and dies in 2020, then number of years to death is 2020 - 2019 = 1
- Example 3: If the patient develops hypertension on 19 Jan 2017, and dies in 2020, then number of years to death is 2020 - 2017 = 3
- Example 4: If the patient develops hyperlipidaemia on 23 Feb 2016, but does not die within the timeframe of the dataset, then number of years to death is 0.
- Example 5: If the patient has no first diagnosis of type 2 diabetes or hypertension or hyperlipidaemia in all his visits, then number of years to death is 0.

# Submission Format
For every **Patient\_No** in *submission.csv*, output the corresponding **Time\_To\_Death**

*submission.csv* contains 474 rows, and two columns (**Patient\_No**, **Time\_To\_Death**).
The first row is the header row, and there will be subsequently 473 rows of unique **Patient No**. 
Note that *submission.csv* only contains a subset of **Patient No** which appears in the entire test dataset.

# Extra: Training Set Time to Death (To check for Training Purposes)
The time to death from time of first diagnosis can be calculated from the training dataset directly itself. However, to assist you to verify if you have calculated correctly, we also provide *train\_time\_to\_death.csv*, which contains a header row followed by 2677 rows of unique **Patient No** and their corresponding **Time\_To\_Death** from the training dataset.","# Dataset Description

# Train and Test Folders
The dataset is split into a train folder (aws-challenge-phase2-train) and test folder (aws-challenge-phase2-test).

For each folder, there are five csv files:

- casevisitsyn ¨C visits and encounters
- labresultsyn ¨C lab tests and results
- patientdemosyn ¨C patient demographics
- surgicalsyn ¨C surgery and procedures
- medicationordersyn ¨C medication orders

# Dataset Description
A detailed description of the dataset can be found in **MedicalSyn-Dataset-Description.pdf**. Do note that the number of rows stated differ as that mentioned in the pdf due to the train-test split. Some columns such as **Deceased_Ind** and **Death_Date** in the test set of **patientdemosyn.csv** are deleted as they are the target metric for Phase 2.

# Diagnosis and Complications
The list of codes for diagnosis and complications relevant for this competition can be found in **Challenge-Statement.pdf**. 
The list of relevant surgery codes associated with complications can be found in **DHL surgery codes.xlsx**.

# Submission
The submission file format is **submission.csv**. The values of the column **Time\_To\_Death** is set as default to 0, and will need to be amended to the correct number of years to death (Please see Overview > Evaluation for more information). Do note that the **Patient\_No** column only has unique values, and only contains a subset of **Patient No** in the test dataset.

To check accuracy of your prediction of number of years to death in the training dataset, the correct values are given in **train\_time\_to\_death.csv**",True,True
3824,predicting-electricity-consumption,"# Description

This is a Kaggle in-class competition for the course Data Science for Business at Paderborn University. The competition is inspired by the [ASHRAE - Great Energy Predictor III](https://www.kaggle.com/c/ashrae-energy-prediction) competition. In this competition, you¡¯ll develop accurate models of metered building elecricity usage. The data comes from 100 buildings over a one-year timeframe.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Metric</h2>
<p>This competition uses root-mean-square error (RMSE) as an evaluation metric.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>
id,meter_reading<br>
3 - 2016-01-01 00:00:00,0<br>
5 - 2016-01-01 00:00:00,0<br>
</pre>","# Dataset Description

This competition challenges you to build predictive models for electricity consumption based on building metadata, historic usage, and weather data. The dataset includes hourly meter readings from 100 buildings at several different sites around the world.

**train.csv** / **test.csv**

- id - a combination of building_id and time_stamp (only present in test.csv)
- building_id - ID of the building.
- timestamp - When the measurement was taken.
- primary_use - Indicator of the primary category of activities for the building based on EnergyStar property type definitions.
- square_feet - Gross floor area of the building.
- year_built - Year building was opened.
- floor_count - Number of floors of the building.
- air_temperature - Degrees Celsius.
- cloud_coverage - Portion of the sky covered in clouds, in oktas.
- dew_temperature - Degrees Celsius.
- precip_depth_1_hr - Millimeters.
- sea_level_pressure - Millibar/hectopascals.
- wind_direction - Compass direction (0-360).
- wind_speed - Meters per second.
- meter_reading - The target variable. Energy consumption in kWh.

**sample_submission.csv**

A valid sample submission.",True,True
3825,iehack1,"# Description

<p> 
This is a simple multi(3)-class classification problem.
Do whatever you want, use whatever methods you need to (except for manual identification, we'll be going through the code, graphs, vals etc. so don't scam xd), and try to predict the correct classes that the images in the ""test"" directory belong to.  Try to implement whatever you're learned till now. 
<br><br>
 Generate a PDF (using Python to do this is in your best interest). Best of luck!
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>
Let's keep it simple. Highest accuracy wins. Don't try to overfit the model, we'll be using some out-of-dataset images to test how your model performs on more general real-world data.
</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>
The dataset is divided into the following sub-folders:
<li> Train</li>
<li> Test </li>

Train and Test are further divided into:
<li> Cats</li>
<li> Dogs</li>
<li> Pandas</li>

These 3 are the classes you must predict for your test data. Ignore the python file ""segregator.py"" in there, it was just a utility script to split the dataset into train and test.

You'll have to submit code so we'll know if you train on the test set. Do NOT train on the test set. Training is to be done only on the Train set. 
</p>",True,True
