3832,spring2021-hw1p2,"# Description

<p> In the assignment, you will use a feed forward neural network to design an end-to-end system for Speech recognition. This speech recognition will mainly include frame level classification of speech or audios as given to you. More details about the data and the implementation are given in the Hw1p2 writeup available on the course website. Data can be downloaded from the 'Data' tab on this same page on Kaggle. Good luck! </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
The evaluation metric for this competition is frame-level accuracy. There are a total of 1,618,835 frames in the test set. You will be ranked by unweighted accuracy on those phoneme state labels.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Submission files should contain two columns: Id and Label.

Id: the 0-based index of the frame in the test set [0-1618835]

Label: the predicted label of the phoneme [0-70]
Id=0 is the first frame in the first utterance. Id=1,618,835 is the last frame in the last utterance. A sample submission file is available on the Data page.

<h2>Rules</h2>
Your model MUST use an MLP (no RNNs or ConvNets). We will check your code to make sure this is the case. However, you are free to use auto-differentiation libraries like PyTorch or Tensorflow.

(Note: Support will most likely only be provided for PyTorch.)
----------------------
# Implementation Tips

<h1>Implementation Tips</h1>
<h2>Network Architecture</h2>
*You MUST use MLPs (e.g. no RNN or CNN)*

Feel free to experiment with different architectures. The simplest approach is to build a feedforward network that takes in a single frame (input size 40) and outputs probabilities across phoneme states using a softmax output to normalize the logits.

<h2>Context</h2>
Temporal context is important for distinguishing elements of speech and you can experiment with adding context to your ANN model. For example, you can try concatenating k mel spectrogram frames around the current time step. This technique would make the size of the input vector 40 * (2k + 1). You will need to adjust your data preprocessing and batching logic to adapt for frame context. Writing code that will let you experiment with variable context sizes easily is recommended.

Intuition: Since each mel spectrogram frame is only 25ms of the speech audio data, a single frame is unlikely to represent a complete phoneme. Concatenating nearby ""k"" frames will thus be helpful. Here ""k"" can be viewed as a hyperparameter for you to try out.

(Hint: This is the key to boost your score!)

<h2>Data Batching</h2>
It is important to consider that the data is provided in the form of utterance samples and not frame samples. The property of mini-batch SGD that gradients over all batches in an epoch approximate the true gradient only holds if each batch is IID (Independent and Identically Distributed). If we sample uniformly over utterances and also sample uniformly over frames within the utterance, our batches will surely not be IID. For example, different utterances will have considerably different numbers of phoneme instances. Sampling uniformly biases the training data by over-representing phonemes in the utterance with fewer phonemes (and therefore its phoneme states). You can try a different sampling technique to batch your training data to mitigate this issue.

Many approaches are possible. See what can get you to the top of the leaderboard!","# Dataset Description

<h1>File descriptions</h1>
Data files are available for download from this page.

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
train.npy - training features ;
train_labels.npy - training labels ;
dev.npy - development/validation features ;
dev_labels.npy - development/validation labels ;
test.npy - test features ;
sample.csv - sample submission in correct format ;
phonemes.txt - distinct phonemes  
<h2>Feature Files</h2>
[train|dev|test].npy contain a numpy object array of shape [utterances]. Each utterance is a float32 ndarray of shape [time, frequency], where time is the length of the utterance. Frequency dimension is always 40 but time dimension is of variable length.

<h2>Label Files</h2>
[train|dev]_labels.npy contain a numpy object array of shape [utterances]. Each element in the array is an int32 array of shape [time] and provides the phoneme state label for each frame. There are 71 distinct labels [0-70], one for each subphoneme.",True,True
3833,localizationandclassification,"# Description

<p>This is the final class assignment for *Application of Deep Learning with Limited Labeled Data* by Justin Shenk, University of Osnabr¨¹ck, WS 2020-2021 </p>
<p> You are expected to utilise and combine the knowledge of previous 2 assignments to train a robust **Food Detector and a Category Classifier** for this contest. 
</p>
<p>
You are expected to work in groups of 2 or 3 for this assignment.
</p>
<h2>Data Overview</h2>
<p>You are given 2 different datasets as training set. You are free to explore and experiment with these datasets. 
</p>
<ul>
<li> Osnabr¨¹ck Canteen dataset : Apparent from the name, this dataset is collected from Osnabr¨¹ck Canteen and has more than 12,000 images! The original dataset had 179 classes belonging to 12 categories. 
</li>
<li> Osnabr¨¹ck Cafeteria dataset : This dataset is collected from Osnabr¨¹ck cafeteria and has more than 7,000 images! The original dataset had 107 classes belonging to 12 categories. 
 </li>
</ul>
<p>
The testing dataset for this contest will be from a different canteen in Aramark. You need to make sure your models are robust in the sense that they run on a dataset which has a different distribution than the test set. 

</p>

<h2>Submission</h2>
You will have to submit the results of your model on test-videos. You can have a look at the Starter-Notebook to know more about training-datasets and submission guidelines. 

<p>
A reminder for everyone to keep your notebook private and not share your approach with other teams. 
</p>
<h2>Grading</h2>
<p>
Since the challenge is a team effort, we expect a lot of extras from bigger teams, namely (in the order of decreasing importance):
</p>
<ul>
<li>Readable, well-documented and reproducible notebook</li>
<li>Visualizations (eg, feature space with PCA via TensorBoard)</li>
<li>Fast inference time measured on standard Colab GPU (K80) for a full pipeline and/or classification network</li>
<li>Fast train time of the classification network</li>
<li>Compact models that take up less space</li>
</ul>

<p></p>

**Data Usage**
This is the Kaggle competition as the project for *Application of Deep Learning with Limited Labeled Data* by Justin Shenk, University of Osnabr¨¹ck, WS 2020-2021. The dataset is provided by VisioLab exclusively for use in this course and should not be used for anything other than this competition.

----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>
Refer the Description Page of the contest.
</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>
Refer the Description page of the contest.
</p>",True,True
