3856,3c-shared-task-purpose-v2,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the second edition of the 3C Citation Context Classification shared task based on Purpose. This year, we organise this competition as part of the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/index.html"" target=""_blank"">Second Workshop on Scholarly Document Processing (SDP) @ NAACL 2021</a>.</p>
<p>The subtask A of the 3C Shared task aims at classifying citations based on their purpose. This mutli-class classification problem has the following classes:
</p><ul>
  <li> BACKGROUND</li>
  <li>COMPARES_CONTRASTS</li>
  <li>EXTENSION</li>
  <li>FUTURE</li>
  <li>MOTIVATION</li>
  <li>USES</li>
</ul><p></p>
<p>More details about the shared task can be viewed <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/sharedtasks.html#3c"" target=""_blank""> here</a>.</p>

<p><b>Task participants are required to</b>:
</p><ul>
           <li>Develop methods addressing the task and submit the results via Kaggle</li>
           <li>Document and submit their method as a short paper as specified on the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/cfp.html#submission"" target=""_blank""><strong>SDP 2021</strong></a> website</li>
           <li>Provide source code for each method</li>

</ul><p></p>

<p>The paper and code should be submitted via <a href=""https://www.softconf.com/naacl2021/sdp2021/user/scmd.cgi?scmd=submitNew"" target=""_blank"">Softconf</a>. More details regarding the submission will be made available on the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/sharedtasks.html#3c"" target=""_blank"">SDP</a> website. Please make sure, your team is registered <a href=""https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdwWEONvPN_QzAm-vKBLM-XOSg9TM2eVcx7L0jUGcfnPmNF9w/viewform"" target=""_blank"">here</a> (in case you have not done this already).</p>

<p>The overview paper describing the first 3C Shared task and the submitted systems can be  accessed from <a href=""https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/2020.wosp-1.12/"" target=""_blank"">
here. </a>To view the last year's competition leaderboard, please click this <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/3c-shared-task-purpose/leaderboard"" target=""_blank"">
link. </a></p>
<p>To participate in the subtask B of this shared task, please click <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/3c-shared-task-influence-v2/overview/description"" target=""_blank"">here.</a></p>

<b>Kaggle Submission deadline: 30 April, 2021</b>
<b>Paper and Code Submission deadline: 17 May, 2021 (Extended)</b>

<h2>References</h2>

1. Pride, David, and Petr Knoth. ""An Authoritative Approach to Citation Classification."" Proceedings of the ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries in 2020. 2020.<br>
2. Pride, David, Petr Knoth, and Jozef Harag. ""ACT: an annotation platform for citation typing at scale."" 2019 ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries (JCDL). IEEE, 2019.<br>
3. Kunnath, Suchetha N., et al. ""Overview of the 2020 wosp 3c citation context classification task."" Proceedings of the 8th International Workshop on Mining Scientific Publications. Association for Computational Linguistics, 2020.
4. Teufel, Simone, Advaith Siddharthan, and Dan Tidhar. ""Automatic classification of citation function."" Proceedings of the 2006 conference on empirical methods in natural language processing. 2006. <br>
5. Jurgens, David, et al. ""Measuring the evolution of a scientific field through citation frames."" Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics 6 (2018): 391-406. <br>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h1>Evaluation</h1>
<p>The submitted systems will be evaluated using the withheld test data, containing 1000 instances. The evaluation metric used will be the F1-macro score and is given by:</p>

$$F-macro = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{2\times P _{i} \times R _{i}}{P _{i} + R _{i}}$$

<p>where P and R denotes the precision and recall for class i and n represents the number of classes. We chose macro f-score in light of the disproportionate distribution of output categories in our dataset and to encourage the task participants to focus on the detection of the minority classes, which are particularly crucial for advancing the field of research metrics beyond just counting citations.</p>

<p>We will use 50% of the testset for evaluation initially and this will appear on the public leaderboard as the competition progresses. The rest of the dataset will be used for the final scoring. This result will be displayed on the private leaderboard, based on which the winners will be decided.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The submission files need to be in CSV format. Each row in the submission file should contain two columns, with values corresponding to the fields, unique_id, and citation_class_label. The unique_id values are textual numbers with a preceding ""CCT"", followed by a number in the range 1 and 1000.  The predicted labels should be a single value between 0 and 5 representing the different classes. The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>unique_id,citation_class_label
CCT1,0
CCT2,0
CCT3,1
CCT4,5
CCT5,5
CCT6,4
...
...
CCT998,3
CCT999,0
CCT1000,1</pre>

<p>Please make sure that the values in the unique_id and the citation_class_labels field lie in the range mentioned above. Wrong values in the submission file could result in a failed submission. Also, verify the number of instances in the submissions is equal to 1000 before making submissions.</p>

<p>Submit your CSV file using the 'Submit Predictions' section. You can check your score from the 'My Submissions' section and choose the one to make it final score by checking the 'Use for Final Score' checkbox.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Training Dataset</h2>
<p>The labelled training dataset contains 3000 instances, annotated using the <a href=""http://oro.open.ac.uk/60670/1/JCDL2019_submission199_Pride_Harag_Knoth.pdf"" target=""_blank"">ACT platform</a>.

The dataset is provided in csv format and contains the following fields:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Unique Identifier</li>
  <li>COREID of Citing Paper</li>
  <li>Citing Paper Title</li>
  <li>Citing Paper Author</li>
  <li>Cited Paper Title</li>
  <li>Cited Paper Author</li>
  <li>Citation Context</li>
  <li>Citation Class Label</li>
</ol>
<p>where citation context represents the sentence containing the citations. Each ciation context in the dataset contains the label, ""#AUTHOR_TAG"", which represents the citation that is being considered. The citation class labels correspond to values between 0 and 5, where each value represents the following categories: <br>
<br>

0 - BACKGROUND<br>
1 - COMPARES_CONTRASTS<br>
2 - EXTENSION<br>
3 - FUTURE<br>
4 - MOTIVATION<br>
5 - USES<br>
</p>

<h2>Test Dataset</h2>
<p>
The test dataset, in cvs format, contains 1000 instances, with all the above mentioned fields, except labels.</p>


<h2>Full Text</h2>
<p>Besides the training and the test sets, we also provide the participants with the full text files corresponding to the coreid's in our dataset. This can be accessed from <a href=""https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-ttSp65NHIU-p1D4WasUV7nuG6OjVaWG/view?usp=sharing"" target=""_blank"">here</a>. The name of the text files in the full text dump represents the COREID. The participants are encouraged to use this additional information for enhancing the performance of the classifiers. The fulltext for each paper in the dataset is derived from the <a href=""https://core.ac.uk/services/api/"" target=""_blank"">CORE API</a>.</p>

<p>Any use of the above mentioned full text dataset or additional resources, besides the ACT dataset for this competition should be clearly documented in the short description paper. Also, please make sure, the short paper includes the appropriate references mentioned in this <a href=""https://core.ac.uk/about/research-outputs/#supporting-research-assessment-and-evaluation"" target=""_blank"">page</a>.</p>",True,True
3857,wsrj-ykt2021-test,"# Description

<h2>§¥§Ñ§ß§ß§í§Ö</h2>
<p>§¥§Ñ§ß §Ú§ã§Ü§å§ã§ã§ä§Ó§Ö§ß§ß§à §ã§Ô§Ö§ß§Ö§â§Ú§â§à§Ó§Ñ§ß§ß§í§Û §Õ§Ñ§ä§Ñ§ã§Ö§ä §Ü§Ó§Ñ§â§ä§Ú§â §Ú §Õ§à§Þ§à§Ó §ã §å§Ü§Ñ§Ù§Ñ§ß§Ú§Ö§Þ §è§Ö§ß§í, §á§Ý§à§ë§Ñ§Õ§Ú, §Ü§à§Ý§Ú§é§Ö§ã§ä§Ó§Ñ §Ü§à§Þ§ß§Ñ§ä §Ú §Õ§â.</p> 
<p>§±§à§Õ§â§à§Ò§ß§Ö§Ö §ã§Þ. §Ó§Ü§Ý§Ñ§Õ§í§ê Data.</p>

