3888,caltech-cs-155-2021-mp1-part-1,"# Description

## Miniproject #1.
For this Kaggle competition, you will use data about the size, location, and time of reported wildfires to predict their cause. These wildfires were reported from the states of California and Georgia from 1992 to 2015, from small flames to massive forest fires.

There can be 10 submissions per day per team and submission window will close on Wednesday 10 February 2021 at 2:00PM PST.

You may not use any additional data sources.

## Acknowledgements
We thank the national Fire Program Analysis (FPA) fire-occurrence database and Rachael Tatman for this data, in addition to Paul O'Neill for the data cleaning. This dataset was sourced from a different Kaggle competition (1.88 Million US Wildfires). **It is not permitted to visit that Kaggle page, read/use the associated Notebooks in any way, nor use any code online that was made for modeling this dataset, in order to make the competition fair. Doing so will be considered a violation of the Honor Code. Thanks for understanding!**
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is the Mean Column ROC AUC. This means we treat each column as its own binary classification problem, and calculate the area under curve (AUC) of the receiver operating curve (ROC). It allows us to measure a balance of the false positive and true positive rate. It is elaborated on [here](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html).

The equivalent [sklearn command](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html) used by the Kaggle grader would be: `sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score(y_ground_truth, y_predicted_probabilities, multi_class='ovr')`

## Submission Format

For every row of data in WILDFIRES_TEST.csv, submission files should contain five columns:
* `id`: contains integers equal to the id's in the WILDFIRES_TEST.csv file
* `P1`: contains the probability (float from 0 to 1) that the label is 1 (natural cause of fire)
* `P2`: contains the probability (float from 0 to 1) that the label is 2 (accidental cause of fire)
* `P3`: contains the probability (float from 0 to 1) that the label is 3 (malicious cause of fire)
* `P4`: contains the probability  float from 0 to 1) that the label is 4 (other cause of fire)

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,P1,P2,P3,P4
285382,0.16,0.64,0.09,0.11
285383,0.17,0.2,0.411,0.219
...
358416,0.53,0.12,0.17,0.18
```
----------------------
# Tips

Credits to Jim Zhang for helping with these tips

## Anaconda
If using Python, pandas is recommended for data manipulation. For prediction, one can start with some typical models in scikit-learn. pandas, numpy, sklearn are all included in Anaconda.

## Useful pandas functions
`pd.read_csv('filename.csv', index_col='id')`
`pd.DataFrame.to_csv('submission.csv', index_label='id')`

## Don't be afraid to try things you haven't heard of before
After some basic models, one can progress to some more advanced models in other libraries. Maybe search around a bit for cool or successful models that people have used for classification.

## IDE/Shortcuts
Learn all the shortcuts to your development environment. Spyder and Jupyter are both included in Anaconda, and there are plenty of shortcuts to run specific code sections swiftly.

## Missing data
Think of how to fill in the missing/nan data.

## Feature engineering
See if you can create new features out of the existing columns, that would make the model perform better / have an easier time fitting the data

## Model blending
Why use one model when you can take the average of multiple model outputs, or even use another model on top of the model outputs?

## Probability calibration
Some models don't have accurate classification probabilities, but one can calibrate them using various techniques. This doesn't help much for individual models due to the AUC metric, but allows for ""fairer"" model blending if you plan to take a simple average.

## Hyperparameter tuning
Gain some intuition and then try some automatic tuners! Be aware, poor model types usually will not improve much with tuning.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
* WILDFIRE_TRAIN.csv - the training set, including labels
* WILDFIRE_TEST.csv - the test set, excluding labels
* WIDFIRE_SAMPLE_SUBMISSION.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format.  There should be 5 columns: an ID column, and four columns (P1, P2, P3, P4). For example, for row **i**, columns P2 will contain the probability of sample **i** being from class 2. The four columns should sum up to 1 for each row.

## Data Fields

* `LATITUDE` = Latitude (NAD83) for point location of the fire (decimal degrees).
* `LONGITUDE` = Longitude (NAD83) for point location of the fire (decimal degrees).
* `STATE` = Two-letter alphabetic code for the state in which the fire burned (or originated), based on the nominal designation in the fire report.
* `DISCOVERY_TIME` = Time of day that the fire was discovered or confirmed to exist.
* `FIRE_SIZE` = Estimate of acres within the final perimeter of the fire.
* `FIPS_NAME` = County name from the FIPS publication 6-4 for representation of counties and equivalent entities.
* `FIPS_CODE` = Three-digit code from the Federal Information Process Standards (FIPS) publication 6-4 for representation of counties and equivalent entities.
* `SOURCE_REPORTING_UNIT_NAME` = Name of reporting agency unit preparing the fire report, based on code/name in the source dataset.
* `DATE` = Date that the fire was first discovered, in YYYY-MM-DD format
* `LABEL` = Description of the (statistical) cause of the fire.
    * `1` = Natural cause (Lightning)
    * `2` = Accidental cause (in order of decreasing frequency: Debris Burning, Equipment Use, Children, Campfire, Smoking, Railroad, Powerline, Fireworks, Structure)
    * `3` = Malicious cause (Arson)
    * `4` = Other (Miscellaneous, or Missing/Undefined)

## Task
Your task is to predict the target in `WILDFIRES_TEST.csv` to the best of your ability. It is generally encouraged to submit probabilities from your models instead of 0/1 predictions, as you get rewarded for having a non-zero probability of the correct class even if it is not the highest probability for that sample. This also represents real world conditions, where you would like to know the relative likelihoods of each class being the right one.",True,True
3889,structured-l0-time-series-hackathon,"# Description

### Data

The dataset contains the sales history of residential property from 2007 to 2018. 
The property can be of two types ¨C house or unit which may have 1,2,3 or 4 bedroom(s).

### Questions

**a.**  Consider the monthly avg prices of 1-bedroom units.
-	Plot the data and infer insights regarding the time series components by decomposing the data without using any package.
-	Perform outlier checks and missing value treatments (if needed).
-	Predict the average price of 1-bedroom units to be sold for the last 24 months.

**b.** Construct an error computation function which takes the following parameters:
?	Original Values (list of original values). Eg: [1,33,653,431,143]
?	Predicted Values (list of predicted values). Eg: [34,54,14,426,642]
?	Error Type (name of error in string format). Eg: ¡®mape¡¯.
For the different values of error type, use the error metrics you think are appropriate for time series data.
Use the above function to compute the error for any model evaluation you may be performing for question (a).

### Additional Notes:
?	Try different forecasting models for question (1) and reason out your final choice of model for forecasting. 
?	Please provide proper explanation, plots and inferences for each question, in a word document or in the R/Python notebook itself.
?	**Please note the datesold column in sample_submission.csv is different from train data (to escape system inconsistencies of datetype columns) and create your submission files in accordance to the format provided.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The error metric for evalation of predictions in this competition is MAPE.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p> The data contains the sales history of residential property from 2007 to 2018. 
The property can be of two types ¨C house or unit which may have 1,2,3 or 4 bedroom(s).</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_data.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>datesold</strong> - date on which a property has been sold</li>
<li><strong>postcode</strong> - postal code of the property</li>
<li><strong>price</strong> - price at which the property has been sold</li>
<li><strong>propertyType</strong> - type of property (house or unit)</li>
<li><strong>bedrooms</strong> - no. of bedrooms in the property</li>
</ul>",True,True
