</p><p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Please submit a .csv file with two columns, ""id"" and ""prediction"". ""id"" should have the number of the testing image as listed in the ""test_data"" directory. ""prediction"" should be 0 for ""No COVID"" and 1 for ""COVID"" for each image in the testing dataset.</p>

<p>Please reach out in the Slack if you have any questions about submission.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>The training data is broken down into two sub-directories: COVID and non-COVID. Please use these designations as your labels for your models. Note that the testing data does not contain these distinctions in sub-directories. There is roughly a 4:1 split between the training data and testing data.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>test_data</strong> - Testing data</li>
<li><strong>ct_data</strong> - Un-cleaned training data</li>
<li><strong>ct_data_clean</strong> - Cleaned training data</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data Format</h2>
<p>The cleaned training data is 100x100x1 images (meaning they are grayscale, 1 channel images). While you are allowed to preprocess the ct_data (uncleaned training set), please note that the testing data is also 100x100x1, so you need to make sure that your model takes 100x100x1 input.</p>

<p>You can find the folder with all data at this link: https://github.com/owencqueen/utkML_projects/tree/main/covid_ct_scans. Also, if you're interested in doing this challenge in a language other than Python, please let Owen know in the Slack and he can get you the labels in some other form.</p>",True,True
3905,dramancompany-researchteam,"# Description

--asdfasd1dfa1
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

--asdfasd1dfa1",True,True
3906,shaastra-wells-fargo-hackathon,"# Description

## **Competition Description**

In today¡¯s society, the superior training of human resources is a necessity. With more and more occupations requiring advanced education, a college degree is not only critical to one¡¯s success in the workforce but also towards leading a better quality of life. Many graduate from college with a sense of accomplishment and discipline, some realize their passion along the way, and almost all, leave with heightened communication and responsibility. However, there are several socio-economic factors that prevent a person from going to college or dropping out in between. Gender is one such factor that has been brought under the scanner recently with universities trying their best to fix the skewed gender ratios on their campuses by encouraging more women to apply. 

In this competition, you're challenged to build a model that predicts the headcount of male and female students enrolled in various graduate and undergraduate programs in a year. You'll be using a dataset with enrollment data for previous years.

### **Why is a model that forecasts enrollments necessary?**

Colleges are increasingly interested in identifying the factors that maximize and minimize their enrollment. These factors allow enrollment management administrators to identify the applicants who have a higher tendency to enroll at their institutions and reach out to them as well as allocate their money rewards (i.e.,scholarship and financial aid) towards encouraging those who enroll in smaller amounts. It helps the institution to make necessary changes such as building new girls' hostels and having a committee to help international students. On the whole, the university can diversify its student set and provide a more holistic educational environment.

### **Prizes worth 60K up for grabs** ###

Join [this link](https://chat.whatsapp.com/FWhIvCmq3BYI9v2PJG3rtq) for any queries.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2> Goal </h2>

It is your job to predict the unique headcount of people in that particular program of the university.


<h2>Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is root-mean-square error (RMSE). RMSE is a frequently used measure of the differences between values (sample or population values) predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>The submission files should contain two columns: Id and Unique Headcount.</strong></p><strong>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Unique Headcount<br>1,1
<br>2,1 <br>
3,0

</pre>
</strong>","# Dataset Description

<h3>Dataset description</h3> 

<p>The dataset provides you demographic data on the students of a University in Canada.</p> <p>The information is organized in rows, where each row specifies the number of students enrolled (unique headcount) with each combination of attributes.</p>

<p>The information includes:</p>
<ol>
<li>Year - 2008-09 to 2015-16</li>
<li>Term type - Fall term, spring term, winter term</li>
<li>Degree type - Undergraduate, Graduate</li>
<li>Program level - Doctoral, Masters, Non degree and Bachelors</li>
<li>Study year - D (Doctoral), M (Masters), 1, 2, 3, 4</li>
<li>Faculty grouping - ARTS, ENG (engineering), SCI (Science), MATH etc.</li>
<li>Program grouping - Economics, Psychology, Mechanical engineering etc.</li>
<li>Term type - Academic, Co-op</li>
<li>Work Term - Academic term, Work term</li>
<li>Attendance - Full-Time, Part-Time</li>
<li>Visa status - Canadian, International, Canadian Permanent Resident</li>
<li>Gender</li>
</ol>

<p>Your goal of this competition is to predict the unique headcount of students enrolled in each program based on their gender and other attributes associated with the program.</p> <p>The train and test datasets are split based on time: training data from 2008-09 to 2015-16, while test data are from 2016-17.</p> 

<h3>File description</h3>
<ol>
<li>wells_fargo_train.csv - the training set</li>
<li>wells_fargo_test.csv - the test set</li>
<li>wells_fargo_sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>",True,True
3907,3c-shared-task-influence-v2,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the second edition of the 3C Citation Context Classification shared task based on Influence. This year, we organise this competition as part of the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/index.html"" target=""_blank"">Second Workshop on Scholarly Document Processing (SDP) @ NAACL 2021</a>.</p>
<p>The subtask B of the 3C Shared task aims at classifying citations based on their influence. This binary classification problem has the following classes:
</p><ul>
  <li> INCIDENTAL</li>
  <li>INFLUENTIAL</li>
  
</ul><p></p>
<p>More details about the shared task can be viewed <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/sharedtasks.html#3c"" target=""_blank""> here</a>.</p>

<p><b>Task participants are required to</b>:
</p><ul>
           <li>Develop methods addressing the task and submit the results via Kaggle</li>
           <li>Document and submit their method as a short paper as specified on the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/cfp.html#submission"" target=""_blank""><strong>SDP 2021</strong></a> website</li>
           <li>Provide source code for each method</li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p>The paper and code should be submitted via <a href=""https://www.softconf.com/naacl2021/sdp2021/user/scmd.cgi?scmd=submitNew"" target=""_blank"">Softconf</a>. More details regarding the submission will be made available on the <a href=""https://sdproc.org/2021/sharedtasks.html#3c"" target=""_blank"">SDP</a> website. Please make sure, your team is registered <a href=""https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdwWEONvPN_QzAm-vKBLM-XOSg9TM2eVcx7L0jUGcfnPmNF9w/viewform"" target=""_blank"">here</a> (in case you have not done this already).</p>

<p>The overview paper describing the first 3C Shared task and the submitted systems can be  accessed from <a href=""https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/2020.wosp-1.12/"" target=""_blank"">
here. </a>To view the last year's competition leaderboard, please click this <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/3c-shared-task-influence/leaderboard"" target=""_blank"">
link. </a></p>
<p>To participate in the subtask A of this shared task, please click <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/3c-shared-task-purpose-v2/overview/description"" target=""_blank"">here.</a></p>

<b>Kaggle Submission deadline: 30 April, 2021</b>
<b>Paper and Code Submission deadline: 17 May, 2021 (Extended)</b>

<h2>References</h2>

1. Pride, David, and Petr Knoth. ""An Authoritative Approach to Citation Classification."" Proceedings of the ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries in 2020. 2020.<br>
2. Pride, David, Petr Knoth, and Jozef Harag. ""ACT: an annotation platform for citation typing at scale."" 2019 ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries (JCDL). IEEE, 2019.<br>
3. Kunnath, Suchetha N., et al. ""Overview of the 2020 wosp 3c citation context classification task."" Proceedings of the 8th International Workshop on Mining Scientific Publications. Association for Computational Linguistics, 2020. <br>
4. Valenzuela, Marco, Vu Ha, and Oren Etzioni. ""Identifying meaningful citations."" Workshops at the twenty-ninth AAAI conference on artificial intelligence. 2015. <br>
5. Zhu, Xiaodan, et al. ""Measuring academic influence: Not all citations are equal."" Journal of the Association for Information Science and Technology 66.2 (2015): 408-427. <br>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
<p>The submitted systems will be evaluation using the withheld test data, containing 1000 instances. The evaluation metric used will be the F1-macro score.</p>
$$F-macro = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{2\times P _{i} \times R _{i}}{P _{i} + R _{i}}$$
<p>We use F1-macro score in order to give more weight to minority classes and to encourage the task participants to focus on the detection of these minority classes, which are particularly important for advancing the field of research metrics beyond just counting citations. However, we recognize that micro-F might be a better metric if our objective was to build a classifier making the least number of mistakes in production.</p>
<p>We will use 50% of the test set for evaluation initially and this will appear on the public leaderboard as the competition progresses. The rest of the dataset will be used for the final scoring. This result will be displayed on the private leaderboard, based on which the winners will be decided.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>The submission files need to be in CSV format. Each row in the submission file should contain two columns, with values corresponding to the fields, unique_id, and citation_influence_label. The unique_id values are textual numbers with a preceding ""CCT"", followed by a number in the range 1 and 1000. The predicted labels should be a single value, either 0 or 1, representing the different classes.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre><code>
unique_id,citation_influence_label
CCT1,0
CCT2,0
CCT3,1
CCT4,0
CCT5,0
CCT6,0
CCT7,0
CCT8,1
CCT9,0
CCT10,0
......
CCT997,1
CCT998,0
CCT999,0
CCT1000,0
</code></pre>

<p>Please make sure that the values in the unique_id and the citation_class_labels field lie in the range mentioned above. Wrong values in the submission file could result in a failed submission. Also, verify the number of instances in the submissions is equal to 1000 before making submissions.</p> 
<p>Submit your CSV file using the 'Submit Predictions' section.
You can check your score from the 'My Submissions' section and choose the one to make it final score by checking the 'Use for Final Score' checkbox.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Training Dataset</h2>
<p>The labeled training dataset contains 3000 instances, annotated using the <a href=""http://oro.open.ac.uk/60670/1/JCDL2019_submission199_Pride_Harag_Knoth.pdf"" target=""_blank"">ACT platform</a>.
The dataset is provided in csv format and contains the following fields:

</p>
<ol>
  <li>Unique Identifier</li>
  <li>COREID of Citing Paper</li>
  <li>Citing Paper Title</li>
  <li>Citing Paper Author</li>
  <li>Cited Paper Title</li>
  <li>Cited Paper Author</li>
  <li>Citation Context</li>
  <li>Citation Influence Label</li>
</ol>
<p>where citation context represents the sentence containing the citations. Each ciation context in the dataset contains the label, ""#AUTHOR_TAG"", which represents the citation that is being considered. The citation class labels correspond to values 0 and 1, where <br>
<br>
<dl>   
                              <dd>0  -  INCIDENTAL</dd> <br>
                              <dd>1  -  INFLUENTIAL</dd>
  			 </dl>

</p>
<h3>Test Dataset</h3>
<p>The test dataset, in csv format, contains 1000 instances, with all the above mentioned fields, except labels. </p>

<h2>Full Text</h2>
<p>Besides the training and the test sets, we also provide the participants with the full text files corresponding to the coreid's in our dataset. This can be accessed from <a href=""https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-ttSp65NHIU-p1D4WasUV7nuG6OjVaWG/view?usp=sharing"" target=""_blank"">here</a>. The name of the text files in the full text dump represents the COREID. The participants are encouraged to use this additional information for enhancing the performance of the classifiers. The fulltext for each paper in the dataset is derived from the <a href=""https://core.ac.uk/services/api/"" target=""_blank"">CORE API</a>.</p>

<p>Any use of the above mentioned full text dataset or additional resources, besides the ACT dataset for this competition should be clearly documented in the short description paper. Also, please make sure, the short paper includes the appropriate references mentioned in this <a href=""https://core.ac.uk/about/research-outputs/#supporting-research-assessment-and-evaluation"" target=""_blank"">page</a>.</p>",True,True
3908,diamonds-data-ft-nov-2020,"# Description

&gt; The goal of this competition is the prediction of the price of diamonds based on their characteristics (weight, color, quality of cut, etc.), putting into practice all the machine learning techniques you know.

![intro](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3824396%2Fbeaf52135483332c90a86b157e178715%2Fimage%20(12).png?generation=1588785521255575&amp;alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation
The evaluation metric chosen for this competition is the [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation) (Root Mean Squared Error):

### Submit results
- Submissions are limited to 20 times a day.
- The test set is divided into a public part (with which the public leaderboard is calculated, accessible during the competition) and another private part, with which the final positions are calculated, after the end of the competition.
- Predictions will be sent in the format indicated in the `sample_submission.csv` file in the data section.

![diamond2](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3824396%2Ff6235a3402992f0fc94af9e0ca3465c2%2Finbox_3824396_4554b5824665256889dafdd5c4c59890_Bbf0GWk.jpg?generation=1588773703548655&alt=media)
----------------------
# Timeline

### This competition will end the [07/02/2021 at 11:59 PM](http://time.unitarium.com/utc/1059)
![minion](https://media.giphy.com/media/C0NhnyKT89QdO/giphy.gif)



----------------------
# Prizes

You will receive a great `applause` from your classmates and teachers ??
and `knowledge`, a bunch of it.

![prize](https://media.giphy.com/media/l2Je00TmqeLx0gavS/giphy.gif)","# Dataset Description

### Data
In this section you will find data needed to train your model and a detailed description of it.

### Files
- `diamonds_train.csv`: training set
- `diamond_test.csv`: use it **ONLY** to make the prediction
- `sample_submission.csv`: sample submission

### Features
- **id**: only for test & sample submission files, id for prediction sample identification
- **price**: price in USD
- **carat**: weight of the diamond
- **cut**: quality of the cut (Fair, Good, Very Good, Premium, Ideal)
- **color**: diamond colour, from J (worst) to D (best)
- **clarity**: a measurement of how clear the diamond is (I1 (worst), SI2, SI1, VS2, VS1, VVS2, VVS1, IF (best))
- **x**: length in mm
- **y**: width in mm
- **z**: depth in mm
- **depth**: total depth percentage = `z / mean(x, y) = 2 * z / (x + y) (43--79)`
- **table**: width of top of diamond relative to widest point (43--95)",True,True
