3952,uclacs188,"# Description

<p>Trucking is interested in better understanding the drivers in its network. The ultimate goal is to both build a predictive model capable of determining overall network capacity to handle incoming shipping requests as well as more effectively recruit high-performing drivers to enhance their network. As a first step, they are interested in developing a classification model to better determine whether or not a driver will be a high-performing one. </p>  

<p>You and your team (yes you will be allowed to work in groups!), will serve as consultants to Trucking. You will be asked to develop a predictive model for this task and report out your findings to them. </p>

<p>This project will include both a structured component, where much like Projects 1 and 2, you will be given a specific set of instructions to complete. There will also be an unstructured contest for you to complete as well where you will be competing against your classmates to achieve the best model. </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every label in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Prediction. ID should correspond to the ID in the score dataset, and Prediction should be a 0 or 1 label.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Prediction<br>83414,1<br></pre>",# Dataset Description,True,True
