3970,business-analytics-project-regression,"# Description

Within the context of Eleme delivery service, at every moment the command platform of ""Smart Logistics"" sends orders from customers to delivery-men for instant delivery. The decision being made by the delivery-men are mainly two-fold: pick-up in store, and delivery to customers. At one specific moment, the delivery-man may receive orders assigned to him, while he has unfinished orders which are previously assigned. He would need to make a decision as to what to do next, based on his order status and geographical location. The goal is to build a model to predict the delivery-man's next move, based on his historical decision and current status.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAE-Score.  The MAE score is given by:</p>
<p>(\[MAE = \sum_{i=0}^{n}{\left|predict\_time_i - expect\_time_i\right|}\])</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every item in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain one column called ID, with a sequence from 0 to 22393. and a column of predicted expected time. The file should have a header, ""ID"" and ""expect_time"" for the two columns, and the file should have 22395 rows in total. The submission should be in CSV format</p>","# Dataset Description

### Data Overview

The data was stored in four separate folders: action, courier, distance, order. Each folder contains a dataframe, labeled by date. Here's some brief overview of some of the attributes, the rest can be explored on your own.

#### Action

The ActionStep attribute records the delivery-man's action, you may group by wave based on courier_id and wave_index. the expect_time is the timestamp when the action was completed, and can be sorted to give the courier's sequence of actions. 'courier_wave_start_lng' and 'courier_wave_start_lat' records the geographic locations. 

#### Distance

'source_type' and 'target_type' are categorical values containing three values: 'Assign', 'PickFood', and 'DeliverFood'. When type is 'Assign', the location attributes represent the courier's location; When type is 'PickFood' the location attributes represent the vendor's location; When type is 'DeliverFood' the location attributes represent the customer's location.

#### Order

| attribute     | explanation                      |

| ------------- | -------------------------------- |

| create_time   | The time the order was created   |

| confirm_time  | The time the order was confirmed |

| assigned_time | The time the order was assigned  |

| aoi_id        | Unique ID indicating an area     |

| shop_id       | Unique ID indicating a vendor    |

### Prediction

For each 'action', group by courier and wave, and check 'expect_time' attribute. The non-zero values are the actual timestamps of completed actions. Your job is to predict the timestamps of zero values in this column. Ideally, after you've predicted all timestamps, ordering by the timestamps gives you the chronological order of the actions.

The available data is from 2.1 to 2.28, and we want you to predict for 2.29",True,True
3971,business-analytics-project-classification,"# Description

Within the context of Eleme delivery service, at every moment the command platform of ""Smart Logistics"" sends orders from customers to delivery-men for instant delivery. The decision being made by the delivery-men are mainly two-fold: pick-up in store, and delivery to customers. At one specific moment, the delivery-man may receive orders assigned to him, while he has unfinished orders which are previously assigned. He would need to make a decision as to what to do next, based on his order status and geographical location. The goal is to build a model to predict the delivery-man's next move, based on his historical decision and current status.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>(\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \])</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every item in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain one column called ID, with a sequence from 0 to 22393. and a column of predicted action_type . There are two types of actions: ""DELIVERY"" and ""PICKUP"". We want you to label ""DELIVERY"" as 1 and ""PICKUP"" as 0.
The file should have a header, ""ID"" and ""action_type"" for the two columns, and the file should have 22395 rows in total. The submission should be in CSV format</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metaData.csv</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>customer_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given customer</li>
<li><strong>movie_id</strong> - the id of a movie</li>
<li><strong>movie_title</strong> - the title of a movie</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>",True,True
3972,learn-to-classify,"# Description

<p>This problem aimed at the case of customers default payments in Taiwan and compares the predictive accuracy of probability of default among six data mining methods.</p>


<p>This research employed a binary variable, <b> default payment (Yes = 1, No = 0) </b>, as the <b> response /target variable</b>. This study reviewed the literature and used the following 23 variables as explanatory variables:</p>
<p><b>Amount of the given credit (NT dollar)</b>: it includes both the individual consumer credit and his/her family (supplementary) credit.</p>
<p><b>Gender</b>: (1 = male; 2 = female).</p>
<p><b>Education</b>: (1 = graduate school; 2 = university; 3 = high school; 4 = others).</p>
<p><b>Marital status</b>: (1 = married; 2 = single; 3 = others).</p>
<p><b>Age</b>: (year).</p>
<p><b>History of past payment</b>: We tracked the past monthly payment records (from April to September, 2005) as follows: X6 = the repayment status in September, 2005; X7 = the repayment status in August, 2005; . . .;X11 = the repayment status in April, 2005. The measurement scale for the repayment status is: -1 = pay duly; 1 = payment delay for one month; 2 = payment delay for two months; . . .; 8 = payment delay for eight months; 9 = payment delay for nine months and above.</p>
<p><b>Amount of bill statement (NT dollar)</b>: X12 = amount of bill statement in September, 2005; X13 = amount of bill statement in August, 2005; . . .; X17 = amount of bill statement in April, 2005.</p>
<p><b>Amount of previous payment (NT dollar)</b>: X18 = amount paid in September, 2005; X19 = amount paid in August, 2005; . . .;X23 = amount paid in April, 2005.</p>

<p>You have to train a classifier model to predict the values of default_payment as 0 or 1 for the test data</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every instance in the test dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and default_payment. default_payment should be a space-delimited list. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,default_payment<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>credit_card_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>cred_card_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<p>This research employed a binary variable, <b> default payment (Yes = 1, No = 0) </b>, as the <b> response /target variable</b>. This study reviewed the literature and used the following 23 variables as explanatory variables:</p>
<p><b>Amount of the given credit (NT dollar)</b>: it includes both the individual consumer credit and his/her family (supplementary) credit.</p>
<p><b>Gender</b>: (1 = male; 2 = female).</p>
<p><b>Education</b>: (1 = graduate school; 2 = university; 3 = high school; 4 = others).</p>
<p><b>Marital status</b>: (1 = married; 2 = single; 3 = others).</p>
<p><b>Age</b>: (year).</p>
<p><b>History of past payment</b>: We tracked the past monthly payment records (from April to September, 2005) as follows: X6 = the repayment status in September, 2005; X7 = the repayment status in August, 2005; . . .;X11 = the repayment status in April, 2005. The measurement scale for the repayment status is: -1 = pay duly; 1 = payment delay for one month; 2 = payment delay for two months; . . .; 8 = payment delay for eight months; 9 = payment delay for nine months and above.</p>
<p><b>Amount of bill statement (NT dollar)</b>: X12 = amount of bill statement in September, 2005; X13 = amount of bill statement in August, 2005; . . .; X17 = amount of bill statement in April, 2005.</p>
<p><b>Amount of previous payment (NT dollar)</b>: X18 = amount paid in September, 2005; X19 = amount paid in August, 2005; . . .;X23 = amount paid in April, 2005.</p>",True,True
3973,mdss-basic-stream,"# Description

<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>Text classification is a key step for managing research into user activity in platforms such as Facebook, Twitter or related social media portals. In this mini-Datathon, you are given some tweets crawled from Twitter, the task is to develop classification models which can make predictions and return the corresponding label fields of the source. Different from coarse grained classification tasks like sentiment analysis, this is a fine-grained classification task where there are 4 filed classes in total. There are many machine learning methods that can be used in the classification task. They can be categorised into supervised method (like SVM) and unsupervised method (like clustering). </p>
<br>
<h2>The aim of this challenge </h2>
<p>Challenge is to develop a classifier that can assign a set of tweet text to their corresponding labels as accurately as possible.</p>

<h2>Challenge Description</h2>
<br>
<a href=""https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/104cehTPSfjKvtcPTFVypEd5Whw6DLZqE?usp=sharing"">Link to Challenge Description</a>
<br>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank the Faculty of IT, MGA and Monash Talent for their support in hosting this mini-datathon .</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision""> Accuracy Score </a>. The accuracy score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the number of observations that are correct as a result. The accuracy score is given by:</p>
\[ Accuracy = \frac{(number\;of\;correct\;predictions)}{(number\;of\;all\;predictions)} \]

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>all the groups are required to submit file in the below format</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: the <strong>test id column<strong>, and <strong>label<strong> column. </strong></strong></strong></strong></p><strong><strong><strong>
<p>Remember the first row of your file should be ¡°Train_id¡± and ¡°label¡±.</p>
<pre>Train_id,label<br>1,""covid""<br></pre></strong></strong></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><p>Below is the data files required for the competition, they are divided into train and test datasets. All tweets are in english and anything that goes against this is to be considered data errors and obstacles. a few special non UTF-8 charecters have been removed and the text has been normalised as well. The file columns and data are given below.</p>


<h2>Data fields</h2>
<h3>Data fields-train</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>created_at</strong> - tweet creation time</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the tweet data/text </li>
<li><strong>label</strong> - the categorical label of a tweet</li>
</ul>
<br>
<h3>Data fields-test</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given customer</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the text tweets</li>
</ul>",True,True
