3980,dataton-dataknow,"# Description

<h2>Reto</h2>
<p>Los datos corresponden a una compa?¨ªa del sector retail, para el canal comercial TAT Tienda a Tienda, donde la empresa realiza ventas por toda Colombia, a trav¨¦s de 5 principales regionales (Centro, Bogot¨¢, Santander, Sur, Norte) </p>

<h2>Conjunto de Datos</h2>
<p>El conjunto de datos corresponde al hist¨®rico de ventas de los ¨²ltimos 3 a?os, a nivel de facturaci¨®n semanal por Regional y Referencia SKUs. Contamos con un hist¨®rico de 157 semanas, desde 01/01/2018 hasta 03/01/2021  en el formato dd-mm-aaaa.

La necesidad del cliente, es realizar la proyecci¨®n de ventas a nivel de REGIONAL-REFERENCIA (Ver Conjunto de datos) para un horizonte de 12 semanas. Es decir, para los meses de Diciembre 2020, Enero y Febrero 2021. </p>

<h2>Objetivo del Reto</h2>
<p>Tener un pron¨®stico en unidades de ventas acertado, que logre ajustar y optimizar compras evitando tener mercanc¨ªa ociosa o faltante. Generando pron¨®sticos de ventas en funci¨®n de modelos de an¨¢lisis de series de tiempo, implementando metodolog¨ªas como ESM, SARIMAX, CLM, RNN, entre otros.

Generando un archivo plano con la estructura requerida por el evaluador a los niveles de (Regional/SKU) y cuyo desempe?o este medido por el SMAPE.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>El conjunto de datos corresponden al hist¨®rico de ventas de los ¨²ltimos 3 a?os, a nivel de facturaci¨®n semanal por Regional y Referencia (SKUs). Contamos con un hist¨®rico de 157 semanas, desde 01/01/2018 hasta 03/01/2021 (dd/mm/yyyy).

La necesidad del cliente, es realizar la proyecci¨®n de ventas a nivel de regionales y referencias para un horizonte de 12 semanas.</p>
<h2>Descripci¨®n de los archivos</h2>
<p> La descripci¨®n de los archivos y su detalle se encuentra disponible en el Data Explorer de la Competencia (Ver abajo) <p>",True,True
3981,cs-475-spring21-mnist-small,"# Description

<p>This is a noisy handwritten digit recognition competition.</p>
<h2> Data </h2>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Each sample is a 24x24 grayscale image. We vectorize each image as a 1x576 vector and stack N samples to represent the dataset as a matrix X of size Nx576. Each label takes value y in the set {0,1,2,...,9}.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>Sometimes it will be convenient to represent each label as a one-hot-vector, with one element set to one and the rest zero. The dataset labels can then be represented as a matrix, Y \in {0,1}^{Nx10} such that the sum of each column adds up to 1.</p>
<h2> File format </h2>
<pre>&lt;pre&gt;/small_train/data array (400, 576) [float]
/small_train/labels array (400, 10) [int]
/val/data array (2000, 576) [float]
/val/labels array (2000, 10) [int]
/kaggle/data array (1000, 576) [float]&lt;/pre&gt;</pre>
<p>There are three datasets:
large_train: training data (7000 samples)
val: validation data (2000 samples)
kaggle: kaggle competition test data (1000 samples, no labels provided)</p>

<p>
You should train on the training data and validate your models using the validation data. Once you are happy with your performance, you should predict the samples in kaggle and submit the results (see Evaluation).</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy.</p>

<h2>
Submission Format
</h2>

<p>
For every image in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and predicted label. Id is the index of each image in the gaggle set and predicted label is one of {0,1,2,...,9}.
</p>

<p>
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
</p>

<pre>Id,Prediction
0,2
1,5
2,6
3,0
4,2
</pre>",# Dataset Description,True,True
