4002,hackathon-isae-2021-patch-retrieval,"# Description

# Hackathon 2021

# Description

Given a set of satellite image time series (i.e. images acquired at the same location but at different dates) and a query image, we aim to identify the time series where the query image belongs to. Even though the problem scenario seems straightforward, the solution is not! It is worth noting that several factors can make this problem more complicated such as atmospheric conditions, seasonal changes, occlusions produced by clouds. Additionaly, we performed some transformations on the query image to make the problem more challenging :)

An example of time series retrieval is provided in the figure below. An image displaying the San Francisco's coast has been transformed by applying a 90-degree rotation and a brightness level change. Ideally, the proposed model should be able to predict that the query image belongs to the time series 1. To do so, the proposed model should be invariant to this kind of transformations (e.g. rotation, horizontal/vertically flip, random contrast jitter, occlusions, etc).

![Alt text](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/24889239/109431366-841fc880-7a06-11eb-99b1-96f2cbb1641b.png)

# Data
During this Hackathon, you will work with Sentinel-2 image time series. For the sake of simplicity, data is provided in png format along with a pandas dataframe. We selected 42 tiles (i.e. 100km x 100km areas on the Earth's surface) containing several regions of interest such as the Amazon rainforest, the Dead Sea, the city of Los Angeles, the Great Sandy Desert, circular fields in Saudi Arabia, among others.

From these tiles, we extracted 5000 time series each of which consists of 12 images whose shape is 224 x 224 x 3 (height x width x number of channels). Each time series is split into two time series: one containing 8 images and the other one keeping the remaining 4 images. These 8-image-length time series are collected to create a time series dataset. On the other hand, the remaining images are transformed via a data augmentation function (e.g. rotation, horizontal/vertically flip, random contrast jitter, etc). As explained previously, the main goal is to find the time series ID where these transformed images belong to. Therefore, the time series dataset consists of 5000 time series and the image dataset consists of 20000 images.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Mean average precision at 5 (MAP@5)","# Dataset Description

Des s¨¦ries temporelles de patchs sont fournies en entra?nement.
 L¡¯objectif est de d¨¦terminer ¨¤ quelle s¨¦rie temporelle appartient un patch futur,
¨¦galement ces patchs peuvent ¨ºtre transform¨¦s par des fonctions
(rotation, effacement etc.) rendant la tache plus ardue.
Pour r¨¦soudre cette tache, les ¨¦tudiants devront impl¨¦menter des r¨¦seaux de neurones
 invariants ¨¤ des transformations diverses mais ¨¦galement formuler le probl¨¨me diff¨¦remment des approches classiques en machine learning. 

Vous fournirez pour chaque patches de la base de donn¨¦e de test une liste de cinq s¨¦rie temporelle.

La m¨¦trique est la MAP@20.

**Fichier de soumission**: `sample_submission.csv`",True,True
4003,scitech-epoch-1,"# Description

# What's the challenge about?
You are a Member of a young unicorn Start-up and your CEO has approached you for help to identify who is a potential candidate for promotion and for that he has shared employees' data with you in tabular format. Earlier the process for selection of employees involved a series of training and evaluation programs, whose performance scores were later sent to HQ team for further discussion which delayed the whole process to some extent.

Now, due to time and resource constraints, Current CEO has looked up this situation and has asked your help to optimise it using your Data Science Background.

#Your Job?
To use the Data, and derive insights so that you can help your CEO with the same.

#Acknowledgement:
This dataset has been taken from Analytics Vidhya. However, data has been modified for use in this competition. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **ROC-AUC Score**. Kindly refer to the attached document to understand the metric better and use it accordingly.
(reference : https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/model_selection/plot_roc.html)","# Dataset Description

# Data Description : 

### Following Features are present in the training data. The test data obviously, does not have the final target variable 'is_promoted', which you are expected to predict.

- **employee_id**  : Unique ID for employee.
- **department** : Department of employee
- **region** : Region of employment (unordered)
- **education** :  Education Level.
- **gender**  : Gender of Employee.
- **recruitment_channel** : Channel of recruitment for employees.
- **no_of_trainings** : no. of other training completed in previous year on soft skills, technical skills etc.
- **age** :  Age of Employee.
- **previous_year_rating** : Employee Rating for the previous year.
- **length_of_service** : Length of service in years.
- **KPIs_met >80%** : If Percent of KPIs(Key performance Indicators) >80% then 1 else 0.
- **awards_won?** : If awards won during the previous year then 1 else 0.
- **avg_training_score** : Average score in current training evaluations.
- **is_promoted**  : (Target) Recommended for promotion.",True,True
