4049,nlp-for-news-headlines,"# Description

<p>Try to predict the category of news headlines! Given a news headline, try to predict the category into which this headline falls under. Some examples of categories are ""POLITICS"", ""WEIRD NEWS"", ""WEDDINGS"", and ""HEALTHY LIVING"" - just to name a few. This challenge will give you a chance to get hands-on experience with sentiment analysis, an important technique in the field of natural language processing. There are many ways that this data can be approached, so give it your best shot and see how well you can do!</p>

<p>We will release the source for this dataset after the competition is over.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

For this challenge, we are using the basic accuracy metric. i.e. ( number of correct predictions ) / ( total test samples ). Please keep the labels in your submission as the exact same labels in the test set, i.e. the ""POLITICS"", ""WEDDINGS"", etc. labels.","# Dataset Description

<p>The data are stored in csv files.  </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>news_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>news_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>category</strong>  - labels</li>
<li><strong>headline</strong> - the text of the headline for each article</li>
</ul>",True,True
4050,us-election-twitter-mini-datathon-advanced,"# Description

<h1>Introduction</h1>
<p>Text classification is a key step for managing research into user activity in platforms such as Facebook, Twitter or related social media portals. In this mini-Datathon, you are given some tweets crawled from Twitter, the task is to develop classification models which can make predictions and return the corresponding label fields of the source. Different from coarse grained classification tasks like sentiment analysis, this is a fine-grained classification task where there are 4 filed classes in total. There are many machine learning methods that can be used in the classification task. They can be categorised into supervised method (like SVM) and unsupervised method (like clustering).
</p>
<br>
<h1>The aim of this challenge</h1>
<p>Challenge is to develop a classifier that can assign a set of tweet text to their corresponding labels as accurately as possible.</p>
<br>
<h1>Challenge Description</h1>
<a href=""https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/104cehTPSfjKvtcPTFVypEd5Whw6DLZqE?usp=sharing"">Link to Challenge Description</a>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank the Faculty of IT, MGA and Monash Talent for their support in hosting this mini-datathon .</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision""> Accuracy Score </a>. The accuracy score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the number of observations that are correct as a result. The accuracy score is given by:</p>

\[ Accuracy = \frac{(number\;of\;correct\;predictions)}{(number\;of\;all\;predictions)} \]

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>all the groups are required to submit file in the below format</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: the <strong>test id column<strong>, and <strong>label<strong> column. </strong></strong></strong></strong></p><strong><strong><strong>
<p>Remember the first row of your file should be ¡°Train_id¡± and ¡°label¡±.</p>
<pre>Train_id,label<br>1,""covid""<br></pre></strong></strong></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><p>Below is the data files required for the competition, they are divided into train and test datasets. All tweets are in english and anything that goes against this is to be considered data errors and obstacles. the data is divided into 3 different formats and will all need to be used, the data. All reverse engineering attempts will be considered as an offense of the rules and will lead to immediate disqualification, the codes will be reviewed after the competetion ends. The file columns and data are given below.</p>

<br>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<h3>Data fields-train</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>created_at</strong> - tweet creation time</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the tweet data/text </li>
<li><strong>label</strong> - the categorical label of a tweet</li>
</ul>
<br>
<h3>Data fields-test</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train_id</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given tweet</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the text tweets</li>
</ul>",True,True
