4060,swiss-german-court-rulings,"# Description

## Task Details
The legal language is very special in many regards compared to regular natural language. It is highly structured, rather complicated, contains its own special terms, and uses certain words differently than they are used in regular text.

Text classification is simple to define but has a myriad of possible applications and good systems can provide immense value. Common general applications of text classification include for example:
- Spam filtering
- Email priority rating
- Topic classification

And in the legal domain:
- Legal judgement prediction (predict outcome of a case based on description of case's facts)
- Legal area prediction

So in this task, you will predict the chamber based on the text of a court decision. The chamber is structured in the form of {federal level}_{court}_{chamber number} (e.g. SG_KG_002 => St. Gallen, Kantonsgericht, 002).

## Expected Submission
Please submit a csv file containing the ids of the test set and your associated predicted chambers.

## Evaluation
Your solution will be rated by the achieved F1-Score.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

### Context

This dataset has been created as part of the NRP 77 research project [""Open Justice vs. Privacy""](https://www.digitale-nachhaltigkeit.unibe.ch/research/artificial_intelligence/nrp77/index_eng.html). 

### Content

The dataset contains 100 decisions of each of the 10 most frequently occurring court chambers of Swiss German court decisions downloaded from the website of entscheidsuche.ch. The dataset has been downloaded from entscheidsuche.ch/docs on February 1st, 2021, and then subsequently been converted into raw text files and cleaned basically.

### Acknowledgements

The court decisions have been scraped from the court websites by the association entscheidsuche.ch.

### Inspiration

Can the exact court be predicted only by having access to the court decision of a certain court?

### File Descriptions
- train.csv - the training set
- val.csv - the validation set
- test_public.csv - the test set
- sampleSubmission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format
- court_chambers.json - the hierarchy of possible court chambers

### Data fields
- id - an anonymous id unique to a given court decision
- text - the basically cleaned text from the court decision
- chamber - the chamber id of the court decision in the form {federal level}_{court}_{chamber}",True,True
4061,ece597-697,"# Description

This is a dataset consisting of a training set of 60,000 examples and a test set of 10000 examples. Each example is a 28x28 grayscale image, associated with a label from 10 classes

Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255. The training and test data sets have 785 columns. The first column consists of the class labels (see above), and represents the article of clothing. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.

To locate a pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27. The pixel is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix.




##Labels

Each training and test example is assigned to one of the following labels:

0 T-shirt/top
1 Trouser
2 Pullover
3 Dress
4 Coat
5 Sandal
6 Shirt
7 Sneaker
8 Bag
9 Ankle boot


TL;DR

Each row is a separate image
Column 1 is the class label.
Remaining columns are pixel numbers (784 total).
Each value is the darkness of the pixel (1 to 255)</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

Some things to keep in mind:

1. Know how to load the data. The label column indicates the label for each image

2. Each row in test and train combined is a unique image with a unique ID

3. Use the train.csv to generate your models and validation.csv for testing

4. Use the test to generate your submission.csv file 

The labels of test.csv are hidden from you all. The goal is to generate a submission.csv that is like sample.csv but with correct labels",True,True
4062,dsia2021,"# Description

### Erreur dans le d¨¦ploiement et la comp¨¦tition ne peut ¨ºtre supprim¨¦e. Les donn¨¦es ont ¨¦t¨¦ neutralis¨¦es et une autre compet d¨¦ploy¨¦e.

- - -
Il s'agit d'une comp¨¦tition priv¨¦e ouverte aux ¨¦tudiants de l'unit¨¦ DSIA-4301A de la fili¨¨re ""[Datascience et intelligence artificielle](https://perso.esiee.fr/~bercherj/dsia)"" de ESIEE Paris, aux ¨¦tudiants du [master D2SN](https://www.cfadescartes.fr/formation/master-data-science-numerique-d2sn/) qui suivent ¨¦galement cette unit¨¦, ainsi qu'aux autres ¨¦tudiants et personnels de l'¨¦cole.

## Probl¨¨me pos¨¦

Un concours d'entr¨¦e ¨¤ un groupe d'¨¦coles d'ing¨¦nieurs comprend une note sur dossier. Le but est de pr¨¦dire la note sur dossier en fonction de l'ensemble des donn¨¦es fournies. Au-del¨¤ de cela, il s'agit de comprendre quels sont les param¨¨tres d¨¦terminants dans l'¨¦valuation du dossier, voire d'approcher ou retrouver l'algorithme. 

Les donn¨¦es ont ¨¦t¨¦ compl¨¨tement anonymis¨¦es et les diff¨¦rentes notes d¨¦cal¨¦es de quantit¨¦s al¨¦atoires.

Votre but est donc, ¨¤ partir de l'ensemble des donn¨¦es disponibles, de pr¨¦dire la note de dossier obtenue par un candidat. 

Pour traiter le probl¨¨me, vous pouvez utiliser l'ensemble des m¨¦thodes vues en cours et au-del¨¤. Vous devez analyser les donn¨¦es, ¨¦ventuellement imaginer et cr¨¦er de nouvelles variables explicatives (feature engineering) par r¨¦gression ou par analyse de texte text analytics, fusionner des tables, regrouper les donn¨¦es clustering, PCA, utiliser des approches non lin¨¦aires, etc¡­ Il faudra vous montrer inventif et rigoureux, notamment dans le choix de vos param¨¨tres et en ¨¦valuant syst¨¦matiquement les performances de vos choix¡­

## Evaluation 

Pour les ¨¦l¨¨ves de l'ESIEE et du master, l'¨¦valuation comprendra une part sur la performance pure (¨¦valu¨¦e par le RMSE), une part de rapport sur la construction et la validation de vos algorithmes, et une part sur l'analyse critique des donn¨¦es, l'importance des variables dans le  r¨¦sultat, et commentaires.A) Le score obtenu ¨¤ cette comp¨¦tition d¨¦pendra des performances du meilleur des 3 mod¨¨les que vous s¨¦lectionnerez en fin de comp¨¦tition. Ces performances seront ¨¦valu¨¦es sur une partie (50%) cach¨¦e de la base de test. 

B) En outre, vous devrez t¨¦l¨¦charger, via Moodle/Blackboard 

1- un fichier vide dont le nom est le pseudo que vous avez utilis¨¦ sur kaggle (pour v¨¦rification)
2- un rapport sur la fa?on dont vous vous y ¨ºtes pris (analyse des donn¨¦es, features engineering, choix des algorithmes, choix des param¨¨tres, validation)
3 - vos trois (au maximum) scripts finaux pour ¨¦valuation et comparaison. Ces scripts devront ¨ºtre r¨¦-ex¨¦cutatbles et reproduire exactement les r¨¦sultats obtenus sur Kaggle. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Les soumissions au format correct seront ¨¦valu¨¦es selon le R2.
Rappelez-vous que 50% du test sont ¨¦valu¨¦es pour fournir le Leaderboard public. Les 50% restants, que vous n'aurez donc jamais vu, seront utilis¨¦s pour le classement final !","# Dataset Description

