4065,deep-learning-hw2p2-part-2-verification,"# Description

<p>This is part 2 Face verification for HW2P2. In addition to this, you should also finish part 1 Face classification.</p>

<p>In part 2 Face verification, you will use Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to design an end-to-end system for face verification. Your system will be given two images as input and will output a score that quantifies the similarity between the faces in these images. This helps us decide whether the faces from the two images are of the same person or not.</p>

<p>You can build on your face classification model, or use other methods like Siamese Networks. Read the writeup for more hints.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve is created by plotting the True Positive Rate (TPR) against the False Positive Rate (FPR) at various threshold settings. The Area Under the Curve (AUC) for the ROC curve is equal to the probability that a classifier will rank a randomly chosen similar pair (images of same people) higher than a randomly chosen dissimilar one (images from two different people) (assuming 'similar' ranks higher than 'dissimilar' in terms of similarity scores).</p>

<p>As mentioned in the part 2 handout, the utils.py utility is:</p>
<pre>from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score
import sys
import pandas as pd

def get_auc(y_true, y_score):
    auc = roc_auc_score(y_true, y_score)
    return auc

if __name__ == '__main__':
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    if len(args)  ""
        exit()
    score_csv = args[0]
    label_csv = args[1]
    y_score = pd.read_csv(score_csv).score.values
    y_true = pd.read_csv(label_csv).score.values
    auc = get_auc(y_true, y_score)
    print (""AUC: "", auc)</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Please refer to the writeup</p>",True,True
4066,11785-spring2021-hw3p2,"# Description

<p>The feature data of this homework are the same as homework 1 part 2. There are many variable length utterances comprised of 40 dimensional timesteps. You can use recurrent layers and their variants. You may also use convolutional layers if you so choose.</p>
<p>Your task will be to take the sequence of features and map them to a sequence of phonemes. Keep in mind the number of phonemes will not equal the number of timesteps. Refer to the write-up for more information.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric will be the Levenshtein distance (character level minimum edit distance) from the ground truth. The labels will have the sequence of phonemes, but not the alignment. To handle this CTC Loss will be used during training and a search should be used on the output distribution for prediction. More information is provided in the writeup.</p>
<pre>id,label<br>0,.N pHhEk fYszSiH.<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h>File Descriptions</h>
<p>
- phoneme_list.py: contains mapping of phonemes to indices<br>
- train.npy: training features<br>
- train_labels.npy: training labels which are phoneme sequences<br>
- dev.npy: validation features<br>
- dev_labels.npy: validation phoneme sequences<br>
- test.npy: test data features<br>
- sample_submission.csv: sample submission<br>
<\p>
<p>Please refer to the write-up.</p>",True,True
4067,cs475-sp21-hw3,"# Description

<p>In this problem, we will develop a sentiment analysis tool. We have provided a data set containing short customer reviews, or snippets of reviews, for products. Each has been labeled as a positive or negative review. For instance, below is an example of a positive review
</p>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;i downloaded a trial version of computer associates ez firewall and antivirus and fell in love with a computer security system all over again&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>and a negative one</p>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;i dont especially like how music files are unstructured ; basically they are just dumped into one folder with no organization , like you might have in windows explorer folders and subfolders&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>from the training set. We will use Support Vector Machines to learn to classify such review sentences into positive and negative classes.

We will use the word occurrence features: if a particular word (or more generally, a token, which may include punctuation, numbers, etc.) w occurs in an example, the corresponding feature is set to 1, otherwise to 0.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy.
</p>",# Dataset Description,True,True
4068,taa2021-proyecto1,"# Description

P¨¢gina principal del Proyecto 1  - Taller de Aprendizaje Autom¨¢tico 2021 (Facultad de Ingenier¨ªa, UdeLaR)

Recuerden mirar la documentaci¨®n: https://higgsml.lal.in2p3.fr/files/2014/04/documentation_v1.8.pdf

----------------------
# Evaluation

Esta p¨¢gina describe como deben preparar el env¨ªo para que pueda ser calculado de forma autom¨¢tica. 

El proyecto tiene dos medidas de desempe?o:
- AUC: El ¨¢rea bajo la curva de la ROC 
- AMS: Approximate Median Significance: 

\text{AMS} = \sqrt{2\left((s+b+b_r) \log \left(1 + \frac{s}{b + b_r}\right)-s\right)}$

La m¨¦trica que interesa en realidad es AMS. El problema es que no es posible seleccionar esta m¨¦trica para la evaluaci¨®n autom¨¢tica, ya que no se encuentra entre la lista de m¨¦tricas disponibles.  Por lo tanto, hemos optado por utilizar la AUC como m¨¦todo alternativo. Sin embargo, es importante recordar que la verdadera m¨¦trica es AMS.

<h2>Formato del archivo a enviar ( submission format )</h2>
En la secci¨®n ""data"" hay un ejemplo (sample_submission_small.csv) de c¨®mo debe enviarse el formato del resultado para ser evaluado adecuadamente. Son s¨®lo 2 columnas: EventId (que debe ser el mismo que el de test.csv) y Label, que debe tener valores de 1 para signal (s) y 0 para background (b).","# Dataset Description

Los datos de training y test son los mismos que los datos del challenge original (https://www.kaggle.com/c/higgs-boson/data): 

- training.csv - Training set of 250000 events, with an ID column, 30 feature columns, a weight column and a label column.
- test.csv - Test set of 550000 events with an ID column and 30 feature columns.

Los siguientes archivos fueron modificados respecto al challenge original:

- sample_submission_small.csv: ejemplo de como debe enviarse el formato del resultado para ser evaluado adecuadamente. Son s¨®lo 2 columnas: EventId (que debe ser el mismo que el de test.csv) y Label, que debe tener valores de 1 para signal (s) y 0 para background (b). 
- HiggsBosonCompetition_AMSMetric_updated.py - Esta es una versi¨®n actualizada a Python3 y al uso de Pandas del archivo original.

Nota: es importante notar que si bien las etiquetas para la clasificaci¨®n con ""s"" y ""b"", en el env¨ªo que har¨¢n deber¨¢n convertirlo a 0 y 1 respectivamente. Lo mismo aplica si van a utilizar el HiggsBosonCompetition_AMSMetric_updated.py para analizar su resultado.

**Algunos detalles importantes**:
- Todas las variables son en punto flotante, excepto PRI_jet_num que es entero.
- Las variables que comienzan con PRI (PRImitivas), son datos obtenidos directamente de la medida en el detector. 
- Las variables que comienzan con DER (DERivadas) son datos que se obtienen a partir de las medidas primitivas. 
- En el caso de que una medida no se haya podido obtener o medir, tendr¨¢ el valor -999.0, que est¨¢ fuera del rango v¨¢lido de cualquiera de las variables.",True,True
