4090,cmpe-257-lab-2-part-2,"# Description

<p>This challenge is part of the CMPE 257 Lab 2 assessment. Please refer to the lab document for the detailed instructions</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>The CIFAR-10 data consists of 50,000 32x32 color images in 10 classes. There are 40,000 training images and 10,000 test images in the data. We have preserved the train/test split from the original dataset.</p>
<p>The label classes in the dataset are: </p>
<ul>
<li>airplane</li>
<li>automobile </li>
<li>bird </li>
<li>cat </li>
<li>deer </li>
<li>dog </li>
<li>frog </li>
<li>horse </li>
<li>ship </li>
<li>truck</li>
</ul>
<p> The classes are completely mutually exclusive. There is no overlap between automobiles and trucks. ""Automobile"" includes sedans, SUVs, things of that sort. ""Truck"" includes only big trucks. Neither includes pickup trucks. </p>",True,True
4091,housedestruction,"# Description

<p>There was a natural calamity and a lot of houses were destroyed in the process. To protect the locality from being affected in the future, the municipality wants a model in place which can predict in advance which houses will have high chances of getting destroyed in the future.</p>
<br>
<p>Come up with the best possible classification that maximises the chances of predicting correctly which house will have high chances of being destroyed.</p>
<br>
<p>The feature description is present in the 'Feature description.docx' file in the Data tab.<p>

<br>
Good Luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation


<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/intuition-behind-log-loss-score-4e0c9979680a#:~:text=Log%2Dloss%20is%20indicative%20of,is%20the%20log%2Dloss%20value.""> Log Loss</a>. defined as the negative log-likelihood of a logistic model that returns y_pred probabilities for its training data y_true. The log loss is only defined for two or more labels. Log Loss it useful to compare models not only on their output but on their probabilistic outcome.</p>

<p>The logloss is simply Loss = P ln P + (1-P) ln (1-P) where ?? is simply the probability attributed to the real class.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every building in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: building_id and Category. 
</p><p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>building_id,Category<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows the dataset available. 'training_data.csv' is the file to be used for training purposes of the model. <p>
<br>
<p>'testing_data.csv' will be used for model evaluation and scoring. Save the output after fitting the data from 'testing_data.csv' on the model, and save it in the similar format of 'testing_data.csv'. The submission should have exact number of rows as in 'submission_format.csv' and the output should be 0 and 1. <p><br><p>Here 1 means that the house was destroyed and 0 saying that the house was okayish after the calamity.<p>",True,True
