4127,sacm2021,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the SACM 2021 Hackathon Classification Challenge!</p>
<p>Here, you are tasked with classifying chest X-rays taken from COVID-19 patients.</p>
<p>You are to submit up to 20 prediction files per day.</p>
<p>Submission deadline closes <b>March 28 at 1:30pm</b>.</p>

<h2>Submission clarification</h2>
Your submission file should contain the labels that your model predicts for the Test dataset only. The Training dataset should be used for model building and internal evaluation.

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>Tsai, E., Simpson, S., Lungren, M.P., Hershman, M., Roshkovan, L., Colak, E., Erickson, B.J., Shih, G., Stein, A.,Kalpathy-Cramer, J., Shen, J.,Hafez, M.A.F., John, S., Rajiah, P., Pogatchnik, B.P., Mongan, J.T., Altinmakas, E., Ranschaert, E., Kitamura, F.C., Topff, L., Moy, L., Kanne, J.P., &amp; Wu, C. (2021). Data from Medical Imaging Data Resource Center (MIDRC) - RSNA International COVID Radiology Database (RICORD) Release 1c - Chest x-ray, Covid+ (MIDRC-RICORD-1c). The Cancer Imaging Archive.  DOI: https://doi.org/10.7937/91ah-v663.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Your classification program should create a submission file, which you will upload to Kaggle for scoring. Please see the Data tab for the format of the submission file.</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorization Accuracy, which is the percentage of labels that were correctly identified.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>For more information about the data, refer to the <a href=""https://wiki.cancerimagingarchive.net/pages/viewpage.action?pageId=70230281&fbclid=IwAR3pINxmWd2yT3TjbcBSNGIjeX9OpcmVNFG5d48Hcz0fvnVv9yRFo2NyMTQ#702302814dc5f53338634b35a3500cbed18472e0"">data source</a>.

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>TestImages</strong> - The CXR images in the testing set</li>
<li><strong>TrainImages</strong> - The CXR images in the training set</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - the ID of the CXR</li>
<li><strong>classification</strong> - 0/1/2/3 correspond to Negative for Pneumonia, Atypical Appearance, Indeterminate Appearance and Typical Appearance</li>
</ul>",True,True
4128,csc532-2,"# Description

This is a repeat challenge from Phase 3 of DLAI3: (https://www.kaggle.com/c/dlai3-phase3). Please watch out for another challenge from DLAI5 soon.

------------

This is the Phase 3 challenge from the Hackathon of the 3rd Deep Learning and AI Summer/Winter School (DLAI3). More details about the previous challenge can be found at https://www.kaggle.com/c/dlai3/.

Medical image analysis has continually been an area of prominent and growing importance, in both the research and application aspects. However, it is important to realize that the algorithms, methodology, as well as the source of data, need to be strictly scrutinized. Phase 3 is a challenge that provides a mixed dataset of publicly available COVID-19 chest x-ray images from various sources, a pneumonia dataset obtained from a study on children, partially verified thorax CXR images of adults from NIH, and unverified no-finding images from NIH. See below for the sources of these data.

<h2>Goal</h2>
<p>The goal is to provide the participants a chance to test their skills to come up with a good performance model in turns of the macro F1 score to classify COVID-19 chest x-ray images. The Phase 3 DLAI3 Hackathon will award the top three entries that have a full paper submission to the 11th International Conference on Computational Systems-Biology and Bioinformatics (<a href=""http://www.csbio.org/2020/"">CSBio2020</a>), under the theme of CHAT-2020 (COVID-19 Health, Analytics, and Technologies). The top three individuals/teams will be provided with complimentary registration for their full paper submission to the ACM ICPS Proceedings of CSBio2020 and will be invited to submit an extended work to a post-conference journal special issue.</p>

<h2>Dataset citation</h2>
Please cite the kaggle dataset as follows: Jonathan H. Chan, ¡°DLAI3 Hackathon Phase3 COVID-19 CXR Challenge.¡± Kaggle, doi: [10.34740/KAGGLE/DSV/1347344](https://doi.org/10.34740/KAGGLE/DSV/1347344).

<h1>Acknowledgments</h1>

The following are the sources and related publications for this dataset:

1) Pneumonia CXR images of children retrieved from Mendeley at:
[https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rscbjbr9sj/2
](https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rscbjbr9sj/2)
Related publication:
https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(18)30154-5

2) Cropped COVID-19 + selected images from the following (retrieved July 17, 2020):
[https://github.com/agchung/Actualmed-COVID-chestxray-dataset
](https://github.com/agchung/Actualmed-COVID-chestxray-dataset)
Related publication:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.09871
](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.09871)v4 Mon, 11 May 2020

3) COVID-19 chest x-ray images from:
[https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset
](https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset)(Retrieved July 13, 2020)

Images:
[https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset/blob/master/images
](https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset/blob/master/images)
Metadata:
[https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset/blob/master/metadata.csv
](https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset/blob/master/metadata.csv)
4) Selected images from:
[https://nihcc.app.box.com/v/ChestXray-NIHCC
](https://nihcc.app.box.com/v/ChestXray-NIHCC)
Publication:
ChestX-ray8: Hospital-scale Chest X-ray Database and Benchmarks on
Weakly-Supervised Classification and Localization of Common Thorax Diseases

[https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02315
](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.02315)[v5] Thu, 14 Dec 2017

Semi-verified by:
[https://lukeoakdenrayner.wordpress.com/2017/12/18/the-chestxray14-dataset-problems/comment-page-1/](https://lukeoakdenrayner.wordpress.com/2017/12/18/the-chestxray14-dataset-problems/comment-page-1/)
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric is the F1 score, which combines the statistical measures of precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>

<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>

<p>The macro F1 is the arithmetic mean of the F1-score of each class.</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other. For multi-class, the evaluation score is based on the macro average of all classes</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: unlabelled image name and predicted label as ""0"" for No-Finding; ""1"" for Thorax-Disease other than COVID-19; and ""2"" for COVID-19. </p>","# Dataset Description

<p>This challenge has two multi-class datasets: one labelled and one unlabelled.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>DLAI3_Phase3.zip</strong> - the multi-class training set containing three folders of no-finding, thorax-diseases, and covid</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>VALIDATE.zip</strong> - unlabelled set to be tested and submit in form of the sampleSubmission.csv which is two columns with first column as the filename and the second being ""0"" for No-Finding; ""1"" for Thorax-Diseases; and ""2"" for COVID-19.</li>
</ul>",True,True
4129,eki-challenge-n1-tabular-data,"# Description

<p>This is the first monthly data challenge competition organised within Ekimetris. For this competition you will learn how to manipulate and model data from a tabular dataset, created from the open source dataset Forest Cover Type</p>
<p>This is not only a code competition where you compete to get the best possible score on a test dataset, but also an analytics one, where you can express your creativity by building a use case around the provided data</p>
<h2>Problematic Overview</h2>
<p>During the past century, global warming grew as one of the most challenging problems ever faced by human kind. Many initiative have been taken so far to slow down and reverse the trend, from governmental measures like carbon taxation, to private initiative to plat some new trees.</p>
<p>With this competition, you'll leverage a public dataset to learn to predict which kind of forest can be grown depending on several geologic informations, and build around this modelisation to create a full environnmental strategy designed to help private initiatives to optimize the effectivness of planting new trees.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>This competition will be evaluated according to three main axes</p>
<hr>

<h1>Excellence</h1>
<p>This competition being evaluated on a classification task with fairly balanced classes, the metric of evaluation will be the accuracy</p>
<p>The metric will be computed not only on your best submission (displayed on the leaderboard), but also on best score for three distinct methodologies : </p>
<li>Standard ML : Make the most out of classical libraries like sklearn, lighgbm and xgboost</li>
<li>AutoML : Leverage powerfull automl and stacking approaches to get the best possible score</li>
<li>DeepLearning : Unleach the full power of deep neural network to achieve state of the art scores</li>
In order to be evaluated on one of these three dimension on top of the main evaluation, you will need to publish you notebook in the notebook section, and the notebook will be rerun to ensure reproducibility of your method. The name of the notebook will need to contain one of the following snippet : [StandardML], [AutoML], [DeepML]

<h1>Creativity / Business Sense</h1>
<p>The second evaluation criteria will be around your business expertise, and your ability to build and developp a convincing use case around the provided data.</p>
<p>In order to compete on the dimension, you'll need to create a presentation support with your team and send it to the jury before the competition deadline. We will schedule a meeting for a 10-20 min restitution</p>

<h1>Transmission</h1>
<p>The goal of the competition being to learn, the last evaluation criteria will be your ability to transmit your knowledges to others</p>
<p>To compete on this dimension, you can publish your notebook with an EDA, a methodology or a lesson, and include the snippet [Transmission] in the notebook name. The notebook with the most upvote will win</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every rows in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: id and CoverType.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format (same as sample_submission):</p>
<pre>id,Cover_Type<br>1,1<br></pre>
----------------------
# Contacts

<p>For any questions about the competitions or if you want to submit a use case for the Creativity evaluation, send an email to:</p>
<li>gabriel.olympie@ekimetrics.com</li>
<li>pierre.biousse@ekimetrics.com</li>
<li>nicolas.chesneau@ekimetrics.com</li>

----------------------
# Timeline And Details

This competition will be hosted during the month of April 2021, and will remain open for late submission after that.

Each team will be able to submit at most 5 attempts per days, and select 2 of the attempts for the final submission.

Test set is splitted randomly (50/50) between Public and Private test set, that will be used respectively for intermediate and final evaluation of the performance.","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_set.csv<p>The training set, with the target columns (Cover_Type)</li>
<li><strong>test_set.csv<p>The test set, without the target columns</li>
<li><strong>covtype.info<p>The description of the dataset as proposed by the original one</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv<p>The format to use to submit submission</li>
</ul>

<p>Original Dataset Source : https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/covertype</p>",True,True
