4145,csce5300-competition,"# Description

The goal of this competition is to predict bike share use, given the hour, day, and information about the weather. Companies like Divvy try to predict how much demand there will be for bikes on any given day to allocate resources to redistribute bikes so that, ideally, very few bike stations are ever full (when you can¡¯t park your bike) or empty (when you can¡¯t pick up a bike if you want to).

The [data tab][1] provides detailed information on the data set and necessary downloads.

See the class google doc page on details about teams, grading, and updated tips.


  [1]: https://www.kaggle.com/c/csce5300-competition/data
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submission Format

Your submission file must have a header line and should be structured in the following format:

     datetime,count
     2011-01-20 00:00:00,0 
     2011-01-20 01:00:00,0
     2011-01-20 02:00:00,0
     ...

The tutorial code should demonstrate how to generate such a file from a very simple prediction model. Note, these prediction are to be done on the test file under the data tab, where you do not know the actual count, and should match the rows of the test file in count and order. 

Your predictions will be scored by comparing to the ground truth information submitted when this competition was created. Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).
----------------------
# Tutorial

There is a starter tutorial Jupyter notebook using python 3 in the [data tab][1]. Simply open the notebook with the associated data files in the same folder. If you run each step in succession, it will generate a simple prediction on the test data that is in the form to submit to the site. 

You should first run this notebook, generate a sample submission file, and submit it to the site before making changes, to make sure everything is set up properly before experimenting. 

 [1]: https://www.kaggle.com/c/csce5300-competition/data","# Dataset Description

You are provided hourly rental data spanning two years. For this competition, the training set is comprised of the first 16 days of each month, while the test set is the 17-19th day of the month. You must predict the total count of bikes rented during each hour covered by the test set, using only information available prior to the rental period. That is, predict ""count"" without using ""count"" or it's components ""casual"" and ""registered"".


Data Fields


* **datetime** - hourly date + timestamp  
* **season** -  1 = spring, 2 = summer, 3 = fall, 4 = winter 
* **holiday** - whether the day is considered a holiday
* **workingday** - whether the day is neither a weekend nor holiday
* **weather** -  
    * 1 - Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy
    * 2 -  Mist + Cloudy, Mist + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist
    * 3 - Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds  
    * 4 - Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog 
* **temp** - temperature in Celsius
* **atemp** - ""feels like"" temperature in Celsius
* **humidity** - relative humidity
* **windspeed** - wind speed
* **casual** - number of non-registered user rentals initiated
* **registered** - number of registered user rentals initiated
* **count** - number of total rentals",True,True
