4195,cs-475-generative-models-mnist,"# Description

<p>This is a handwritten digit recognition competition.</p>
Data
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Each sample is a 24x24 binary image. We vectorize each image as a 1x576 vector and stack N samples to represent the dataset as a matrix X of size Nx576. Each label takes value y in the set {0,1,2,...,9}.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
Data
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Sometimes it will be convenient to represent each label as a one-hot-vector, with one element set to one and the rest zero. The dataset labels can then be represented as a matrix, Y \in {0,1}^{Nx10} such that the sum of each column adds up to 1.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
File format
<pre>&lt;p&gt;
mnist/train/data
mnist/train/labels 
mnist/val/data 
mnist/val/labels
mnist/kaggle/data
&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>
There are three datasets:<br>
large_train: training data (7000 samples)<br>
val: validation data (2000 samples)<br>
kaggle: kaggle competition test data (1000 samples, no labels provided)
</p>
<p>
You should train on the training data and validate your models using the validation data. Once you are happy with your performance, you should predict the samples in kaggle and submit the results (see Evaluation).
</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy. <br><br>

Submission Format<br><br>

For every image in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and predicted label. Id is the index of each image in the gaggle set and predicted label is one of {0,1,2,...,9}.<br><br>

The file should contain a header and have the following format:<br><br>

Id,Prediction<br>
0,2<br>
1,5<br>
2,6<br>
3,0<br>
4,2<br>
</p>",# Dataset Description,True,True
4196,ece5300sp21-tree,"# Description

This ECE-5300 Sp21 final-project competition is intended for only the tree-based classification methods like random forests and XGBoost.

After your model is trained, you would predict the labels of the test features and upload them (in a properly formatted .csv file...see the Evaluation tab to the left) to see how you are doing relative to your classmates.

Your relative performance in this competition will be used as part of your final-project grade.  It is important to enter only **tree-based** classifiers in this contest.  If you upload predictions from a non-tree classifier, points will be deducted.

Important: you are allowed to submit at most 2 entries per day, so don't wait until the last minute!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **classification accuracy**.

**Submission Format:**
Submission files should be .csv files containing two columns: Id and Label. The Id column should be the integer list 0,1,2,3,...,9216 and the Label column should contain integers from the set {0,1,...,5}. The first row of the file should contain the column labels and the remaining 9217 rows should contain numerical values. For example, the first few rows should look like: <pre>Id,Label<br>0,5<br>1,2<br>2,1<br>3,4</pre>","# Dataset Description

The test features are given in `Xts.csv`.  You can make a prediction of the test labels using your code, and then upload the (properly formatted) .csv file to Kaggle, which will score your prediction.  Note that it reports the score on the ""public"" fold of the test partition; there is also a ""private"" fold who's score is reported only to the instructor and TAs.",True,True
4197,dsir-322-project-2-regression-challenge,"# Description

<p>Welcome the Kaggle challenge for Project 2! As part of a successful completion of Project 2, you will make at least one submission of predictions from your model. </p>

<p>
In this challenge, you will use Ames housing data to create a regression model that predicts the price of houses in Ames, IA. You should feel free to use any and all features that are present in this dataset. You may engineer features based on the existing features.
</p>

<h2>Goal</h2>
<p>
It is your job to predict the sales price for each house. For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the SalePrice variable.
</p>

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
<p>
Kaggle leaderboard standings will be determined by root mean squared error (RMSE).
</p>

<h2>Submission File Format </h2>
<p>
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
</p>
<pre> Id, SalePrice</pre>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
<p>
Kaggle leaderboard standings will be determined by root mean squared error (RMSE). Remember that you are only scored on 60% of your predictions for the public leaderboard. The final, private leaderboard is scored on the other 40% of your predictions. Ranks will likely change!
</p>

<h2>Submission File Format </h2>
<p>
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
</p>
<pre> Id, SalePrice</pre>","# Dataset Description

There are three files:

- train.csv -- this data contains all of the training data for your model.
The target variable (SalePrice) is removed from the test set!
- test.csv -- this data contains the test data for your model. You will feed this data into your regression model to make predictions.
- sample_sub_reg.csv -- An example of a correctly formatted submission for this challenge (with a random number provided as predictions for SalePrice. Please ensure that your submission to Kaggle matches this format.  

## Codebook / Data Dictionary:

VARIABLE DESCRIPTIONS:
Tab characters are used to separate variables in the data file. The data has 82 columns which include 23 nominal, 23 ordinal, 14 discrete, and 20 continuous variables (and 2 additional observation identifiers).

