4208,109-2-uta-dl-app-hw2,"# Description

In Homework 2 we are going to train models that can recommend hashtags based on the content of an image. We will use the dataset ""HARRISON"". Please refer to the paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.05054) for more details about the dataset.

The original data of HARRISON can be downloaded here: (https://drive.google.com/file/d/1B9NZf42J_GpslRNlTTAxvM5c9WRVl_1Z/view?usp=sharing). You can download the images and take a look, but this is not mandatory. We already extracted the image features for you and upload to Kaggle as ""harrison_features.npz"".

The demo code separates the training process into two stages: word2vec training and image-to-word2vec training. The notebook of last semester can be found here: https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-devise
You can export and import the code to this competition.

To evaluate the model performance, the top 10 hashtags (KNN=10) recommended from your model will be used to calculate the hamming loss of ground truth.  Get your computer ready and have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

We will use the Hamming loss function to evaluate the performance:
<p> \[ \frac{1}{|Y|} \sum_{i=1}^{|N|} \sum_{j=1}^{|H_i|} XOR(y'_{ij}, y_{ij}) \] </p>

<p> where \\(  |N| \\) is the total number of images, \\( |Y| \\) is the total number of labels, \\(  |H_i| \\) is the number of labeled hashtags of image  \\( i \\). </p>
<p> The \\(  y'_{ij} \\) is a binary output (True or False ) that predicts the existing of a hashtag  \\( h_{ij}  \\). The $y_{ij}$ is the ground truth. </p>","# Dataset Description

The files are briefly described below:

<ol>
<li> Code files
<p> There are three iPython notebooks that show you how to train a model and create a prediction output in Kaggle format. Please run the code to understand the concepts, and modify them to build your own model. For functions of each file please read the ""README.txt"". The ""utility.py"" is used the demo code. </p>
</li>

<li> Data files
<p>
<ul>
<li>
""harrison_features.npz"" - Pre-extracted features of images in harrison dataset. We extracted ImageNet and places365 CNN features.
</li>
<li>
""tag_list.txt"" - The list of hashtags for all image files.
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</li>
</ol>",True,True
4209,advance-nlp-hackathon-2,"# Description


<h2>Advance NLP Hackathon - 2 </h2>

<p>Classification of tweets text into three segments/classes

The main intention of this hackathon is to assess the understanding of concepts discussed so far in this course. Here the problem statement is a simple text classification on whether the provided tweet is a general tweet or is for stating empathy or do we need to report tweet as it is a abusive tweet.
These tweets are scraped from Twitter. Now our task is to classify all those tweets into specific classes.
</p><li>Report - 1</li>

<li>Empathy - 2</li>

<li>General - 3</li>
<p></p>

<h2> Steps to follow : </h2>

<ul>
<li>Read train.csv file which contains three columns tweet_id, tweet and label. Then build a classification model on top of this dataset .</li>
<li>Using the model create class labels for each record in test.csv and save it as in the form of provided sample submission file.</li>
<li>Convert all the columns type to string(str)</li>
<li>Now click on submit button.</li>
</ul>

<h2>NOTE : </h2>
<p>Upload your Assessment Notebook with FileName as YourName_Submission (In case using multiple files, upload a zip file) here <a>https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Ow-8epIPNDuxYPUjEKRGawZMn_HpIWLA?usp=sharing</a>
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

**1. Evaluation Metric**

The evaluation metric for this competition are
<li>F1-score for each class</li>
<li>Micro Averaged F1 scores</li>

**2. Submission Format**
For every text in the test set, submission files should contain two columns: id and target.

The submission file should contain the column headers: tweet_id, label

NOTE :: All coumns in the submission file file should be in string format. Please do this conversion before submission of results.

Example:
tweet_id,	label
1.268745e+18, 3 
1.269175e+18, 3","# Dataset Description

<p>In recent decades we have noticed a considerable increase in reports or confession posts of abuse victims on twitter. Most of the time victims do not report it to their guardians or the concerned authorities. Teenagers and minorities are the most affected group of abuse. Part of these victims tweets about their incident to let go of pain and suffering or as a cry for help. Identifying such reports are challenging because of the unavailability of annotated training data, and a high degree of data sparsity. To address this we are hosting this competition, So all the best for all of you.!</p>


<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> -  test data file</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>tweet_id</strong> - tweet id unique for each record</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - tweet text</li>
<li><strong>label</strong> - class label and classes are as follows - { 1 : Report, 2 : Empathy, 3 : General}</li>
</ul>",True,True
4210,pecfest-f1-challenge,"# Description

<p>All the best</p>
<p>You have been provided with a  training dataset containing tweets of different users describing their flight experience with different airlines. Along with the tweets, different information have been provided.</p>
<p>  The sentiment of the tweet has been mentioned in the 'airline_sentiment' column  of the dataset. The sentiment can have value - 'positive', 'negative'  and 'neutral'.</p>
<p> For the test dataset, all the columns of the training dataset, except the 'airline_sentiment' column will be present.</p>
<p> Your task is to predict the sentiment of the tweets present in the test dataset, as to whether it is 'positive', 'negative' or 'neutral'. </p>
<p> Submit a 'predictions.csv' file containing the columns 'Id' and 'Predicted'. </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: 'Id' and 'Predicted'. Predicted columns should have only three values - 'positive', 'negative' or 'neutral'. A different spelling will lead to incorrect answer.</p>
<p> The predictions file should have header - 'Id' and 'Predicted'.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Given Twitter US Airline Sentiment Dataset, which contains data for over 7500 tweets, your task is to predict the sentiment of the tweet i.e. positive, negative or neutral.</p>
<p>You are given:
<ol>
<li> A Training dataset csv file with X train and Y train data </li>
<li> A X test File and you have to predict and submit predictions for this file. </li>
</ol>
</p>
<h2>Read Instructions carefully -</h2>
<strong><p>
<ol>
<li> Files are in csv format. </li>
<li> Submit a csv file with only predictions for X test data. File should not have any headers and should only have one column i.e. predictions. </li> 
<li> Submit your ipynb file on this link - https://forms.gle/ACiQimNmD8z7BgaX6</li>
<li> Your score is based on number of accurate predictions. </li>
<li>  You need to upload both predictions and notebook file to submit.  </li>
<li>  Please make sure that your submissions are genuine and you haven¡¯t cheated/copied from anywhere.  <b> In such scenario your submission will be disqualified </b> </li>
</ol>
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>Test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>tweet_id</strong> </li>
<li><strong>airline</strong> </li>
<li><strong>airline_sentiment</strong></li>
<li><strong>airline_sentiment_gold</strong> </li>
<li><strong>name</strong></li>
<li><strong>negativereason_gold</strong></li>
<li><strong>retweet_count</strong> </li>
<li><strong>text</strong></li>
<li><strong>tweet_coord</strong> </li>
<li><strong>tweet_created</strong> </li>
<li><strong>tweet_location</strong> </li>
<li><strong>user_timezone</strong> </li>
</ul>",True,True
