4232,retencin-en-telefona-celular-2110,"# Description

Las comunicaciones por tel¨¦fono celular siempre han sido objeto de una fiera competencia, en la que hay, usualmente, un n¨²mero reducido de competidores (operadores) en el mercado que oscila entre cuatro y seis. Los operadores deben hacer fuertes inversiones de entrada para poder operar y prestar un servicio adecuado. El mercado de telefon¨ªa celular est¨¢ usualmente saturado (poca posibilidad de ampliaci¨®n del mercado), y por tanto, el conjunto de consumidores disponibles es reducido, por lo que los operadores usualmente cambian su estrategia de la adquisici¨®n de clientes a la retenci¨®n porque en el mundo se calcula que el costo de adquirir un nuevo cliente es cinco veces superior al de la retenci¨®n, sin contar con el posible CLTV (customer lifetimevalue) del cliente, esto es, el retorno a largo plazo que se genera por un cliente que contin¨²a consumiendo los servicios de un operador. Otra cifra clave es que la tasa de deserci¨®n (churn) suele rondar el 2% mensual promedio para un operador de telecomunicaciones. Desde el punto de vista de la inteligencia de negocios, bajo la perspectiva de CRM hay dos esfuerzos fundamentales de Anal¨ªtica: a) predecir la deserci¨®n (churn) de manera adecuada y b) evaluar la manera m¨¢s costo/efectiva de lo que un operador puede hacer para la retenci¨®n (incluyendo no hacer nada).

Un operador de telecomunicaciones les ha contratado para resolver las dos inquietudes anal¨ªticas presentadas anteriormente. Para la primera, desea que, a partir de una base de datos de entrenamiento que es una muestra aleatoria simple de la base de datos total de la compa?¨ªa que contiene el resultado de retenci¨®n en los ¨²ltimos seis meses, se realice la mejor predicci¨®n posible (basada en el ¨¢rea bajo la curva COR) para una base de datos de prueba en un concurso de Kaggle. Para la segunda parte, adicional a un reporte con metodolog¨ªa CRISP-DM (de m¨¢ximo 18 p¨¢ginas) de los modelos realizados, desea que se le presente una estrategia (incluyendo puntos de corte, costos y posibles ganancias) para atacar la retenci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>La base de datos de entrenamiento tiene una muestra aleatoria simple de los clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.019 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.020). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.019.  Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago). </p>

<p>La base de datos de prueba contiene una muestra no aleatoria adicional de clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2.019 (el corte es primero de Enero de 2.020). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2.019. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de sucripci¨®n (son clientes pospago).  La variable de posible churn (resultado) no se incluye, pues es la variable a predecir</p>
<h2>Archivos</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>traintelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de entrenamiento</li>
<li><strong>testelco.xlsx</strong> - La base de datos de prueba</li>
<h2>Campos de la base de datos</h2>
<ul>

<li><strong>id</strong> - id an¨®mino que identifica al cliente</li>
<li><strong> Fecha de nacimiento </strong> - Fecha de nacimiento del cliente, dd/mm/yyyy</li>
<li><strong>tipo cliente</strong> - Tipo de cliente: 1 - Hombre, 2-Mujer, 3-Cliente empresarial</li>
<li><strong>Factura online</strong> - Binario que indica si el cliente recibe ¨²nicamente su factura online</li>
<li><strong>Antiguedad equipo</strong> Meses de antiguedad del equipo</li>
<li><strong>Plan de datos</strong> - Binario. 1=el cliente tiene plan premium de datos.</li>
<li><strong>Facturaci¨®n</strong> - Total facturaci¨®n de los ¨²ltimos seis meses (suma) </li>
<li><strong>Mora</strong> D¨ªas de mora acumulados</li>
<li><strong>Fecha inicio contrato </strong> - Fecha de inicio de su primer contrato de plan pospago en la compa?¨ªa</li>
<li><strong>minutos</strong> - total minutos consumidos en los ¨²ltimos seis meses</li>
<li><strong>resultado</strong> - 0=No hubo deserci¨®n; 1=Hubo deserci¨®n (churn) </li>",True,True
4233,sliced-s00e04,"# Description

In this episode of SLICED, contestants are challenged to predict the popularity of datasets on Kaggle using the Meta Kaggle dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/kaggle/meta-kaggle. Specifically, they'll use the file Datasets.csv which is a running account of all the uploaded Kaggle datasets and all related info, including upvotes, downloads, kernels created, and dataset sizes.


## About SLICED
[SLICED](https://www.notion.so/SLICED-Show-c7bd26356e3a42279e2dfbafb0480073) is like the TV Show Chopped but for data science. Competitors get a never-before-seen dataset and two-hours to code a solution to a prediction challenge. Contestants get points for the best model plus bonus points for data visualization, votes from the audience, and more.

SLICED is streaming on Twitch Tuesdays at 8:30PM ET starting June 1st: https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Algorithm</h2>
Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the predicted value and the observed total views.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every dataset in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Id and TotalViews.
</p><p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,TotalViews<br>1,100<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

https://www.kaggle.com/kaggle/meta-kaggle 
Specifically, this is the Datasets.csv and some related dataset CSV. The dataset is a running account of all the uploaded Kaggle datasets and all related info, including upvotes, downloads, kernels created, and dataset sizes.",True,True
4234,ntuphys-cpintro-mlcomp-2021,"# Description

<h3>This is one of the two competitions hosting for this semester! <br>(NTU computational physics course)</h3>
<p>The goal is to provide a correct label of the jet data: <strong>a boosted Higgs boson</strong>, or just <strong>a high momentum (light) quark</strong>. A jet is a collection of multiple particles which are originated from a particle participating hadronic interactions, such as a quark or gluon.</p> 
<p>In our samples, there are a collection of simulated Higgs boson, and are decaying into a pair of bottom quark. Although the source of the events are from <strong>two quarks</strong> but since the momentum of the mother Higgs boson is very high hence the products of the two quarks are merged into one single jet object. While another set of samples are consisting of one high momentum quark, and also resulting a single jet object.</p>
<p>
All you need to do is use any possible machine learning technique you have learned so far to provide a good classification of these two types of jets, based on the provided two training samples. Apply your algorithm to the testing sample and upload/submit your decision back to this web accordingly.
</p>
<p>
You should be able to find the two shared example codes in the ""Code"" section: lda_demo and ann_demo. They are just minimal ML tasks (LDA and simple fully-connected NN) built with two features: counting of particles in the jet and the invariant mass of the whole jet object. The number of particles in the final state for Higgs boson should be higher than the single quark jet, since the Higgs boson decaying into two b-quarks! The invariant mass is also very obvious: the mass of Higgs boson should be around 125 GeV, while the light quark jet should be low (but with a long tail extended to high mass region).
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is based on the accuracy, what is the average chance your submitted result matching to the true class.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the csv dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: <strong>""id""</strong> and <strong>""class""</strong>. ""id"" should be an integer from 0 to 39999, matching to the first column of the given testing sample. ""class"" should be your predicted class of the jets, 1 (Higgs boson) or 0 (light quark). </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,class<br>0,1<br>1,0<br>2,1<br>3,1<br>......<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>The data file <strong>sample_hbb.csv</strong> contains 40,000 jets consisting of high momentum Higgs bosons decaying into bottom quark pairs. </p>
<p>The data file <strong>sample_q.csv</strong> contains 40,000 jets from high momentum light quark. </p>
<p>The data file <strong>sample_test.csv</strong> contains another 40,000 jets to be processed with your machine learning algorithm. </p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>sample_hbb.csv</strong> - the training set (Higgs signal, class = 1)</li>
<li><strong>sample_q.csv</strong> - the training set (quark background, class = 0)</li>
<li><strong>sample_test.csv</strong> - the test set, to be processed and produce your corresponding predictions</li>
<li><strong>my_predicts.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - an id unique to an event (0-39999). Note an event can contain variable number of particles, hence all of the particles from the same event shared the same event id.</li>
<li><strong>code</strong> - the particle code (type)</li>
<ul>
<li><strong>0</strong> - a photon, charge = 0</li>
<li><strong>1</strong> - an electron, charge = -1</li>
<li><strong>2</strong> - a position (anti-electron), charge = +1</li>
<li><strong>3</strong> - a muon, charge = -1</li>
<li><strong>4</strong> - an anti-muon, charge = +1</li>
<li><strong>5</strong> - a negatively-charged hadron (pion, kaon, or proton), charge = -1</li>
<li><strong>6</strong> - a positively-charged hadron (pion, kaon, or proton), charge = +1</li>
<li><strong>7</strong> - a neutral hadron (long-lived kaon, or neutron), charge = 0</li>
</ul>
<li><strong>px</strong> - particle momentum in x direction in unit GeV/c</li>
<li><strong>py</strong> - particle momentum in y direction in unit GeV/c</li>
<li><strong>pz</strong> - particle momentum in z direction in unit GeV/c</li>
</ul>",True,True
4235,hackathon2021,"# Description

<p>MNIST Handwritten Digit Classification . This is one of the interesting machine learning projects. Deep Learning and neural networks have found use cases in many real-world applications like image recognition, automatic text generation, driverless cars, and much more. However, before you delve into these complex areas of Deep Learning, you should begin with a simple dataset like the MNIST dataset. So, why not use your skills to develop an impressive machine learning project based on MNIST?

The MNIST digit classification project is designed to train machines to recognize handwritten digits. Since beginners usually find it challenging to work with image data over flat relational data, the MNIST dataset is best for beginners. In this project, you will use the MNIST datasets to train your ML model using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs).</p>



----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation shall be made by PANEL Framed by TKRCET & Cephas Research. Decision made by the committee shall be full and final. Top 5 Performers shall be given an opportunity to work on open source projects in collaboration with CEPHAS RESEARCH under Internship Program. 

----------------------
# Organizers Information

Dr. Vicky Nair - https://www.kaggle.com/vickynairr

Organized by Department of Computer Science and Engineering, TKRCET in collaboration with CEPHAS RESEARCH.","# Dataset Description

The data files train.csv and test.csv contain gray-scale images of hand-drawn digits, from zero through nine.

<p>Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive. </p>

<p>The training data set, (train.csv), has 785 columns. The first column, called ""label"", is the digit that was drawn by the user. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image. </p>

<p>Each pixel column in the training set has a name like pixelx, where x is an integer between 0 and 783, inclusive. To locate this pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27, inclusive. Then pixelx is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix, (indexing by zero). </p>

For example, pixel31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.

Visually, if we omit the ""pixel"" prefix, the pixels make up the image like this:



<p>The test data set, (test.csv), is the same as the training set, except that it does not contain the ""label"" column.
Your submission file should be in the following format: For each of the 28000 images in the test set, output a single line containing the ImageId and the digit you predict. <./p>
<br>
<a href=""https://imgbb.com/""><img src=""https://i.ibb.co/xfQWvYw/Capture.png"" alt=""Capture"" border=""0""></a>

<p>Your submission file should be in the following format: For each of the 28000 images in the test set, output a single line containing the ImageId and the digit you predict. For example, if you predict that the first image is of a 3, the second image is of a 7, and the third image is of a 8, then your submission file would look like: </p>

<a href=""https://imgbb.com/""><img src=""https://i.ibb.co/Vjc6Tb5/Capture1.png"" alt=""Capture1"" border=""0""></a>

<br>
<p>The evaluation metric for this contest is the categorization accuracy, or the proportion of test images that are correctly classified. For example, a categorization accuracy of 0.97 indicates that you have correctly classified all but 3% of the images.</p>",True,True
