4263,msehackathon11,"# Description

<p>
Each row in the training data is a unique country.
The variable ""c3_agree_c"" tells you the percentage of respondents in that country who gave a specific response to the question asked in a survey conducted by Facebook (refer to CodeBookC3.csv).
All the other variables give you more information about that country. for eg. GDP, internet penetration, etc.
You have to predict c3_agree_c using the remaining variables.
You must use OLS Linear Estimators.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

A file called ""SampleSubmissionC3.csv"" has been provided to you. It is a sample submission file in the correct format. You have to upload your submission in the exact same format. First column will be as it is and the second column will be the predicted values that you have got after running the regression on the ""testingc3.csv"" data.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>trainingc3.csv</strong> - The training dataset</li>
<li><strong>testingc3.csv</strong> - The test dataset</li>
<li><strong>SampleSubmissionC3.csv</strong> - A sample submission file in the correct format. You have to upload your submission in this format.</li>
<li><strong>CodeBookC3.csv</strong> - Information about the data</li>
</ul>",True,True
4264,prml-data-contest-jan-2021,"# Description

![Banner](https://miro.medium.com/max/2520/1*5Z1mVJQMpLLzz7E5ruo7eg.jpeg)

## Welcome to  **PRML Jan-2021** Data Contest. 
Here you will build recommendation models for a Music Platform and try to predict what ratings customers will assign to songs.  You can use all the extra information provided, such as customers's previous rating scores, his/her list of songs saved for listening in future, labels assigned to songs etc.  

Dataset contains ~1.3million ratings, split into about 700k training and 600k test ratings. There are about 14k distinct customers and 10k distinct songs. This dataset is derived from a real world scenario; so take care of sanitizing/handling real data. 


Use the data in creative ways to come up a ML model that predicts customers rating scores for songs Supervised data is available about customer's preferences in **train.csv** 

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the **Mean Squared Error**.

### Submission Format

Your submission must contain exactly the same number of lines as the **test.csv** file. Each line in your submission must be a comma-separated list of two values, a line number (starting from 0) indicating the line of the test.csv file, and a rating prediction for that line. 
Check the **dummy\_submission.csv** file for a template.","# Dataset Description

Please note that some entries in the real-data might be missing due to incomplete information; students are advised to take care of such missing entries.    
The following 6 files are available for the data contest.    
    
------------- 
### train.csv
**train.csv** has rows corresponding to previous rating scores given by a particular customer to the specified song.    
- *customer\_id*: Unique identifier for a customer.    
- *song\_id*: Unique identifier for particular song.    
- *score*: [0-5] rating score assigned.    

*NOTE*: It is possible that some songs don't have metadata available in further files mentioned below, handle such cases appropriately.     

----------------- 
### test.csv
**test.csv** same as above train data, except for the last column of score is missing.    
- *customer\_id*: Unique identifier for a customer.    
- *song\_id*: Unique identifier for particular song.    
    
--------------------
### songs.csv
**songs.csv** consists metadata about the songs.     
- *song\_id*: Unique identifier for particular song, ranges from [1-10,000].    
- *platform\_id*: Another identifier given to a song by the platform from which data is derived [alphanumeric].     
- *released\_year*: Year in which song was first released.     
- *language*: Code for language of the song. Within the same language, the information is more fine-grained, such as *eng*, *eng-US*, *eng-GB*. Not all songs have this info.     
- *number\_of\_comments*: Numeric to denote how many customers have commented on the specific song (a measure of its popularity).     
    
-------------------------- 
### song\_labels.csv
**song\_labels.csv** has label information about songs.    
- *platform\_id*:  Another identifier given to a song by the platform from which data is derived [alphanumeric].       
- *label\_id*: numeric identifier of labels (like hip-hop, jazz, classic etc.) assigned by the customers on the platform.    
- *count*: Number of customers who have assigned the particular label to given song.    

------------------------------------ 
### save\_for\_later.csv 
**save\_for\_later.csv** consists of the songs marked by customers to listen in future (**might** be a signal of customer's preference).    
- *customer\_id*: Unique identifier for a customer.       
- *song\_id*: Unique identifier for particular song, ranges from [1-10,000] .    
Information in this file **might** give hints about customer's preference.   


------------------------------------ 
### dummy\_submission.csv 
This is an example of expected Output file for submission on Kaggle.      
Your code must produce a CSV file that contains exactly the same number of lines as the **test.csv**.   
Each line in your submission must be a comma-separated list of two values, a line number (starting from 0) indicating the zero-indexed line of the **test.csv** file, and a rating prediction for that line.
Check the **dummy_submission.csv** file for a template.

------------------------------------------ 
**PLEASE NOTE**   
We don't have similar metadata available for all the songs, and hence there might be songs without labels or songs not appearing in save\_for\_later preferences.",True,True
