- Private Leaderboard 1?","# Dataset Description

<p>The data files train.csv and test.csv contain gray-scale images of hand-drawn digits, from zero through nine, in the Kannada script.</p>

<p>Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive.</p>

<p>The training data set, train.csv, has 785 columns. The first column, called label, is the digit that was drawn by the user. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.</p>

<p>Each pixel column in the training set has a name like pixel{x}, where x is an integer between 0 and 783, inclusive. To locate this pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27, inclusive. Then pixel{x} is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix, (indexing by zero).</p>

<p>For example, pixel31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.</p>

<p>Visually, if we omit the ""pixel"" prefix, the pixels make up the image like this:</p>

<pre>
000 001 002 003 ... 026 027
028 029 030 031 ... 054 055
056 057 058 059 ... 082 083
  
728 729 730 731  ...  754 755
756 757 758 759  ...  782 783
</pre>",True,True
4279,ml4moleng-cancer-progression,"# Description

<p>Predicting Cancer Progression with ML.</p>

In this problem, we will expand our analysis to include other tumor and immune features to identify what features are most predictive of treatment response. Measuring progression-free survival (PFS) is one metric clinicians use to quantify how well a tumor responds to treatment.  PFS is the length of time (e.g.  days) after treatment that a patient lives without cancer getting worse

The data you will use include 29 signals taken from ~2400 patients. Each data point is labeled with a binary label 0 or 1. (I need some more explanations about label and input features) 

We prepared test data for you to provide predictions. Please download `X_test.csv` and used these features to predict labels for each sample. We prepared an example submission file as an example. After you train your model on the training data, use the model to compute the probabilities for the positive label (1) and store your predictions in the submission file. Upload your solution and you will be able to see your ranking on the leaderboard computed with 40% if the test data. The final ranking will be calculated including 100% of the data points.


----------------------
