# Explainability Prize

In addition to the Code Competition with leaderboard prizes, the host is also offering an Analytics Competition for notebooks that provide the **best analysis for which features most contribute to fan/digital engagement**. The best submissions will earn winners three [Explainability Prizes](https://www.kaggle.com/c/mlb-player-digital-engagement-forecasting/overview/prizes) up for grabs! The evaluation criteria and submission process are described below.

### Evaluation Criteria

In addition to the Code Competition with leaderboard prizes, the host is also offering an Analytics Competition for notebooks that provide the best analysis for which features most contribute to fan/digital engagement. The best submissions will earn winners one of three [Explainability Prize](https://www.kaggle.com/c/mlb-player-digital-engagement-forecasting/overview/prizes) up for grabs! The evaluation criteria and submission process are described below.

### What is an Analytics Competition?

In analytics competitions, the hosts will determine the best submissions by evaluating your notebook analyses against multiple criteria. If you've participated in any of our Data Science for Good Challenges, you¡¯re familiar with the format.

You analyze the data using any method you want. You can use machine learning, visualizations, statistical analysis...anything! For this competition specifically, your final submission needs to be in the form of a presentation with your analysis done in a notebook. Ultimately, your goal is to address the prompt and convince the host that your approach is the best by presenting your evidence through data storytelling.

### Evaluation Criteria

Your challenge is to investigate which features most contribute to the digital engagement targets that have been defined in this competition.

Submissions will be scored using the following rubric:

* Presentation & Methodology (5 pts)
     * Did the author document why/what/how a set of methods was chosen and used for their analysis? Is the methodology used appropriate and reasonable?
Is the notebook documented in a way that is easily reproducible (e.g., code, additional data sources, citations)?
     * Did the author make effective use of data visualizations to communicate their findings? Are their interpretations based on the analysis appropriate and convincing?

* Application (5 pts)
     * Did the author provide insights and evidence of how their analyses can be applied to growing the digital engagement measures?
     * What impact do the chosen features have on digital engagement? How useful is their analysis?

* Novelty & Creativity (5 pts)
     * Does the notebook leverage novel methods and/or visualizations that help reveal insights from data and/or communicate findings?
     * Did the author consider novel and impactful data sources or consider creative factors which are influential of fan engagement?

### Considerations
As you build your analysis, here are some factors for consideration and inspiration:

* You should not be limited by the data provided in the competition, which is specific to the design of the predictive task for the code competition. Have you looked at any number of external features that could influence fans¡¯ digital engagement with players? Market size? Player performance? Player popularity? Social presence? Etc.
* Are there differences in which factors influence each of the targets differently?
* How did the uniqueness of the 2020 season (pandemic season of only 60 regular season games compared to 162) change digital engagement?

### Submission Instructions

You can make as many submissions as you like, but we will only consider your latest submission before the deadline of July 31st.

If you are submitting as a team, all team members must be listed as collaborators on the submitted Notebook, and all team members must accept the competition rules before the submission deadline. It is not necessary to compete in the code competition to participate in the analytics competition and vice versa. It is also not required that you submit with the same team that you compete with in the code competition. However, do ensure you are not breaking the competition rules for private sharing across teams.

A valid submission will be your analysis written in a Kaggle Notebook. A [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/c/mlb-player-digital-engagement-forecasting/code) is a cloud-based environment that enables storytelling with code and analyses using data.  [This notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/jpmiller/creating-a-good-analytics-report) is a great resource for creating a good analytics report. You may submit one or more notebooks containing the analysis to support your proposal. All notebooks submitted must be made public by the submission deadline, July 31st, to be eligible.

