4319,application-failure-prediction-part2,"# Description

# Challenge 1.2: Computer System Failure Data Analysis (Model Abridged)

With the growing scale of supercomputing systems , scientists are now able to solve challenging computing problems in a matter of seconds which would take hundreds of years on a personal computer. However, with increasing scale (and complexity thereof) grows the probability of application failure, either due to hardware or software errors. Such application failures not only delay scientific progress, but also leads to a tremendous amount of wasted resources, both in terms of time and energy consumption. If we are able to predict when an application would fail due to a system error or due to software bugs, preventive mechanisms such as checkpointing can be initiated to save intermediate results, thereby, reducing the amount of wasted computation.

This assignment deals with predicting failure of application executions (referred to as ¡°job¡±) on Purdue ITaP¡¯s central computing cluster. This is data that we have collected, collated, and analyzed as part of two projects from the National Science Foundation (NSF), one completed (¡°Computer System Failure Data Repository to Enable Data-Driven Dependability Research¡±, Project No. CNS-1513197) and one ongoing (¡°Open Computer System Usage Repository and Analytics Engine¡±, Project No. CNS-2016704). 

For each job, we have data about the resources the job uses and whether the job succeeded or failed. The resources for which we have data are:
 1.	Memory
 2.	Network
 3.	Local IO
 4.	Network File System (NFS)

We are releasing the training data from 20,000 jobs, which has about 8% failure data (this is referred to as the ¡°positive class¡±). You will build Machine Learning models in Python to predict whether a job will fail or not, given the resource usage data. More details about the training and the test data are presented in the ""Data"" section. 

The assignment, including the data, is available at the following Github repo:
https://github.com/bagchi/application-failure-prediction

There are two parts for this challenge problem with slightly different objectives: 
 [Challenge 1.1](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part1/overview) and [Challenge 1.2](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part2/overview). You must complete both parts and **upload your solutions separately**.

For **Challenge 1.2** you will perform the following steps:

1. Your objective is to create a Machine Learning model that achieves the highest **[balanced accuracy](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part2/overview/definitions)** for predicting application failures given the resource usage data. However, compared to [Challenge 1.1](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction/overview), you have the following restrictions.
2. In this assignment, you are only allowed to use **three features** from the dataset. You will use Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to identify which three features to use. You will again try to achieve the highest balanced accuracy with the three-feature model. We will call this _¡°Model Abridged¡±_. 
3. Give a short writeup explaining:
    a.	What did you do for the _Model Abridged_? Give the confusion matrix and the balanced accuracy that you obtained on the training data.
    b.	Compare the result you achieved for _Model Complete_ (Challenge 1.1) vs. _Model Abridged_ (Challenge 1.2).
    c.	Upload this writeup. You will upload the writeup to this (as well as integrated with the writeup for Challenge 1.1) to [this Dropbox folder](https://www.dropbox.com/request/f58oGdpPA94cLQUAk7t5).
4. Upload your Python code with a `README` that tells us how we can run your code. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Please note that your overall score for Challenge Problem 1 will be based on both [Challenge 1.1](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part1/overview) and [Challenge 1.2](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part2/overview).

There are two evaluation components for this challenge.

## Automated evaluation

For **automated evaluation**, we use balanced accuracy as the evaluation metric for your model. This will be based on the output of `model_abridged_test.csv`. The **[balanced accuracy](https://www.kaggle.com/c/application-failure-prediction-part2/overview/definitions)** is computed by assigning equal weight to the positive and the negative class accuracy. Challenge participants are expected to achieve a minimum balanced accuracy of 60%, however, the winning teams will likely have an accuracy of 80% or higher.

## Offline evaluation

We will also evaluate selected teams based on their final reports and their presentations. This process is offline and may not be reflected on the competition leaderboard immediately. For details please see: https://syp.computer.org/gsc/

## Submission format

- Your code should generate the output file for _Model Abridged_ (named `model_abridged_test.csv`). This will be used for automated evaluation in Kaggle.
- The output files must have two columns: 
      - `job_id`: Unique ID for the job.
      - `failed`: Based on the resource usage metrics, can you predict if the job will fail? A value of `1` indicates failure.
- You must also submit a report explaining the following:
      a. What did you do for the _Model Abridged_? Give the confusion matrix and the balanced accuracy that you obtained on the training data.
      b. Compare the result you achieved for _Model Complete_ vs. _Model Abridged_.
- Upload your Python code with a README that tells us how we can run your code.
----------------------
# Definitions

## Balanced Accuracy

Balanced accuracy is defined as the average of positive class accuracy and the negative class accuracy. Assume your data set has 20 positive samples and 80 negative samples. Your model predicted 23 samples as positive and 77 samples as negative as shown in the confusion matrix below.

|                            | Predicted positive  | Predicted negative | Total |
|-----------------|--------------------|---------------------|------|
| Actual positive  |         TP = 15            |          FN = 5            | 20     |
| Actual negative |         FP = 8              |          TN =72          | 80     |
| Total                   |             23                |               77              | 100   |

Based on this confusion matrix: 
your _**positive class accuracy**_ = 15 / (15+5) = 0.75 
your _**negative class accuracy**_ = 72 / (72+8) = 0.90
Therefore, your _**balanced accuracy**_ = (0.75 + 0.90) / 2 = 0.825","# Dataset Description

# Training Data

The training data for this assignment can be found in the file `train_data.csv`. This file contains resource consumption metrics of 20,000 jobs run on one of the HPC clusters at Purdue University. A **job** is typically an execution of a scientific application on the HPC cluster, e.g., training your model with one set of parameters can be considered as a **job**.

The training data consists of about 8% failure data (this is referred to as the ¡°positive class¡±). You will build Machine Learning models in Python to predict whether a job will fail or not, given the resource usage data.

The columns in the data file are:

- **`job_id`**: Unique identifier for a job.
- **`memory_GB`**: Aggregate memory utilization for a job.
- **`network_log10_MBps`**: Aggregate network utilization for a job (in logarithmic scale).
- **`local_IO_log10_MBps`**: Aggregate local disk IO for a job (in logarithmic scale).
- **`NFS_IO_log10_MBps`**: Aggregate remote filesystem (NFS) IO for a job (in logarithmic scale).
- **`failed`**: Failure status of a job. A failed job is marked as `1`.

# Test Data

Test data for this competition may be found in the file `test_data_unlabeled.csv`. This file contains resource usage metrics from 10,000 jobs. The columns in the the test data are identical to the training data except for the column `failed`. Your Machine Learning model should predict the value of this column.

The test data contains about 20% validation data which will be used to determine your ranking in the Public leaderboard. The rest 80% of test data will be used to determine your final score.",True,True
4320,dlai5-bad-apples,"# Description

<p>This is a ""crowdsourcing"" hackathon challenge from The 5th Deep Learning and Artificial Intelligence (DLAI5) Summer School, running from May 6-10, 2021.</p>
<h2>Bad Apples!</h2>
This hackathon was created to demonstrate the process of setting up a competition, along with the peril of using public datasets. This competition is to classify ""fresh"" vs ""rotten"" apples, bananas, and oranges. However, such classification can be rather subjective and the training/testing datasets suggest that it is really ""fresh"" vs ""not-fresh"". The validation dataset was mostly crowdsourced from participants at the DLAI5 hackathon from pics taken by themselves, with minimal data augmentation. Data labeling was a partially crowdsourced effort as well.

Special thanks to the following contributors who provided real-life images, in chronological order of images received: Ramil Joaquin, Kmer Jaeriz Mesina, Adrian-Ver Federizo, Renel R. Dilao, Kelsey Kng, Sandipan Majhi, Sharmistha Bhattacharya.

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank all participants from DLAI5 hackathon session on Sat, May 8, 2021 who participated in the creation of this challenge.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is macro <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score""> F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>

F1 = 2 p r / [p + r] where p = tp / [tp + fp] and r = tp / [tp +fn]

<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<p>The macro F1 is the arithmetic mean of the F1-score of each class.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and Code. Code should be a comma-delimited list. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header of ID and Code and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID=imagefilename.csv, Code={1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>The training and testing data are retrieved from an existing kaggle dataset:<br> 
<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/sriramr/fruits-fresh-and-rotten-for-classification"">https://www.kaggle.com/sriramr/fruits-fresh-and-rotten-for-classification</a></p>
The dataset contains augmented data by cropping and rotation and flipping.
<p>Usage Information
<br>
License                 Unknown <br>
Visibility                Public<br>
Dataset owner     Sriram Reddy Kalluri<br>
Last updated       2018-08-24<br>
Date created       2018-08-24<br>
Current version   Version 1</p>

<p>The validation data was mostly crowdsourced from the participants of the DLAI5 hackathon. Special thanks to the following contributors who provided real-life images, in chronological order of images received: Ramil Joaquin, Kmer Jaeriz Mesina, Adrian-Ver Federizo, Renel R. Dilao, Kelsey Kng, Sandipan Majhi, Sharmistha Bhattacharya.</p>

A few validation images were extracted from <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/moltean/fruits"">https://www.kaggle.com/moltean/fruits</a> (Version 5).

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train folder</strong> - the training set in 6 sub-folders of freshapples, freshbanana, freshoranges, rottenapples, rottenbanana, rottenoranges</li>
<li><strong>test folder</strong> - the test set in 6 sub-folders of freshapples, freshbanana, freshoranges, rottenapples, rottenbanana, rottenoranges</li>
<li> <strong>validation folder</strong> - unseen images of freshapples, freshbanana, freshoranges, rottenapples, rottenbanana, rottenoranges
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format with the following code: <br> ""1"" for fresh apples; ""2"" for fresh banana; ""3"" for fresh oranges; ""4"" for rotten apples; ""5"" for rotten banana; ""6"" for rotten oranges</li>",True,True
4321,sssso-mdcl-competition-ii,"# Description

Om Sri Sai Ram. Welcome to the final leg of your training program.

This is a competition in which you need to predict whether the customer would buy a credit card or not. Relevant data has been provided to you.

The competition will end at 1 PM sharp. Please start submitting when you have 30 mins in hand.
----------------------
# Evaluation


<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
Please have a look at the sample submission file provided before submitting the data. Reach out to me if you are facing any concerns on Zoom call over chat.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>tobepredicted.csv</strong> - predictions to be made on this dataset</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - Sample submission file. This is the format your final output format should be in.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - customer id</li>
<li><strong>Age</strong> - age of the customer</li>
<li><strong>Experience</strong> - experience</li>
<li><strong>Income</strong> - income of the customer in Thousand Dollars</li>
<li><strong>ZIP Code</strong> - ZIP Code</li>
<li><strong>Family</strong> - Number of family members</li>
<li><strong>CCAvg</strong> - Average spending on credit card per month</li>
<li><strong>Education</strong> - income of the customer in Thousand Dollars</li>
<li><strong>Mortgage</strong> - Loan account</li>
<li><strong>Personal Loan</strong> - Average spending on credit card per month</li>
<li><strong>Securities Account</strong> - Does the customer have a securities account with the bank?</li>
<li><strong>CD Account</strong> - Does the customer have a certificate of deposit (CD) account with the bank?</li>
<li><strong>Online</strong> - Does the customer use internet banking facilities?</li>
<li><strong>CreditCard</strong> - This is the y variable which needs to be predicted</li>
</ul>",True,True
4322,ssaiu-dla-project-b-test,"# Description

Template Description

<p>This is the home page of the competition. You don't need a subtitle here. The competition sub-title will appear above. This is where you introduce the problem. You can upload images using the ""select files"" widget on the left in the competition wizard. Upload an image, refresh the page, copy its URL, then insert within the wizard's editor.</p>
<p>If you are copy-pasting from another application, like Word or your browser, try to make sure the html formatting is clean. You can view a page's html using the button at the top right of the editor's toolbar.</p>
<h2>This is a subtitle</h2>
<p>To format pages, stick to the following conventions:</p>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Paragraphs should go in p tags&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<pre>&lt;pre&gt;Code should go in pre tags&lt;/pre&gt;</pre>
<pre>&lt;h2&gt;Subtitles should go in h2 tags&lt;/h2&gt;</pre>
<p>You can display equations using LaTeX enclosed in escaped brackets. For example, this:</p>
<p>\[ \epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\log(p_i + 1) - \log(a_i+1))^2 } \]</p>
<p>is created by this:</p>
<pre>\[
\epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\log(p_i + 1) - \log(a_i+1))^2 }
\]</pre>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Professor Plum, Ph.D. for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Template Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Template Data

This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
