4328,sorghum-biomass-prediction,"# Description

<p>Sorghum is widely used as an agricultural feed substitute, a gluten-free ancient grain, a source of bio-fuel, and even as popcorn in some food communities. Demand for sorghum for these variety of purposes has risen in an increasingly global economy, motivating the need to rigorous plant breeding strategies to select for traits that are valued for each purpose (e.g., more grain for food uses or more biomass for bio-fuel production).</p>

In 2016, the Advanced Research Project Agency--Energy (ARPA-E) funded the Transportation Energy Resources from Renewable Agriculture Phenotyping Reference Platform, or TERRA-REF. The TERRA-REF project stood up a state-of-the-art gantry based system for monitoring the full growth cycle of over an acre of crops with a cutting-edge suite of imaging sensors, including stereo-RGB, thermal, short- and long-wave hyperspectral, and laser 3D-scanner sensors.

![TERRA-REF Gantry System](https://terraref.org/sites/terraref.org/files/TERRA-REF-Scanner.jpg)

In this competition, you will be working with data from the stereo-RGB sensor to predict end-of-season biomass.

End-of-season biomass is the single most relevant measure of how useful a particular crop will be for biofuel usage. The more biomass a plant has, the more biofuel can be produced!

An accurate machine learning system that could predict end-of-season biomass from early season visual data could help breeders and farmers in only dedicating precious resources to crops that are likely to be useful for biofuel.
----------------------
# Dataset

<h2>Training Data</h2>
The training data consists of 277,327 images, from 176 different sorghum species grown in 349 unique plots. Metadata for the training dataset can be found in the train\_dataset\_by\_image.csv file, which contains the following information:
- path: the path to the image in the data directory
- plot: an identifier for the plot that each image comes from
- cultivar: the species of sorghum shown in the particular image
- date_captured: the date on which the image was captured
- days\_after\_planting: the number of days after the seed was planted
- accumulated\_heat\_units: the amount of sunlight accumulated at the field site by this point in the growing cycle
- camera\_height\_off\_ground: the height in meters of the camera off of the ground (the camera moves upwards over the course of the season)
- end\_of\_season\_dry\_biomass: the weight of the plants cut down in the particular plot of interest

The TERRA-REF field consisted of multiple plots for each cultivar (the same species was grown in multiple different locations in the field). The training data is organized by plot, with all of the images from the same plot being stored inside of the same folder, in the following structure:

* data
    * train
        * cultivarName1\_plotId1
            * image1.png
            * image2.png
        * cultivarName1\_plotId2
            * image1.png
            * image2.png
        * cultivarName2\_plotId1
            * image1.png
            * image2.png
        * cultivarName2\_plotId2
            * image1.png
            * image2.png

End of season dry biomass was collected and measured per plot. The train\_dataset\_by\_image.csv file provides the per plot end of season dry biomass label for every image. We additionally provide the train\_labels\_by\_plot.csv metadata file which contains the end\_of\_season\_dry\_biomass for each plot (this is the same information as in the train\_dataset\_by\_image.csv file but aggregated by plot for convenience).

<h2>Testing Data</h2>
The testing data consists of 19,442 images from 110 unique plots. The plots are from unknown cultivars (although there is no overlap between the training and testing cultivars).

1/3 of the plots include images from early in the growing cycle, 1/3 from mid-growing season, and 1/3 from late in the growing season. Metadata for the testing imagery is included in the test\_dataset.csv file, with the following columns:

- path: the path to the image in the data directory
- plot_id: an identifier for the plot that each image comes from
- phase: whether this plot and its images come from early, mid or late season
- date_captured: the date on which the image was captured
- days\_after\_planting: the number of days after the seed was planted
- accumulated\_heat\_units: the amount of sunlight accumulated at the field site by this point in the growing cycle
- camera\_height\_off\_ground: the height in meters of the camera off of the ground (the camera moves upwards over the course of the season)

The test data is organized by phase and then by plot, in the following structure:

* data
    * test
        * early
              * plotId1
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png
              * plotId2
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png
        * mid
              * plotId1
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png
              * plotId2
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png
        * late
              * plotId1
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png
              * plotId2
                  * image1.png
                  * image2.png

In the evaluation, you are expected to predict the end-of-season dry biomass for each ***plot*** in the test dataset.
----------------------
# Submission & Evaluation

Contestants should submit a csv file that contains two columns: plot\_id and end\_of\_season\_dry\_biomass.

There should be one estimate of end_of_season_dry_biomass per plot. The plot\_id is zero-padded to have 3 numbers (so plot 50 should be 050, and plot 100 is just 100). These plot\_ids match the folder names in the test dataset.

A full example submission file can be found in the data (example\_submission.csv), but the general form should look like:

| plot\_id | end\_of\_season\_biomass |
| --- | --- |
| 050 | 23137.5 |
| 050 | 23137.5 |
| 101 | 23137.5 |
| 007 | 23137.5 |
| 056 | 23137.5 |

Approaches will be evaluated with Weighted Root Mean Square Error, with predictions for plots that only contain early season data being weighted 3X, plots that only contain midseason data weighted 2X and plots only containing late season data weighted 1X.

This rewards approaches that are better at the challenging but important task of accurately predicting end of season dry biomass from early season visual sensor data.
----------------------
# ICCV 2021 ¨C CVPPA

This competition is associated with the Computer Vision Problems in Plant Phenotyping and Agriculture (CVPPA) Workshop at the 2021 International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV).

We encourage competition participants who have developed novel approaches to predicting end of season sorghum biomass from visual data to submit an academic paper to the workshop.

The deadline for paper submissions related to this challenge will be July 16, 2021. Authors should report their final test set score in their paper and highlight the academic contribution of their work. All papers will go through double blind-peer review. Superlative paper submissions (evaluated both on their rank in the competition and their academic contribution) will be invited to present at CVPPA and be published in the ICCV workshop proceedings.

Further information about the workshop, author instructions, and submission guidelines are available at: [https://cvppa2021.github.io/](https://cvppa2021.github.io/).

More information about CVPPA:

CVPPA, previously known as CVPPP, has been a successful workshop in recent years at ECCV, BMVC, ICCV, and CVPR. CVPPA this year is held in conjunction with ICCV 2021. The goal of this workshop is to continue to showcase the challenges raised by and extend the state of the art in computer vision for plant phenotyping and agriculture. Plant phenotyping is the identification of effects on plant structure and function (the phenotype) resulting from genotypic differences (i.e., differences in the genetic code) and the environmental conditions a plant has been exposed to. Knowledge of plant phenotypes is a key ingredient of the knowledge-based bioeconomy, which not only literally helps to feed the world, but is also essential for feed, fibre and fuel production. We want to identify key but unsolved problems, expose the current state-of-the-art, and broaden the field and the community.


When citing the data used in this competition, please use the following reference:

