4352,wood-anomaly-detection,"# Description

<p>Surface or Texture-based Anomaly detection is a field within industrial quality inspection that is focused on detecting errors related to the texture of the objects, for instance, stains, color mismatch, cracks, etc. This anomalies may lead to a faulty or even dangerous final product depending on the field, making quality inspection necessary. This is commonly performed employing cameras or other sensors that can detect the patterns of the object's texture. Being able to automatize this process is crucial, as manual inspection has proven to be tedious, slow and inaccurate for many industries.</p>

<p>Moreover, wooden textures present high variability in their structure and color making really difficult to ""just learn the correct wooden textures"" in order to detect anomalies. However, industry users cannot usually compile all the possible anomalies that might occur during production and their appearances as there are potentially infinite possibilities. Furthermore, manual labeling is a rather subjective and time-consuming task. Taking this into account, one-class classification is the most feasible approach to follow in order to address the problem from a practical industrial perspective.</p>

<p>Given this approach, creating a new anomaly detection process only requires to gather a set of objects that will be classified as ""correct"", and an algorithm will be trained using them. Then, every other object under inspection will be compared to the initial set. Given a significant deviation from the normality, the object can be automatically rejected. </p>

<p> For this reason we propose a challenge to identify anomalies in wood objects training only with non-abnormal textures. For more details regarding capture process visit <a href=""https://www.zerogravity3d.com/"">zg3d device webpage</a> </p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> Evaluation is done through  Intersection over Union metric implemented in kaggle, for an awesome explanation please visit this post <a href=""url"">https://www.kaggle.com/pestipeti/explanation-of-scoring-metric</a> </p>

<p> All the images in the test folder must be segmented once, if an image does not contain an anomaly just predict an empty string "" ""</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>In this competition, you are required to locate anomalies in wood textured objects images identifying all the abnormal pixels in the image. Some images do not contain any anomaly while others may contain more than one abnormal zone. Different defects are present in the images such as knots, stains, cracks or porosity.</p>

<p>Training and validation images, available in ""train"" and ""val"" folders do not contain any defect so this is a one-class classification challenge where a generalization of the ""wood texture"" is required. The folder ""complete_test"" contains all the images that must be predicted in a submission. ""submission.csv"" shows a valid submission file, but it is NOT a ground-truth file. </p>

<p>Valid submissions must predict anomalies for all the test images, if an image has no anomaly an empty string "" "" should be predicted. Anomalies are encoded in a run-length encoding from top left pixel to bottom right pixel in order to be computed through an intersection over union metric. For a detailed explanation of how Intersection over union works check 
<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/pestipeti/explanation-of-scoring-metric"">this awesome explanation</a>.</p>

<p>As it is a one-class classification problem no training labels are required, however ""public test"" masks can be consulted in the published <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/itiresearch/wood-anomaly-detection-one-class-classification"">dataset</a> as well as a detailed description of capture methodology.</p>",True,True
4353,largefinefoodai-iccv-recognition,"# Description

Generic visual analysis (e.g., image recognition, detection and segmentation) has shown remarkable improvement over the past few years, exemplified in part by the large-scale visual analysis challenge, such as the Imagenet classification challenge, MS-COCO Object detection and segmentation challenge, and YouTube-8M large-scale video understanding, where error rates continue to drop every year. In order to continue advancing the state of the art in computer vision, many researchers are now putting more focus on fine-grained visual analysis. It is a fundamental problem, and has become ubiquitous in diverse real-world applications among various research areas of computer vision. As one fine-grained visual analysis task, food analysis is becoming one hot topic for its many health applications, such as nutrition analysis, diet management and food recommendation. It can also support both fundamental and complicated vision and learning tasks, such as fine-grained food recognition and retrieval, multi-label food attribute learning, food image detection and segmentation, cross-modal food image-recipe retrieval meal image synthesis and cooking video understanding. However, a significant obstacle for research in this area has been the lack of large annotated food datasets.

As part of the [LargeFineFoodAI ](https://platform.sankuai.com/foodai2021.html#index) workshop at [ICCV 2021](http://iccv2021.thecvf.com/), we are conducting the Large-scale Fine-grained Food Analysis Challenge 2021. Moreover,  we are excited to advance large-scale fine-grained food analysis by introducing the large-scale food dataset with 1,500 food categories and about 80,0000 images. And 1000 food categories will be used for food recognition. Therefore, besides talks from domain experts in food computing as invited speakers and presentation from accepted papers, as the main part of this Workshop, we will organize a competition on large-scale food image recognition and retrieval based on the released dataset.

Large-scale fine-grained food recognition is sponsored by Meituan.

## The final result of the challenge was announced: https://foodai-workshop.meituan.com/foodai2021.html#result
----------------------
# Evaluation

For this large-scale fine-grained food recognition, 1000 food classes and their corresponding images are used, namely Food1K. Food1K is divided into two parts, the training set and the test set. Each food image has one ground truth label. Top-1 classification accuracy is used for evaluation.

## Submission File

We are using top-1 accuracy, for each image in the test set **you must predict its corresponding category labels**.  The csv file should contain a header and have the following format:

Id,Predicted
1,92 
2,382
3,411

The `Id` column corresponds to the test image name. The `Predicted` column corresponds to the predicted category ids, separated by spaces. You should have one row for each test image. The Kaggle leaderboard will use top-1 accuracy as the metric.
----------------------
# Timeline

- **August 24th 2021** - Entry deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.
- **August 24th, 2021** - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants may merge teams.
- **September 1th, 2021** - Final submission deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.




----------------------
# ICCV 

The complementarity is summarized as follows. First, this workshop on large-scale food analysis is one important topic of fine-grained visual analysis, which is one fundamental task in computer vision, and thus is very complementary to this topic in the main ICCV conference. Second, we introduced a large-scale food dataset for recognition and retrieval and hold on competition for evaluation, and thus is very relevant to the topic of datasets and evaluation in the main ICCV conference. Third, large-scale food analysis is interdisciplinary, involving different fields, such as computer vision, food science, nutrition and health, resulting in new vision applications and systems from the topic of the main ICC conference. Finally, this workshop is also relevant to many food-relevant tasks, such as multi-label food attribute learning, food image detection and segmentation, cross-modal food image-recipe retrieval meal image synthesis and cooking video understanding, which are complimentary to topics, such as multi-label learning, multimodal learning, image detection and segmentation from the main ICCV conference.","# Dataset Description

## Data 
For this track, we will use our own constructed dataset collected from Meituan, and this dataset consists of 1,000 classes and about 50,0000 images. To build one high-quality dataset, after initial food image collection, all the images are finally evaluated by the professional annotators from the data annotation and quality inspection team in the Meituan company. The dataset for food recognition can be downloaded via the link below:

Train : https://s3plus.meituan.net/v1/mss_fad1a48f61e8451b8172ba5abfdbbee5/foodai-workshop-challenge/Train.tar

Val : https://s3plus.meituan.net/v1/mss_fad1a48f61e8451b8172ba5abfdbbee5/foodai-workshop-challenge/Val.tar

Test : https://s3plus.meituan.net/v1/mss_fad1a48f61e8451b8172ba5abfdbbee5/foodai-workshop-challenge/Test.tar

Label : https://s3plus.meituan.net/v1/mss_fad1a48f61e8451b8172ba5abfdbbee5/foodai-workshop-challenge/Label.tar

## File descriptions

- **kaggle_sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format. Note that you must provide exactly 5 predictions per test image.",True,True
