4364,campanha-de-telemarketing,"# Description

A Seguradora Arapiraca est¨¢ direcionando uma campanha de telemarketing para um de seus produtos. Por¨¦m, antes de iniciar a campanha o gerente respons¨¢vel entrou em contato com a equipe de ci¨ºncia de dados, pois tinha algumas preocupa??es:
* Durante a campanha de 2019, devido ao inc?modo gerado pela liga??o, alguns clientes resolveram cancelar suas contas no banco.
* O valor aplicado para a campanha permite que sejam feitas 2 mil liga??es.
* Espera-se que pelo menos 600 contratos sejam fechados, para garantir a meta da ¨¢rea e evitar a demiss?o do gerente.

Em 2019, foram realizadas cerca de 42 mil liga??es numa campanha para o mesmo produto. A ¨¢rea possui uma base de dados com informa??es de cada cliente e com a marca??o se ele fechou ou n?o acordo naquela campanha.
----------------------
# Evaluation

1. Acur¨¢cia
Mede o percentual de amostras que s?o corretamente classificadas, ou seja, todas as amostras positivas e negativas classificadas corretamente em rela??o ao n¨²mero total de amostras.","# Dataset Description

<p>This page shows up underneath the data files. Here you describe what files you have provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitons, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.</p>
<p>Participants should be able to answer questions like these after reading the data description: What files do I need? What should I expect the data format to be? What am I predicting? What acroynms will I encounter?</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metaData.csv</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>

## Vari¨¢veis explicativas
1 - age (numeric)

2 - job : type of job (categorical: 'admin.','blue-collar','entrepreneur','housemaid','management','retired','self-employed','services','student','technician','unemployed','unknown')

3 - marital : marital status (categorical: 'divorced','married','single','unknown'; note: 'divorced' means divorced or widowed)

4 - education (categorical: 'basic.4y','basic.6y','basic.9y','high.school','illiterate','professional.course','university.degree','unknown')

5 - default: has credit in default? (categorical: 'no','yes','unknown')

6 - housing: has housing loan? (categorical: 'no','yes','unknown')

7 - loan: has personal loan? (categorical: 'no','yes','unknown')

8 - contact: contact communication type (categorical: 'cellular','telephone')

9 - month: last contact month of year (categorical: 'jan', 'feb', 'mar', ..., 'nov', 'dec')

10 - day_of_week: last contact day of the week (categorical: 'mon','tue','wed','thu','fri')

11 - duration: last contact duration, in seconds (numeric). Important note: this attribute highly affects the output target (e.g., if duration=0 then y='no'). Yet, the duration is not known before a call is performed. Also, after the end of the call y is obviously known. Thus, this input should only be included for benchmark purposes and should be discarded if the intention is to have a realistic predictive model.

12 - campaign: number of contacts performed during this campaign and for this client (numeric, includes last contact)

13 - pdays: number of days that passed by after the client was last contacted from a previous campaign (numeric; 999 means client was not previously contacted)

14 - previous: number of contacts performed before this campaign and for this client (numeric)

15 - poutcome: outcome of the previous marketing campaign (categorical: 'failure','nonexistent','success')

16 - emp.var.rate: employment variation rate - (numeric)

17 - cons.price.idx: consumer price index - (numeric)

18 - cons.conf.idx: consumer confidence index - (numeric)

19 - euribor3m: euribor 3 month rate - (numeric)

20 - nr.employed: number of employees - (numeric)

## Vari¨¢vel dependente:

y - has the client subscribed a term deposit? (binary: '1','0')",True,True
4365,bcu-ratings-2021,"# Description

<h2>Objetivo</h2>
<p>El objetivo de esta competici¨®n es poner en pr¨¢ctica algunos de los conceptos tratados en el curso de ""Modelizaci¨®n estad¨ªstica"".</p>

<h2>Funcionamiento de la competici¨®n</h2>

<p>Para poder participar en la competici¨®n, se debe seguir los siguientes pasos:</p>

<ul>
<li>Aceptar las normas de participaci¨®n (bot¨®n azul en la parte derecha, bajo el cuadro con el t¨ªtulo de la competici¨®n)</li>

<li>Consultar los ejemplos que se ir¨¢n colgando. Para ello, ir al apartado Notebooks y ver las notebooks p¨²blicas colgadas. El resultado de estas notebooks se utilizar¨¢ como benchmark en la leaderboard</li>

<li>Entrenar un modelo y hacer predicciones. Para ello, se puede realizar un modelo en local (instalar Python, los packages necesarios, etc.) o crear una notebook privada (recomendado, ya que ser¨¢ m¨¢s f¨¢cil compartir los resultados e instalar los packages necesarios)</li>

<li>Subir los resultados del modelo en forma de submission. Para ello, clickar en el bot¨®n submit predictions en la parte derecha de la pantalla, debajo del cuadro con el t¨ªtulo de la competici¨®n</li>
</ul>

<p>Para cualquier duda, pod¨¦is colgar un post en el apartado Discussion (preferible, ya que as¨ª otros compa?eros pord¨¢n consultar las dudas que tengan) o contactarme directamente por mail chat.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluaci¨®n de la bondad del modelo</h2>

<p>La m¨¦trica utilizada para la evaluaci¨®n de la competici¨®n es el ¨¢rea bajo la curva ROC. El ¨¢rea bajo la curva ROC ¨¢¨¦ es una m¨¦trica muy utilizada en problemas de clasificaci¨®n, y da una idea de la capacidad del modelo de distinguir entre dos clases distintas.</p>

<p>Este art¨ªculo explica en detalle en qu¨¦ consiste la m¨¦trica: </p>
<a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5"">https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5</a>

<p>La m¨¦trica AUC tiene relaci¨®n directa con otra m¨¦trica muy utilizada en problemas de clasificaci¨®n: el GINI o ¨ªndice de Poder ¨®. El c¨¢lculo del GINI a parir del AUC es el siguiente:</p>

\[ GINI = 2 ¡¤ AUC - 1\]","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<p>Se han colgado 3 archivos CSV y un .txt que contiene la descripci¨®n de las variables. Los archivos CSV son los siguientes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - la muestra de train, incluye la columna TARGET, que es el valor a predecir</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - la muestra de test, contiene los datos para los que hay que predecir la variable TARGET. El resultado de estas predicciones es el que se utilizar¨¢ para evaluar el modelo en la Leaderboard</li>
<li><strong>solution0.csv</strong> - un ejemplo de CSV que muestra el formato con el que se tienen que subir los datos para que se acepte como un resultado v¨¢lido y se contabilice el resultado en la leaderboard</li>
</ul>",True,True
4366,cse-465-kickstarter-projects-competition,"# Description

<h2>Can we predict if a kickstarter project will succeed or not?</h2>

<p>With different tools and ideas explored through the semester in CSE465 ML Course, this project (competition) can be the grand finale as a challenge to test our understanding for everything we have been through together.</p>

<p>And while the idea in itself is useful for everyone it's not the actual goal of this competition, the true target is to go with students in a new journey of challenges where they learn new and interesting ideas in data preprocessing and model development. They will get the chance to deal with encoded data formats like (unix timestamps), json dumped columns and NaN values across the whole dataset.</p>

<h2>What should participants learn from this competition ? </h2>
1. `sklearn` and its wide variety of algorithms and functionalities.
2. `pandas` and how to preprocess data for better accuracy.
3. Data Visualization using `matplotlib` or any other package.
4. Working with large datasets in different formats.
5. Using **Kaggle** notebooks for sharing code and ideas with others


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation
The evaluation metric for this competition is the Categorization Accuracy. It is the number of correct predictions made divided by the total number of predictions. Upload in the competition your predictions that reach the best accuracy and specify the parameters that you used.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the class variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
id,state
307,failed
308,successful
309,successful
310,failed
311,successful
312,successful
```","# Dataset Description

<p>This dataset was scrapped from [Kickstarter](https://www.kickstarter.com/) to provide an opportunity for enthusiasts to build a success predictor for different projects hosted there</p>
<p> Data here is close enough of what real problems can be like, different problems will be tackled through the building of the model and different trade offs will be explored in hope of achieving better accuracy. </p>
  
<h2>Files descriptions</h2>

- `train.csv` has all the data you will need for training and validation
- `test.csv` what you need to predict
- `sample.csv` a sample of how the submitted file should look like (`id` are from `train.csv`)

<h2>Data fields</h2>
1. `id` : unique id for every record in the whole dataset (train + test)
2. `name` : project name (from kickstart)
3. `main_category` :  field of operation of the project (take care of the NaNs)
4. `backers_count` :  number of investors in the project
5. `blurb` :  project claim to achieve
6. `category` :  specific field of category (needs cleaning)
7. `converted_pledged_amount` :  amount of money invested in the project after currency conversion
8. `country_displayable_name` :  the country which will host the project 
9. `created_at` :  time of project creation (in UNIX timestamp)
10. `creator` :  name and other details of the creator (json dumped data)
11. `currency_trailing_code` :  is the project money tracked or not
12. `currency` :  the project native currency (not the backer's currency)
13. `deadline` :  when will the kickstarter end
14. `goal` :  the amount of money the project is attempting to raise
15. `launched_at` :  when the project started to be funded
16. `pledged` : the amount that has been pledged to the project. (in the currency of the `currency` column)
17. `slug` :  creator-selected keyword for the project
18. `source_url` : url of the project in kickstarter
19. `spotlight` :  is the project spotlighted of not, learn more about spotlight from [here](https://help.kickstarter.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005135834-What-is-Spotlight-)
20. `staff_pick` :  is the project recommended by kickstarter staff or not
21. `state_changed_at` :  when the project state was changed
22. `static_usd_rate` :  conversion rate
23. `urls` :  project url with its rewards (if any), (in json format)
24. `usd_pledged` :  amount of investment in us dollars 
25. `usd_type` : type of the dollar invested (international or not)  
26. `usd_pledged_real` :  real us dollars which was invested
27. `usd_goal_real` :  real goal in us dollars
28. `state` :  the end result of the project (`failed`, `successful`, `canceled`)",True,True
4367,dspt12-water-pump-challenge,"# Description

## Can you predict which water pumps are faulty?

Using data from Taarifa and the Tanzanian Ministry of Water, can you predict which pumps are functional, which need some repairs, and which don't work at all? Predict one of these three classes based on a number of variables about what kind of pump is operating, when it was installed, and how it is managed. A smart understanding of which water pumps will fail can improve maintenance operations and ensure that clean, potable water is available to communities across Tanzania.

This predictive modeling challenge comes from [DrivenData](https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/7/pump-it-up-data-mining-the-water-table/), an organization that helps non-profits by hosting data science competitions for social impact. The competition has open licensing: ""The data is available for use outside of DrivenData."" We are reusing the data on Kaggle's InClass platform so we can run a weeklong challenge just for your Lambda School Data Science cohort.

The data comes from the Taarifa waterpoints dashboard, which aggregates data from the Tanzania Ministry of Water. In their own words:

> Taarifa is an open-source platform for the crowd sourced reporting and triaging of infrastructure related issues. Think of it as a bug tracker for the real world which helps to engage citizens with their local government. We are currently working on an Innovation Project in Tanzania, with various partners.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Predictions should be made for each water pump in the <strong>test dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `status_group` 
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id, status_group<br>3287, functional<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

## File Descriptions
- `train_features.csv`: the training set features
- `train_labels.csv`: the training set labels
- `test_features.csv`: the test set features
- `sample_submission.csv`: a sample submission file in the correct format

## Features
Your goal is to predict the operating condition of a waterpoint for each record in the dataset. You are provided the following set of information about the waterpoint:

- `amount_tsh`: Total static head (amount water available to waterpoint)
- `date_recorded`: The date the row was entered
- `funder`: Who funded the well
- `gps_height`: Altitude of the well
- `installer`: Organization that installed the well
- `longitude`: GPS coordinate
- `latitude`: GPS coordinate
- `wpt_name`: Name of the waterpoint if there is one
- `num_private`:
- `basin`: Geographic water basin
- `subvillage`: Geographic location
- `region`: Geographic location
- `region_code`: Geographic location (coded)
- `district_code`: Geographic location (coded)
- `lga`: Geographic location
- `ward`: Geographic location
- `population`: Population around the well
- `public_meeting`: True/False
- `recorded_by`: Group entering this row of data
- `scheme_management`: Who operates the waterpoint
- `scheme_name`: Who operates the waterpoint
- `permit`: If the waterpoint is permitted
- `construction_year`: Year the waterpoint was constructed
- `extraction_type`: The kind of extraction the waterpoint uses
- `extraction_type_group`: The kind of extraction the waterpoint uses
- `extraction_type_class`: The kind of extraction the waterpoint uses
- `management`: How the waterpoint is managed
- `management_group`: How the waterpoint is managed
- `payment`: What the water costs
- `payment_type`: What the water costs
- `water_quality`: The quality of the water
- `quality_group`: The quality of the water
- `quantity`: The quantity of water
- `quantity_group`: The quantity of water
- `source`: The source of the water
- `source_type`: The source of the water
- `source_class`: The source of the water
- `waterpoint_type`: The kind of waterpoint
- `waterpoint_type_group`: The kind of waterpoint

## Labels
There are three possible values:

- `functional`: the waterpoint is operational and there are no repairs needed
- `functional needs repair`: the waterpoint is operational, but needs repairs
- `non functional`: the waterpoint is not operational",True,True
4368,fit5149-2021-a2,"# Description

Here is the home page of the FIT5149 2021S2 Assessment 2 competition. All the descriptions and data sets can be found on Moodle

We only use this in-class competition as a temporary test, the final results still rely on your Moodle submissions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is Multi-Column AUC measure which is a common performance measure for multi-label classification problems. </p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset (ID) </strong>, submission files should contain six columns: id, ac, ev, oven, wash and dryer. ID values should be exactly the same as the available test set in Moodle. **In your submitted csv file, the naming of all the columns <strong> MUST </strong> be exactly the same as above with the same order otherwise, your score might be wrong. ** Below, you can see the schema of the submitted predictions.</p>


|id |ac|ev|oven|wash|dryer|	
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
|1| 1 |	1 |	0	 |0	 |1 |
|2| 0 |	1 |	1	 |0	 |1 |
|3| 0 |	0 |	1	 |1	 |0 |","# Dataset Description

In order to train your model, you must use the following train set.",True,True
