4384,cse144-hw4-part2,"# Description

This is an image classification in-class competition. The task is to use images of 10 different animals and train a model to classify them correctly. The details about the data is in the data tab. 

You are to download the data, which is made of two parts: train, which has both data and the label, and test, which only has data and not the label. You will train a model, and then predict the labels of the test dataset, and submit that result here in Kaggle. 

The accuracy score of your result will be shown in the leaderboard. The public leader board is scored on 50% of the test data, and the private leader board is scored on the other 50%. During the competition, you can submit results up to 20 times a day and see your result on public leaderboard. And when the competition is over, the final score will be shown in the private leaderboard. The two leaderboards, is to avoid people overfitting to the public leaderboard. 

You are free to use any programming languages, packages, and machine learning models. 

The grading of this part of the homework will be mostly based on the private leaderboard accuracy and ranking (40pts), and partially on the report (20pts). 
----------------------
# Evaluation


### Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy Score, which is defined by the percentage of correct classification among all samples. 

### Submission Format
Submission file should contain two columns: id and Label. id is the index of the test data, starting from 0 to 1999 for 2000 test samples. Label is corresponding classification label of that data, which is an integer from 1 to 10.  
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,label<br>0,1<br>1,3<br>2,10<br>3,2<br>...<br>1999,5</pre>","# Dataset Description

The data is a subsample of[ ImageNet](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5206848). 

### Files
There are four files in the dataset: `train.pkl` and `test.pkl`, `helper.ipynb`, and `sample_submission.csv`.

* `helper.ipynb`: code snippets for you to read the train and test data with pickle, and to display an image. You need to use Python3. 

* `train.pkl`: training data serialized with pickle. there are 10000 samples and 10000 corresponding labels. 

* `test.pkl`: test data serialized with pickle. there are 2000 samples, no label. 

* `sample_submission.csv`: to submit your predictions, you should use the exact same format as this. (except for changing the numbers in the label column to the one that's output from your model). The file should contain two columns: id and Label. id is the index of the test data, starting from 0 to 1999 for 2000 test samples. Label is corresponding classification label of that data, which is an integer from 1 to 10.  <p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
> id,label<br>0,1<br>1,3<br>2,10<br>3,2<br>...<br>1999,5


### Data Format
`train.pkl`, after unpickling, is a dictionary with ""data"" and ""label"" fields. `test.pkl` contains just ""data"", no ""label"". There are 10000 samples in train and 2000 samples in test. 

""data""
> numpy array with samples x 12288. For each sample, first 4096 numbers represent red channel, next 4096 numbers green channel, last 4096 numbers represent blue channel.

""label""
> number representing image class, indexing starts at 1 and ends at 10. Here are the ten classes:
 1: 'dog',
 2: 'rabbit',
 3: 'deer',
 4: 'elephant',
 5: 'horse',
 6: 'squirrel',
 7: 'tiger',
 8: 'wolf',
 9: 'chimpanzee',
 10: 'panda'.",True,True
4385,cifar100-image-classification-with-noisy-labels,"# Description

## MLDL 2021 Spring @ GIST
Coding assignment #2: Image category classification with CIFAR-100 dataset with noisy labels

(Note: this is an individual basis assignment!)

## Task
Given a set of images and their label, please train your model to classify a test image into the label. The label is class id string (looks like a number) - {00, 01, ¡­, 09}. So, total of 100 classes.

The image size is 32 (width) by 32 (height) pixels in RGB (3) channels.

## Evaluation metric
Top-1 accuracy: Compare with the true label with the predicted label by your trained model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

Top-1 accuracy: Compare with the true label with the predicted label by your trained model.","# Dataset Description

## CIFAR100 dataset with noisy labels
Modified by Jonghyun Choi (2021.5.23)

## Note
1. Filenames are all randomized.
1. Some of the training samples' label are randomized for the noisy label
1. Testing samples' label are *not* randomized
1. Number of samples for training and testing are the same to the CIFAR100 dataset (except two files (one for training and one for testing) are removed from the original dataset for the duplicated label)
1. Now, labels of the testing samples are removed for submission.

### To use the dataset for the best test accuracy, plz do either
1. Split the training set into training and validation
2. Use cross validation",True,True
4386,cifar100-image-classification-with-long-tail,"# Description

## MLDL 2021 Spring @ GIST
Coding assignment #3: Image category classification with CIFAR-100 dataset with long tail distribution

(Note: this is an individual basis assignment!)

## Task
Given a set of images and their label, please train your model to classify a test image into the label. The label is class id string (looks like a number) - {00, 01, ¡­, 09}. So, total of 100 classes.

The image size is 32 (width) by 32 (height) pixels in RGB (3) channels.

**Note**: Unlike CA1 and CA2, the dataset of CA3 has unequal number of samples across the classes.

## Evaluation metric
Top-1 accuracy: Compare with the true label with the predicted label by your trained model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

Top-1 accuracy: Compare with the true label with the predicted label by your trained model.","# Dataset Description

## CIFAR100 dataset with long tail distribution
Modified by Jonghyun Choi (2021.5.23)

*Disclaimer*: This dataset is not the same to the CIFAR100-LT dataset you can download from the web.

## Note
1. Filenames are all randomized.
1. Some of the training samples' label are randomized for the noisy label
1. Testing samples' label are *not* randomized
1. Number of samples for training and testing are the same to the CIFAR100 dataset (except two files (one for training and one for testing) are removed from the original dataset for the duplicated label)
1. Now, labels of the testing samples are removed for submission.
1. Unlike the dataset for CA1 and CA2, number of samples per class is all different.

### To use the dataset for the best test accuracy, plz do either
1. Split the training set into training and validation
2. Use cross validation",True,True
4387,pr-21-competition-classification,"# Description

<p>Competition For Pattern Recognition Course Spring 21 at Faculty of Computer &amp; information Sciences Ain Shams University </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
4388,maria-hoster,"# Description

MARIA PART2 TEST TEST TEST

This is TEST Competition. Only for me and my dark mind.

The Challenge
The sinking of the DarkMind is one of the most infamous shipwrecks in history.

On April 15, 2022, during her maiden voyage, the widely considered ¡°unsinkable¡± RMS DarkMind sank after colliding with an Valera. Unfortunately, there weren¡¯t enough rescue capsules for everyone onboard, resulting in the death of 10 out of 19 passengers and crew.

While there was some element of luck involved in surviving, it seems some groups of people were more likely to survive than others.

In this challenge, we ask you to build a predictive model that answers the question: ¡°what sorts of people were more likely to survive?¡± using passenger data (ie name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc).
----------------------
# Evaluation

Goal
It is your job to predict if a passenger survived the sinking of the Titanic or not.
For each in the test set, you must predict a 0 or 1 value for the variable.

Metric
Your score is the percentage of passengers you correctly predict. This is known as accuracy.

Submission File Format
You should submit a csv file with exactly 19 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond PassengerId and Survived) or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

PassengerId (sorted in any order)
Survived (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)
PassengerId,Survived
892,0
893,1
894,0
Etc.
You can download an example submission file (gender_submission.csv) on the Data page.","# Dataset Description

Overview The dataset is used for this competition is synthetic but based on a real dataset (in this case, the actual Titanic data!) and generated using a CTGAN. The statistical properties of this dataset are very similar to the original Titanic dataset, but there's no way to ""cheat"" by using public labels for predictions. How well does your model perform on truly unseen data? The data has been split into two groups: training set (train.csv) test set (test.csv) The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each passenger. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like passengers¡¯ gender and class. You can also use feature engineering to create new features. The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each passenger. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each passenger in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether or not they survived the sinking of the Synthanic. Data Dictionary Variable Definition Key survival Survival 0 = No, 1 = Yes pclass Ticket class 1 = 1st, 2 = 2nd, 3 = 3rd sex Sex Age Age in years sibsp # of siblings / spouses aboard the Titanic parch # of parents / children aboard the Titanic ticket Ticket number fare Passenger fare cabin Cabin number embarked Port of Embarkation C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton Variable Notes pclass: A proxy for socio-economic status (SES) 1st = Upper 2nd = Middle 3rd = Lower age: Age is fractional if less than 1. If the age is estimated, is it in the form of xx.5 sibsp: The dataset defines family relations in this way... Sibling = brother, sister, stepbrother, stepsister Spouse = husband, wife (mistresses and fianc¨¦s were ignored) parch: The dataset defines family relations in this way... Parent = mother, father Child = daughter, son, stepdaughter, stepson Some children travelled only with a nanny, therefore parch=0 for them.",True,True
