4421,csce5300-prediction-competition,"# Description

The goal of this competition is to predict bike share use, given the hour, day, and information about the weather. Companies like Divvy try to predict how much demand there will be for bikes on any given day to allocate resources to redistribute bikes so that, ideally, very few bike stations are ever full (when you can¡¯t park your bike) or empty (when you can¡¯t pick up a bike if you want to).

The [data tab][1] provides detailed information on the data set and necessary downloads.

See the class google doc page on details about teams, grading, and updated tips.


  [1]: https://www.kaggle.com/c/csce5300-prediction-competition/data
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submission Format

Your submission file must have a header line and should be structured in the following format:

     datetime,count
     2011-01-20 00:00:00,0 
     2011-01-20 01:00:00,0
     2011-01-20 02:00:00,0
     ...

The tutorial code should demonstrate how to generate such a file from a very simple prediction model. Note, these prediction are to be done on the test file under the data tab, where you do not know the actual count, and should match the rows of the test file in count and order. 

Your predictions will be scored by comparing to the ground truth information submitted when this competition was created. Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).","# Dataset Description

You are provided hourly rental data spanning two years. For this competition, the training set is comprised of the first 16 days of each month, while the test set is the 17-19th day of the month. You must predict the total count of bikes rented during each hour covered by the test set, using only information available prior to the rental period. That is, predict ""count"" without using ""count"" or it's components ""casual"" and ""registered"".


Data Fields


* **datetime** - hourly date + timestamp  
* **season** -  1 = spring, 2 = summer, 3 = fall, 4 = winter 
* **holiday** - whether the day is considered a holiday
* **workingday** - whether the day is neither a weekend nor holiday
* **weather** -  
    * 1 - Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy
    * 2 -  Mist + Cloudy, Mist + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist
    * 3 - Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds  
    * 4 - Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog 
* **temp** - temperature in Celsius
* **atemp** - ""feels like"" temperature in Celsius
* **humidity** - relative humidity
* **windspeed** - wind speed
* **casual** - number of non-registered user rentals initiated
* **registered** - number of registered user rentals initiated
* **count** - number of total rentals",True,True
4422,final-competition-mate06,"# Description

<p>This is a competition intended to evaluate student's skills about CNN theory teaching in MATE06-VCRP. All necessary details and tools are described in the Laboratory Practice, which must have been concluded before any submission to this competition.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions.</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F-Score</a>. The F1 score (also F-score or F-measure), commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Here are describe all files included in dataset and the format of each one.</p>
<h2>Descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Folder train</strong> - the training set of cats and dogs</li>
<li><strong>Folder validation </strong> - the test validation of cats and dogs</li>
<li><strong>Folder test</strong> - the test set without annotations of cats and dogs</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmissionPets.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>Readme.txt</strong> - supplemental information about the files</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - the identification of the image</li>
<li><strong>label</strong> -the label to a given class (0-cat or 1-dog)</li>
</ul>",True,True
4423,sliced-s01e02-xunyc5,"# Description

In this episode of SLICED, contestants are challenged to predict whether an aircraft strike with wildlife causes damage. The evaluation algorithm is LogLoss.

<div class=""note"">
<strong>Audience Participation and Prize!</strong><br>
Audience members can follow along, too, during the live show. If you participate, DO NOT CHEAT (e.g., use exploits in the data, use public data). The top audience participant at the end of the show will get a free Kaggle swag!
</div>


## How to participate

1. Log in or create a Kaggle account
2. Accept the competition's rules
3. Download the data (click on the ""Data"" tab) or create a notebook directly on Kaggle (click on the ""Code"" tab)
4. Train a model on `train.csv` predicting on `test.csv`
5. Generate a `submission.csv` file in the same format as the `sample_submission.csv`
6. Upload your `submission.csv` file on ""My Submissions""

**You get TEN SUBMISSIONS during the episode. They must be submitted before the end of the two-hour live coding is up.** You can hand select a submission from the ""My Submissions"" tab, otherwise the submission with the best public score (on 1% of the test data) will be taken and scored on the private leader board (on 99% of the test data).

## About SLICED
[SLICED](https://www.notion.so/SLICED-Show-c7bd26356e3a42279e2dfbafb0480073) is like the TV Show Chopped but for data science. Competitors get a never-before-seen dataset and two-hours to code a solution to a prediction challenge. Contestants get points for the best model plus bonus points for data visualization, votes from the audience, and more.

SLICED is streaming on Twitch Tuesdays at 8:30PM ET for twelve weeks starting June 1st: https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci. 

## About SLICED Season 1, Episode 2

- **Date**: June 8th at 8:30PM on https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci
- **Contestants**: Tony ""ItsMeToeKnee"" Elhabr, Julia Silge, Josh Pohlkamp-Hartt, Jordan ""data_dude"" Wilheim
- **Winner**: Jordan
- **Audience winner**: DataLab
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission format

Your submission should contain the columns `id, damaged` (a probability from 0 to 1). See `sample_submission.csv` on the ""Data"" tab for an example of what your file should look like. Make a submission by clicking on ""My Submissions"" and uploading your submission file. The evaluation algorithm is LogLoss.

**You only get TEN SUBMISSIONS during the episode. They must be submitted before the end of the two-hour live coding is up.** Select your preferred final submission on the ""My Submissions"" tab.","# Dataset Description

<p>The data is from the FAA. Contestants will use metadata about airstrikes against wildlife to predict whether the aircraft is damaged.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<h3>Data Dictionary</h3>
<ul>
<li>id: Incident identification number</li>
<li>incident_year: Year the incident occurred  (1990-2015)</li>
<li>incident_month: Month the incident occurred (1-12)</li>
<li>incident_day: Day the incident occurred (1-31)</li>
<li>operator_id: International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) code for aircraft operators</li>
    <ul><li>For example: FDX is the ICAO code for Fedex Express</li></ul>
<li>operator: Name of the Airline</li>
<li>aircraft: Model of aircraft involved in the strike</li>
    <ul><li>For example: B-777 means a Boeing 777 was struck in the incident</li></ul>
<li>aircraft_type</li>
    <ul>
    <li>'A': ""multi-engined aeroplanes powered by turbo-propeller engines with an MOPSC of more than nine or a maximum take-off mass exceeding 5 700 kg, and all multi-engined turbo-jet powered aeroplanes"" [1]</li>
    <li>'B': ""aeroplanes powered by propeller engines with an MOPSC of nine or less and a maximum take-off mass of 5 700 kg or less"" [1]</li>
    </ul>
<li>aircraft_make: International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) code for Aircraft Make</li>
<li>aircraft_model: International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) code for Aircraft Model</li>
<li>aircraft_mass</li>
    <ul>
    <li>1 = 2,250 kg or less</li>
    <li>2 = 2,251-5,700 kg</li>
    <li>3 = 5,701-27,000 kg</li>
    <li>4 = 27,001-272,000 kg</li>
    <li>5 = above 272,000 kg</li>
    </ul>
<li>engine_make: Engine Make Code</li>
<li>engine_model: Engine Model Code</li>
<li>engines: Number of engines powering the aircraft</li>
<li>engine_type: The way the engine is powered [2]</li>
    <ul>
    <li>'A' - Reciprocating</li>
    <li>'B' - Turbojet</li>
    <li>'C' - Turboprop</li>
    <li>'D' - Turbofan</li>
    <li>'E' - None (glider)</li>
    <li>'F' - Turboshaft (helicopter)</li>
    <li><b>Special Note:</b> Some aircrafts can have two engine types</li>
        <ul><li>Example: 'B/D'</li></ul>
    </ul>
<li>engine1_position: Where engine 1 is mounted on aircraft</li>
<li>engine2_position: Where engine 2 is mounted on aircraft</li>
<li>engine3_position: Where engine 3 is mounted on aircraft</li>
<li>engine4_position: Where engine 4 is mounted on aircraft</li>
<li>airport_id: Location of the strike given as the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) airport identifier</li>
<ul><li>For example: KAPA is the ICAO airport identifier for Centennial Airport</li></ul>
<li>airport: Name of the airport where the strike occurred</li>
<li>state: Abbreviated state from US, Canada</li>
<li>faa_region: FAA region where the airport is located [3][4]</li>
<ul>
<li>The US has 9 regions (AAL, ACE, AEA, AGL, ANE, ANM, ASO, ASW, AWP)</li>
<li>Canada has 5 regions (ATL, AWP, ONT, P&N, PAC, QUE)</li>
<li>All other regions of the world are considered foreign regions (FGN) </li>
</ul>
<img src=""https://www.faa.gov/airports/runway_safety/media/regions_.gif"" alt=""US Region Codes"" style=""width:380px;height:336px;"">
    <img src=""https://i.imgur.com/JN0czQk.gif"" alt=""Canada Region Codes"" style=""width:380px;height:336px;"">
<li>flight_phase: Phase of flight during which the strike occured</li>
    <ul>
     <li>APPROACH</li>
     <li>ARRIVAL</li>
     <li>CLIMB</li>
     <li>DEPARTURE</li>
     <li>DESCENT</li>
     <li>EN ROUTE</li>
     <li>LANDING</li>
     <li>LANDING ROLL</li>
     <li>LOCAL</li>
     <li>PARKED</li>
     <li>TAKEOFF RUN</li>
     <li>TAXI</li>
    </ul>
<li>visibility: Time of day when the strike occurred</li>
    <ul>
    <li>DAWN</li>
    <li>DAY</li>
    <li>DUSK</li>
    <li>NIGHT</li>
    <li>UNKNOWN</li>
    </ul>
<li>precipitation</li>
    <ul>
    <li>FOG</li>
    <li>RAIN</li>
    <li>SNOW</li>
    <li>NONE</li>
    <li><b>Special Note:</b> Can have multiple values</li>
    <ul><li>Example: FOG, RAIN</li></ul>
    </ul>
<li>height: Feet About Ground Level where the strike occurred (0-24,000)</li>
<li>speed: Velocity in Knots (0-2500)</li>
<li>distance: How many miles from the airport (0-100)</li>
<li>species_id: International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) code for type of wildlife</li>
<li>species_name: Common name for type of wildlife struck</li>
<li>species_quantity: Amount of wildlife struck</li>
    <ul>
    <li>1</li>
    <li>2-10</li>
    <li>11-100</li>
    <li>Over 100</li>
    </ul>
<li>flight_impact: How the strike impacted the flight</li>
<ul>
    <li>ABORTED</li>
    <li>TAKEOFF</li>
    <li>ENGINE SHUTDOWN</li>
    <li>NONE</li>
    <li>OTHER</li>
    <li>PRECAUTIONARY LANDING</li>
</ul>
<li>damaged</li>
 <ul>
    <li>0 - There was no damage</li>
    <li>1 - There was damage</li>
</ul>
<!-- Commented out until after the competition is completed
References<br>
[1] <a href=""https://eur-lex.europa.eu/LexUriServ/LexUriServ.do?uri=OJ:L:2012:296:0001:0148:En:PDF"">COMMISSION REGULATION (EU) No 965/2012</a><br>
[2] <a href=""https://wildlife.faa.gov/search"">FAA Wildlife Strike Database Search Engine</a>
[3] <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/airports/runway_safety/regions/"">FAA Runway Safety Group</a><br>
[4] <a href=""https://tc.canada.ca/en/corporate-services/regionshttps://tc.canada.ca/en/corporate-services/regions"">Regions</a>

-->",True,True
