4447,talpiot-limonackathon-2021,"# Description

<p>As you might know, the threat of rockets being shot at our cities and citizens is a major problem IDF deals with. In this task you will analyse this threat and help solving a key question regarding it: the problem of classifying the type of rocket launched at us.</p>
<p>In order to do so we used are tight connections with the country of Talpiland. Sadly, this fractured country has to deal with isles of rebels within its boundaries. Those rebels constantly launch missiles of different types on Talpiland's poor citizens. They agreed to share with us their large datasets regarding those missiles, so we could try solving the question at hand.</p><p>
</p><p>You are given the file train.csv. It contains information about the tracks of rockets launched at Talpiland. Talpiland used its many radars to record it. Each record has the followinng properties:</p>
1. traj_ind - an id of a specific rocket's trajectory
2. time_stamp - the time stamp of a point in the trajectory, as recorded in the radar.
3. x,y,z - coordinates of the point in the trajectory, as recorded in the radar.
4. label - the type of the rocket.

<p>Your submission file should look like the example given to you.</p><p>

</p><p>Your grade will be calculated as follow: For each class, the accuracy of your predictions will be calculated. Your grade will be the mean of these accuracies.</p><p>

</p><p>\[ MeanAcc= \frac{1}{\#classes}\sum_{i \in classes} {Acc(i)}\]</p><p></p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>This challenge is heavily based on a hackathon hosted by Ofek unit.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Your grade will be calculated as follow: For each class, the accuracy of your predictions will be calculated. Your grade will be the mean of these accuracies.</p><p>

</p><p>\[ MeanAcc= \frac{1}{\#classes}\sum_{i \in classes} {Acc(i)}\]</p><p></p>
<hr>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For every trajectory index in the test data set you should predict a label of the given possible labels.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set. check it out ;)</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission_1.csv</strong> - an example for a submission file</li>",True,True
4448,sliced-s01e03-DcSXes,"# Description

In this episode of SLICED, contestants are challenged to predict profit from a dataset of superstores. The evaluation algorithm is RMSE.

<div class=""note"">
<strong>Audience Participation!</strong><br>
Audience members can follow along, too, during the live show. If you participate, DO NOT CHEAT (e.g., use exploits in the data, use public data).
</div>



## How to participate

1. Log in or create a Kaggle account
2. Accept the competition's rules
3. Download the data (click on the ""Data"" tab) or create a notebook directly on Kaggle (click on the ""Code"" tab)
4. Train a model on `train.csv` predicting on `test.csv`
5. Generate a `submission.csv` file in the same format as the `sample_submission.csv`
6. Upload your `submission.csv` file on ""My Submissions""

**You get TEN SUBMISSIONS during the episode. They must be submitted before the end of the two-hour live coding is up.** You can hand select a submission from the ""My Submissions"" tab, otherwise the submission with the best public score (on 1% of the test data) will be taken and scored on the private leader board (on 99% of the test data).

## About SLICED
[SLICED](https://www.notion.so/SLICED-Show-c7bd26356e3a42279e2dfbafb0480073) is like the TV Show Chopped but for data science. Competitors get a never-before-seen dataset and two-hours to code a solution to a prediction challenge. Contestants get points for the best model plus bonus points for data visualization, votes from the audience, and more.

SLICED is streaming on Twitch Tuesdays at 8:30PM ET for twelve weeks starting June 1st: https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci. 

SLICED is possible thanks to support from:

* RStudio
* Streamlit
* NVIDIA
* Kaggle

## About SLICED Season 1, Episode 3

- **Date**: June 15th at 8:30PM on https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci
- **Contestants**: Ethan Douglas, Michael Mullarkey, Kyle ""nschampions2004"" Ligon, Landon ""piqcyl"" Buechner
- **Winner**: TBD!
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission format

Your submission should contain two columns: `id` and `profit`. See `sample_submission.csv` on the ""Data"" tab for an example of what your file should look like. Make a submission by clicking on ""My Submissions"" and uploading your submission file. The evaluation algorithm is RMSE.

**You only get TEN SUBMISSIONS during the episode. They must be submitted before the end of the two-hour live coding is up.** Select your preferred final submission on the ""My Submissions"" tab.","# Dataset Description

<p>The data is about Super Store sales. Contestants will use metadata about Super Store sales to predict profit. Each row is a product and volume of sales for a single product in a given store.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<p>The test set (test.csv) is a holdout of several states' orders.</p>

<h3>Data Dictionary</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> unique id per row</li>
<li><strong>ship_mode</strong> what mode was used to ship the item: 'First Class', 'Same Day', 'Second Class', 'Standard Class'</li>
<li><strong>segment</strong> whether the recipient is corporate or consumer</li>
<li><strong>country</strong> always United States</li>
<li><strong>city</strong> where the item was shipped (a city in the United States)</li>
<li><strong>state</strong> where the item was shipped (a state in the United States)</li>
<li><strong>postal_code</strong> ZIP Code in the United States</li>
<li><strong>region</strong> region in the United States: 'Central', 'East', 'South', 'West'</strong></li>
<li><strong>category</strong> type of item: 'Furniture', 'Office Supplies', 'Technology'</li>
<li><strong>sub_category</strong> sub-type of item: 'Accessories', 'Appliances', 'Art', 'Binders', 'Bookcases', 'Chairs', 'Copiers', 'Envelopes', 'Fasteners', 'Furnishings', 'Labels', 'Machines', 'Paper', 'Phones', 'Storage', 'Supplies', 'Tables'</li>
<li><strong>sales</strong> sales made for the order (USD)</li>
<li><strong>quantity</strong> quantity sold of the item</li>
<li><strong>discount</strong> discount applied to the order(from 0 to 1)</li>
<li><strong>profit</strong> profit made on the order (USD)</li>
</ul>",True,True
