4480,sda-eant-4,"# Description

# **Objetivo**
Competencia del 3er Workshop del curso de Social Data Analytics de EANT (Escuela Argentina de Nuevas Tecnolog¨ªas. La idea es generar un modelo de aprendizaje autom¨¢tico b¨¢sico para predecir qu¨¦ ¨¢rbol de la ciudad es un Fresno americano.

# **Fuente**
Dataset adaptado del [dataset de Arbolado P¨²blico Lineal](https://data.buenosaires.gob.ar/dataset/arbolado-publico-lineal) publicado por el GCBA en su portal de datos abiertos.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>La evaluaci¨®n de la competencia va a ser con una m¨¦trica tan difundida como simple: Accuracy, o como se conoce en Kaggle, ""Categorization Accuracy"". Basicamente, es el porcentaje de registros predichos correctamente. Es decir, la suma de verdaderos positivos y verdaderos negativos sobre el total de registros. </p>
<h2>Formato de subida</h2>
<p>El archivo a subir debe contener una fila con el id correspondiente del dataset de testing, y una columna llamada ""fres_am"" cuyos valores son ""si"" para indicar si es un Fresno Americano y ""no"" si no lo es (generada por las predicciones del modelo).</p>
<p>el archivo debe tener el siguiente formato:</p>
<pre>id,fres_am<br>1,si<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Aqu¨ª pueden encontrar toda la data necesaria para la competencia. Cuenta con 3 archivos: un archivo de training para entrenar el modelo, otro de test del cual deben generar las predicciones a partir del modelo que generen con training y un archivo de ejemplo de c¨®mo hay que estructurar el archivo para subir las predicciones (solo la columna de id y la columna a predecir, la que indica si el ¨¢rbol es un fresno americano o no</p>
<h2>Archivos</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_sub.csv</strong> - archivo de ejemplo para subir las predicciones</li>
</ul>",True,True
4481,softec21-artificial-intelligence-competition,"# Description

<h2>Artificial Intelligence Competition Rules </h2>
1. Each team can consist of a minimum of 1 and a maximum of 3 members. 
2. As the competition is online, teams will use their laptops
3. Any collaboration with other teams is prohibited. 
4. Problems will be posted on Kaggle, and teams are required to submit their solutions on
Kaggle. 
5. Use of Internet is allowed. 
6. Duration of the event is 24 hours. 
7. The problem statement and dataset will be made available on Kaggle when the competition
starts. 
8. 30 minute long evaluation will be conducted for each team and questions will be asked by
our panel of judges.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top (the page title appears above). For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have copied here:</p>
<hr>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/wiki/MeanFScore""> Mean F1-Score</a>. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:</p>
<p>\[ F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \]</p>
<p>The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: AuthorId and DuplicateAuthorIds. DuplicateAuthorIds should be a space-delimited list. Every AuthorId counts as his/her own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids.&nbsp;For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list (A,A B C), (B,B A C), (C,C A B).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds<br>1,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>Please refer to the question statement and data set provided by the competition head via Email!</p>",True,True
4482,DATACUP-ROUND1,"# Description

