4501,wikipedia-image-caption,"# Description

A picture is worth a thousand words, yet sometimes a few will do. We all rely on online images for knowledge sharing, learning, and understanding. Even the largest websites are missing visual content and metadata to pair with their images. Captions and ¡°[alt text](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Accessibility/Alternative_text_for_images)¡± increase accessibility and enable better search. The majority of images on Wikipedia articles, for example, don't have any written context connected to the image. Open models could help anyone improve accessibility and learning for all.

Current solutions rely on simple methods based on translations or page interlinks, which have limited coverage. Even the most advanced computer vision image captioning isn't suitable for images with complex semantics.


In this competition, you¡¯ll build a model that automatically retrieves the text closest to an image. Specifically, you'll train your model to associate given images with article titles or complex captions, in multiple languages. The best models will account for the semantic granularity of Wikipedia images.

If successful, you'll be contributing to the accessibility of the largest online encyclopedia. The millions of Wikipedia readers and editors will be able to more easily understand, search, and describe media at scale. As a result, you¡¯ll contribute to an open model to improve learning for all.

--
This competition is organized by the [Research](https://research.wikimedia.org/) team at the [Wikimedia Foundation](https://wikimediafoundation.org/). This competition is based on the [WIT dataset](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/wit) published by Google Research as detailed in this [SIGIR paper](https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3404835.3463257).


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [NDCG@5](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discounted_cumulative_gain#Normalized_DCG) (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain).

## Submission File
The submission should be a list of `id`,`caption_title_and_reference_description` pairs ranked from top to bottom according to their relevance (i.e., the top `id` is the most relevant `caption_title_and_reference_description`), with up to 5 predictions per `id`. Each line should be a single `id`,`caption_title_and_reference_description` pair.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,caption_title_and_reference_description
    0,kaggle the home of data science
    0,lorem ipsum
    ...
    1,the quick brown fox
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **September 13, 2021** - Start Date.

* **December 2, 2021** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **December 2, 2021** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **December 9, 2021** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

The top three winning teams will receive Wikipedia-branded merchandise","# Dataset Description

The objective of this competition is to predict the target `caption_title_and_reference_description` given information about an images. The targets for this competition are in multiple languages.

## Files

*   **train-{0000x}-of-00005.tsv** - the training data (tab delimited)
*   **test.tsv** - the test data; the objective is to predict the target `caption_title_and_reference_description` for each row `id`
*  **test_captions_list.csv** - a list of captions to retrieve for test predictions
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format; note that multiple predictions (up to 5) are allowed for each `id` in the test data.
*   **image_data_test/**
  * **image_pixels/test_image_pixels_part-{0000x}.csv.gz**
      * `image_url`: url to the original image file, e.g. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Hovden.jpg
      * `b64_bytes`: base64 encoded bytes of the image file at a 300px resolution
      * `metadata_url`: url to the commons page of the image, e.g. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hovden.jpg
  * **resnet_embeddings/test_resnet_embeddings_part-{0000x}.csv.gz**
      * `image_url`: url to the original image file, e.g. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Hovden.jpg
      * `embedding`: a comma separated list of 2048 float values
* **image_data_train** - Due to the size of the training image data (~275 Gb), it is hosted separately and can be found [here](https://storage.cloud.google.com/wikimedia-image-caption-public/image_data_train.tar). Note that not all of the training observations have corresponding image data.",True,True
4502,bcc-2021-datathon-1,"# Description

Jagad adalah akuntan publik di salah satu lembaga penyedia jasa kredit. Selama bulan september sampai desember, Jagad menyimpan data transaksi dari customer jasa kredit tempat jagad bekerja. 

Dalam data tersebut beberapa customer yang gagal bayar pada bulan selanjutnya. Jagad ingin membuat model yang dapat memprediksi apakah seorang customer gagal bayar atau tidak berdasarkan data yang diberikan. 

Bantu lah jagad dan berikan rekomendasi menggunakan model machine learning. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Penilaian</h2>
<p>Penilaian menggunakan metrik Categorization Accuracy</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>File berformat csv berisi header berupa nama kolom, yaitu id setiap customer pada file test dan default_payment setiap customer</p>
<pre>Id,default_payment<br>1,1<br>3,0<br>12,1<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_data.csv</strong> - Dataset Traint</li>
<li><strong>test_dataSubmissionSample.csv</strong> - Contoh submisi data</li>
<li><strong>test_dataAsk.csv</strong> - Dataset test untuk diprediksi labelnya</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - id customer</li>
<li><strong>LIMIT_BAL</strong> - Jumlah pinjaman kredit yang diberikan</li>
<li><strong>SEX</strong> - Jenis kelamin (1=Laki, 2 = Cewek)</li>
<li><strong>EDUCATION</strong> - Pendidikan Terakhir  (1=graduate school, 2=university, 3=high school, 4=others, 5=unknown, 6=unknown)</li>
<li><strong>MARRIAGE</strong> - Status pernikahan (1=menikah, 2=single, 3=lainnya)</li>
<li><strong>AGE</strong> -Umur (dalam tahun)</li>
<li><strong>PAY_0</strong> - Pembayaran pada bulan september (-2 = Tagihan dibayar penuh, -1 = Tagihan dibayar penuh,  0=Tidak ada transaksi, 1= Tagihan telat 1 bulan, 2=Tagihan telat 2 bulan, dst)</li>
<li><strong>PAY_1</strong> - Pembayaran pada bulan oktober </li>
<li><strong>PAY_2</strong> - Pembayaran pada bulan november</li>
<li><strong>PAY_AMT1</strong> - Jumlah tagihan yang dibayar pada bulan september</li>
<li><strong>PAY_AMT2</strong> - Jumlah tagihan yang dibayar pada bulan oktober</li>
<li><strong>PAY_AMT3</strong> - Jumlah tagihan yang dibayar pada bulan november</li>
<li><strong>BILL_AMT1</strong> - Jumlah tagihan pada bulan september</li>
<li><strong>BILL_AMT2</strong> - Jumlah tagihan pada bulan okteber</li>
<li><strong>BILL_AMT3</strong> - Jumlah tagihan pada bulan november</li>
<li><strong>BILL_AMT3</strong> - Status pemberian kredit di bulan selanjutnya</li>
<li><strong>default payment</strong> - Gagal bayar pelanggan pada bulan selanjutnya (0=Ya, 1 = Tidak)</li>
</ul",True,True
4503,data-mining-proyecto-1,"# Description

Objective: To understand the basic concepts of classification such as general process of a classification task, training/test data, model validation, overfitting, model selection, evaluation measures, etc.. By designing experiments and comparing the performance of different classifiers, to understand each advanced classification technique and its advantages.  

# Instructions

### 1. Select several classifiers that you think appropriate on this classification task. You are suggested to try at least one from each category: 
Category 1: KNN, Decision Trees, Naive Bayes
Category 2: SVM, AdaBoost, RandomForest, Neural Network

Don¡ät program the algorithms. Use Python, R, Matlab, etc..

### 2. Train the classifiers using the training data provided in this page. Predict labels for the testing set, and upload your predictions for the classifiers by April 15, 2024.

You will need to upload each classifier's predictions in a separate submission. Please title your submissions to reflect which algorithm was used, and specify the algorithm in the submission description.


### 3. Write project report (no less than 5 pages). 
You are suggested to use the following template (not obligated, but at least include all the following elements): 


****
I.	Introduction 
This section includes introduction of classification, the algorithms you used in the project, evaluation metrics.  Remember mention the objective of the project. 
II.	Data sets description
In this section, characteristics of the data set such as size, number and type of attributes, classes should be clearly described to present the problem you are solving. 
III.	Classifiers
Introduce the models/algorithms you used to solve the classification problem. Additionally, explain the reason why you chose them.  
IV.	Experiments and Results 
Introduce the platform or toolbox you used. 
Experiment design, which includes parameter values, number of runs, evaluation measures, overfit problem addressing, etc.
Show experiment results, comparisons, analysis and discussions. It is necessary to illustrate the results and comparisons in different ways such as table, figure, screenshots, etc. Remember make explanation on the figures and tables, don¡ät just leave them there without any comments in the context. 
V.	Conclusion
Conclude the project, discuss the limitations and possible improving methods.
VI.	References
Put the references you took in the project design and report writing, including books, papers, websites of data source, software documents, etc. 
****
  
### 4. Deliver the project report by April 15, 2024. 


### 5. Prepare a 20-minutes presentation and present your project in the class on Wednesday April 17, 2024. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean F1-Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives *tp* to all predicted positives *tp+fp*. Recall is the ratio of true positives *tp* to all actual positives *tp+fn*. The F1 score is given by:

\\( F1 = 2\frac{p \cdot r}{p+r}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp},\ \ r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn} \\)

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

Mean F1-Score calculates F1 metrics for each label, and finds their average, weighted by support, i.e. the number of true instances for each label. Calculated with http://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html using average='weighted'.
In your report you are encouraged to show your results in various metrics such as precision,  accuracy, recall, etc.,  and explain the relations among them.  

# Submission File

For each id in the test set, you must predict the target variable ""income"". The file should be in csv format and contain a header. See the sample submission ""zero_solution.csv"" under the Data tab for a complete example.

----------------------
# Grading

# Report

80% of your grade will be based on the report.

# Presentation

20% of your grade will be based on the class presentation.

# Extra Credit

The student with the top ranking submission in the private leaderboard will receive 5% extra credit in the course.","# Dataset Description

This page shows up underneath the data files. The data comes from https://www.kaggle.com/uciml/adult-census-income, and was cleaned to remove missing entries. 

- training.csv
Train your models using the training set. 

- testing.csv
Predict the income for the testing set using your models.

- zero_solution.csv
 This is a sample submission, you should submit your solution to this platform in the same format.",True,True
