<li><strong>movie_title</strong> - the title of a movie</li>
<li>etc...</li>
</ul>asdfasdf",True,True
4571,beat-the-system-snu21,"# Description

# You have been given a stop &amp; reverse based moving averages crossover strategy that needs to be optimized.
Practically everyone knows the standard moving average crossover signal. If the fast moving average crosses above the slower (longer period) moving average, it is time to buy. Vice versa for short trades.

This type of signal used to work pretty well with the default values for the fast and slow periods, but as new algorithmic traders entered the scene, practically everyone started trading it! This reduced the usefulness of the signal.

But all is not lost. The strategy stills works if the periods are adapted for that particular asset or market.


## This forms the crux of this challenge.
Given a MA crossover strategy, you're required to quantitatively find the optimal fast and slow MA periods for the given asset that works well over the years. You can use any optimization method, pure Algo or even Machine Learning based.

The optimization is multi-objective, meaning you'll have to find the optimal balance between 3 parameters, namely:

- Net Profit &amp; Loss
- Average Profit &amp; Loss
- Win Percentage
Your aim is to minimize your MAPE score in both the public and private leaderboards to as close to 0 as possible.
## Note:
1. Refer to the sample notebook for help understanding how to use the Backtest system, visualize important metrics, and generate submission files.
2. The train data provided is for you to run your optimization method on and see how varying MA periods affect the strategy. The test data is withheld and will only be used for out-of-sample evaluations. The GenerateSubmission module will automatically run the best parameters provided by you to it and give you the out-of-sample score.
3. Because of the above constraint, you're required to code in the Kaggle Notebook Environment only.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Refer to the sample submission file provided to see how the format needs to be. Additionally, using the GenerateSubmission class provided in the sample notebook should help you instantly create a submission file.

We have the best available parameters and we use the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of your strategy metrics and the best possible metrics to generate a Public and a Private Score.

# Your aim is to minimize your MAPE score to as close to 0 as possible","# Dataset Description

The train data provided is for you to run your optimization method on and see how varying MA periods affect the strategy. The test data is withheld and will only be used for out-of-sample evaluations. The GenerateSubmission module will automatically run the best parameters provided by you to it and give you the out-of-sample score.

### File descriptions
1. train_data.csv - the training set
2. sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format
### Data fields
- Timestamp - Date and time corresponding to the OHLC candle formed
- Open - Candle body open
- High - Candle wick high
- Low - Candle wick low
- Close - Candle body close",True,True
4572,show-your-data-skills-snu21,"# Description

# Given the 5minute OHLCV data of a stock, can you clean and convert it to it's daily (24hour) OHLCV data?
## Steps for cleaning:
1.Remove any NaN/Null values, if any.
2.Remove those rows which have volume less than 15.
3.Keep only timestamps between 10 and 22 hours (10<=timestamp hour<=22).
4.Resample the resultant pandas DataFrame to 24hour intervals. While resampling, take special care to set the values by the below logic:
- Open is the Open of the first row in the given interval
- High is the highest High in the given interval
- Low is the lowest Low in the given interval
- Close is the last Close in the given interval
- Volume is the sum of all the volume traded during that interval
### Ensure that the resultant DataFrame has no NaN values and has Timestamp either as a datetime index or column. The resultant DataFrame to be saved should also have 5 columns: Open, High, Low, Close, Timestamp with valid values.

## Save and submit this resultant resampled 24hour interval DataFrame.
## Important: Do all this work in a Kaggle Notebook itself, since we would need to see your code as well.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation
# We expect 874 rows of resampled data and 5 columns: Timestamp, Open, High, Low, Close.
Evaluation will be done using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). Your objective is to achieve a RMSE score of as close to 0 as possible (Ideally the score should be 0 for a perfect competition of the tasks).","# Dataset Description

