4590,iml2021summer,"# Description

Cars4U, is an on-demand car aggregator and rental service operating in New York. Under its purview, the company has cars which are either owned by the company itself or is listed on the platform by other car owners (Airbnb for Cars). The company has recently raised a $ 10 million Series A funding and is looking to expand its services. In order to do so the company looking to strategically position its cars in various parts of the city by predicting the demand.

Cars4U is looking for talented data scientists such as you to help them with predicting the demand for their cars. The company has provided a dataset detailing their historical trends and is now looking for help to predict the demand.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is root-mean-square error (RMSE). The RMSE score, is a frequently used measure of the differences between values (sample or population values) predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed. The RMSE score is given by:

![](https://i.ibb.co/G7tqcWY/rmse-1.jpg)

# Submission Format
For every unique 'Date' and 'Time' combination in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Demand.

The file should have the following format:

```
Id,Demand
1,23
```","# Dataset Description

'Demand' is the dependent variable that has to be predicted using the remainder of the independent varibales provided in the dataset.

#File descriptions

train.csv - The training dataset.

test.csv - The test dataset.

sample_submission.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format. This is the format in which you are expected to submit your solution.

# Data fields

Id - Unique Identifier

Date - Date

Time - Time

Season - spring, summer, fall, winter

IsHoliday - Is the given 'date' a holiday

IsWorkingDay - Is the given 'date' a working day i.e. neither a holiday nor weekend

Weather - Clear, Cloudy, Light Rain, Heavy Rain

Temperature - Acutal temperature recorded (in Celcius)

AdoptedTemperature - Adopted or feels like temperature (in Celcius)

Humidity - Humidity

WindSpeed - WindSpeed

Demand - Number of cars at the service location",True,True
4591,fds-flower-classification-2021,"# Description

Did you know there are approximately 369,000 flowering plants discovered around the world in 2016? According to a report titled ¡°State of the World¡¯s Plants¡±, released by researchers at the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew, in the United Kingdom, there are around 391,000 species of vascular plants discovered and 94 percent of them are flowering plants.

However, in this competition, you are not going to classify all of 369,000 flowering plants, but only 17 of them. You will classify them via their images and you will be working with a dataset consisting of 1,088 labeled images used for training your model and 272 unlabeled images used in this competition to evaluate how well can your model classify flowers.

## Acknowledgements
We thank Maria-Elena Nilsback and Andrew Zisserman for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Categorization Accuracy](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy). The Categorization Accuracy score, commonly used in multi-class classification, measures accuracy by calculating number of correct predictions divided by total number of predictions. The Categorization Accuracy score is given by:

![An image](https://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Cdpi%7B150%7D%20%5Ctext%7BAccuracy%7D%20%3D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Ctext%7BNumber%20of%20correct%20predictions%7D%7D%7B%5Ctext%7BTotal%20number%20of%20predictions%7D%7D)

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: **image** and **class\_id** (**image** should be a list of filenames and **class\_id** should be predicted label for each image

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
image,class_id
test_000000.jpg,1
test_000001.jpg,5
test_000002.jpg,4
```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions

**training** - the training data 

**testing** - the testing data

**train.csv** - the training labels (0 -> 16)

**sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

**mapping.csv** - a file contains names for each label


## Data fields

**train.csv / sample_submission.csv**

- `image` - the name of a image file

- `class_id` - the ID for the flower

**mapping.csv**

- `class_id` - the ID for the flower

- `class_name` - the name of the flower",True,True
4592,time-series-l1-hackathon-1,"# Description

<p>A global pollution control board wants to use their vast data source to build an efficient forecasting model to predict the pollution scores for each of the 21 regions in their portfolio using historical average pollution score data for the past ~16 years (1995 to 2011) on a monthly basis. An average uncertainty metric of pollution score is also available for each month - region wise.</p>

<p>However, other geo-environmental information of the regions is not available. Can we still forecast the pollution score values for every region for the next 24 months accurately?</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAPE(Mean Absolute Percentage Error), The MAPE is a measure of how much the forecasts deviate w.r.t the actual values. Lower the deviation, better the forecasts.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
- <p>For every <strong>Region x Date</strong> combination in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: ID and Forecast.
- Forecast should be a non-negative number.

```
ID,Forecast
R-001_01-10-2011,100
```</p>","# Dataset Description

