4616,beat-the-systems-jiit,"# Description

<p> </p><h2>You have been given a stop &amp; reverse based moving averages crossover strategy that needs to be optimized.</h2><p></p>

<p> Practically everyone knows the standard moving average crossover signal. If the fast moving average crosses above the slower (longer period) moving average, it is time to buy. Vice versa for short trades. </p><p>This type of signal used to work pretty well with the default values for the fast and slow periods, but as new algorithmic traders entered the scene, practically everyone started trading it! This reduced the usefulness of the signal. </p><p> <b>But all is not lost. The strategy stills works if the periods are adapted for that particular asset or market.</b></p>

<p> <b></b></p><h2><b>This forms the crux of this challenge.</b></h2><p></p><p> Given a MA crossover strategy, you're required to quantitatively find the optimal fast and slow MA periods for the given asset that works well over the years. You can use any optimization method, pure Algo or even Machine Learning based. 

</p><p>The optimization is multi-objective, meaning you'll have to find the optimal balance between 3 parameters, namely: 
</p><ul><li>Net Profit &amp; Loss</li> <li>Average Profit &amp; Loss</li> <li>Win Percentage</li></ul>

## Your aim is to minimize your MAPE score in both the public and private leaderboards to as close to 0 as possible.

<p></p>

<p></p><h3><b>Note:</b></h3>
<ol><li>Refer to the sample notebook for help understanding how to use the Backtest system, visualize important metrics, and generate submission files.</li>
<li>The train data provided is for you to run your optimization method on and see how varying MA periods affect the strategy. The test data is withheld and will only be used for out-of-sample evaluations. The GenerateSubmission module will automatically run the best parameters provided by you to it and give you the out-of-sample score.</li>
<li>Because of the above constraint, you're required to code in the Kaggle Notebook Environment only.</li></ol><p> </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> Refer to the sample submission file provided to see how the format needs to be. Additionally, using the GenerateSubmission class provided in the sample notebook should help you instantly create a submission file. </p>

<p> We have the best available parameters and we use the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of your strategy metrics and the best possible metrics to generate a Public and a Private Score. </p>

## Your aim is to minimize your MAPE score to as close to 0 as possible","# Dataset Description

<p>The train data provided is for you to run your optimization method on and see how varying MA periods affect the strategy. The test data is withheld and will only be used for out-of-sample evaluations. The GenerateSubmission module will automatically run the best parameters provided by you to it and give you the out-of-sample score.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_data.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timestamp</strong> - Date and time corresponding to the OHLC candle formed</li>
<li><strong>Open</strong> - Candle body open</li>
<li><strong>High</strong> - Candle wick high</li>
<li><strong>Low</strong> - Candle wick low</li>
<li><strong>Close</strong> - Candle body close</li>
</ul>",True,True
4617,xenophobic-tweets-tc2011,"# Description

<p>This is the home page of the competition for the course <strong>TC2011-202113 Intelligent Systems</strong>, by Dr. Leonardo Chang at Tecnologico de Monterrey, campus Santa Fe.</p>

<p>Upon completion of this course as well as this competition, students will be able to formulate, design and develop simple intelligent systems based on different machine learning approaches.</p>

<h2>Problem Description</h2>
<p><strong>Xenophobia</strong> has been a social behavior present in people since the beginning of humanity. Fear and rejection of the different have led many people to distrust, belittle, and even hate other people who belong to a different social environment. This xenophobic behavior is so strong that it has managed to join the internet through social networks. Hence, in this course, you should create a classifier able for detecting Xenophobic Tweets using only the text of some tweets.</p>

<p>You can download a training dataset (TrainingDS) with 1,500 objects, which contain the text of the tweet as well as a label for each tweet defining if it contains a Xenophobic sentiment (1) or not (0). An example of them are:</p>

<pre>496,""Trump wants to escalate the racism of his base because that's his (and his party's) only message -- you're better than them, you"",0</pre>

<pre>497, ""And you can't send them back to use in gen 7 or 6 afterwards, so they're trapped in home with no way to ever bring them into a game to use"",1</pre>

<p>Note that each object has the format [ID],[text],[class]. Also, you can download a testing file (TestingDS.csv) for assessing your model. All objects are into a CSV file. In the Evaluation and Data sections, you can find more information about the feature model and the metric for evaluating your model.</p>

<h2>Deadline</h2>
<p>November 23, 2021 [23:59 UTC] is the deadline for this competition. 
Upon completion of this course as well as this competition, students will be able to formulate, design and develop simple intelligent systems based on different machine learning approaches.</p>


----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>There are several measures to evaluate the performance of a classifier. Nevertheless, the most used measure for class imbalance problems is the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic curve (AUC)(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic#Area_under_the_curve). As bot detection is labeled as a class imbalance problem, the evaluation metric for this competition is the AUC, which is computed as:</p>

<p>\[AUC = \frac{1+TP_{rate} - FP_{rate}}{2}\]</p>

<p>where TPrate is the ratio of objects belonging to the xenophobic class that are well-classified and FPrate is the ratio of misclassified objects belonging to the no-xenophobic class.</p>

<p>The AUC metric weights TPrate and FPrate equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both TPrate and FPrate simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.</p>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p>For every tweet in the testing dataset, submission files should contain two columns: ID and Class. The Class column should contain the binary classification 0 or 1 of your model for the associate tweet.</p>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,Class
1,0
2,1
3,1 
4,0
etc.
</pre>

<p>It important to highlight that your submission file should contain 501 rows, one for the header and the remaining ones for each classification result.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>About the data</h2>

<p>This page is devoted to show all information regarding the dataset used in this competition.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given tweet</li>
<li><strong>Text</strong> - the text of a given tweet </li>
<li><strong>Class</strong> - the class associated to a given tweet in the TrainingDS.csv and TestingDS.csv files (1 for Xenophobic and 0 for No-Xenophobic)</li>
</ul>",True,True
